/**
 * Teach Me explanations for literature, written in advance so they load
 * instantly, work offline and cost nothing per student. Keyed by
 * passageId|question|options (see teachMeKey in app.js). A question with no
 * entry here falls back to the live AI explanation.
 */
(window.TEACH_ME = window.TEACH_ME || {})["literature"] = {
 "|In Chinua Achebe's 'Things Fall Apart', what was Okonkwo's greatest fear?|Poverty|Appearing weak and unmanly like his father|Losing his land|The arrival of the missionaries": "Chinua Achebe's Things Fall Apart (1958) is set in Umuofia, an Igbo clan, just before and during the arrival of the British. Its hero, Okonkwo, is a famous wrestler and warrior whose whole life is shaped by fear of being like his father, Unoka: a lazy, debt-ridden flute player whom the clan called an agbala (a man with no title, also a word for \"woman\"). Achebe says Okonkwo's life was ruled by \"the fear of failure and of weakness\". This fear makes him harsh to his family and leads him to kill Ikemefuna so as not to look weak: B.\nMemory tip: Okonkwo runs from Unoka; everything he does is the opposite of his father.",
 "|The statement 'My love is a red, red rose' is an example of:|A simile|A metaphor|Personification|Irony": "A metaphor compares two things directly by saying one IS the other, without \"like\" or \"as\". As printed, \"My love is a red, red rose\" says the love is a rose, so it is a metaphor: B. A simile makes the comparison openly with \"like\" or \"as\". Robert Burns's real line is \"O my Luve is like a red, red rose\", and that version is a simile. So always judge the words actually printed in front of you.\nThe trap: A (simile) catches students who remember Burns's line with \"like\"; the printed version has no \"like\".\nMemory tip: Simile Says \"like\"; Metaphor Means \"is\".",
 "|Which of the following best defines a 'tragedy' in classical literature?|A light-hearted story with a happy ending|A serious work involving the downfall of a dignified character through a fatal flaw|A comic play about ordinary people|A religious narrative about saints": "In classical (Aristotelian) terms, a tragedy is a serious play in which a person of high rank and good character falls from happiness to ruin through hamartia (a tragic flaw or error of judgement). The fall arouses pity and fear in the audience and ends in catharsis, a cleansing of those emotions. Oedipus Rex is the model; Okonkwo and Odewale are African examples of the tragic hero: B. A light-hearted story with a happy ending describes comedy.\nMemory tip: Tragedy = High rank + Flaw + Fall + Pity and fear.",
 "|The literary device in which the end of one line is carried over to the next without a pause is called:|Caesura|Enjambment|End-stopping|Alliteration": "Enjambment (a \"run-on line\") is when a sentence or phrase runs on from one line of verse into the next with no punctuation or pause at the line end: B. In J.P. Clark's \"Ibadan\" the words \"like broken / china in the sun\" run across the line break. A line that ends with a pause or punctuation is end-stopped. A caesura is a pause in the MIDDLE of a line.\nThe trap: A (caesura) is also about pauses, but a caesura is a break inside a line, while enjambment is the absence of a break at the end of one.\nMemory tip: Enjambment = the line \"jumps\" on; caesura = a \"cut\" in the middle.",
 "|In Wole Soyinka's 'The Lion and the Jewel', Lakunle represents:|Traditional African values|Blind imitation of Western ways and blind rejection of tradition|Wise elder leadership|Religious extremism": "In Wole Soyinka's comedy The Lion and the Jewel, set in the Yoruba village of Ilujinle, Lakunle is the young schoolteacher who wants to marry Sidi, the village belle. He mocks the bride price as a \"savage custom\", dreams of a \"modern\" wife who walks beside him, and parrots half-understood Western ideas, while the old Bale, Baroka (the \"lion\"), stands for cunning traditional power. Lakunle stands for shallow imitation of Western ways and blind contempt for tradition: B. In the end Sidi chooses Baroka.\nMemory tip: Lakunle = Loud, Learned-looking, but Lacking understanding.",
 "|A 'deus ex machina' in literature refers to:|A mechanical stage device|An unlikely, contrived resolution introduced suddenly to resolve the plot|A type of tragic flaw|The narrator of a story": "\"Deus ex machina\" is Latin for \"god from the machine\". In Greek theatre a crane (the mechane) lowered an actor playing a god onto the stage to sort out an impossible plot. The term now means any sudden, unlikely event or character brought in to resolve the plot artificially, such as an unknown rich uncle appearing to pay all debts: B. Critics see it as a weakness, because the ending does not grow out of the characters and action.\nThe trap: A (a mechanical stage device) is where the name comes from, but as a literary term it means the contrived resolution.\nMemory tip: Deus ex machina = a \"sudden rescue\" from outside the story.",
 "|The sonnet form 'Shall I compare thee to a summer's day?' was written by:|John Milton|John Keats|William Shakespeare|William Wordsworth": "\"Shall I compare thee to a summer's day?\" opens Sonnet 18 by William Shakespeare (1564–1616), from his sequence of 154 sonnets published in 1609: C. It praises a loved one whose beauty, unlike summer, will never fade, because it lives on in the poem (\"So long lives this, and this gives life to thee\"). It is a Shakespearean (English) sonnet: three quatrains and a rhyming couplet, rhyming ABAB CDCD EFEF GG. Milton, Keats and Wordsworth also wrote sonnets, but not this one.\nMemory tip: \"Summer's day\" = Shakespeare's Sonnet 18; Shakespearean sonnet = 4 + 4 + 4 + 2 lines.",
 "|In Ola Rotimi's 'The Gods Are Not to Blame', the protagonist Odewale is based on:|Hamlet|Oedipus Rex|King Lear|Macbeth": "Ola Rotimi's play The Gods Are Not to Blame (first staged 1968) adapts Sophocles' Greek tragedy Oedipus Rex into a Yoruba setting. Like Oedipus, Odewale is told by the gods that he will kill his father and marry his mother; he unknowingly kills King Adetusa, becomes king of Kutuje and marries Queen Ojuola, his mother. When the truth comes out he blinds himself and leaves the land: B. Rotimi stresses Odewale's hot temper (his \"tribal\" anger) as his flaw.\nMemory tip: Odewale = Oedipus; both start with \"O\" and both blind themselves.",
 "|Which literary term describes a narrator who is unreliable or deceptive in their account?|Omniscient narrator|First-person narrator|Unreliable narrator|Third-person narrator": "An unreliable narrator tells the story in a way the reader cannot fully trust, because he or she is naive, mad, biased, lying or self-deceived: C. The reader must read between the lines. A first-person narrator (\"I\") is often unreliable, but not always, so \"first-person\" is a point of view, not this quality. An omniscient narrator knows everything and is normally trusted.\nThe trap: B (first-person narrator) catches students because many unreliable narrators speak as \"I\", but the question asks for the term for an untrustworthy narrator.\nMemory tip: Unreliable = \"Under suspicion\".",
 "|What is the significance of the title 'Things Fall Apart'?|It refers to the collapse of Okonkwo's yam farm|It is taken from W.B. Yeats' poem 'The Second Coming' and symbolises the disintegration of Igbo society under colonialism|It describes a natural disaster|It refers to the death of Okonkwo's father": "Achebe took his title from W.B. Yeats's poem \"The Second Coming\": \"Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold; / Mere anarchy is loosed upon the world.\" Yeats wrote about the collapse of order in Europe after the First World War. Achebe applies it to Umuofia: when the missionaries and the colonial government arrive, the clan splits (Nwoye converts, the elders lose authority) and Okonkwo, who cannot accept the change, hangs himself. The title stands for the break-up of Igbo society under colonialism: B.\nMemory tip: Things Fall Apart ← \"The Second Coming\" (Yeats); the centre (the clan) cannot hold.",
 "|The term 'in medias res' means:|At the end of a story|In the middle of things — starting a narrative in the middle of the action|At the beginning with backstory|A type of rhyme scheme": "\"In medias res\" is Latin for \"into the middle of things\". A story that begins in medias res opens in the middle of the action, and earlier events are filled in later through flashback or reported speech: B. Homer's Iliad and Odyssey begin this way, and it became a feature of the epic. The opposite is \"ab ovo\" (\"from the egg\"), starting at the very beginning.\nThe trap: C (at the beginning with backstory) is the opposite approach; in medias res drops the reader straight into action.\nMemory tip: Medias = middle; you join the story halfway.",
 "|Which of the following is NOT a characteristic of the African novel?|Use of oral tradition elements like proverbs and folktales|Exploration of the colonial encounter|Exclusive focus on romance and love|Questioning of traditional values": "The African novel grew up in the colonial and post-colonial period, so it typically deals with the colonial encounter, culture conflict, identity, politics, corruption and social change. It often borrows from oral tradition, using proverbs, folktales, songs and the rhythm of indigenous speech; Achebe calls proverbs \"the palm-oil with which words are eaten\". Many novels also question traditional values, for example the treatment of women or twins. An exclusive focus on romance and love is NOT typical: C.\nMemory tip: African novel = culture conflict + colonialism + oral tradition.",
 "|An 'aside' in drama is when a character:|Exits the stage quietly|Speaks words intended to be heard by the audience but not by other characters|Whispers to another character on stage|Delivers a long solo speech": "An aside is a short remark a character speaks so that the audience hears it but the other characters on stage, by stage convention, do not: B. It lets the audience share a secret thought, often creating dramatic irony. In Macbeth, when Macbeth hears he is Thane of Cawdor, he says aside, \"Glamis, and Thane of Cawdor: / The greatest is behind.\" A long solo speech, with the character alone on stage, is a soliloquy.\nThe trap: D (a long solo speech) describes a soliloquy, the term most often confused with an aside. An aside is short and spoken with others present.\nMemory tip: Aside = A short secret; Soliloquy = Solo speech.",
 "|Which of the following is a characteristic of oral poetry?|It is written and published in books|It is performed, often with musical accompaniment, and passed down orally|It follows strict written conventions|It is only for literate communities": "Oral poetry is composed and passed on by word of mouth, not in writing. It is performed before an audience, often with drums, music, dance and audience response, and it relies on repetition, refrain, praise names and parallelism to aid memory: B. Examples include Yoruba oriki (praise poetry) and ijala (hunters' chants), Igbo dirges and the Hausa songs of court praise singers. Each performance can vary, which makes the performer also a creator.\nMemory tip: Oral = \"mouth and ear\", performed and passed on.",
 "|The central conflict in Chinua Achebe's 'Arrow of God' is between:|Christianity and Islam|Ezeulu and the colonial administration and between traditional and Christian forces|Two rival villages|A father and his son": "Achebe's Arrow of God (1964) is set in Umuaro in the 1920s. Ezeulu, chief priest of the god Ulu, is caught between his rivals at home (Nwaka and the priest of Idemili) and the colonial administration: Captain Winterbottom detains him at Okperi when he refuses to be made a warrant chief. Back home he refuses to announce the New Yam feast, the people face famine, and many turn to the Christian harvest offering. The conflict is between Ezeulu and colonial rule, and between tradition and Christianity: B.\nMemory tip: Arrow of God = Ezeulu, Ulu and the yams; tradition loses to Christianity.",
 "|A 'foil' character in literature is one who:|Provides comic relief|Contrasts with the protagonist to highlight the protagonist's qualities|Narrates the story|Represents the antagonist's ally": "A foil is a character whose qualities contrast with another's, usually the protagonist's, so that the protagonist's traits stand out more clearly: B. In Things Fall Apart, gentle, thoughtful Obierika is a foil to hot-tempered Okonkwo; in The Lion and the Jewel, shallow Lakunle and cunning Baroka set each other off. A foil need not be funny, and he need not be on the villain's side.\nMemory tip: A foil is like the shiny foil behind a jewel: it makes the main character \"shine\" by contrast.",
 "|Which of the following poetic forms is associated with 14 lines and a turn (volta)?|Haiku|Limerick|Sonnet|Ballad": "A sonnet is a lyric poem of 14 lines, usually in iambic pentameter, with a fixed rhyme scheme and a volta (turn) where the thought shifts: C. The Petrarchan sonnet turns between the octave and sestet; the Shakespearean sonnet often turns at the final couplet. A haiku has 3 lines (5-7-5 syllables), a limerick has 5 comic lines (AABBA), and a ballad tells a story in quatrains.\nMemory tip: Sonnet = 14 lines + 1 turn.",
 "|The theme of alienation is MOST strongly expressed in:|Fairy tales|Romantic comedies|Existentialist literature|Nursery rhymes": "Alienation means being cut off from society, from others or even from oneself. Existentialist writing (Camus's The Outsider; Kafka's Metamorphosis, usually read as a forerunner; the absurdist plays of Beckett) shows lonely individuals in a world without clear meaning, so alienation is central to it: C. Fairy tales, romantic comedies and nursery rhymes usually end with harmony and belonging, the opposite of alienation.\nMemory tip: Existentialism = the lonely self in a meaningless world.",
 "|Which of the following describes the structure of a typical Petrarchan sonnet?|Three quatrains and a couplet (ABAB CDCD EFEF GG)|An octave (8 lines) and a sestet (6 lines) with a volta between them|14 couplets|4 stanzas of 4 lines each": "The Petrarchan (Italian) sonnet, named after Petrarch, splits its 14 lines into an octave (8 lines, rhyming ABBAABBA) that sets out a problem or situation, and a sestet (6 lines, often CDECDE or CDCDCD) that answers or reflects on it. The volta comes between them, at line 9: B. Three quatrains and a couplet (ABAB CDCD EFEF GG) is the Shakespearean (English) sonnet.\nThe trap: A describes the Shakespearean sonnet, the other sonnet form students learn.\nMemory tip: Petrarchan = 8 + 6; Shakespearean = 4 + 4 + 4 + 2.",
 "|In Flora Nwapa's 'Efuru', the central theme is:|The triumph of colonialism|The position of women in traditional Igbo society and the tension between personal fulfilment and societal expectations|Political independence in Africa|The civil war in Nigeria": "Flora Nwapa's Efuru (1966), one of the first novels in English by a Nigerian woman, follows Efuru, a beautiful, hard-working and successful trader in an Igbo lakeside town. Both her husbands, Adizua and Eneberi (Gilbert), fail her, her only child dies, and the town measures her worth by motherhood. In the end she becomes a devotee of Uhamiri, the lake goddess who is rich and beautiful but childless. The novel examines the place of women and the clash between personal fulfilment and society's expectations: B.\nMemory tip: Efuru: a woman's worth beyond marriage and motherhood.",
 "|What is a 'ballad'?|A long narrative poem about heroic deeds|A short narrative poem, often of folk origin, typically in quatrains with a refrain|A poem expressing personal emotions|A dramatic monologue": "A ballad is a short narrative poem, often of folk (oral) origin and meant to be sung. It usually uses four-line stanzas (the ballad stanza rhymes ABCB), simple language, dialogue, repetition and often a refrain, and it tells a dramatic, tragic or adventurous story: B. Examples are the traditional \"Sir Patrick Spens\" and Coleridge's literary ballad The Rime of the Ancient Mariner.\nThe trap: A describes an epic, which is also narrative but long, grand and heroic; a ballad is short and simple.\nMemory tip: Ballad = a \"story-song\" in quatrains.",
 "|The literary device where the end of a story circles back to its beginning is called:|Flash-forward|Circular narrative|Epistolary structure|Stream of consciousness": "In a circular (cyclical) narrative the story ends where it began, often returning to the same place, image or situation. This suggests that nothing has really changed, or that life repeats itself: B. A flash-forward jumps ahead in time, an epistolary structure uses letters, and stream of consciousness follows a mind's flow of thoughts. None of these describes a story that loops back to its start.\nMemory tip: Circular = the story comes full circle.",
 "|In J.P. Clark's poem 'Ibadan', which of the following best captures the central image?|A busy marketplace|The city of Ibadan seen as a running splash of rust and gold, scattered among seven hills like broken china in the sun|A peaceful rural landscape|A colonial city in conflict": "J.P. Clark (later J.P. Clark-Bekederemo) wrote \"Ibadan\" as one long image: \"Ibadan, / running splash of rust / and gold — flung and scattered / among seven hills like broken / china in the sun.\" Pictured as if from a hilltop in sunlight, the rusty and shining zinc roofs of the sprawling, unplanned city look like a splash of rust and gold, or bits of broken china thrown over the hills. The simile \"like broken china\" suggests both beauty and disorder: B.\nMemory tip: Ibadan = rust, gold and broken china in the SUN.",
 "|Gabriel Okara's poem 'Piano and Drums' contrasts:|Classical and popular music|European and African cultural values symbolised by the piano and drums|Two musicians competing|Ancient and modern Nigeria": "Gabriel Okara's \"Piano and Drums\" contrasts two worlds. The drums, heard \"at break of day at a riverside\", stand for Africa: simple, direct and natural (\"mystic rhythm\", \"my mother's lap\"). The piano, with its \"tear-furrowed concerto\", \"counterpoint\" and \"crescendo\", stands for complex Western culture. The speaker, an educated African, ends \"lost in the morning mist / of an age at a riverside\", torn between the two: B.\nMemory tip: Drums = Africa, piano = Europe; the speaker is caught in between.",
 "|Which of the following is the correct definition of 'hubris' in the context of Greek tragedy?|A type of chorus in Greek drama|Excessive pride or arrogance that leads to the hero's downfall|A moral lesson at the end of a play|The tragic hero's moment of recognition": "Hubris (Greek) is excessive pride or arrogance that makes a hero defy the gods, fate or moral limits; it often brings about the tragic hero's downfall: B. Oedipus's proud certainty that he can outrun the oracle is an example. The moment of recognition is anagnorisis, and the hero's reversal of fortune is peripeteia.\nThe trap: D (the moment of recognition) is anagnorisis, another Greek tragic term from the same lesson; do not mix the two.\nMemory tip: Hubris = \"Huge pride before a fall\".",
 "|The novel 'Purple Hibiscus' by Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie explores:|The Nigerian civil war|Religious fundamentalism, domestic violence and the coming-of-age of a teenage girl in post-independence Nigeria|Traditional marriage customs in Igboland|The transatlantic slave trade": "Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie's first novel, Purple Hibiscus (2003), is narrated by 15-year-old Kambili Achike in Enugu. Her father, Eugene, is a rich, devout Catholic and a fearless newspaper owner in public, but at home he beats his wife and children in the name of religion. A stay with Aunty Ifeoma's free, loving family in Nsukka (and Father Amadi) helps Kambili and her brother Jaja find their voices. The background is a military coup. The novel deals with religious fanaticism, domestic violence and coming of age: B.\nMemory tip: Purple Hibiscus = Kambili's silence breaking; purple hibiscus = rare freedom.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of a tragic flaw (hamartia)?|Romeo's love for Juliet|Okonkwo's fear of weakness and his excessive pride|Hamlet's intelligence|Macbeth's bravery": "Hamartia (tragic flaw) is the weakness or error of judgement in a tragic hero that leads to his fall. Okonkwo's overwhelming fear of looking weak, joined to his pride and rashness, drives him to kill Ikemefuna, to exile after accidentally killing a youth, and finally to killing the court messenger and hanging himself: B. Hamlet's intelligence and Macbeth's bravery are virtues; Macbeth's flaw is ambition.\nMemory tip: Hamartia = the hero's \"hole\" that sinks the ship.",
 "|The term 'motif' in literature refers to:|The main character of a story|A recurring element — image, symbol, or theme — that has significance in the work|The climax of a plot|A type of irony": "A motif is an image, symbol, idea or situation that recurs in a work and builds up its theme: B. In Things Fall Apart, yams, fire and the drum recur; in Macbeth, blood and sleep recur, building the theme of guilt. A theme is the central idea; a motif is a repeated element that points to it.\nMemory tip: Motif = \"more than once\": it keeps coming back.",
 "|In John Pepper Clark's 'Song of a Goat', the central issue is:|Colonial oppression|Impotence, jealousy, and the destruction it brings to a family|Traditional healing practices|Economic inequality": "J.P. Clark's verse tragedy Song of a Goat, set in the Niger Delta, centres on Zifa, who is impotent. The Masseur (a healer) advises that Zifa's younger brother, Tonye, should take his place with his wife, Ebiere. Zifa refuses, but Tonye and Ebiere later sleep together. Zifa's jealous rage brings disaster: Tonye hangs himself and Zifa walks into the sea, and the family line is destroyed. It deals with impotence, jealousy and the ruin they cause: B.\nMemory tip: Song of a Goat: the goat is sacrificed, and so is the family.",
 "|Which of the following correctly defines 'verisimilitude' in fiction?|The use of fantasy and supernatural elements|The quality of appearing true or real — the sense that the narrative could happen|The number of characters in a story|A type of rhyme": "Verisimilitude means \"likeness to truth\": the quality that makes a story seem real and believable even though it is invented: B. Writers achieve it through realistic settings, everyday detail, convincing dialogue and characters who behave in a believable way. A novel set in a real Nigerian city with real street names and believable people has high verisimilitude. Fantasy and supernatural elements usually lower it.\nMemory tip: Verisimilitude = \"very similar to\" real life.",
 "|In Christopher Okigbo's poetry, the dominant theme is:|Romance and love|A spiritual journey of return to ancestral roots combined with modernist aesthetics|Political independence and Marxist revolution|Nature and the seasons": "Christopher Okigbo (1932–1967), who died fighting in the Nigerian Civil War, wrote difficult, musical poetry (Heavensgate, Limits, collected with later poems in Labyrinths, 1971). His central theme is a spiritual journey of a \"prodigal\" returning to his ancestral roots, especially the river goddess Idoto (\"Before you, mother Idoto, / naked I stand\"), using modernist techniques influenced by Eliot and Pound: B. Only his last poems (Path of Thunder) turn openly to politics.\nMemory tip: Okigbo = the prodigal returning to Idoto.",
 "|Which of the following is NOT a feature of modernist literature?|Stream of consciousness|Fragmented narrative|Adherence to traditional forms and rhyme schemes|Subjective point of view": "Modernist literature (about 1900–1940: Joyce, Woolf, Eliot) broke with tradition. It used stream of consciousness, fragmented narrative, shifting subjective points of view, free verse and experiment with form. Strict adherence to traditional forms and rhyme schemes is the opposite of modernism, so it is NOT a feature: C. In a \"NOT\" question, find the option that does not belong.\nMemory tip: Modernism = \"make it new\" (Ezra Pound's slogan).",
 "|The play 'Death and the King's Horseman' by Wole Soyinka is based on a real event that occurred in:|1946 in Oyo|1946 in Kano|1947 in Lagos|1960 in Abuja": "Wole Soyinka's Death and the King's Horseman (1975) is based, as his Author's Note says, on events in Oyo, the ancient Yoruba city, in 1946. When an Alaafin died, his horseman was expected to follow him by ritual death; the colonial authorities stopped it. In the play the District Officer, Simon Pilkings, arrests Elesin, and his son Olunde dies in his place: A. Soyinka moved the action back to the Second World War years.\nMemory tip: Horseman: Oyo, 1946.",
 "|Which of the following best defines 'allegory'?|A poem with no clear meaning|A narrative in which characters and events represent abstract ideas or moral qualities|A type of dramatic monologue|A form of comedy": "An allegory is a story in which characters, events and places stand for abstract ideas, moral qualities or real political events, so the story has a second, hidden meaning: B. George Orwell's Animal Farm is an allegory of the Russian Revolution; John Bunyan's The Pilgrim's Progress is a religious allegory with characters such as Christian and Faithful. A symbol is a single object standing for an idea; an allegory is a whole story built that way.\nMemory tip: Allegory = a story with a hidden \"other\" story.",
 "|In Ben Okri's 'The Famished Road', the protagonist Azaro is a:|Colonial officer|Spirit child (abiku) who chooses to remain in the world of the living|Politician in post-independence Nigeria|Traditional healer": "Ben Okri's The Famished Road (Booker Prize, 1991) is narrated by Azaro, an abiku, a spirit child who is meant to die young and return to the spirit world. Azaro chooses to stay with his poor parents in a Nigerian ghetto, moving between the world of spirits and the hard world of the living (Madame Koto's bar, politics, poverty): B. The novel mixes the real and the magical (magical realism).\nMemory tip: Azaro the abiku stays on the \"famished road\" of life.",
 "|The term 'pathetic fallacy' refers to:|A false argument used to justify evil|The attribution of human emotions to nature and inanimate objects|A character's irrational fear|A weak metaphor": "Pathetic fallacy, a term coined by John Ruskin in 1856, gives human feelings to nature: \"the angry sea\", \"the weeping sky\", \"the cruel wind\". Writers often match the weather to the mood, such as a storm on the night of a murder in Macbeth: B. It is a special form of personification, applied to nature.\nMemory tip: Pathetic = \"pathos\" (feeling); the weather shares the feeling.",
 "|Which of the following describes an 'epistolary novel'?|A novel written entirely in verse|A novel told through a series of letters, diary entries, or documents|A novel set in ancient history|A novel with no identifiable narrator": "An epistolary novel tells its story through letters, and sometimes diary entries or other documents: B. Samuel Richardson's Pamela (1740) is a classic English example. For Nigerian students the best-known is Mariama Bâ's So Long a Letter (1979), written as one long letter from Ramatoulaye to her friend Aissatou. The letters give an intimate first-person view.\nMemory tip: Epistle = letter, so epistolary = told in letters.",
 "|In Achebe's 'Things Fall Apart', what does the Oracle of Agbala represent?|Western religion|The repository of traditional spiritual authority and wisdom in Igbo society|Political power of the elders|The voice of the colonisers": "In Things Fall Apart, Agbala is the god of the Oracle of the Hills and the Caves, whose priestess is Chielo. People consult it on war, disputes and misfortune, and its word is final: Chielo even carries Ezinma to the caves at night while Okonkwo and Ekwefi follow. The Oracle stands for the spiritual authority of traditional Igbo religion: B. It is the Oracle that decrees Ikemefuna must die.\nMemory tip: Agbala's Oracle = Umuofia's spiritual \"supreme court\".",
 "|The term 'diaspora literature' refers to:|Literature written by authors living in their home country|Literature produced by people who have been displaced from their homeland and explore themes of identity, belonging and cultural tension|Literature about migration of birds|Literature translated from other languages": "\"Diaspora\" means a people scattered from their homeland. Diaspora literature is written by people living away from their home country, whether forced out (slavery, exile, war) or as migrants. It explores identity, belonging, memory of home, racism and the clash of cultures: B. Adichie's Americanah, about Nigerians in America and Britain, is a modern example.\nMemory tip: Diaspora = \"scattered seeds\", writing from away about home.",
 "|Which of the following is an example of dramatic irony in Shakespeare's 'Macbeth'?|Macbeth's soliloquy about ambition|The audience knowing the witches are evil while Macbeth trusts them|Lady Macbeth's sleepwalking scene|The battle scenes at the end": "Dramatic irony is when the audience knows something a character does not. In Macbeth the audience sees the witches plotting mischief (they torment a sailor and plan to \"meet with Macbeth\") and hears Banquo warn that \"instruments of darkness tell us truths\" to betray us. Macbeth trusts them, and later trusts the apparitions' promises about Birnam Wood and \"none of woman born\", which turn out to be traps: B. A soliloquy and a sleepwalking scene are not in themselves dramatic irony.\nMemory tip: Dramatic irony = the audience is \"in the know\", the character is not.",
 "|The narrative structure in which a story is told within another story is called:|Parallel narrative|Frame narrative (story within a story)|Omniscient narration|Retrospective narration": "A frame narrative is an outer story that contains one or more inner stories, as in The Thousand and One Nights (Scheherazade telling tales), Chaucer's Canterbury Tales (pilgrims telling tales) and Conrad's Heart of Darkness (Marlow's tale told on a boat on the Thames): B. A parallel narrative runs two stories side by side instead of placing one inside the other.\nThe trap: A (parallel narrative) also involves more than one story, but they run alongside each other, not one inside the other.\nMemory tip: Frame = a picture frame around the inner story.",
 "|Which of the following is a characteristic of the epic as a literary form?|It is always written in prose|It is a long narrative poem celebrating heroic deeds often involving supernatural forces|It features anti-heroes only|It originated in the Renaissance period": "An epic is a long narrative poem in a grand style about the heroic deeds of a great figure, often involving gods or supernatural forces and the fate of a nation or people: B. Examples are Homer's Iliad and Odyssey, Virgil's Aeneid, Milton's Paradise Lost and the Sundiata epic of old Mali. Epic conventions include beginning in medias res, invoking the muse and epic similes. Epics are mostly verse (not prose) and are far older than the Renaissance.\nMemory tip: Epic = Enormous poem about a Heroic people.",
 "|A 'tragic flaw' (hamartia) in drama refers to:|A mistake in the plot|The fundamental character weakness that leads to a tragic hero's downfall|A flaw in a play's staging|A grammatical error in dialogue": "Hamartia, Aristotle's term in the Poetics, is the tragic flaw or error of judgement in a hero that brings about his downfall: B. Examples: Macbeth's ambition, Othello's jealousy, Okonkwo's fear of weakness, Odewale's hot temper. It is a weakness of the character, not a fault in the plot or staging.\nMemory tip: Hamartia = \"missing the mark\" (the Greek word comes from archery).",
 "|The narrative technique in which a character's thoughts are presented as a direct, uninterrupted flow is called:|Third-person omniscient|Stream of consciousness|Dramatic monologue|Free indirect discourse": "Stream of consciousness presents a character's thoughts, feelings and memories as they flow, often without logical order or full punctuation: B. James Joyce's Ulysses and Virginia Woolf's Mrs Dalloway are famous examples; William James coined the phrase in psychology. Free indirect discourse blends the narrator's voice with a character's thoughts but keeps normal grammar. A dramatic monologue is a poem spoken by one character to a silent listener.\nThe trap: D (free indirect discourse) also shows thoughts, but it is filtered through the third-person narrator, not an unbroken flow.\nMemory tip: Stream = thoughts flowing like a river, unedited.",
 "|In Chinua Achebe's 'Things Fall Apart', the title is taken from a poem by:|John Keats|William Butler Yeats|T.S. Eliot|Walt Whitman": "Achebe borrowed his title from W.B. Yeats's \"The Second Coming\" (written 1919, published 1920): \"Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold; / Mere anarchy is loosed upon the world.\" Yeats was Irish and won the Nobel Prize in 1923. His poem describes the collapse of order, and Achebe uses it for the collapse of Igbo society: B. Achebe's next novel, No Longer at Ease (1960), takes its title from T.S. Eliot's \"Journey of the Magi\".\nThe trap: C (T.S. Eliot) catches students who remember that another Achebe title comes from Eliot.\nMemory tip: Things Fall Apart = Yeats; No Longer at Ease = Eliot.",
 "|Dramatic irony occurs when:|The actor forgets their lines on stage|The audience knows something significant that one or more characters do not know|A character speaks directly to the audience|Two characters have conflicting opinions": "Dramatic irony occurs when the audience knows something important that a character does not, so the character's words or actions carry a meaning he cannot see: B. In The Gods Are Not to Blame, the audience knows that Odewale's curse on the killer of King Adetusa falls on himself. In Hamlet, the audience knows the cup is poisoned. A character speaking directly to the audience is an aside or soliloquy.\nMemory tip: Dramatic irony: \"we know, they don't\".",
 "|The Negritude movement in African literature was associated with:|British colonial literature|A celebration of African cultural values and identity as a response to French colonialism, led by writers like Léopold Sédar Senghor and Aimé Césaire|Nigerian Pidgin poetry|Arabic literary traditions": "Negritude began among Black French-speaking students in Paris in the 1930s, led by Léopold Sédar Senghor (Senegal), Aimé Césaire (Martinique) and Léon-Gontran Damas (French Guiana). Reacting against French assimilation policy, it celebrated African culture, history and identity: B. Senghor's \"Black Woman\" (\"Naked woman, black woman\") is a typical example. Wole Soyinka mocked it: \"A tiger does not proclaim its tigritude; it pounces.\"\nMemory tip: Negritude = French-speaking Black pride (Senghor, Césaire, Damas).",
 "|In poetry, a 'volta' refers to:|The repeated refrain in a ballad|The rhetorical or emotional shift or turn in a poem — especially in a sonnet|A type of stanza with six lines|The final couplet in a poem": "\"Volta\" is Italian for \"turn\". It is the point in a poem, especially a sonnet, where the argument, mood or tone shifts: B. In a Petrarchan sonnet it comes at line 9, between octave and sestet; in a Shakespearean sonnet it may come at line 9 or at the final couplet (lines 13–14). A volta is a shift in thought, not a stanza type or a refrain.\nThe trap: D (the final couplet) is where a Shakespearean sonnet's turn often falls, but the volta is the turn itself, not the couplet.\nMemory tip: Volta = \"turn\" (think of a U-turn in the poem).",
 "|Wole Soyinka's 'Death and the King's Horseman' is primarily concerned with:|Military coups in Nigeria|The clash between Yoruba tradition (ritual suicide obligation) and colonial British authority|A love story set in Lagos|The Nigerian Civil War": "Soyinka's Death and the King's Horseman (1975) is based on a 1946 incident in Oyo. When the king dies, his horseman, Elesin, must die by ritual to accompany him to the ancestors. The British District Officer, Simon Pilkings, arrests him; Elesin's son Olunde, back from studying medicine in England, dies in his place, and Elesin strangles himself. Soyinka said the play is about a metaphysical clash, not just a \"clash of cultures\", but the conflict is between Yoruba tradition and colonial British authority: B.\nMemory tip: Horseman = duty to the dead king versus colonial law.",
 "|Which literary device involves a reference to a well-known person, place, event, or work of literature within a text?|Allegory|Allusion|Anaphora|Apostrophe": "An allusion is a brief reference to a well-known person, place, event or text that the reader is expected to recognise, such as calling a strong man \"a Samson\" (the Bible) or a traitor \"a Judas\": B. Achebe's title Things Fall Apart is itself an allusion to Yeats. Anaphora is repeating words at the start of lines, and apostrophe is addressing an absent person or thing (\"O Death!\").\nThe trap: A (allegory) sounds similar, but an allegory is a whole story with a hidden meaning, while an allusion is a passing reference.\nMemory tip: Allusion = a quick \"nod\" to something famous.",
 "|The term 'bildungsroman' refers to:|A poem with 14 lines|A novel that traces the psychological and moral growth of its protagonist from youth to adulthood|A play set in ancient times|A short story with a surprise ending": "Bildungsroman (German, \"novel of formation\") is a novel tracing the growth of a young protagonist from childhood to maturity, through experiences that shape his or her mind and character: B. Examples are Dickens's Great Expectations, Charlotte Brontë's Jane Eyre, Camara Laye's The African Child (L'Enfant noir) and Adichie's Purple Hibiscus, where Kambili grows from silence into voice.\nMemory tip: Bildungsroman = a \"building-up\" novel; a child is built into an adult.",
 "|'Careened,\nThese oily tears\nDripping down the tears on your depressed face,\nWill one day be staunched,\nI swear!'\nIbiwari Ikiriko, Oily Tears.\n\nThe tone of the poet is|optimistic|supplicatory|sympathetic|piteous": "Tone is the poet's attitude, shown by the words he chooses. Ibiwari Ikiriko's \"Oily Tears\" is about the suffering of the Niger Delta, whose people weep \"oily tears\" because oil wealth has brought them pollution and poverty. Yet these lines do not simply weep: the poet promises that the tears \"Will one day be staunched\" (stopped), and he backs it with an oath, \"I swear!\" Looking forward with confidence to a better day is an optimistic tone: A. The trap: C (sympathetic) is close, because the poet does feel for the \"depressed face\", but the key words \"will one day be staunched, I swear!\" express hope and certainty, not just pity. Memory tip: future tense plus a promise (\"will... I swear\") points to optimism.",
 "|'Lift not the painted veil which those who live\ncall life: though unreal shapes be pictured there,\nAnd it but mimic all we would believe\nWith colours idly spread, - behind, lurk fear,'\nP.B. Shelley, Sonnet.\n\nThe stanza above is an example of a|quatrain|couplet|free verse|limerick": "A stanza is a group of lines in a poem, named by the number of lines: couplet (2), tercet (3), quatrain (4), sestet (6), octave (8). Shelley's sonnet \"Lift not the painted veil\" opens with four lines rhyming abab: live / there / believe / fear. Four lines forming one unit is a quatrain: A. It is not a couplet (two lines), not free verse (it rhymes and has a regular iambic metre), and not a limerick (a five-line comic poem rhyming aabba). The paper prints the lines run together, but the poem's own lines end at \"live\", \"there\", \"believe\" and \"fear\". Memory tip: quatrain, like quarter and quad, means four.",
 "|'Symbol of fruitfulness, symbol of barrenness,\nMother and destroyer, the calm and the storm!\nLife and desire and dreams and death\nAre born of the sea; this swarming land'\nFrank Collymore, Hymn to the Sea.\n\nThe address to the sea in the lines above is done by the use of|personification|praise|symbolism|apostrophe": "Personification gives human qualities or roles to something that is not human. In \"Hymn to the Sea\", the Barbadian poet Frank Collymore calls the sea \"Mother and destroyer\" and says that \"Life and desire and dreams and death / Are born of the sea\". Being a mother, destroying, and giving birth are human roles, so the sea is personified: A. The trap: D (apostrophe) tempts because the question says \"address\", but apostrophe needs the speaker to talk directly to the absent or non-human thing (\"O Sea, you...\"), and these lines describe the sea rather than speak to it. Symbolism (C) appears too (\"symbol of fruitfulness\"), but the human qualities make the main device personification. Memory tip: personification = making a thing a person; apostrophe = talking to it.",
 "|'When he gave me the bottle, and I opened it,\nI was shocked by the odour that hit my nose.\nI immediately corked it again, and spat on the floor.'\nIsidore Okpewho, The Last Duty.\n\nThe reaction in the passage above is that of|rejection|anger|protest|nausea": "In Isidore Okpewho's novel The Last Duty, set in the Nigerian civil war, a character is handed a bottle whose smell overwhelms him. Look at his body's reaction: he is \"shocked by the odour that hit my nose\", corks the bottle \"immediately\", and \"spat on the floor\". Spitting after a foul smell is the body's response to sickness and disgust in the stomach, which is nausea: D. Rejection (A) and protest (C) are deliberate attitudes towards a person or idea, and nothing here shows anger (B). Memory tip: when the reaction is physical (smell, spitting, retching), think nausea.",
 "|'CYCLIST gets down and begins to prop his bicycle.\nCYCLIST: All right. If you're sure it won't take long.\nBARBER: I am known for my lightning clippers. Even the soldiers know me. I can shave the head of an entire battalion between one coup and the next. Sit down and relax your back. Cycling is not easy when you've abandoned it for some time...'\nWole Soyinka, The Beautification of Area Boy.\n\nThe literary devices in the dialogue above are|humour and irony|allusion and paradox|irony and parody|hyperbole and allusion": "Hyperbole is deliberate exaggeration; allusion is a passing reference to a well-known event, person or text. In Wole Soyinka's The Beautification of Area Boy, set on a busy Lagos street, the barber boasts that he can \"shave the head of an entire battalion\" (hundreds of soldiers), an obvious exaggeration: hyperbole. \"Between one coup and the next\" alludes to the frequent military coups of Nigeria's history, and \"Even the soldiers know me\" points to military rule: allusion. Together: D. The trap: A (humour and irony) catches students because the boast is funny, but humour is an effect, not the device the question asks for, and nothing is ironic here. Memory tip: hyperbole = big talk; allusion = a nod to something real.",
 "|'For seven days it rained that June:\nA storm half out to sea kept turning around like a dog trying to settle himself on a rug; We were the fleas that complained in his hair.'\nJohn Updike, Wash.\n\nThe images set in the lines above are of|the days of Noah|rain and flood in June|discomfort and likely destruction|an animal, insects and fun": "Imagery is word-pictures that appeal to the senses and carry feeling. In John Updike's \"Wash\", seven days of June rain and a storm that \"kept turning around like a dog trying to settle himself on a rug\" make the weather a huge restless animal, and the people are \"the fleas that complained in his hair\". Fleas are tiny, irritated and could be crushed at any moment if the dog scratches. So the images express discomfort and likely destruction: C. Option D lists the literal pictures (dog, fleas) but \"fun\" is wrong, and B only restates the weather without the images. Memory tip: ask what the picture makes you feel, not just what it shows.",
 "|'We have been asleep, Mr. Speaker, my fellow citizens; asleep in our dream of security! Asleep in our well-meaning, sportsmanlike way of wishing the other fellow well. Asleep in the false security of accepting all men of goodwill who would be free as men of honour.'\nRalph Ellison, Juneteenth.\n\nThe force of the speech above is sustained on|delivery|musicality|repetition|oratory": "Repetition is the deliberate repeating of a word or phrase for emphasis. In this political speech from Ralph Ellison's novel Juneteenth, \"asleep\" comes four times: \"We have been asleep... asleep in our dream of security! Asleep in our well-meaning... Asleep in the false security...\" Each repetition hammers home the charge that the nation has been careless, building force sentence by sentence: C. Delivery and oratory (A, D) are about how a speech is spoken, which we cannot hear on the page, and musicality (B) is too vague. Memory tip: when one word keeps coming back, the force rests on repetition.",
 "|'Thomas Gradgrind, sir. A man of realities. A man of facts and calculations. A man who proceeds upon the principle that two and two are four, and nothing over, and who is not to be talked into allowing for anything over.'\nCharles Dickens, Hard Times.\n\nFrom the passage above, Thomas Gradgrind is|dogmatic|hostile|theoretical|eloquent": "Dogmatic describes someone who holds rigidly to fixed beliefs and will not consider other views. In Dickens's Hard Times, Thomas Gradgrind, the schoolmaster of Coketown, introduces himself as \"A man of facts and calculations\", who believes \"two and two are four, and nothing over\" and \"is not to be talked into allowing for anything over\". He refuses even to listen to imagination or feeling: dogmatic, A. The trap: C (theoretical) is close because Gradgrind lives by a fixed theory of facts, but the passage stresses that he is \"a man of realities\" and above all that nobody can talk him out of his principle, which is the mark of a dogmatic man. Memory tip: Gradgrind grinds everything down to facts and will not budge.",
 "|'Jose Palacios, his oldest servant, found him floating naked with his eyes open in the purifying waters of his bath and thought he had drowned. He knew this was one of the many ways the General meditated, but the ecstasy in which he lay drifting seemed that of a man no longer in this world.'\nGabriel Garcia Marquez, The General in His Labyrinth.\n\nThe master in the passage above is obviously in a state of|labyrinth|drowning|forgetfulness|trance": "A trance is a dream-like state in which a person is absorbed and seems unaware of the world around. In Gabriel Garcia Marquez's The General in His Labyrinth, about the last journey of the South American liberator Simon Bolivar, his servant José Palacios finds him floating in his bath with eyes open and fears he has drowned. But the servant \"knew this was one of the many ways the General meditated\", and his \"ecstasy\" made him seem \"a man no longer in this world\". Deep meditation that cuts one off from the world is a trance: D. Drowning (B) is only the servant's first mistaken thought. Memory tip: read to the end of the passage; the \"but\" sentence corrects the first impression.",
 "|Based on Ernest Hemingway's The Old Man and the Sea: \nThe skeleton of the marlin implies the|sharks' predatory inclination|futility of human struggle|old man's lack of faith|old man's incompetence in fishing": "Ernest Hemingway's The Old Man and the Sea (1952) is a short novel about Santiago, an old Cuban fisherman who has gone 84 days without a fish. Far out in the Gulf Stream he hooks a giant marlin, fights it for three days and kills it, but sharks devour it as he sails home, and he reaches harbour with only the bare skeleton. The skeleton stands for what his heroic effort finally earns him: nothing he can sell. It implies the futility of human struggle: B. Yet Hemingway also shows that Santiago is \"destroyed but not defeated\": the skeleton proves the greatness of his fight, and the fishermen admire it. Option A only states what sharks are like. Memory tip: a skeleton is struggle with the reward eaten away.",
 "|Based on Ernest Hemingway's The Old Man and the Sea: \nSantiago loses his catch to the sharks because|the blood attracts them|he does not sail home early|the sharks are predators|it is too dark": "In The Old Man and the Sea, Santiago ties the huge marlin to the side of his small skiff because it is too big to bring aboard. Blood from its wounds spreads through the water, and Hemingway tells us the first shark, a mako, \"had come up from deep down in the water as the dark cloud of blood had settled and dispersed\" and followed the scent. More sharks, the galanos, come the same way, and they strip the fish to the bone: A. Option C is true of sharks in general, but it does not explain why they found this fish; the blood trail does. Santiago did not delay (B), and the first attack is in daylight (D). Memory tip: in the sea, blood is a signal that calls the sharks.",
 "|Based on Camara Laye's The African Child: \nLaye's stay with Uncle Mamadou reveals a lot about|his unwillingness to stay with his parents|his affection for his uncle|the strength of communal living in Africa|the irresponsibility of his parents": "Camara Laye's The African Child (L'Enfant noir, 1953) is his autobiography of growing up among the Malinke of Kouroussa in Guinea. When he goes to the technical college in Conakry, he lives with his uncle Mamadou, who receives him warmly and treats him as one of his own children, as does the uncle's wife. In African society a child belongs to the whole extended family, and relatives share the duty of raising him. So the stay shows the strength of communal living in Africa: C. Laye loves his parents deeply (A, D are wrong), and his affection for his uncle (B) is part of the picture, not the main point. Memory tip: in the African Child, it takes a whole family to raise a boy.",
 "|Based on Camara Laye's The African Child: \nThe setting of The African Child is a kingdom with|Islamic and modern traditions|pre-islamic and Islamic traditions|pre-islamic traditions|pre-islamic and modern traditions": "The African Child is set in Kouroussa, Upper Guinea, among the Malinke people, in the old Mande lands of the Mali empire. Laye's family are Muslims who pray and keep Ramadan, yet older, pre-Islamic beliefs live on beside Islam: the little black snake that is his father's guardian spirit, the magic of the goldsmith's forge, the masked Kondén Diara ceremony and the circumcision rites. That blend is pre-Islamic and Islamic traditions: B. The modern world appears only later, at school in Conakry and on his departure for France. Memory tip: in Kouroussa the mosque and the snake spirit live side by side.",
 "|Based on Camara Laye's The African Child: \nLaye's first-person narrative technique|makes the work very humorous|makes his work less realistic|quickens the tempo of his narrative|makes his story more believable": "First-person narration means the story is told by \"I\", a character who took part in the events. In The African Child, Camara Laye remembers his own childhood: watching his father work gold, his mother's powers, school, initiation and leaving for Conakry and Paris. Because the narrator lived through what he describes, the reader trusts the account as an eyewitness report; first-person narration makes the story more believable: D. It makes the work more realistic, not less (B), and the book's gentle, nostalgic tone is not mainly humorous (A). Memory tip: \"I was there\" is the strongest proof a story can give.",
 "|Based on Buchi Emecheta's Second Class Citizen: \nThe marriage of Adah and Francis collapses because of|Adah's unfaithfulness to Francis|the influence of British culture|Adah's desire for personal fulfillment|external pressures on the couple": "In Second Class Citizen, Adah is ambitious: she works as a library assistant, wants to study, and writes her first novel, The Bride Price. Francis, lazy and failing his exams, resents her success, forces her to have children, beats her and finally burns her manuscript. Adah's desire for personal fulfilment as a worker, mother and writer cannot survive under his control, so she leaves him: C. Adah is not unfaithful (A). British racism and poverty (B, D) add strain, but the novel makes the break turn on her struggle to be her own person. Memory tip: the burnt manuscript is the end of the marriage.",
 "|Based on Buchi Emecheta's Second Class Citizen: \nThe central theme in Second Class Citizen is that women should|be involved in national and international politics|beware of men and their sweet words|seek spiritual guidance before going into marriage|free themselves from the negative forces of patriarchy": "Second Class Citizen shows how patriarchy keeps women down: as a child in Lagos, Adah is valued less than her brother and almost denied schooling; as a wife in London, Francis treats her as a source of money and children, and his family expect her to obey. Adah resists, educates herself, writes, and finally leaves Francis to raise her children alone. The central theme is that women should free themselves from the negative forces of patriarchy: D. The novel is partly drawn from Emecheta's own life. Options A to C are not what the story is about. Memory tip: Adah's journey is from \"second class\" to standing on her own.",
 "|Based on Femi Osofisan's Morountodun: \nThe war in the play symbolizes the struggle between the|capitalists and the socialists|bourgeoisie and the proletariat|market women and the men|farmers and the consumers": "Femi Osofisan's Morountodun (1979) reworks the Yoruba legend of Moremi, who betrayed her enemies for her people, around the Agbekoya farmers' revolt of 1968–69 in western Nigeria. Titubi, daughter of the rich market leader Alhaja Kabirat, goes into the farmers' camp as a spy but comes to share their cause. The farmers' war against the government and the wealthy stands for the class struggle between the privileged bourgeoisie and the exploited proletariat, a Marxist reading Osofisan intends: B. The trap: D (farmers and consumers) tempts because the rebels are farmers, but their fight is against exploitation by the ruling class, not against consumers. Memory tip: Osofisan's wars are class wars.",
 "|Based on Femi Osofisan's Morountodun: \nThe diction of the play can be described as|political and rhetorical|arrogant and condemnatory|urgent but apolitical|poetic but difficult": "Diction is an author's choice of words. Morountodun is a political play: it argues about injustice, taxes, exploitation and revolt, and its characters (the farmers, Titubi, the Superintendent, the Director) debate, persuade and make speeches to each other and to the audience. Language built to argue and persuade on public issues is political and rhetorical: A. The play is clearly not apolitical (C), and its language, though it uses songs and poetry, is meant to be understood by a popular audience, not to be difficult (D). Memory tip: a protest play talks like a rally: political and rhetorical.",
 "|Based on Femi Osofisan's Morountodun: \nThe frequent intervention of the Director makes the stage experience of Morountodun very|prolonged|boring|informal|uncoordinated": "Morountodun opens with a Director who steps forward, speaks straight to the audience, and keeps interrupting the action to comment on the play and remind them that it is a performance. This technique, borrowed from Brecht's epic theatre and from African storytelling, breaks the \"fourth wall\" between stage and audience. The effect is an informal, open theatre experience in which the audience is invited to think rather than be lost in illusion: C. The interruptions are planned, not uncoordinated (D), and their purpose is not to prolong the play (A). Memory tip: when the Director chats to you, the theatre feels informal.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Twelfth Night: \nMalvolio: Not yet old enough for a man, nor young enough for a boy; as a squash is before 't is a peascod, or a codling when 't is almost an apple; 't is with him in standing water, between boy and man. He is very well favoured, and he speaks very shrewishly; one would think his mother's milk were scarce out of him.\n\nThe images in the quotation above express the speaker's|contempt and admiration|Hatred and pity|contempt and disregard|disregard and pity": "Shakespeare's Twelfth Night is a comedy set in Illyria. Viola, shipwrecked, disguises herself as a young man, Cesario, and serves Duke Orsino, who sends her to woo Countess Olivia. Here the steward Malvolio describes the youth at the gate with images of unripe fruit: a \"squash\" (an unripe pea-pod) and a \"codling\" (an unripe apple), \"between boy and man\", with \"his mother's milk... scarce out of him\". That is contempt. Yet he admits \"He is very well favoured\", meaning handsome: admiration. Together: A. The trap: C (contempt and disregard) catches students who notice only the sneer and miss \"well favoured\". Memory tip: look for the one kind word inside an insult.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Twelfth Night: \nCome away, come away, death,\nAnd in sad cypress let me be laid...\n\nIn the lines above, the character is|expressing his preference for a cypress coffin|expressing his unhappiness about death|lamenting over his unreciprocated love|wishing for death to come": "\"Come away, come away, death\" is the song Feste sings for Duke Orsino in Act 2 of Twelfth Night. Its speaker asks death to come and lay him in a \"sad cypress\" coffin because \"I am slain by a fair cruel maid\": he is dying of love that is not returned. That suits Orsino, who pines for Olivia, who does not love him. So the song is a lament over unreciprocated love: C. Read on their own, the two quoted lines also simply call on death to come, so D is accepted too. Option A takes \"cypress\" too literally. Memory tip: in love songs, \"I am dying\" usually means \"she does not love me\".",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Twelfth Night: \nThe traditional verse form of speech as dialogue in Twelfth Night is used to symbolize the|impressiveness of the character|distinction between verse and prose|function of poetry in the play|traditionality of the play": "In Shakespeare's plays, verse (usually blank verse, unrhymed iambic pentameter) is the speech of noble, serious or romantic characters, while prose is mostly for servants, clowns and comic scenes. In Twelfth Night, Orsino, Viola and Olivia speak verse in their love scenes, while Sir Toby, Sir Andrew and Maria mostly speak prose. So the verse form marks the dignity and impressiveness of the character speaking: A. Option B merely restates that verse differs from prose, and D says nothing about why verse is used. Memory tip: in Shakespeare, lords speak in verse and clowns in prose.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Twelfth Night: \nDuke: Be clamorous, and leap all civil bounds, Rather than make unprofited return.\n\nDuke's statement above to Cesario means that he should|make trouble to see Olivia|jump the fence to see Olivia|shout to be seen and heard by Olivia|fortify himself against any denial to see Olivia": "In Act 1 Scene 4 of Twelfth Night, Duke Orsino sends Cesario (Viola in disguise) to court Olivia, who has refused to see any suitor. He tells her: \"Be clamorous, and leap all civil bounds / Rather than make unprofited return.\" Clamorous means noisy; \"leap all civil bounds\" means go beyond good manners; \"unprofited return\" means coming back without success. In short: make a fuss, even trouble, until you are admitted to Olivia: A. The trap: C (shout to be seen and heard) takes \"clamorous\" too literally; the Duke means insist rudely if necessary, not merely shout. Memory tip: \"leap all civil bounds\" = forget your manners.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Twelfth Night: \nTwelfth Night is preoccupied with the juxtaposition of|death and disappointment|appearance and reality|life and disappointment|love and death": "Juxtaposition means placing two contrasting things side by side. Twelfth Night is full of things that are not what they seem: Viola is a woman who appears to be the young man Cesario; Olivia falls in love with this \"man\"; Sebastian and Viola are mistaken for each other; Malvolio believes a forged letter shows that Olivia loves him; Feste plays \"Sir Topas\" the priest. The play keeps setting appearance against reality: B. Death and disappointment (A, C, D) are not its chief concerns; it is a comedy ending in marriages. Memory tip: in Twelfth Night, look twice at everyone: nothing is what it seems.",
 "|In Niyi Osundare's 'They Too Are The Earth' musical effect is achieved by|repetition|double rhymes|assonance|end rhymes": "Niyi Osundare, the Nigerian poet, wrote \"They Too Are The Earth\" in praise of ordinary working people, the farmers and labourers who are \"the earth\" too. Like much of his poetry, it borrows from Yoruba oral tradition, and its music comes mainly from repetition: the refrain \"They too are the earth\" and repeated words and patterns that make it sound like a chant. The poem is in free verse, so it does not depend on end rhymes or double rhymes (B, D). Answer: A. Memory tip: oral poetry sings through repetition, like a chorus.",
 "|'...In Tempe or the dales of Arcady?\nWhat men or gods are these? What maidens loth?\nWhat mad pursuit? What struggle to escape?\nWhat pipes and timbrels? What wild ecstasy?'\n\nIn Keats' 'Ode on a Grecian Urn', the recurrent use of the rhetorical questions in the lines above suggests|admiration and amazement|confusion and fear|shock and surprise|worry and sadness": "John Keats's \"Ode on a Grecian Urn\" (1819) is addressed to an ancient Greek vase painted with scenes of a festival and a chase. In the first stanza the speaker pours out rhetorical questions: \"What men or gods are these? What maidens loth? What mad pursuit?... What wild ecstasy?\" He does not expect answers; he is so struck by the life and energy of the carved figures that he can only wonder at them. The questions express admiration and amazement: A. Nothing in the tone suggests fear, worry or sadness (B, D), and \"shock\" (C) is too negative. Memory tip: a string of excited \"What...?\" questions is the sound of wonder.",
 "|The rhyming pattern in the last stanza of Okigbo's 'Hurrah for Thunder' makes the stanza|an octave|a quatrain|a couplet|a sestet": "A couplet is two lines that rhyme together. Christopher Okigbo's \"Hurrah for Thunder\", from his Path of Thunder poems written before the Nigerian civil war, warns of the violence to come after the 1966 coups. It ends with the poet speaking of himself: \"If I don't learn to shut my mouth I'll soon go to hell, / I, Okigbo, town-crier, together with my iron bell.\" Two lines rhyming \"hell\" and \"bell\" make a couplet: C. The lines proved prophetic: Okigbo was killed fighting in the war in 1967. Memory tip: couple = two, so a couplet is a rhyming pair.",
 "|The theme of Browning's 'My Last Duchess' is|an aristocrat's marital problems|the pitfalls of a duchess now dead|an ambassador's personal life|the pride and arrogance of an aristocrat": "Robert Browning's \"My Last Duchess\" (1842) is a dramatic monologue spoken by the Duke of Ferrara to an envoy arranging his next marriage. Showing the portrait of his late wife, he complains that she smiled on everyone and valued a gift of cherries as much as his \"nine-hundred-years-old name\", and he would not \"stoop\" to correct her: \"I gave commands; / Then all smiles stopped together.\" The poem exposes the pride and arrogance of an aristocrat who treated his wife as a possession: D. The trap: A (an aristocrat's marital problems) is close, but the marriage only shows the deeper theme, the Duke's pride. Memory tip: the Duke will never \"stoop\": pride is the theme.",
 "|In Mapanje's 'When This Carnival Finally Closes', the poet is describing the|role of spirits in a carnival|preparation for a carnival|role of drumming and dancing in a carnival|role of sycophancy and bad leadership": "Jack Mapanje is a Malawian poet who was jailed without trial in 1987 by the dictator Kamuzu Banda. His poem \"When This Carnival Finally Closes\" uses a carnival, with dancers, praise-singers and drummers performing for the \"Life President\", as a symbol of a nation made to sing and dance in flattery. The poet attacks the sycophants who praise the leader for gain and the bad leadership that thrives on them, and he looks forward to the day the carnival ends: D. The drumming and dancing (C) are the surface picture, not the subject. Memory tip: in protest poems, a carnival often stands for empty praise of a tyrant.",
 "|From the tone of Wole Soyinka's 'Telephone Conversation', the two persons talking hold each other in|mutual suspicion|deep affection|high esteem|outright contempt": "Wole Soyinka's \"Telephone Conversation\" is a satirical poem in which an African man rings a London landlady about a room and honestly says \"I am African.\" She, with careful good manners, asks \"HOW DARK?\", \"ARE YOU LIGHT OR VERY DARK?\" She is suspicious of him because of his colour, and he in turn is wary, \"Caught I was, foully\", and answers with mocking irony about his \"peroxide blond\" bottom. Neither trusts the other: mutual suspicion, A. The trap: D (outright contempt) is close, but the landlady stays politely cautious and the speaker answers with irony, not open contempt. Memory tip: polite voices and careful questions on both sides signal suspicion.",
 "|In Rubadiri's 'Stanley Meets Mutesa', the poet portrays the hardship experienced by the explorer's party through|visual and verbal images|visual and tactile images|olfactory and visual images|auditory and olfactory images": "Imagery appeals to the senses: visual (sight), auditory (hearing), tactile (touch and bodily feeling), olfactory (smell) and gustatory (taste). David Rubadiri's \"Stanley Meets Mutesa\" describes the explorer H. M. Stanley's march to meet Kabaka Mutesa of Buganda in 1875. We see \"the thin weary line of carriers\" and the weak falling back (visual), and we feel the scorching sun, the heat, the thirst and the heavy loads on their heads (tactile, physical sensation). Together these make the hardship vivid: B. Smell and sound (C, D) play little part in the hardship. Memory tip: hardship on a march is what you see and what you feel in your body.",
 "|Browning's 'My Last Duchess' apparently shows Duke's|hatred for common love|desire to marry beautiful women|appreciation of works of art|praise of Fra Pandolf": "In \"My Last Duchess\", the Duke of Ferrara draws back a curtain to show the envoy his late wife's portrait, names the painter, \"Fra Pandolf's hands / Worked busily a day\", and at the end points out a bronze statue of Neptune \"which Claus of Innsbruck cast for me\". On the surface (apparently) he is a cultured collector proud of his art: C. The poem's real point is darker: he treats his wife, like his art, as a possession, and her portrait is the only form of her he can control. Option D is wrong because he names the painter but does not praise him. Memory tip: \"apparently\" asks for the surface, not the hidden meaning.",
 "|John Keats' 'Ode on a Grecian Urn' expresses the idea that|the Grecian Urn is ancient|the Grecian Urn is mysterious|every work of art is beautiful|life is long, art is longer": "John Keats wrote \"Ode on a Grecian Urn\" in 1819. The speaker looks at an ancient Greek vase carved with lovers, musicians and a sacrifice. The figures are frozen for ever: the lover can never kiss the girl, but \"she cannot fade\". Human life passes, while the work of art lasts: \"When old age shall this generation waste, / Thou shalt remain\". So the poem's idea is that art outlives life, which is what \"life is long, art is longer\" expresses: D. The option echoes the old saying \"art is long, life is short\". The trap: B, because the opening questions treat the urn's scenes as a puzzle, but that is where the poem starts, not the idea it develops. Memory tip: the urn outlasts the people who look at it.",
 "|In Oswald Mtshali's 'Nightfall in Soweto', night|drives man's enemy|fools man's enemy|hides man's enemy|aids man's enemy": "Oswald Mtshali, a South African poet, wrote \"Nightfall in Soweto\" about the Black township of Soweto under apartheid. Nightfall comes \"like a dreaded disease\": when darkness falls, criminals and the police roam, and the speaker, hiding in his small house, becomes their prey. The poem treats night itself as an enemy working with the attackers, so night aids man's enemy: D. The crimes are also done under the cover of darkness, so night hides the attackers too, and C is accepted as well. Options A and B have no support in the poem. Memory tip: in Soweto, night is the attacker's partner and his cloak.",
 "|'The curfew tolls the knell of parting day,\nThe lowing herd wind slowly o'er the lea,\nThe plowman homeward plods his weary way,\nAnd leaves the world to darkness and to me.'\n\nIn the lines above from Gray's 'Elegy Written in a Country Churchyard', the imagery is|of an approaching night in a rustic setting|of an approaching death in a village|about a village under a curfew one night|of a man abandoned in his homeward journey": "Thomas Gray's \"Elegy Written in a Country Churchyard\" (1751) opens at dusk in an English village: the curfew bell tolls the end of the day, the lowing cattle wind slowly over the meadow (lea), and the tired ploughman plods home, leaving the world \"to darkness and to me\". All the pictures are of evening falling in the countryside, so the imagery is of an approaching night in a rustic setting: A. The word \"knell\" hints at death, which the poem goes on to discuss, but these lines describe nightfall (B is too strong), and \"curfew\" here simply means the evening bell (C). Memory tip: herd, lea and ploughman mean rustic; parting day and darkness mean night.",
 "|The most important concepts in poetry are|rhythm and action|plot and rhythm|rhythm and imagery|plot and character": "Poetry is the most concentrated form of literature, and it works above all through sound and pictures. Rhythm is the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables that gives a poem its beat and music; imagery is the use of word-pictures that appeal to the senses. These two are the core of poetry: C. Plot, action and character (A, B, D) are the basic elements of prose fiction and drama, which tell stories; many poems tell no story at all. Memory tip: poetry is music (rhythm) plus pictures (imagery).",
 "|The literary device which anticipates that an event will take place is best described as|foreshadowing|rising action|flashback|parody": "Foreshadowing is a hint early in a work of what will happen later. For example, the witches' prophecy in Macbeth foreshadows his rise and fall, and a warning or an omen in a novel prepares the reader for a disaster. Because it anticipates a future event, the answer is A. Rising action (B) is the part of the plot where tension builds; flashback (C) goes back to past events, the opposite of foreshadowing; parody (D) is a mocking imitation of another work. The trap: C (flashback) is the closely related time device that students confuse with foreshadowing. Memory tip: foreshadowing throws a shadow forward; flashback flashes back.",
 "|The flat character in a literary work|is the same as the round character|cannot be found in an allegory|is capable of growing|always embodies a quality": "E. M. Forster divided characters into flat and round. A flat character is built round a single idea, quality or trait (the miser, the jealous husband, the faithful servant), stays the same throughout and can be summed up in a sentence. A round character is complex, has many sides and grows or changes. So a flat character always embodies a quality: D. It is the opposite of a round character (A), it does not grow (C), and allegories are actually full of flat characters who stand for one quality, such as Faithful in The Pilgrim's Progress (B). Memory tip: flat = one face; round = many sides.",
 "|The overwhelming pride that destroys the tragic hero is called|tragedy|nemesis|catharsis|hubris": "Hubris is the excessive pride or arrogance that leads a tragic hero to defy the gods, fate or moral law, and so brings about his downfall. Oedipus's confidence that he can escape his fate and Okonkwo's pride in Things Fall Apart are examples: D. The trap: B (nemesis) is closely linked: nemesis is the punishment or retribution that falls on the hero because of his hubris, not the pride itself. Tragedy (A) is the whole type of play, and catharsis (C) is the audience's emotional release. Memory tip: hubris is the sin; nemesis is the punishment.",
 "|A farce refers to a drama that has elements of the|serious|satiric|comic|tragic": "A farce is a type of comedy that aims only at laughter, using wildly improbable situations, mistaken identities, slapstick and exaggerated characters, with little concern for deep meaning. Its elements are comic: C. A satire (B) also uses comedy, but its aim is to attack and correct vice or folly, while a farce simply entertains. The serious and the tragic (A, D) belong to drama of a different kind. Memory tip: farce = fun for its own sake.",
 "|Catharsis is experienced|after one has written a play|when a play is still being staged|before watching a play|after watching a play": "Catharsis is Aristotle's word (in the Poetics) for the purging or release of pity and fear that the audience of a tragedy experiences. As the tragic hero falls on stage, the spectators feel pity for him and fear that such a fate could be theirs, and these emotions are released, leaving them calmer. This happens as the play is performed, rising to its height at the climax and catastrophe: B. Because the release is completed as the tragedy ends, many textbooks say catharsis is felt after watching the play, so D is accepted too. It does not belong to the writer (A) or come before the play (C). Memory tip: catharsis happens in the audience, through the tragedy they watch.",
 "|An extended fictional narrative which is realistic is known as a|diary|novel|novella|short story": "A novel is a long (extended) prose narrative of fiction that presents realistic characters and events, with a developed plot, setting and themes, such as Chinua Achebe's Things Fall Apart. Answer: B. A novella (C) is a shorter work of fiction, between a short story and a novel in length, like The Old Man and the Sea; a short story (D) is shorter still, usually readable at one sitting. A diary (A) is a personal daily record, not fiction. The trap: C (novella) catches students because it is also realistic prose fiction, but \"extended\" points to the full-length novel. Memory tip: novel = long and realistic; novella = a little novel.",
 "|The technique in which both the stage and the auditorium are involved in a play production is called|theatre of the absurd|role-playing|audience participation|total theatre": "Audience participation is a technique in which the barrier between actors and spectators is removed: actors speak to the audience, enter the auditorium, invite responses, songs or clapping, and may even bring spectators into the action. Both the stage and the auditorium become part of the play: C. It is common in African traditional performance and in plays such as Osofisan's. Theatre of the absurd (A) is drama about the meaninglessness of life (Beckett's Waiting for Godot); role-playing (B) is acting out a part. The trap: D (total theatre) is close, but total theatre means combining all the arts (music, dance, mime, spectacle) in one production, not involving the audience. Memory tip: participation means the audience takes part.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Animal Farm: \nOld Major ends his speech to the animals with a song in order to|spur them to action|entertain them|display his ability to sing|teach them how to sing an anthem": "George Orwell's Animal Farm (1945) is a short satirical novel, a beast fable about animals who overthrow their human farmer, Mr Jones, only to be enslaved by their own leaders, the pigs. It satirises the Russian Revolution and Stalin's rule. In Chapter 1 the old boar Old Major tells the animals of his dream of a world without Man and urges them to rebel. He ends by teaching them the song 'Beasts of England', which fires them with excitement and hope; it becomes the anthem of the rebellion. The song is meant to rouse them and spur them to action: A. Memory tip: Old Major sows the seed, the song makes it grow.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Animal Farm: \nThe execution of some of the animals on the orders of Napoleon is to|prevent Snowball from coming back to the farm|make the pigs constitute themselves into a special class|achieve the pigs' socialist goals without further delay|pave way for Napoleon to become an absolute ruler": "In Chapter 7 of Animal Farm, Napoleon calls a meeting at which several pigs, hens and other animals 'confess' to plotting with the exiled Snowball, and his dogs tear their throats out on the spot. Snowball is only a scapegoat; the real purpose is terror. After the killings no animal dares to question Napoleon, and he becomes a dictator whose word is law: D. The scene echoes Stalin's show trials and purges of the 1930s. The trap: A (preventing Snowball's return) uses Napoleon's own excuse, but Snowball never comes back and is only blamed for everything. Memory tip: in Animal Farm, every 'enemy' is an excuse for more power.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Animal Farm: \nThe message of the novel is that|capitalism is as bad as communism|humanism is better than animalism|communism is better than capitalism|no society exists where everyone is equal": "Animal Farm begins with the Seven Commandments, the last being 'All animals are equal'. By the end the pigs walk on two legs, wear clothes, trade with men and rewrite the commandment as 'All animals are equal, but some animals are more equal than others'. The other animals are as poor and overworked as under Mr Jones. The novel's bitter message is that the promise of perfect equality is betrayed: those who seize power become a new privileged class, so a society where everyone is truly equal does not exist: D. Orwell was attacking the betrayal of revolutions by tyrants rather than praising either capitalism or communism. Memory tip: 'some animals are more equal than others'.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Animal Farm: \nAfter the expulsion of Snowball from the farm, the triumvirate that ran the farm was composed of|Minimus, Napoleon and Squealer|Jessie, Napoleon and Bluebell|Boxer, Old Major and Napoleon|Squealer, Boxer and Napoleon": "After Napoleon's dogs chase Snowball off the farm, Napoleon abolishes the Sunday debates and the farm is run by the pigs. The power lies with three of them: Napoleon, the dictator; Squealer, the clever talker who 'could turn black into white' and explains away every change; and Minimus, the poet who writes the songs and the poem praising 'Comrade Napoleon': A. Jessie and Bluebell are dogs whose puppies Napoleon trains as his guards, Boxer is the loyal carthorse who is worked to death, and Old Major dies early in the story. Memory tip: the ruler, the mouthpiece and the praise-singer: Napoleon, Squealer and Minimus.",
 "|Based on S.I. Osammor's The Triumph of the Water Lily: \nThe dominant impression of the first person narrative in the novel gives it|an omniscient character|an epistolary character|a biographical character|an autobiographical character": "S.I. Osammor's The Triumph of the Water Lily is a Nigerian novel told in the first person: the narrator says 'I' and tells the story of the narrator's own life and experiences. A first-person narrative by the person who lived the events reads like an autobiography, a person's own account of their own life: D. An omniscient narrator (A) stands outside the story and knows everything, which is third-person narration. An epistolary novel (B) is told through letters. A biography (C) is a life written by someone else. The trap: C (biographical) is close, but a biography is written by another person; auto- means 'self'. Memory tip: auto = self, so autobiography = my story told by me.",
 "|'But there was a stillness about Ralph as he sat that mark him out: there was his size and attractive appearance; and most obscurely, yet powerfully, there was the conch. The being that had blown that, had sat waiting for them on the platform with the delicate thing balanced on his knees, was set apart.'\nGolding, Lord of the Flies.\n\nIn the passage above, the conch symbolizes|bestiality|authority|dictatorship|civilization": "William Golding's Lord of the Flies (1954) tells of schoolboys stranded on an island. Ralph finds a conch shell, blows it to call the scattered boys together, and is elected leader. From then on the conch is the sign of authority and order: whoever holds it may speak at meetings. The passage says that the conch, 'most obscurely, yet powerfully', marks Ralph out; the boy who blew it is 'set apart'. It gives him the right to lead: B. The trap: D (civilization) is close, since the conch also stands for rules and civilised order, but in this passage its effect is to mark out the leader, which is authority. Memory tip: who holds the conch holds the floor.",
 "|'The old Squire was an implacable man: he made resolutions in violent anger, but he was not moved from them after his anger subsided as fiery as volcanic matters cool and harden into rock'\nGeorge Eliot, Silas Marner\n\nIn the statement above, the Squire is portrayed as a|kind and generous friend|father to everyone in the community|miserly and ungracious ruler|quick tempered and adamant man": "George Eliot's Silas Marner (1861) is set in the village of Raveloe; Squire Cass is the village's chief landowner and the father of Godfrey and Dunstan. The passage calls him 'implacable': he makes resolutions 'in violent anger' (he is quick-tempered), and he is 'not moved from them' after his anger dies down, just as hot volcanic lava cools and hardens into rock (he is adamant, unbending). So he is a quick-tempered and adamant man: D. Nothing in the passage shows kindness, generosity or care for the community, and miserliness is not mentioned. Memory tip: hot like lava, hard like rock.",
 "|'I'm not going to be drawn into that old trap of an argument and be picked up by the tail like some wet rat out of a sewer.' The speaker in the statement above is being|symbolical|analogical|metaphorical|parabolical": "To speak metaphorically is to describe one thing in terms of another without meaning it literally. The speaker calls an argument an 'old trap' and pictures himself being 'picked up by the tail like some wet rat out of a sewer' if he loses. He is not really talking about traps or rats; he uses figurative comparisons to show how humiliating the argument would be: C. Symbolical (A) would mean an object standing for an idea; analogical (B) means reasoning through a point-by-point comparison; parabolical (D) means telling a parable, a short moral story. Memory tip: if words are not meant literally but picture one thing as another, it is metaphorical.",
 "|'Oshor: ... Winds howl forth-howl forth, hold back hold back-is it the time-oh endless time-slung in oblivion, make minced meat of your prey.'\nCharity Angya, The Cycle of the Moon\n\nThe mood captured in the lines above is one of|fear|happiness|indifference|joy": "Mood is the feeling or atmosphere a passage creates. In this extract from Charity Angya's play, Oshor calls on the winds to 'howl forth' and to 'make minced meat of your prey', and speaks of 'endless time-slung in oblivion'. The howling wind, the violent destruction of 'prey' and the idea of being lost in endless oblivion all build up a feeling of dread and terror: A. Nothing in the lines suggests happiness or joy, and the urgent, broken cries are the very opposite of indifference. Memory tip: to find the mood, list the strongest words (howl, prey, minced meat, oblivion) and ask how they make you feel.",
 "|'Oshor: ... Winds howl forth-howl forth, hold back hold back-is it the time-oh endless time-slung in oblivion, make minced meat of your prey.'\nCharity Angya, The Cycle of the Moon\n\nThe literary device used is|parallelism|irony|onomatopoeia|repetition": "Repetition is the deliberate repeating of a word or phrase for emphasis and rhythm. In the extract the speaker says 'howl forth-howl forth', 'hold back hold back' and 'time-oh endless time': each phrase is doubled, building urgency: D. Parallelism (A) is the use of similar grammatical structures in a series, not the same words. There is no irony (B) here. The trap: C (onomatopoeia) catches students because 'howl' imitates the sound of the wind, but one sound-word does not make the device of the whole passage; the doubling of phrases does. Memory tip: if you see the same words twice, think repetition.",
 "|'Those years of my travels. Years of innocence and experience...months of twiddling my thumbs with insecurity. In search of my true people. Yes, in search of my true people. But wherever I went I did not find people but caricatures of people who insisted on being taken seriously as people. Perhaps I was on the wrong planet. In the wrong skin.'\nDambudzo Marechera, Black Sunlight.\n\nThe passage above expresses the state of mind of|an alienated man|an adventurer|a renegade soldier|a prisoner": "Dambudzo Marechera (1952–1987) was a Zimbabwean writer whose work (The House of Hunger, Black Sunlight) shows deep restlessness and estrangement. In this passage the speaker has travelled everywhere 'in search of my true people' but found only 'caricatures of people'; he wonders if he was 'on the wrong planet. In the wrong skin.' He feels he does not belong anywhere, among anyone, or even in his own body: this is alienation, so the passage shows an alienated man: A. He travels, but not for adventure (B); nothing suggests a soldier or a prisoner. Memory tip: alienated = feeling like an alien, out of place everywhere.",
 "|'If I can fasten but one cup upon him with that which he had drunk tonight already, He'll be as full of quarrel and offence as my young mistress' dog...'\nShakespeare, Othello\n\nFrom the statement above it can be inferred that the speaker is|disobedient|quarrelsome|servile|scheming": "In Shakespeare's Othello (Act 2, Scene 3), Iago speaks these words in a soliloquy. He plans to make Cassio drink one more cup of wine on top of what he has already drunk, so that he becomes 'as full of quarrel and offence' as a snappish lapdog. The resulting brawl gets Cassio dismissed as Othello's lieutenant, which is exactly what Iago wants. The speaker is therefore plotting secretly against another: scheming, D. The trap: B (quarrelsome) describes what Iago wants Cassio to become, not Iago himself. Memory tip: in Othello, Iago is always the schemer.",
 "|'Thathat is why wwe must acc-c-cept the universal d-d-dome, b-b-because ththere is no d-d-d-direction. The b-b-bridge is the d-d-dome of rreligion and b-b-bridges d-ddon't jjjust g-g-go from hhhere to ththere; a bridge also faces backwards'.\nWole Soyinka, The Interpreters.\n\nThe idiolectal feature of the character's speech is the|repetition of consonant sounds|multiplication of medial consonants sounds|repetition of initial consonant sounds|duplication of consonant sound clusters": "An idiolect is the speech habit peculiar to one person. In Wole Soyinka's The Interpreters (1965), Sekoni, the engineer, stammers. The passage shows the stammer: 'd-d-dome', 'b-b-because', 'd-d-d-direction', 'g-g-go', 'jjjust', 'hhhere'. In almost every case the speaker stumbles over the first consonant sound of a word and repeats it before he can go on: repetition of initial consonant sounds, C. The trap: A (repetition of consonant sounds) is true but too general; it would also describe alliteration or consonance, while C names exactly where the repetition happens. Memory tip: a stammer catches at the start of the word.",
 "|'It was January and Monday. I began to understand. After every weekend, my father had to gather his nerve to go back to teaching. During the Christmas vacation he became slack and in a fury of screw-turning had to retighten himself. The long haul he called the stretch between Christmas and Easter.'\nJohn Updike, The Centaur.\n\nThe long haul in the narrative above describes|a long strike|Christmas and Easter holidays|a long period of hard work|a long vacation": "John Updike's The Centaur (1963) is narrated partly by Peter, son of the schoolteacher George Caldwell. The passage opens 'It was January': the Christmas vacation is over. The father must 'gather his nerve to go back to teaching' and 'retighten himself' after being slack in the holiday. The 'long haul' is the stretch between Christmas and Easter, the long school term with no holiday: a long period of hard work, C. It is not a vacation (D), nor the holidays themselves (B), nor a strike (A). The trap: D (a long vacation) catches students who see 'Christmas' and 'Easter' and think of holidays, but the long haul is the time between them. Memory tip: a 'haul' is hard pulling, not rest.",
 "|'Fierce harmattan sun on innocent tendrils; torrential downpour on leaking thatch roofs; painful comfort of helpless eunuchs; thorny beauty of modern witches; the falcon's call on day-old-chicks, ours is tragi-comic dialogue:... mute echoes of pains'.\n\nThe sonority and rhythmical structure of the lines above are achieved through a pause pattern called|enjambment|caesura|apostrophe|scansion": "A caesura is a pause or break within a line of verse, often marked by punctuation. In these lines the semicolons split the passage into a string of balanced phrases ('Fierce harmattan sun on innocent tendrils; torrential downpour on leaking thatch roofs; ...'), and the pauses between them give the lines their sound and rhythm: B. Enjambment (A) is the opposite, the running on of sense from one line to the next without a pause. Apostrophe (C) is addressing an absent person or thing. Scansion (D) is the analysis of metre, not a pause. The trap: A (enjambment) is the related term students confuse with caesura. Memory tip: caesura cuts (both start with c): a pause in the middle.",
 "|A body of imaginative men and women of letters is generally referred to as|litterateur|laureattes|literaria|literati": "The literati (Latin for 'lettered people') are the educated, literary class, writers, critics and scholars, taken as a group: D. The trap: A (litterateur) is a closely related word, but a litterateur is a single literary person, not a body of them. Laureates are people honoured with an award, such as the Nobel laureate Wole Soyinka or a Poet Laureate, and 'literaria' is not the term used. Memory tip: literati is plural, like the Latin it comes from; one litterateur, many literati.",
 "|A poem written to celebrate one's wedding is|a pastoral|an epithalamium|a prothalamium|a terza rima": "An epithalamium (from Greek 'at the bridal chamber') is a poem or song written to celebrate a wedding, in honour of the bride and groom. Edmund Spenser's 'Epithalamion' (1595), written for his own marriage, is the most famous example in English: B. The trap: C (prothalamium) is a poem written before a wedding, to celebrate the coming marriage (Spenser's 'Prothalamion'). A pastoral (A) idealises country life and shepherds, and terza rima (D) is a verse form of linked three-line stanzas (aba bcb cdc...). Memory tip: pro- = before (prothalamium); epi- = at/upon the wedding (epithalamium).",
 "|An iambic metrical foot consists of|an unstressed syllable following a stressed syllable|a stressed syllable following another stressed syllable|an unstressed syllable following another unstressed syllable|a stressed syllable following an unstressed syllable": "A metrical foot is a unit of stressed and unstressed syllables. The iamb is an unstressed syllable followed by a stressed one, da-DUM, as in 'to-DAY', 'be-LIEVE': D. It is the commonest foot in English poetry; five iambs make iambic pentameter: 'Shall I com-PARE thee TO a SUM-mer's DAY?' The trap: A (an unstressed syllable after a stressed one, DUM-da) describes the trochee, as in 'GAR-den'. B is a spondee (DUM-DUM) and C a pyrrhic (da-da). Memory tip: 'i-AMB' itself sounds da-DUM.",
 "|Empathy is achieved when the audience|feels betrayed by the director|denounces and humiliates the protagonist|vicariously participates in the stage experience|attacks the character foil": "Empathy is the ability to enter into another person's feelings. In drama it happens when the audience identifies so closely with a character that they share his joys and sufferings, as if living through them themselves: they participate vicariously (at second hand) in the stage experience: C. This is what makes us fear and pity a tragic hero and leads to catharsis. Feeling betrayed by the director, humiliating the protagonist or attacking a foil are not empathy at all. Memory tip: empathy = 'in his feelings'; sympathy = 'feeling for him'.",
 "|The overall design of a drama to which all other components relate is its|catharsis|exposition|structure|symbol": "The structure of a play is its overall design or architecture: how the action is arranged into acts and scenes and how the plot moves from exposition through rising action and climax to resolution. Every other element (characters, dialogue, setting, theme) fits into that design: C. Catharsis (A) is the emotional release of the audience at the end of a tragedy; a symbol (D) is an object standing for an idea. The trap: B (exposition) is a part of the structure, the opening that introduces the characters and background, not the whole design. Memory tip: structure is the skeleton the whole play hangs on.",
 "|What does the protagonist do in a picaresque novel?|He embarks on a journey|He slows down the narration|He draws illustrative pictures|He becomes an artist": "A picaresque novel (from Spanish picaro, 'rogue') follows a lively, often roguish hero of low birth through a string of loosely connected adventures as he travels from place to place and meets people of every class. The journey holds the episodes together and lets the writer satirise society. Examples are the Spanish Lazarillo de Tormes, Fielding's Tom Jones and Cervantes's Don Quixote: A. The protagonist does not slow the narration, draw pictures or become an artist. Memory tip: picaresque = a picaro on the road.",
 "|In the plot of a novel emphasis should be placed on|showing causal connection of incidents|the vivid presentation of incidents|the accurate presentation of incidents|narrating the truth of incidents": "E.M. Forster explained the difference: 'The king died and then the queen died' is a story; 'The king died, and then the queen died of grief' is a plot. A plot is a chain of events linked by cause and effect, so the emphasis in a novel's plot is on showing the causal connection between incidents: A. Vivid, accurate or truthful presentation of incidents (B, C, D) are qualities of good narration in general, not what makes a plot. The trap: A could be misread as 'casual', but the point is causal, cause and effect. Memory tip: story asks 'and then?'; plot asks 'why?'.",
 "|An irredeemable reversal of the hero's fortune in a tragedy is called|anagnorisis|peripeteia|purgation|hubris": "In Aristotle's Poetics, peripeteia is the reversal of fortune in a tragedy, the point where the hero's situation turns from good to bad, often unexpectedly: B. In Sophocles' Oedipus Rex, the messenger who comes to cheer Oedipus instead reveals his true birth, and his fortune collapses. The trap: A (anagnorisis) is Aristotle's partner term, the hero's moment of recognition or discovery of the truth, which often comes with the reversal but is not the reversal itself. Purgation (C) is catharsis, and hubris (D) is excessive pride. Memory tip: peripeteia = the plot turns; anagnorisis = the hero learns.",
 "|A system of plot development which creates the future through anticipation and the past through memory is|déjà vu|avant-garde|medieval|metaphysical": "Traditional plots move in a straight line from beginning to end. Avant-garde (experimental, modernist) writers broke with this: they move the story backwards through memory (flashback, recollection) and forwards through anticipation (foreshadowing, flash-forward), as in the novels of Joyce, Woolf or Faulkner. The precise names for the two moves are flashback and foreshadowing (a non-linear plot); no option gives them, so the closest is avant-garde, the movement known for this handling of time: B. Déjà vu (A) is the feeling that something now happening has happened before; it is not a method of plotting. Medieval and metaphysical (C, D) name a period and a school of poetry. Memory tip: avant-garde = the advance guard, writers who experiment ahead of the rest.",
 "|A limerick has|ornate style, humour, irony and seven lines|humour, rhyme scheme and five lines|formal and elaborate style and humour|a stinging climax, rhyme scheme and five lines": "A limerick is a short comic poem of five lines rhyming aabba: lines 1, 2 and 5 are longer, lines 3 and 4 short, and the last line often has a funny twist. Edward Lear made it popular: 'There was an Old Man with a beard, / Who said, It is just as I feared! ...'. So a limerick has humour, a rhyme scheme and five lines: B. It is not ornate, formal or seven lines long. The trap: D also mentions five lines and a rhyme scheme, but the last line is a comic twist, not a 'stinging climax', and D leaves out the humour. Memory tip: five lines, aabba, and a laugh.",
 "|'And my children left their peaceful nakedness for the uniform of iron and blood'. In the lines above from David Diop's Loser of Everything the imagery depicts a displacement of|innocence by violence|nature by science|village life by barrack life|the natural by the artificial": "David Diop (1927–1960) was a Senegalese poet of Negritude who wrote angrily against colonialism. In 'Loser of Everything' he contrasts the happy African past with the destruction colonialism brought. 'Peaceful nakedness' pictures the innocence and simple freedom of African children; 'the uniform of iron and blood' pictures soldiers' uniforms and the bloodshed of war. Innocence has been pushed out by violence: A. The trap: D (the natural by the artificial) also fits nakedness and uniform, but 'peaceful' versus 'iron and blood' is the stronger contrast, innocence against violence. Memory tip: peaceful vs blood = innocence vs violence.",
 "|'Pataki and mustard flowers like blue and yellow eyes peep through the green grass' The literary devices used in the lines above from Mohan Singh's 'A Village Girl' are|enjambment and alliteration|rhyme and limerick|simile and irony|repetition and assonance": "Mohan Singh (1905–1978) was an Indian poet who wrote in Punjabi; 'A Village Girl' paints a village girl against the beauty of the Punjab countryside. In the poem this sentence is not finished at the end of a line but runs on into the next ('peep through the green grass' completes it): that is enjambment. The repeated initial sounds in 'Pataki ... peep' and 'green grass' are alliteration: A. There is a simile ('like blue and yellow eyes') but no irony, so C fails; there is no repeated word, and a limerick is a poem, not a device. Memory tip: enjambment runs on; alliteration repeats the first sound.",
 "|John Donne's Death be not Proud is informed by a|romantic inquest into the nature of mortality|rejection of the belief in eternity|metaphysical attack on the omnipotence of God|metaphysical persuasion on the immortality of man": "John Donne (1572–1631) led the Metaphysical poets, known for witty argument and bold conceits. His Holy Sonnet 10, 'Death, be not proud', addresses Death directly (apostrophe) and argues it down point by point: death is only like rest and sleep, it is a 'slave to fate, chance, kings and desperate men', and 'One short sleep past, we wake eternally, / And death shall be no more; Death, thou shalt die.' The poem is a metaphysical argument that man's soul is immortal: D. It affirms eternity rather than rejecting it (B), and it trusts God rather than attacking him (C). Memory tip: Donne argues that Death dies, so man lives.",
 "|The mood of the narrator in Mohan Singh's 'A Village Girl' is one of|affection|antagonism|admiration|hatred": "In 'A Village Girl' Mohan Singh describes a simple village girl with delight, linking her beauty to the flowers and green grass of the countryside. The speaker looks at her with wonder and praise for her natural, unspoilt grace: his mood is admiration, C. Antagonism and hatred (B, D) are the opposite of the poem's tone. The trap: A (affection) is close, but affection means fondness for someone you are close to, while the speaker praises her from a distance, which is admiration. Memory tip: admire = look at with wonder.",
 "|The dominant imagery in Kofi Awoonor's 'The Cathedral' is|olfactory|auditory|tactile|visual": "Kofi Awoonor (1935–2013) was a Ghanaian poet. 'The Cathedral' is a short poem about colonialism: on a 'dirty patch' a tree once stood, 'shedding incense on the infant corn', its boughs stretched across the heaven; then 'they' sent surveyors and builders who cut the tree down and planted 'a huge senseless cathedral of doom'. Nearly every image is something we see: the patch, the tree, the boughs across the sky, the fires of the tribe, the cathedral. So the dominant imagery is visual, D. The tree stands for African tradition and the cathedral for the foreign religion that replaced it. Memory tip: visual imagery = pictures for the eye.",
 "|'I said I had the tree, It wasn't true. The opposite was true. The tree had me.' The imagery in the tree had me from Robert Frost's 'Wild Grapes' is|the adventures of some wild children in the forest|the dramatic suspension of a child on a tree|the successful climbing of an adventurous boy|reminiscent of man's transcendentalism": "Robert Frost (1874–1963), the American poet, wrote 'Wild Grapes' in the voice of a grown woman remembering a childhood event. As a small girl she grabbed hold of a young birch tree that her brother had bent down to pick grapes; when he let go, the tree sprang up and she was left hanging high in the air, clinging on until he talked her down. 'I said I had the tree. It wasn't true. The opposite was true. The tree had me.' The image is the dramatic suspension of a child on a tree: B. She did not climb it (C), and there were no wild children in a forest (A). Memory tip: the tree caught the girl, not the girl the tree.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \nThe play is essentially about the|theft of Miss Neville's trinket box|comedy of errors of one night|incompatibility of Mr. and Mrs. Hardcastle|love affair between Kate and Marlow": "Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer (1773) is an English comedy of manners whose subtitle is 'The Mistakes of a Night'. Tony Lumpkin tricks Marlow and Hastings into thinking Mr Hardcastle's house is an inn, so Marlow treats his host like an innkeeper, and later mistakes Kate Hardcastle for a barmaid. All these errors happen in one night: B. Both B and D are accepted: the main plot is Kate's courtship of the shy Marlow, whom she wins by 'stooping' to play a barmaid, which gives the play its title. The theft of the jewels (A) is a sub-plot. Memory tip: subtitle 'The Mistakes of a Night' = comedy of errors.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \nFrom the stage directions, it is apparent that Oliver Goldsmith makes use of a|set each for the upper and lower classes|different set for each act|variety of sets to suit the subject matter|single set in the whole play": "Stage directions tell us where each scene takes place. In She Stoops to Conquer, Act 1 opens in a chamber in Hardcastle's old-fashioned house and then moves to an alehouse room, The Three Pigeons, where Tony Lumpkin drinks with his friends; later scenes are in different rooms of the house, and in Act 5 the action moves to the back of the garden, where Mrs Hardcastle is driven round in the dark. Goldsmith changes the set to suit what is happening: C. It is not a single set (D), the changes do not follow one per act (B), and they are not split by class (A). Memory tip: the house, the alehouse and the garden.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \nHardcastle: And I love everything that's old: old friends, old times, old manners, old books, old wines; and I believe, Dorothy (taking her hand) you'll own I have been pretty fond of an old wife.\nMrs. Hardcastle: Lord, Mr. Hardcastle, you're forever at your Dorothy's and your old wife's. You may be a Darby, but I'll be no Joan, I promise you. I am not so old as you'd make me, by more than one good year.\n\nThe dialogue above reveals the couple's|fear of the unknown|difference in lifestyle|love for each other|humorous disposition": "In Act 1 of She Stoops to Conquer, Mr Hardcastle says he loves 'everything that's old': old friends, manners, books, wine and his 'old wife'. Mrs Hardcastle protests that she is no 'Joan' (the faithful old wife of the ballad 'Darby and Joan') and is younger than he makes out. She longs for London fashions and town life, while he loves quiet country ways. The dialogue shows their difference in lifestyle and taste: B. The trap: D (humorous disposition) is tempting because the exchange is funny, but it is Goldsmith who is humorous; the couple themselves are quarrelling about their different ways. Memory tip: he loves the old; she wants the new.",
 "|Based on J.P. Clark's The Wives' Revolt: \nCharacterization in the play is|economical but ineffective|economical and effective|elaborate but ineffective|elaborate and effective": "J.P. Clark (John Pepper Clark-Bekederemo, 1935–2020) was a Nigerian poet and playwright; The Wives' Revolt (1991) is a comedy set in Erhuwaren, an Urhobo community in the Niger Delta. The play has only three characters on stage: Okoro, the town crier; Koko, his wife; and Idama, his friend. Through them Clark shows the whole quarrel between the men and women of the town. Few characters, each clearly drawn and doing real work in the plot, is economical and effective characterisation: B. Memory tip: three people, a whole town's story.",
 "|Based on J.P. Clark's The Wives' Revolt: \nThe play is preoccupied with|the fight by women to keep their goats|the evil that oil money breeds|women liberation from male domination|women's protest against men's wickedness": "In The Wives' Revolt, the oil company pays compensation to Erhuwaren, and the men's elders share it in three equal parts: elders, men and women. The women feel cheated, since the men get two shares. When the men later ban goats, said to be the cause of an epidemic, and blame the women, the wives leave the town in protest. The play's central concern is the women's struggle against decisions imposed on them by men: women's liberation from male domination, C. The goats (A) and the oil money (B) are the occasions of the conflict, not its theme. Memory tip: the revolt is against male control, not goats.",
 "|Based on J.P. Clark's The Wives' Revolt: \nThe highest measure women in the community can take to register their protest is to|take to prostitution|carry out the nude act|abandon their husbands|abandon their children": "In The Wives' Revolt the women of Erhuwaren take their strongest step: they leave their husbands and the town and go to Iyara, refusing to return until their demands are met. The men are left to manage on their own. Abandoning their husbands is the highest form of protest the women take: C. The women do not take to prostitution or stage a nude protest, and although their walk-out leaves their homes behind, the protest is aimed at the husbands, so abandoning the husbands (C), not the children (D), is the measure itself. Memory tip: Erhuwaren without its wives is a town in trouble.",
 "|Based on J.P. Clark's The Wives' Revolt: \nThe plot of the play is lineal as reflected in the|episodic nature of the play|movement of the women|arrangement of the events in the play|progression of the argument of Okoro": "A lineal (linear) plot presents events in chronological order, from beginning to end, without flashbacks or jumps in time. In The Wives' Revolt the action moves straight forward: Okoro's announcement of the sharing of the oil money, the women's anger, the ban on goats, the wives' departure to Iyara, and their return. This straight-line ordering lies in the arrangement of the events in the play: C. An episodic plot (A) is a string of loosely connected episodes, the opposite of a tight linear plot. Memory tip: linear = in a line, one event after another.",
 "|Based on J.P. Clark's The Wives' Revolt: \nOKORO: I'll kill her today.\nIDAMA: No, you won't. There, run, Koko, oh, I say run! [He pushes her gently but firmly towards her room].\n\nThe event above happens in|Iyara when Okoro discovers Koko's infidelity|Erhuwaren when Koko suspects and accuses her husband|Erhuwaren when Okoro suspects Koko of infidelity|Erhuwaren before Koko's return from Iyara": "J.P. Clark's The Wives' Revolt is a three-character play (Okoro, his wife Koko and his friend Idama) set in Erhuwaren, an oil-rich Urhobo town. The angry wives leave for Iyara, but they come back infected with a disease. Okoro thinks only his wife is infected and accuses her of sleeping with men at Iyara; Idama knows all the women caught it, \"a case of pure contamination without contact\". His anger at home in Erhuwaren turns violent, and Idama shields Koko: C. It is Okoro who suspects, not Koko (B); Koko is already back (D); and the scene is not in Iyara, nor is any infidelity proved (A). Memory tip: Okoro suspects, Idama protects.",
 "|'Jibunoh was simply a difficult man. He was hated by all his neighbours and deserted by his relations. He was the first and only person to beat a policeman in the entire village. He also had ten cases instituted against him for either stealing or violating the rights of others. He had no regard for decency or normal conduct. No lover of peace or order could be his friend. It is not surprising that his death elicited jubilation rather than mourning.'\n\nThe passage above reveals that Jibunoh symbolizes|wickedness|insanity|lawlessness|isolation": "To say what a character \"symbolizes\", look at what all the details have in common. Jibunoh beat a policeman, the very person who stands for the law; he had ten court cases for stealing and for violating other people's rights; he had \"no regard for decency or normal conduct\", and \"no lover of peace or order\" could be his friend. Every detail is about breaking the law and rejecting order, so he stands for lawlessness: C. Wickedness (A) is close, but the passage keeps pointing to law and order (policeman, court cases, order), not just to evil in general. The trap: A catches students who stop at \"bad man\" instead of asking what kind of badness the details show. Memory tip: a symbol is the common thread running through all the details.",
 "|'Loveliest of trees, the cherry now\nIs hung with bloom along the bough,\nAnd stands about the woodland ride\nWearing white for Eastertide.'\nA.E. Housman, Loveliest of Trees, the Cherry Now\n\nIn the excerpt above, the subject matter is|love of trees|cherry bloom|snow at easter|woodland ride": "Subject matter is WHAT a passage is about; theme, tone and attitude are what the writer makes of it. In A.E. Housman's poem (No. II of A Shropshire Lad, 1896), the speaker looks at the cherry tree in spring: it \"is hung with bloom along the bough\" and stands \"wearing white for Eastertide\". Everything described is the cherry in blossom, so the subject matter is cherry bloom: B. The speaker's admiration (\"Loveliest of trees\") is his attitude to that subject, which is why some readers give \"love of trees\" (A); but the lines describe one tree in flower, not trees in general. The white blossom is not snow (C), and the woodland ride (D) is only where the tree stands. Memory tip: subject = the thing on show; theme = what the poet says about it.",
 "|'As virtuous men pass mildly away,\nAnd whisper to their souls to go,\nWhilst some of their sad friends do say\nThe breath goes now, and some say, No:'\n\nThe tone of the poem is generally|appreciative|serious|imaginative|conversational": "These are the opening lines of John Donne's \"A Valediction: Forbidding Mourning\" (about 1611), in which the speaker tells his wife not to weep when he goes on a journey. He begins with a quiet deathbed scene: good men die so gently that their friends cannot tell the moment the breath leaves. The subject (death), the hushed scene and the \"sad friends\" give the poem a grave, solemn, serious tone: B. \"Conversational\" (D) describes Donne's voice in some other poems, and the poem is certainly imaginative (C), but tone means the attitude and feeling conveyed, and here it is serious. Memory tip: tone = the speaker's voice and attitude; ask \"how would this sound if read aloud?\"",
 "|'I am informed the day recycles itself today\nWhen I slept in the Lord in February\nI blame no one for an untimely death\nI bless God for a noble departure—His words\nThe Lord taketh the righteous away before evil days.'\n\nThe persona in the poem appears happy to have died|a noble death|during the day|at an old age|an untimely death": "The speaker is a dead person (\"When I slept in the Lord in February\"), speaking calmly about his own death. He says \"I blame no one for an untimely death\" and \"I bless God for a noble departure\". To bless God for something is to be thankful and happy about it, so he is happy to have died a noble death: A. He mentions that the death was untimely (D), but only to say he blames no one; he is not happy about its untimeliness. The trap: D catches students who see the word \"death\" in both options; read which one he \"blesses God\" for. Memory tip: in persona questions, find the verb of feeling (bless, blame, mourn) and see what it attaches to.",
 "|'I am informed the day recycles itself today\nWhen I slept in the Lord in February\nI blame no one for an untimely death\nI bless God for a noble departure—His words\nThe Lord taketh the righteous away before evil days.'\n\nThe lines above express|anniversary|procreation|incarnation|resurrection": "\"I am informed the day recycles itself today\" means the same date has come round again: the day in February when the speaker died is being remembered a year (or years) later. A yearly return of a date that marks an event is an anniversary: A. Resurrection (D) would mean the dead person rising again; he is still \"slept in the Lord\". Procreation is the making of children and incarnation is taking on a human body; neither fits. The trap: D tempts students because the dead man speaks, but he speaks of the day returning, not of himself returning. Memory tip: anniversary comes from Latin annus (year) + versus (turned): the year has turned back to the date.",
 "|'If God is the sole author of man's life and all that befalls him on earth, does it not stand to reason that all evils that befall man are God sanctioned? If man generally turns to God to bail him out of evil or bad occurrences, is one wrong then to conclude that He allows evils to befall man to bring him back to Him?'\n\nThe literary style used in the passage above is|discursive|rhetorical|accusative|interrogative": "Style is the manner of writing. The passage is made up entirely of questions: \"does it not stand to reason that...?\" and \"is one wrong then to conclude that...?\" A style built on questions is interrogative: D. The questions also lean towards an answer, so some readers call the style rhetorical (B); but \"rhetorical\" is a broad label for persuasive writing, while the most visible feature here is that every sentence is a question. Discursive (A) means rambling from topic to topic, and accusative (C) means accusing; neither is the main feature. The trap: B, because students know the term \"rhetorical question\". Memory tip: interrogative = interrogation = questioning.",
 "|'And so Tom awoke and we rose in the dark\nAnd got with our bags and our brushes to work\nThough the morning was cold,\nTom was happy and warm;\nSo if all do their duty they need not fear harm.'\n\nIn the lines above, Tom is warm because he|has just woken from sleep|is doing his duty|is wearing warm clothes|is working indoors": "These lines end William Blake's \"The Chimney Sweeper\" (Songs of Innocence, 1789). Little Tom Dacre has dreamt that an angel freed the sweeps from their black coffins, and he wakes comforted. The morning is cold, yet \"Tom was happy and warm\", and the next line gives the reason in the boy's own words: \"So if all do their duty they need not fear harm.\" He feels warm because he is doing his duty: B. Blake is being ironic: the comfort is a lesson the child has been taught to accept his cruel work. Waking up, clothes and working indoors are not mentioned. Memory tip: in Blake's Innocence poems, a cheerful moral often hides an attack on society.",
 "|'James started showing interest in Buchi a long time ago. In playing the hunter, he realized that he needed a bait. This turned out to be his frequent offer of assistance to the difficult needy young woman. But Buchi has proved to be a difficult game. The more James trailed her the more he realized that she could not be easily caught.'\n\nThe choice of words in the passage above is remarkable because it is|largely confusing|very difficult|quite appropriate|drawn from hunting": "Diction (choice of words) can carry a hidden comparison. Here James \"playing the hunter\" needs \"a bait\", Buchi \"has proved to be a difficult game\" (game = hunted animals), and he \"trailed her\" but she \"could not be easily caught\". All these words belong to hunting, so the courtship is presented as an extended metaphor of a hunt: D. The words are simple and clear, not confusing or difficult (A, B). \"Quite appropriate\" (C) is a vague judgement, while D names what makes the choice remarkable. Memory tip: when several words come from one field (hunting, war, cooking), the writer is building an extended metaphor.",
 "|'To see the world in a grain of sand\nAnd a heaven in a wild flower,\nHold infinity in the palm of your hand\nAnd eternity in an hour.'\nWilliam Blake, To See a World in a Grain of Sand\n\nThe predominant figure of speech used in the lines above is|metaphor|hyperbole|simile|paradox": "These are the famous opening lines of William Blake's \"Auguries of Innocence\" (about 1803). To see a whole world in a grain of sand, heaven in a wild flower, infinity in a palm and eternity in an hour is impossible if taken literally. As deliberate overstatement this is hyperbole: B. But the lines are just as often called paradox (D), because each one sets the infinite inside the tiny, an apparent contradiction that holds a truth (the smallest thing of nature reveals the divine). So both B and D are accepted. There is no \"like\" or \"as\" (not simile), and the main effect is not plain identification (metaphor). Memory tip: hyperbole stretches the truth; paradox seems to break logic but makes sense.",
 "|'The gloom will give way to light\nAnd the thorny path cleared of pain\nThe storms will bow to the prompting of peace\nLost moments of glory will be restored\nAnd strangled opportunities reborn:\nWe shall yet regain the dawn'\n\nThe suggestion that runs through the short poem above is that of|anxiety and worry|anticipation of positive change|sorrow and dejection|fear of change": "The poem moves from bad to good in every line: gloom gives way to light, the thorny path is cleared of pain, storms bow to peace, lost glory \"will be restored\", strangled opportunities are \"reborn\", and \"We shall yet regain the dawn\". The future tense (\"will\", \"shall\") and images of light and dawn show hope and expectation of a better future: anticipation of positive change: B. Anxiety, sorrow and fear (A, C, D) are the moods of the gloom the poem is leaving behind. Memory tip: dawn and light are the classic images of hope; storms and night of trouble.",
 "|Farce thrives on|big events|premonition|absurdity|incidents": "A farce is a light comedy that aims only at laughter through absurd, highly improbable situations: mistaken identities, people hiding in cupboards, doors slamming, chases and slapstick. Character and probability are sacrificed for fast, ridiculous action, so farce thrives on absurdity: C. Big events, premonitions and mere incidents are not what defines it. Examples include Shakespeare's The Comedy of Errors and many Nollywood comedies. Memory tip: farce = far-fetched fun.",
 "|The form of poetry that celebrates with nostalgia the ideal world of the countryside is referred to as|an ode|a ballad|an elegy|a pastoral": "A pastoral (from Latin pastor, shepherd) is a poem that pictures country life, shepherds and nature as simple, peaceful and ideal, usually written by city people longing for a lost innocent world: D. Examples are Marlowe's \"The Passionate Shepherd to His Love\" and Milton's \"Lycidas\" (a pastoral elegy). An ode (A) is a formal poem of praise, a ballad (B) a song that tells a story, and an elegy (C) a poem of mourning. Memory tip: pastoral = pasture = shepherds in the countryside.",
 "|One rhyme scheme typical of the English sonnet is|abab cdcd cdedce|abba cdcd cde cde|abba abba cde dee|abab cdcd efef gg": "The English or Shakespearean sonnet has fourteen lines in iambic pentameter: three quatrains (four-line groups) with alternate rhyme and a final rhyming couplet: abab cdcd efef gg: D. The couplet usually sums up or twists the argument. The Italian (Petrarchan) sonnet has an octave abbaabba and a sestet such as cde cde or cdcdcd, so C (abba abba cde dee) is close to Italian form, not English. The trap: C, because students recognise abba abba as a sonnet pattern; it is the Italian one. Memory tip: English sonnet = 3 quatrains + 1 couplet, ending \"gg\".",
 "|The tragic character is the person whose experiences arouse pity and|terror|horror|frustration|sympathy": "In his Poetics, Aristotle said that tragedy arouses pity and fear (often translated \"terror\") in the audience and then brings a catharsis, a purging of those emotions. We feel pity because the hero suffers more than he deserves, and fear because we see that the same could happen to us: A. Sympathy (D) is close to pity, so it would repeat the same idea; horror (B) is disgust rather than the fear Aristotle meant. The trap: B, horror, which sounds like terror but means revulsion. Memory tip: the tragic pair is \"pity and terror\".",
 "|Allegory is used to describe a work in which|humans are transposed into animals|both animals and humans swap roles|animals are given human characters|abstract ideas are personified": "An allegory is a story in which characters, places and events stand for abstract ideas or real events, giving a second, deeper meaning. In Bunyan's The Pilgrim's Progress, Christian is every believer and the Slough of Despond is despair; abstract qualities appear as persons: D. Animals with human qualities (C) describes a fable or beast fable, which can be one kind of allegory (Orwell's Animal Farm), but it is not the definition. The trap: C, because students remember Animal Farm as an allegory; animals are a common vehicle, but the core of allegory is that things stand for ideas. Memory tip: allegory = a story with a second meaning running underneath.",
 "|The author of a novel sustains readers' interest through the use of|ambiguity|anecdotes|prologue|suspense": "Suspense is the tension a reader feels when uncertain about what will happen next. Novelists create it by delaying information, ending chapters on a cliff-hanger, putting characters in danger and hinting at what is coming. It is the main tool for sustaining interest through a long novel: D. Ambiguity (A) is double meaning, anecdotes (B) are short side stories and a prologue (C) is an introduction; none of them keeps the reader turning pages in the same way. Memory tip: suspense keeps the reader \"suspended\", hanging on to find out.",
 "|'Theatre-in-the-round' is employed to achieve a|quick resolution of conflicts|hilarious ending|contest between the hero and the villain|close rapport between players and spectators": "In theatre-in-the-round (arena staging), the acting area is in the centre and the audience sits on all sides, close to the actors. There is no proscenium arch or curtain to separate them, so actors and spectators share one space. This creates intimacy and a close rapport between players and spectators: D. It is also close to traditional African performance, where the audience forms a circle around the dancers or storyteller. It has nothing to do with how the plot ends (A, B, C). Memory tip: \"in the round\" = audience all around = everyone close.",
 "|In its classic form, the short story is distinguished by its|resolution|narrative form|compact plot|prosaic conception": "The short story, as defined by Edgar Allan Poe, aims at a single effect that can be read in one sitting. So it has a compact plot: one main incident, few characters, a short time span and no sub-plots: C. All fiction has a narrative form (B) and some resolution (A), so these do not distinguish the short story from the novel. Memory tip: short story = single incident, single effect, single sitting.",
 "|In literary convention, 'aside' is used to|make a pointed remark|emphasize scenes as different from acts|distinguish between events|gossip about other characters": "An aside is a short remark made by a character in a play to the audience (or to one other character) which, by convention, the other characters on stage do not hear. It usually reveals the speaker's true thoughts or makes a sharp, pointed comment on what is happening: A. For example, in Hamlet, Hamlet's first line, \"A little more than kin, and less than kind\", is a pointed aside about Claudius. A soliloquy is longer and spoken when alone. The trap: D, because asides often comment on other characters; but their purpose is a brief, pointed remark, not gossip. Memory tip: aside = a quick word \"to the side\", unheard by others on stage.",
 "|The writing convention in which the events in a narrative are scrambled as they come to the writer's mind without any attempt to arrange them in orderly sequence is called|shifting style|narrator's mind style|psycho-consciousness style|stream of consciousness": "Stream of consciousness is a narrative technique that records the flow of thoughts, feelings and memories as they pass through a mind, jumping from one to the next without logical order: D. The term was first used by the psychologist William James and the technique is associated with James Joyce (Ulysses) and Virginia Woolf (Mrs Dalloway). Events appear in the order the mind recalls them, not in time order. \"Narrator's mind style\", \"shifting style\" and \"psycho-consciousness style\" (A, B, C) are not literary terms. The trap: C, which uses the word \"consciousness\" to sound right. Memory tip: thoughts flow like a stream, with no paragraphs in the mind.",
 "|Based on J.P. Clark's The Wives' Revolt: \nThe mutual exchange of abuse in the play is reminiscent of|Ikaki|Ekpe|Udje|Etiyeri": "J.P. Clark (later Clark-Bekederemo, 1935–2020), the Nigerian poet and dramatist, set The Wives' Revolt (1991) in Erhuwaren, an Urhobo town. A quarrel over how money paid by an oil company is shared out among the town's elders, men and women grows into open conflict, and the women walk out of the town. Okoro and his wife Koko quarrel throughout, trading insults in a witty, song-like way. This mutual abuse recalls Udje, the Urhobo dance-song tradition in which rival communities compose satirical songs to abuse each other: C. Ikaki and Etiyeri are Ijaw masquerades and Ekpe is an Efik/Ibibio secret society, so none of them is about exchanging abuse. Memory tip: Udje = Urhobo songs of mutual abuse.",
 "|Based on J.P. Clark's The Wives' Revolt: \n'...Great orators in the assembly, and poor nannies at home.' Those being ridiculed here are the|spinsters|husbands|wives|old women": "In The Wives' Revolt, Clark's comedy about gender roles in the Urhobo town of Erhuwaren, the women leave the town in protest. The men who are so loud and eloquent in the town assembly are left at home to mind the children and cook. The line mocks them: \"Great orators in the assembly, and poor nannies at home.\" The people being ridiculed are the husbands: B. A nanny looks after children, and the joke is the contrast between the men's public boasting and their helplessness in domestic work. Memory tip: in this play, when the wives leave, the husbands' pride is exposed.",
 "|Based on J.P. Clark's The Wives' Revolt: \nIn their flight, the women settle at Iyara in order to|forestall reconciliation|seek peace|trade in its market|hurt their husbands": "In J.P. Clark's comedy The Wives' Revolt (1991), the women of Erhuwaren walk out of the town in protest after the men share the oil-company money in a way that favours men and then ban the goats the women keep. They go to Iyara (also spelt Eyara), described in study notes as Erhuwaren's arch-enemy town and a place of disease and poverty, where they are put up by Ighodayen. Choosing to stay in the enemy town is a deliberate taunt that wounds the men's pride: they settle there to hurt their husbands: D. They are not seeking peace (B); peace comes later, when the men plead for their return. The trap: A, because staying away does delay reconciliation, but the choice of place is aimed at the men. Memory tip: Iyara = the enemy town, chosen to sting the men.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \n'My heart thus pressing—fix'd my face and eye—\nWith a sententious look, that nothing means...'\n\nIn the lines above, the speaker implies that|his gesture means nothing|he is a pious man|he is being sentimental|his heart is pressing against his face and eye": "She Stoops to Conquer (1773) is Oliver Goldsmith's comedy of mistakes; Goldsmith wrote it partly to attack the tearful \"sentimental comedy\" then fashionable. The Prologue, by David Garrick, is spoken by an actor in mourning for the dying \"Comic Muse\". He tries to \"be moral\" like a sentimental actor, pressing his heart and fixing his face \"with a sententious look, that nothing means\", and adds, \"Faces are blocks in sentimental scenes\". He is saying that the sentimental pose is empty: his gesture means nothing: A. He is only pretending to be sentimental, as a joke against that kind of play. The trap: C, because he does act sentimental, but he says plainly that the look means nothing. Memory tip: Goldsmith's comedy laughs at tears, not with them.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \nThe use of the traditional verse form in the play applies to|the character of Sir Charles Marlow|the prologue, songs and the epilogue|male characters|female characters": "She Stoops to Conquer is a prose comedy: all the dialogue between Hardcastle, Kate, Marlow, Hastings, Tony and the rest is in prose. Verse, the traditional form of older drama, appears only in the Prologue (by David Garrick), in Tony Lumpkin's drinking song \"The Three Pigeons\" at the alehouse, and in the Epilogue spoken by Kate (Miss Hardcastle): B. Sir Charles Marlow (A) speaks prose like everyone else, and neither male nor female characters (C, D) as a group speak in verse. Memory tip: prose for the play, verse only for its frame and its song.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \nThe story of the play would have been different but for|Miss Hardcastle's attitude towards her guests|Mr. Hardcastle's ignorance of Young Marlow's true identity|the insensitivity of Mrs. Hardcastle|Tony's misinformation of Young Marlow and Mr. Hastings": "At the Three Pigeons alehouse, young Marlow and Hastings, lost on their way to Mr Hardcastle's house, ask the way. Tony Lumpkin, Hardcastle's mischievous stepson, tricks them by saying the house is an inn. So Marlow treats Hardcastle as an innkeeper and Kate as a barmaid, and this produces all the comic \"mistakes of a night\" (the play's subtitle): D. Without Tony's misinformation the visitors would simply have arrived as guests. Hardcastle knows who Marlow is (B is false), and Kate's stooping (A) only follows from Tony's trick. Memory tip: Tony starts the trouble; Kate stoops to conquer.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \nThe success of the play as a comedy relies on the author's use of|dramatic irony|stock comic characters|comic relief|stage and scenic disguise": "Dramatic irony is when the audience knows something a character does not. In She Stoops to Conquer the audience knows that the \"inn\" is Hardcastle's home, that the \"barmaid\" is Kate, and that Mrs Hardcastle's journey \"forty miles\" is just round her own garden, while the characters do not. The comedy comes from watching characters act on these mistakes: A. Kate's disguise (D) is one source of the mistakes, but it works through dramatic irony, which runs through the whole play. Comic relief (C) is light relief in a serious play, which this comedy does not need. The trap: D, which names one device; dramatic irony is the principle behind all of them. Memory tip: we laugh because we know and they don't.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \nThe Dorothy of the play is|Tony Lumpkin's mother|Young Marlow's mother|Miss Neville's mother|Kate, Hardcastle's daughter": "In Act I, Mr Hardcastle, who loves \"everything that's old\", says to his wife: \"I believe, Dorothy, you'll own I have been pretty fond of an old wife.\" So Dorothy is Mrs Hardcastle, vain, fashion-mad and doting on her son by her first marriage, Tony Lumpkin: A. Young Marlow's mother never appears, Miss Neville is an orphan in Mrs Hardcastle's care, and Kate (D) is Hardcastle's daughter. Memory tip: Dorothy Hardcastle dotes on Tony.",
 "|Based on Abubakar Gimba's Witnesses to Tears: \nThe subject matter of the novel is|marital infidelity|the evils of materialism|child abuse|the gains of love": "Abubakar Gimba (1952–2015) was a Nigerian novelist; Witnesses to Tears appeared in 1986. Hussaina, the only child of the widower Mr Anas Al-Amin, marries Lahab against her father's warning. Lahab, vice-principal of Cabral High School, is driven by greed for wealth and power: he grows rich through bribery and corruption and turns to a marabout, Dr Saahir, and to occult ritual, and the story ends with the death of his own son, Sagir. Critics describe the novel as an attack on corruption, greed and the destructive pursuit of wealth, so its subject matter is the evils of materialism: B. The trap: C, because a child dies, but his death is the price of his father's greed, not the subject of the book. Memory tip: Lahab's tears come from his love of things, not people.",
 "|Based on Abubakar Gimba's Witnesses to Tears: \nThe stealing of three thousand naira by the messenger and the manner of its presentation can be interpreted as Gimba's|sentimental attachment to money|approbation of stealing|condemnation of social injustice|justification of social vices": "In Witnesses to Tears (1986), Abubakar Gimba, an outspoken critic of corruption in Nigerian society, sets small people against powerful ones. Lahab, the greedy vice-principal, steals students' fees, yet, as one study of the novel puts it, it is his \"weak messenger\" who is to be imprisoned for ten years. The messenger's theft is shown beside the far greater greed of the powerful, and it is the poor man who bears the heavy punishment. Presenting it this way exposes an unjust society, so the episode is Gimba's condemnation of social injustice: C. Options A, B (approbation = approval) and D all credit Gimba with approving of money or vice, the opposite of his moral purpose. Memory tip: when the small thief is punished and the big thief prospers, the writer is condemning injustice.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Animal Farm: \nIn the novel, 'animalism' is a system of thought that emphasizes|conformity|equality|animality|disparity": "George Orwell's Animal Farm (1945) is a satirical fable about the Russian Revolution. Old Major's dream becomes the system called Animalism, summed up in Seven Commandments, the last being \"All animals are equal\". Its central idea is that all animals are equal and should share the fruits of their labour without a human master: B. The irony of the book is that the pigs later change it to \"All animals are equal, but some animals are more equal than others\". Disparity (D) is what the pigs create, the opposite of the ideal. Memory tip: Animalism promised equality; the pigs delivered inequality.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Animal Farm: \nThe dominant technique in the book is|irony|foreshadowing|symbolism|reminiscence": "Animal Farm is an allegory, so symbolism is its main technique: Manor Farm stands for Russia, Old Major for Marx and Lenin, Napoleon for Stalin, Snowball for Trotsky, the dogs for the secret police and Boxer for the loyal working class: C. Irony (A) is equally pervasive: a revolution fought for equality produces a new tyranny, the pigs end up walking on two legs, and the final scene shows that pigs and men can no longer be told apart. Both C and A are therefore accepted. Foreshadowing appears (the pigs take the milk early on) but is not dominant. Memory tip: every animal \"stands for\" someone: that is symbolism.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Animal Farm: \nIn the novel, Boxer is respected for his|duplicity|reticence|intellect|hard work": "Boxer is the huge, strong, loyal cart-horse in Animal Farm. He is not clever (he cannot learn the alphabet past D), but all the animals admire him for his endless labour: he rises early, works harder after every setback and takes the mottoes \"I will work harder\" and \"Napoleon is always right\". He represents the hard-working, trusting labourers of the Russian Revolution: D. When he collapses, the pigs sell him to the knacker. He is honest, not duplicitous (A), and he is not admired for intellect (C). Memory tip: Boxer = brawn and hard work, not brains.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Animal Farm: \nThe overthrow of Jones happens sooner than expected because the animals|experience acute suffering|are milked by Jones|are better schemers|receive assistance from Foxwood": "In Chapter 2 of Animal Farm, Mr Jones has been drinking. On Midsummer's Eve he gets so drunk that he does not come home, and his men milk the cows and go off rabbiting without feeding the animals. By evening the starving animals can bear it no longer: a cow breaks into the store-shed, the men attack with whips, and the animals turn on them and chase them out. Orwell says the Rebellion \"was achieved much earlier and more easily than anyone had expected\". Hunger and acute suffering brought it on: A. Foxwood (D) belongs to Pilkington, who does not help. Memory tip: the rebellion came early because empty stomachs could not wait.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Robert Frost's 'Wild Grapes' the girl's brother throws down grapes in the manner he does to|make his sister have her fill|allow himself time in-between to eat|make his sister desire to climb the tree|allow himself to have some fun": "Robert Frost's \"Wild Grapes\" (1923) is told by a woman remembering a childhood adventure. Her brother took her to a birch tree hung with wild grapes. He did the climbing and \"threw me down grapes to miss and scatter / And have to hunt for in sweet fern and hardhack; / Which gave him some time to himself to eat\". He threw them so that she had to search for them, which left him free to eat: B. Later he bent the birch down for her and she was swung into the air, holding on. The poem is about learning to \"let go\" with the hands but not with the heart. Memory tip: the brother scattered the grapes to keep his sister busy.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nDonne's 'Death, be not Proud' is|an epic|a dirge|a ballad|a sonnet": "John Donne (1572–1631), the English metaphysical poet, wrote \"Death, be not proud\" as Holy Sonnet X. It is fourteen lines long, with an Italian-style octave and a sestet ending in a rhyming couplet: \"One short sleep past, we wake eternally, / And death shall be no more; Death, thou shalt die.\" The speaker addresses Death directly (apostrophe) and argues that Death is not mighty but only a short sleep before eternal life: D. It is not a dirge (B), a song of mourning, because it mocks death instead of grieving. The trap: B, because the poem is about death. Memory tip: count the lines: fourteen means sonnet.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe arrangement of David Diop's 'Loser of Everything' in two stanzas reflects his use of|reinforcement|enjambment|contrast|amplification": "David Diop (1927–1960), the Senegalese Negritude poet, wrote \"Loser of Everything\" (Le Temps du martyre) about colonialism. The first stanza shows the past: the sun shining in the hut, beautiful women, children, harmony with nature. The second shows the sudden arrival of the colonisers, bringing guns, destruction and slavery. Placing the happy past and the ruined present in two separate stanzas sets them against each other: contrast: C. Enjambment (B) is the running-on of a sentence from one line to the next, which has nothing to do with the stanza division. Memory tip: two stanzas, two worlds: before and after.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe subject matter of Kassam's 'Maji Maji' is|socio-economic crises|independence|politics|colonialism": "Yusuf O. Kassam's \"Maji Maji\" appears in the anthology Poems from East Africa. Its title recalls the Maji Maji Rebellion (1905–1907), an armed rising against German colonial rule in German East Africa (now Tanzania). A spirit medium, Kinjikitile, gave the fighters a charm of sacred water (\"maji\" is Swahili for water) said to turn German bullets into water; it failed, and the rising ended in massacre and famine. In the poem an old man recalls the Germans' arrival, their guns and the dead. The poem is about colonial conquest and its cost: colonialism: D. The trap: B, because the rising was a fight against foreign rule, but the poem looks back on colonial violence, not on independence, which Tanganyika won only in 1961. Memory tip: maji = water; Maji Maji = the water charm against German guns.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \n'As if men hung here unblown, Their mildewed buds of love like pollen, Late caught, damp in a swollen...'\nThe sound device in the stanza above from Kalu Uka's 'Earth to Earth' is|onomatopoeia|a refrain|rhyme|repetition": "Kalu Uka is a Nigerian poet, playwright and critic. Rhyme is the matching of end sounds. In the lines quoted, \"pollen\" and \"swollen\" share the ending \"-ollen\", and \"unblown\" chimes with them on the \"-ol/-own\" sound. That repeated end sound is rhyme: C. There are no sound-imitating words such as \"buzz\" (A, onomatopoeia), no repeated line or chorus (B, refrain) and no repeated word or phrase (D, repetition). Memory tip: rhyme = same sound at the ends; repetition = same word again.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe tone of Kofi Awoonor's 'The Cathedral' is one of|reverence|contempt|indifference|awe": "Kofi Awoonor (1935–2013), the Ghanaian poet, wrote \"The Cathedral\" as a protest against colonialism. \"On this dirty patch / a tree once stood / shedding incense on the infant corn\"; then \"they\" sent surveyors and builders who cut the tree down, \"planting in its place / a huge senseless cathedral of doom\". The tree stands for African tradition and the cathedral for the alien religion and culture of the colonisers. Words like \"senseless\" and \"doom\" show the speaker's scorn: contempt: B. Reverence and awe (A, D) would mean respect for the cathedral, the opposite of what the poem shows. The trap: A, since one expects reverence for a cathedral; this speaker reveres the tree instead. Memory tip: in African protest poems the cathedral often stands for colonial domination.",
 "|'...a huge senseless cathedral of doom' in Kofi Awoonor's The Cathedral implies|meaningless foreign religious practices|meaningless indigenous religious practices|the architectural beauty of the cathedral|the Catholic church practices": "Kofi Awoonor (1935–2014) was a Ghanaian poet who drew on Ewe dirge traditions. The Cathedral is a short free-verse poem with two halves. Once, on \"this dirty patch\", a tree stood \"shedding incense on the infant corn\", a symbol of the African religion that blessed the land. Then foreign \"surveyors and builders\" came, felled the tree and raised \"a huge senseless cathedral of doom\". The cathedral stands for the imposed foreign religion and colonial culture: to the poet it is huge but senseless, and it brings doom rather than blessing. So the phrase implies meaningless foreign religious practices: A. It does not praise the building's beauty (C), and the poet does not single out Catholic practice (D). Memory tip: tree = African faith that gave life; cathedral = foreign faith that replaced it.",
 "|The persona in Kassam's Maji Maji represents the|warriors|Germans|Mzee|listeners": "Amin Kassam is an East African poet. His Maji Maji recalls the Maji Maji uprising of 1905–07 in Tanganyika (now Tanzania), when African peoples rose against German colonial rule. A spiritual leader gave the fighters \"maji\" (water) medicine which, they were told, would turn German bullets into water. The poem is spoken in the \"we\" voice of the fighters themselves, who trusted the medicine and went into battle, so the persona represents the warriors: A. The Germans (B) are the enemy the persona faces, not the speaker, and the Mzee (C), the elder, is someone the fighters speak about. Memory tip: maji is Swahili for water; the poem speaks for the men who trusted the water.",
 "|David Diop's Loser of Everything can be seen as|a lamentation of the destruction of African values|the suppression of an African village|a celebration of African values|an account of warfare between an African community and an invading force": "David Diop (1927–1960) was a Negritude poet of Senegalese and Cameroonian parentage, known for angry poems against colonialism. In Loser of Everything the speaker first remembers a happy life: the sun \"laughing\" in his hut, wives lovely as palms in the breeze, children paddling fearlessly on the great river. Then \"the conquerors\" come; his women give their lips to the \"steel-eyed\" strangers and his children exchange their \"peaceful nakedness\" for uniforms of iron and blood. The title says it all: he has lost everything. The poem is a lament for African values destroyed by colonial rule: A. It is not a celebration (C), because the joy is all in the past, and it describes a cultural loss rather than a battle report (D). Memory tip: happy past, ruined present, so it is a lament.",
 "|Arthur Nortje's Nightfall is written against the backdrop of|apartheid|revolution|celebration|violence": "Arthur Nortje (1942–1970) was a \"coloured\" South African poet. Under apartheid, the law separated South Africans by race, controlled where non-whites could live and work, and was enforced by police raids, pass laws and detention. Nortje's poems, written first in South Africa and then in exile in Britain and Canada, are full of the loneliness, fear and anger of that system; Nightfall carries the dark, threatening night atmosphere of a police state. So the backdrop is apartheid: A. Violence (D) is part of apartheid, but it is not the backdrop itself; the system is. Memory tip: South African poets of the 1960s, such as Nortje and Dennis Brutus, wrote against the backdrop of apartheid.",
 "|The allusion to Eurydice's experience in Robert Frost's Wild Grapes is an imagery of death and|reincarnation|resurrection|rebirth|growth": "In Greek myth Eurydice, wife of the singer Orpheus, died and went down to the underworld; Orpheus charmed the gods into letting her come back to life. In Robert Frost's Wild Grapes (1923) a woman remembers being swung up into the air as a girl when the birch tree she held sprang up, and being \"come after like Eurydice / And brought down safely from the upper regions\". She says her old beginning \"was wiped out in fear\" and \"the life I live now's an extra life\". The allusion pictures death followed by a return to life, which is resurrection: B. Rebirth (C) fits just as well, since she speaks of a new, extra life, so both answers are accepted. Memory tip: Eurydice = brought back from death.",
 "|In John Donne's Death, be not Proud, the poet believes that death is|immortal|mighty|pleasurable|dreadful": "John Donne (1572–1631) was the leading English metaphysical poet. In his Holy Sonnet \"Death, be not proud\" he addresses death directly (apostrophe) and argues it should not boast. Some have called death \"mighty and dreadful\", but \"thou art not so\". Rest and sleep, which are only \"pictures\" of death, give \"much pleasure\", so \"much more must flow\" from death itself; death is only \"one short sleep\" before we \"wake eternally\", and then \"Death, thou shalt die\". So the poet believes death is pleasurable: C. The trap: B (mighty) and D (dreadful) are words that appear in the poem, but Donne quotes them only to deny them. Memory tip: Donne mocks death as a mere sleep, which is restful and pleasant.",
 "|'Everywhere now, freedom is on the loose, And in its name, men and women slaughter one another with terrible abandon. Carnage has become the means of settling simple scores with our friends'\nOkinba Launko: Pain Remembers, Love Rekindles\n\nThe dominant rhetorical device in the poem above is|innuendo|assonance|oxymoron|limerick": "Okinba Launko is a pen name of the Nigerian playwright and poet Femi Osofisan. In these lines the poet does not name the guilty or attack them openly. Instead he hints with bitter suggestion: \"freedom is on the loose\" and people \"slaughter one another\" in its name, settling \"simple scores with our friends\". The word freedom is made to imply its opposite, killing and chaos. Indirect, suggestive criticism of this kind is innuendo: A. Assonance (B) is repeated vowel sound, an oxymoron (C) puts two contradictory words side by side (e.g. \"bitter-sweet\"), and a limerick (D) is a comic five-line poem. Memory tip: innuendo = hinting, not stating.",
 "|'This thing you are doing is too heavy for you,' he said. 'I went to school only a little, but I have killed many many more years in this world than you have,'\nGabriel Okara: The Voice\n\nIt can be inferred from the passage above that the|listener is a porter|listener is wise|speaker is a murderer|speaker is more experienced": "Gabriel Okara's novel The Voice (1964) is famous for carrying Ijaw thought and word order into English. Here an old man warns the young hero: \"I went to school only a little, but I have killed many many more years in this world than you have.\" To \"kill years\" is an Ijaw idiom for living through them. So the speaker is saying he is much older and has seen more of life: he is more experienced: D. The trap: C (speaker is a murderer) catches students who read \"killed\" literally; nobody is killed, only years are lived. Nothing in the lines shows the listener is a porter or wise (A, B). Memory tip: in The Voice, read Okara's English through Ijaw idiom.",
 "|'The guilty are too well-fed to pass through the needle's eye of our scorn the noose of public contempt hangs idle at the market place'\nOdia Ofeimun: The Poet Lied and Other Poems\n\nThe allusion in the excerpt above is|mythical|biblical|historical|classical": "An allusion is a brief reference to a well-known person, story or text. Odia Ofeimun writes that \"the guilty are too well-fed to pass through the needle's eye of our scorn\". This echoes Jesus' words in the Gospels: \"It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of God.\" The fat, rich guilty men cannot pass through, so the allusion is biblical: B. A mythical or classical allusion (A, D) would point to Greek or Roman gods and heroes, and a historical one (C) to real events or people. Memory tip: camel, needle, rich man: Bible.",
 "|'My brother you flash your teeth in response to every hypocrisy. My brother with gold-rimmed glasses. You give your master a blue-eyed faithful look. My poor brother in immaculate evening dress. Screaming and whispering and pleading in the parlours of condescension'.\nD. Diop: The Renegade\n\nThe poet's attitude here is|paradoxical|envious|ironical|sarcastic": "In The Renegade, David Diop addresses an African \"brother\" who has copied the coloniser: he flashes his teeth at \"every hypocrisy\", wears \"gold-rimmed glasses\", gives his master \"a blue-eyed faithful look\" and pleads \"in the parlours of condescension\". Every detail is chosen to mock and wound; even \"My poor brother\" is said with contempt, not pity. That cutting, scornful mockery is sarcasm: D. The trap: C (ironical) is close, because sarcasm uses irony; but irony is a gap between what is said and what is meant, while sarcasm is open, bitter ridicule aimed at a person, which is what Diop does here. Memory tip: sarcasm comes from the Greek for \"tearing flesh\": it bites.",
 "|MR. BOROFO: I heartily curse the day my wife decided to go to England. Ever since then, I have had nothing but we must do this because it is done in England, we mustn't do that, because it is not done by English people and so on ad nauseam.\n\nThe subject matter of the passage above is the|uncritical acceptance of European values|woes of English marriage|adventures in England|intransigence of an African wife": "Mr Borofo complains that since his wife went to England, everything at home is judged by one rule: \"we must do this because it is done in England, we mustn't do that, because it is not done by English people\". The passage is about an African who copies English ways blindly, without asking whether they suit her own life. That is the uncritical acceptance of European values: A. The marriage is not English (B), there are no adventures described (C), and although the wife is stubborn, her stubbornness is only a sign of the real subject, her worship of everything English (D). Memory tip: \"because it is done in England\" = copying without thinking.",
 "|'Since you left here my mind longs after there. Now in the dark I grope keenly striving to cope'.\nGbemisola Adeoti: Absence\n\nThe dominant technique in the lines above is|irony|rhyme|alliteration|metaphor": "Rhyme is the repetition of the same end sound in words, usually at the ends of lines. In Gbemisola Adeoti's lines the sounds echo clearly: \"here\" chimes with \"there\", and \"grope\" chimes with \"cope\". This musical matching of sounds is the most striking feature of the short extract, so the dominant technique is rhyme: B. There is no gap between what is said and meant (irony, A), and the lines do not repeat initial consonants (alliteration, C). Groping \"in the dark\" is a figurative touch, but it is a small part of the lines compared with the rhyme. Memory tip: read the ends of the lines aloud; if they chime, think rhyme.",
 "|He was an odd-looking duck, Inman was. He was in his mid-fifties but still had a head of thick black hair, which began low on his round skull. Everything about Inman was round. He seemed to be made of a series of balls piled one atop the other.\nTom Wolfe: A Man in Full\n\nThe author's attitude to Inman in the passage above is one of|praise|hatred|ridicule|admiration": "An author's attitude is shown by word choice. Tom Wolfe calls Inman \"an odd-looking duck\", stresses that \"everything about Inman was round\", and says he \"seemed to be made of a series of balls piled one atop the other\". These comic images turn the man into a figure of fun, like a snowman or a cartoon. That is ridicule: C. There is no praise or admiration of his looks (A, D), and the tone is amused rather than full of hatred (B). Memory tip: when a description makes you laugh at a character, the attitude is ridicule.",
 "|'Senhor José got cold during the night. After having uttered those redundant useless words, here she is, he wasn't sure what else he should do. It was true that, after long and arduous labours, he had managed, at last, to find the unknown woman, or rather, the place where she lay, a good six feet beneath an earth that still sustained him'\nJose Saramago: All the Names\n\nWhat happens to the unknown woman in the passage above?|she runs away|she is awake|she falls asleep|she is dead": "José Saramago (1922–2010), the Portuguese Nobel laureate, wrote All the Names (1997) about Senhor José, a lowly clerk in the Central Registry who becomes obsessed with tracing an unknown woman whose record card he found. In this passage his long search ends at last, but he finds only \"the place where she lay, a good six feet beneath an earth that still sustained him\". Six feet under the earth is a grave. The woman is dead and buried, while the earth still holds up the living José: D. The passage gives no sign that she ran away, is awake or fell asleep. Memory tip: \"six feet under\" means buried, so dead.",
 "|'I am alone. And the murmur of my lips. Carry song and tears homewards. From a plain away from home'\nOkogbule Wonodi: Lament for Shola\n\nThe poet persona here expresses a feeling of|elation|anger|nostalgia|sorrow": "Okogbule Wonodi (1936–1973) was a Nigerian poet. In these lines from Lament for Shola the persona is \"alone\", far from home (\"from a plain away from home\"), and sends \"song and tears homewards\". The feeling is a sad longing for home and for what is far away: nostalgia: C. There is no joy (elation, A) or rage (anger, B). The trap: D (sorrow) is tempting because of the word \"Lament\" in the title and the \"tears\", but the lines keep pointing \"homewards\": the sadness is the special longing for home that we call nostalgia. Memory tip: nostalgia comes from Greek nostos (homecoming) + algos (pain): homesick pain.",
 "|Be him English. Be him African. Be him Nigerian\n\nThe lines above are an example of|epitaph|anaphora|tautology|epigram": "Anaphora is the repetition of the same word or group of words at the beginning of successive lines, clauses or sentences, for emphasis and rhythm. Here each sentence opens with \"Be him\": \"Be him English. Be him African. Be him Nigerian\". That repeated opening is anaphora: B. An epitaph (A) is an inscription for a tomb, tautology (C) is saying the same thing twice in different words (\"a free gift\"), and an epigram (D) is a short witty saying. Memory tip: anaphora = repeat at the start; epistrophe = repeat at the end.",
 "|A dramatic composition or musical play in which many or all the words are sung is called|an oratorio|a motet|an opera|a concert": "An opera is a drama set to music in which most or all of the words are sung, performed on stage with costumes, scenery and an orchestra, for example Mozart's The Magic Flute. So a dramatic or musical play in which many or all the words are sung is an opera: C. A motet (B) is a short sacred choral song, and a concert (D) is any musical performance. The trap: A (oratorio) is also a long sung work for soloists, choir and orchestra, usually on a religious theme, such as Handel's Messiah; but an oratorio is performed without acting, costumes or scenery, so it is not a play. Memory tip: opera = sung play; oratorio = sung concert story.",
 "|The poetic device that forcefully brings together two seemingly unrelated ideas or concepts is|conceit|contrast|couplet|diatribe": "A conceit is an elaborate and often startling comparison between two things that seem to have nothing in common, worked out at length. It is the trademark of the metaphysical poets: John Donne compares two parted lovers to the legs of a pair of compasses, and in The Flea a flea bite becomes a marriage bed. Samuel Johnson said that in such poetry \"the most heterogeneous ideas are yoked by violence together\". That forceful yoking of unrelated ideas is the conceit: A. The trap: B (contrast) also puts two ideas side by side, but it sets opposites against each other to show their difference; it does not fuse unlike things into one comparison. Memory tip: conceit = clever concept connecting unconnected things.",
 "|For a play to be successful on stage, it must not be short of|audiences|speeches|actions|characters": "Aristotle defined tragedy as \"the imitation of an action\", and drama has been understood ever since as action shown on stage: the word drama itself comes from the Greek for \"to do\" or \"to act\". Audiences watch things happen; speeches, characters and even an audience are needed, but a play with no action, where nothing develops or is done, dies on stage however fine its speeches. So a play must not be short of actions: C. Memory tip: drama = doing; on stage, action speaks louder than words.",
 "|The setting in pastoral poetry is usually|oceanic|urban|ecumenical|idyllic": "Pastoral poetry (from Latin pastor, shepherd) presents the life of shepherds and country folk as simple, innocent and peaceful, in green fields and meadows far from the city. Such an ideal, peaceful, picture-perfect setting is called idyllic: D. The idyll itself is a short pastoral poem, a form begun by the Greek poet Theocritus. The setting is rural, never urban (B) or oceanic (A); ecumenical (C) has to do with uniting Christian churches. Famous pastorals include Milton's Lycidas and Marlowe's The Passionate Shepherd to His Love. Memory tip: pastoral = pasture = idyllic countryside.",
 "|The exclusive rights given to authors to protect their works from unlawful production is|a copyright|an authority to write|an author's right|a constitutional provision": "Copyright is the exclusive legal right of an author (or the person he assigns it to) to copy, publish, perform, translate or sell a work. Anyone who reprints the work without permission commits piracy, and the law can punish him. In Nigeria it is governed by the Copyright Act and administered by the Nigerian Copyright Commission. So the exclusive right that protects authors from unlawful reproduction is copyright: A. Memory tip: copy + right = the right to make copies belongs to the author.",
 "|In a narration, the first person is|the author|a participant|the publisher|an observer": "Point of view is the angle from which a story is told. In first-person narration the narrator speaks as \"I\" and is a character inside the story, taking part in the events he describes, as the boy does in Camara Laye's The African Child. So the first-person narrator is a participant: B. He is not the same as the author (A), who creates him. The trap: D (an observer) describes a third-person narrator, who stands outside the story and watches; first person means the teller is in the action. Memory tip: I = involved.",
 "|A deliberate imitation of a literary style with the intention to ridicule is|paradox|prosody|pun|parody": "Parody is a deliberate imitation of the style of a particular writer, work or genre, exaggerated in order to mock it or make it look foolish. Cervantes' Don Quixote parodies chivalric romances, and Pope's The Rape of the Lock parodies the epic. So a deliberate imitation of a literary style meant to ridicule it is parody: D. A paradox (A) is a statement that seems self-contradictory but contains a truth, prosody (B) is the study of metre and versification, and a pun (C) is a play on words. Memory tip: parody = parrot + mockery.",
 "|Point of denouement in a literary work is the|point at which the major character is shown in his true colours|point of disagreement in a narrative|point of the resolution of the puzzling issues|cathartic point in a tragedy": "Plot is often pictured as a pyramid: exposition, rising action (complication), climax, falling action, and denouement. Denouement is French for \"untying\": after the climax the knots of the plot are untied, misunderstandings are cleared up, puzzles are explained and the fates of the characters are settled. So the denouement is the point of resolution of the puzzling issues: C. Revealing a character's true colours (A) may happen anywhere in the plot, and catharsis (D) is the emotional cleansing the audience feels, not a stage of the plot. Memory tip: the knot is tied in the complication and untied in the denouement.",
 "|Periphrasis in poetic diction is marked by|circumlocution|irony|proverb|parable": "Periphrasis is the use of a longer, roundabout phrase where a short, plain word would do. Eighteenth-century poetic diction loved it: \"the finny tribe\" for fish, \"the feathered choir\" for birds. Using many words to go round a meaning is circumlocution, so periphrasis is marked by circumlocution: A. Irony (B) is a gap between words and meaning, a proverb (C) is a short traditional saying, and a parable (D) is a short story that teaches a moral. Memory tip: peri- means \"around\" (as in perimeter): periphrasis talks around the point.",
 "|Melodrama is a theatrical performance in which|the serious is treated in a satirical way|subordination of characterization to plot occurs|music and dance are predominant|evil triumphs over good": "Melodrama is a type of play with a sensational, fast-moving plot full of suspense, danger and strong emotion, and with flat, stock characters: the spotless hero, the helpless heroine, the wicked villain. The characters exist to serve the exciting story rather than to be explored in depth, so characterisation is subordinated to plot: B. Good usually triumphs over evil at the end, the opposite of D. Treating serious matters satirically (A) describes burlesque or satire. Music once accompanied early melodramas (the name means \"song-drama\"), but music and dance are not its main feature (C). Memory tip: in melodrama the thrilling plot is king and the characters are its servants.",
 "|Based on J.P. Clark's The Wives' Revolt: \nThe tradition of Erhuwaren endorses|polyarchy|anarchy|matriarchy|patriarchy": "J.P. Clark (John Pepper Clark-Bekederemo, 1935–2020) was a Nigerian poet and playwright. The Wives' Revolt (1991) is a comedy set in Erhuwaren, an Urhobo community in the Niger Delta. When the elders share money paid by an oil company equally among elders, men and women, the wives protest; when the men then ban goats, which only the women keep, the wives leave town for Ukpe in revolt. In Erhuwaren the men hold power: the elders and husbands decide, share the money and issue the decrees, and the women are expected to obey. So the tradition endorses patriarchy: D. The trap: C (matriarchy) catches students who think of the women's revolt, but the women revolt precisely because the system is run by men. Memory tip: patri = father: rule by the men.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \nThe major thematic preoccupation of the play is|the constraints on parental obligations|the problems of class and English mannerisms|the presentation of a mannerless society|changing the traditional view about English comedy": "Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer (1773) is a \"laughing comedy\" of manners. Young Marlow, sent to court Kate Hardcastle, is shy and stammering with ladies of his own class but bold with barmaids and servants. Tricked by Tony Lumpkin into thinking the Hardcastles' house is an inn, he treats his host as an innkeeper, and Kate wins him by posing as a barmaid. The play keeps turning on class and manners: how people of different ranks behave, the contrast between fashionable town manners and country ways, and how class affects love: B. It was also written to bring back laughing comedy against sentimental comedy, but that was Goldsmith's purpose, not the play's theme (D). Memory tip: Marlow's split behaviour (shy with ladies, bold with maids) is a class problem.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \nMiss Hardcastle is able to 'conquer' at the end of the play because of her|indiscretion|beauty|wit|brawn": "In She Stoops to Conquer Kate Hardcastle knows that Marlow freezes in front of respectable ladies. When he mistakes her for a barmaid, she cleverly plays the part, talks freely with him, then poses as a poor relation, and so wins his love before his true feelings are revealed to both fathers. She \"stoops\" to a lower role in order to \"conquer\" him, using her intelligence and quick tongue: wit: C. Her beauty (B) attracts him, but it was not enough while he could not even look at her face as a lady; it is her clever plan that wins. Memory tip: she stoops by disguise and conquers by wit.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \nOne of the enriching features of the play is the|matchmaking attempts by Mrs. Hardcastle|manipulation of appearance and reality|use of rural and urban settings|lopsided resolution of conflicts": "She Stoops to Conquer is built on the gap between how things appear and what they really are. Tony Lumpkin makes Marlow and Hastings believe that Mr Hardcastle's house is an inn and Hardcastle the landlord; Kate appears as a barmaid; Tony drives his mother round her own garden while she thinks she is lost on Crackskull Common. These manipulations of appearance and reality create the comedy's mistakes and laughter: B. The play is set in the country, not in both town and country (C), and the conflicts end in a happy, balanced resolution, not a lopsided one (D). Memory tip: the play's subtitle is The Mistakes of a Night: mistakes come from appearances.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \nThe epilogue of the play deals with the impermanence of|nature|friendship|womanhood|love": "The epilogue of She Stoops to Conquer, spoken by Miss Hardcastle, imitates Jaques's \"All the world's a stage\" (seven ages of man) speech in Shakespeare's As You Like It. It traces the \"acts\" of a barmaid's life: first a simple, blushing country maid, then a bold barmaid, then a fine town lady wedded to a squire who grows proud and fashionable, and finally old. By showing how a woman passes from one stage to the next, it deals with the changing, impermanent state of womanhood: C. Memory tip: Shakespeare's seven ages of man become Goldsmith's ages of a woman.",
 "|Based on Oliver Goldsmith's She Stoops to Conquer: \n'There be two gentlemen in a post-chaise at the door. They have lost their way upo' the forest...'\n\nThe two gentlemen in the excerpt above are|Diggory and Saunders|Marlow and Hastings|Marlow and Hardcastle|Diggory and Hastings": "Early in She Stoops to Conquer, Tony Lumpkin is drinking at the Three Pigeons alehouse when the landlord announces: \"There be two gentlemen in a post-chaise at the door. They have lost their way upo' the forest\". The two travellers are young Marlow and his friend Hastings, on their way to Mr Hardcastle's house, where Marlow is to meet Kate and Hastings hopes to elope with Constance Neville. Tony tricks them into thinking the house is an inn, which sets off the whole comedy: B. Diggory is a clumsy servant of the Hardcastles, and Hardcastle is the host they are looking for, not a traveller. Memory tip: Marlow and Hastings arrive together, lost, and get fooled.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Animal Farm: \nThe turn of events in the novel shows that the novelist is|iconoclastic|fatalistic|rhetorical|optimistic": "George Orwell's Animal Farm (1945) is an allegorical fable of the Russian Revolution. The animals drive out Mr Jones in the hope of freedom and equality, but the pigs, led by Napoleon, slowly take all the power; at the end the other animals look from pig to man and cannot tell which is which. The revolution has turned full circle back to tyranny, suggesting that such revolutions are doomed to be betrayed whatever the hopes behind them. That view of events as bound to end badly is fatalistic: B. The ending is the opposite of optimistic (D). Memory tip: Animal Farm ends where it began, with masters and slaves.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Animal Farm: \nThe events in the novel are set in|a possible social climate|the distant past|a real human society|an imagined world": "Animal Farm is a fable, a story in which animals talk, reason, hold meetings and write commandments. Such a world does not exist; it is imagined, even though Orwell uses it as an allegory of real events (Old Major = Marx/Lenin, Napoleon = Stalin, Snowball = Trotsky). So the events are set in an imagined world: D. It is not a real human society (C), and it is not set in the distant past (B). Memory tip: talking animals = imagined world; their meaning = real history.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Animal Farm: \nThe last major battle in the novel is the battle|of the Cowshed|of the Windmill|to oust Mr. Jones|to overthrow Snowball": "Animal Farm has two great battles. The Battle of the Cowshed comes early, when Mr Jones and his men try to recapture the farm and are beaten off; Snowball fights bravely there. The last major battle is the Battle of the Windmill, when Mr Frederick's men attack and blow up the windmill with explosives; the animals drive them off at great cost but call it a victory. So the last major battle is the Battle of the Windmill: B. The trap: A (the Cowshed) is the better-known first battle, so students who remember only one battle pick it. Memory tip: Cowshed first, Windmill last.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Animal Farm: \nThe ideological leader of the revolution in the novel is|Squealer|Old Major|Napoleon|Snowball": "Old Major, the prize Middle White boar, gathers the animals in the barn and tells them of his dream of a world without humans, teaching them that man is the only enemy and giving them the song Beasts of England. His ideas, later turned into \"Animalism\" by the pigs, inspire the rebellion, though he dies three nights later, before it happens. So he is the ideological leader: B. Napoleon (C) seizes power and Squealer (A) is his propagandist. The trap: D (Snowball) organises and leads the animals after the revolution, but he did not create the ideas behind it. Memory tip: Old Major gave the dream; the others fought over it.",
 "|Based on Abubakar Gimba's Witnesses to Tears: \n'A repulsive game, nonetheless. Majority participation in a vice doesn't turn it into a virtue, no matter the social standing of the participants.'\n\nThe game referred to in the passage above is|graft|gambling|draught|scrambling": "Abubakar Gimba's Witnesses to Tears (1986) is a Nigerian novel about corruption, greed and ritual killing. Hussaina, only child of the widower Mr Anas Al-Amin, a man of strict honesty, marries Lahab, vice-principal of Cabral High School, even though her father warned that a man who bribes the police could be a criminal. In the passage Mr Anas calls bribery \"a repulsive game\": many people, even important people, take part in it, but \"majority participation in a vice doesn't turn it into a virtue\". The game is bribery and corruption, which is graft: A. Gambling (B) and draughts (C) are real games, but the passage uses \"game\" as a figure for a corrupt practice. Memory tip: graft = getting money or favours dishonestly, through bribes.",
 "|Based on S.I. Osammor's The Triumph of the Water Lily: \nThe preoccupation of the novel is|the life and times of Julian Odili|Norman Obi's courtship of Effua|the benefits of the extended family in Nigeria|the travails and successes of Nkem and Odili": "Stella Ify Osammor's The Triumph of the Water Lily is a Nigerian novel narrated by Effua, a journalist who returns home to Lagos. Its central character is her friend Nkem, who cannot have a child; under the strain of their childless marriage she moves out of her husband Odili's house while he takes a new wife. The novel follows the trials of Nkem and Odili and, as its title and the publisher's account say, the final triumph of love and life over their troubles. That is the travails and successes of Nkem and Odili: D. Norman's courtship of Effua (B) is a side story about the narrator, and the extended family (C) adds to the couple's pressure rather than being shown as a benefit. Memory tip: the narrator watches; Nkem and Odili's marriage is the story.",
 "|Based on Ahmed Yerima's Attahiru: \nTo justify the deposition of the Emirs of Bida and Kontagora, the British authorities accuse them of|oppression|mendacity|partiality|embezzlement": "Ahmed Yerima's Attahiru (1999) is a historical play about Muhammadu Attahiru I, the last independent Caliph of Sokoto, who resisted Lord Lugard's British forces and died fighting at Burmi in 1903. Early in the play a letter from Lugard, offering \"friendship\", is read at the Caliph's court. In it Lugard explains why he removed the Emirs of Kontagora and Bida: he says they had been oppressing their people, so he took their crowns and banished them. The charge he uses to justify the deposition is oppression: A. Mendacity means lying, partiality means bias and embezzlement means stealing public funds; none is the reason the letter gives. Memory tip: colonial rulers often justified conquest by claiming to \"rescue\" people from oppressive local rulers.",
 "|Based on Ahmed Yerima's Attahiru: \n'I wish him luck with his infidel friends, but he must remember that there is only one moon at a time, one sun at a time.'\n\nThe speaker in the passage above is referring to a|moon gazer|slave raider|muezzin|usurper": "The proverb \"there is only one moon at a time, one sun at a time\" says that a land can have only one ruler: two suns cannot shine together. The speaker is warning a man who has made \"infidel friends\" (allies among the non-Muslim British) and is reaching for power while a rightful ruler already exists. Someone who seizes or claims a throne that is not lawfully his is a usurper: D. A moon gazer (A) takes \"moon\" literally; a muezzin (C) is the man who calls Muslims to prayer; nothing in the lines is about slave raiding (B). The trap: B, because the play mentions slave raids elsewhere; but the proverb is about two rulers, not about slaves. Memory tip: in African drama, proverbs about one sun or one king are always about rightful power.",
 "|Based on Ahmed Yerima's Attahiru: \nLord Lugard and the British military find it easy to penetrate the Sokoto Caliphate because the|Muslim faithfuls fight with bows and arrows while the British soldiers use rifles|Emir of Zazzau calls the white men to save him from the attack of a fellow Muslim, the Emir of Kontagora|emirates of Sokoto and Zaria have not been friends since the reign of Caliph Abdulrahman|emirates are only united to fight the whitemen when it is the turn of Sokoto": "The British did not conquer the Sokoto Caliphate only by force of arms; they were helped by divisions among the emirates. Historically, Muhammad Kwassau, Emir of Zazzau (Zaria), invited the British in 1901 to protect him from the raids of a fellow Muslim ruler, the Emir of Kontagora, and this gave Lugard a foothold in the Caliphate. The question's word \"easy\" points to this betrayal from within: B. A (bows against rifles) describes the British advantage in weapons, but it does not explain why the way in was easy; C and D invent quarrels that are not the reason given. The trap: A, because superior firepower is true of the war, but the question asks how the British got in. Memory tip: in colonial history, \"divide and rule\" is the usual key.",
 "|Based on Ahmed Yerima's Attahiru: \nCaliph Attahiru's dream about heavy smoke on the battlefield and the ancestors passing the flag of Islam from one head to another serves as|a flashback|a prophecy|foreshadowing|a reverie": "Foreshadowing is a hint early in a work of what will happen later. Attahiru tells of a recurring dream: heavy smoke over a battlefield strewn with dead warriors, and the ancestral Caliphs passing the flag of Islam from one to the next until it reaches him and falls, dripping blood. Critics read the dream as pointing ahead to his death in battle against the British and the fall of the Caliphate, so it serves as foreshadowing: C. A flashback (A) goes back to the past; a reverie (D) is an idle daydream. The trap: B, prophecy, because the dream does come true; but the question asks what the dream does in the play's structure, and the dramatic device that prepares the audience for later events is foreshadowing. Memory tip: foreshadowing = a shadow of the future falls on the present.\nBecause the dream shows what will happen, calling it a prophecy (B) is also accepted.",
 "|Based on Ahmed Yerima's Attahiru: \nMadawaki: The whiteman is an uninvited guest to our land, he must observe, not dictate.\n\nThe statement above is prompted by the|abolition of slave trade|undermining of caliphal authority|introduction of direct rule|imposition of taxes": "Madawaki is one of the Caliph's titled chiefs, and he speaks for those who want war rather than submission. His words come after Lugard's letter shows the British deposing emirs and telling the Caliphate what to do. \"The whiteman is an uninvited guest... he must observe, not dictate\" means the stranger is taking over the right to give orders, which belongs to the Caliph: the undermining of caliphal authority: B. The abolition of the slave trade (A), direct rule (C) and taxes (D) are not what the line answers; in fact, the British in Northern Nigeria later ruled through the emirs (indirect rule). Memory tip: \"observe, not dictate\" = a guest must not act like the master of the house.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \nClaudius, though a murderer, is presented as a character|who has a superlative administrative style|with a living conscience that torments him|to whom past events are of significance|with an admirable physical appearance": "Claudius has murdered his brother, King Hamlet, married the queen and taken the throne, yet Shakespeare does not make him a simple villain. In the prayer scene (Act 3, Scene 3), alone, he confesses: \"O, my offence is rank, it smells to heaven; / It hath the primal eldest curse upon't, / A brother's murder.\" He tries to pray but cannot, because he will not give up the crown and the queen. His guilty conscience is alive and tortures him: B. He is a smooth ruler, but \"superlative administrative style\" (A) is not the point the play makes about him as a murderer. Memory tip: a murderer with a conscience makes a richer character than a pure villain.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \nThe call made on Hamlet to avenge his father's death makes him|purposeful|impoverished|confused|amused": "Hamlet, Prince of Denmark, is told by his father's ghost that Claudius murdered him, and is ordered to take revenge. Instead of acting at once, Hamlet is thrown into doubt and inner conflict: he cries, \"The time is out of joint; O cursed spite, / That ever I was born to set it right!\" He wonders whether the ghost is a devil, feigns madness, broods on suicide (\"To be, or not to be\") and keeps delaying. The call leaves him troubled and confused: C. It certainly does not make him purposeful (A): his delay is the most famous feature of the play. Memory tip: Hamlet's tragedy is thought without action.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \nHamlet: Suit the action to the word, the word to the action; with this special observance, that you o'erstep not the modesty of nature.\n\nHere Hamlet is urging the players to|reflect reality|be good actors|justify their actions|be creative on stage": "In Act 3, Scene 2, Hamlet coaches the travelling players before The Mouse-trap. He tells them not to overact: their gestures should match their words, and they must not \"o'erstep the modesty of nature\", meaning they must stay natural and lifelike. He adds that the purpose of acting is \"to hold, as 'twere, the mirror up to nature\". So he is urging them to reflect reality: A. \"Be good actors\" (B) is too general, since the advice is about one particular quality, naturalness. The trap: B, because the speech is advice to actors; but the special point is realism. Memory tip: \"mirror up to nature\" = drama should reflect life.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \nThe principal function of the play-within-the-play in Hamlet is to|amuse Claudius|confirm the claim of the ghost|divert attention from the main event|create some comic relief in the tragedy": "Hamlet cannot be sure the ghost told the truth; it might be a devil sent to tempt him. So he has the players act a murder like his father's in front of Claudius and watches his uncle's face: \"the play's the thing / Wherein I'll catch the conscience of the king.\" When Claudius rises in alarm and stops the play, Hamlet is convinced: the play-within-the-play confirms the ghost's claim: B. It is not meant to amuse Claudius (A) or to give comic relief (D); it is a trap. Memory tip: the Mouse-trap is a trap for the \"mouse\", Claudius.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \nIn The Mouse-Trap, the murderer is|Claudius|Lucianus|Gonzago|Baptista": "The Mouse-trap (Hamlet's name for the play \"The Murder of Gonzago\") shows Duke Gonzago killed by poison poured in his ear while he sleeps in a garden, just as the ghost said King Hamlet was killed. Hamlet announces the murderer: \"This is one Lucianus, nephew to the king.\" So the murderer in the inner play is Lucianus: B. Gonzago (C) is the victim and Baptista (D) his wife. Claudius (A) is the murderer in Hamlet itself, which the inner play mirrors. The trap: A, Claudius, because the inner play is aimed at him; but within The Mouse-trap the poisoner is Lucianus. Memory tip: Lucianus is a nephew, just as Hamlet is Claudius's nephew, a hidden threat.",
 "|Based on Ayi Kwei Armah's Fragments: \nThe novel suggests that an individual who stays apart from his society will|be praised for his action|be alienated by his society|be broken by his society|live a fulfilled life": "Ayi Kwei Armah's Fragments (1970) follows Baako Onipa, a young Ghanaian \"been-to\" who returns from studying in America. His family and society expect him to bring cars, money and gifts, a \"cargo\" of wealth, but Baako wants to write honest work and refuses corruption. Under constant pressure and misunderstanding he breaks down and is committed to a mental asylum. The novel suggests that one who stands apart from his society's values is crushed by it: C. He is indeed alienated (B), but that is his starting condition; what the society does to him in the end is break him. The trap: B, the word students associate with Armah's heroes. Memory tip: in Armah, the honest man is the one who gets broken.\nBaako is also alienated by his society before he breaks, so B is accepted as well.",
 "|Based on Ayi Kwei Armah's Fragments: \nThe setting of the novel is the|traditional Nigerian society|post-independence Ghanaian society|pre-independence Ghanaian society|modern Nigerian society": "Fragments is set mainly in Accra in the 1960s, in the years after Ghana became independent in 1957. Armah shows a new society obsessed with imported goods, big cars, quick money and show, where a returnee is valued for the wealth he brings. That is post-independence Ghanaian society: B. Pre-independence Ghana (C) would be the colonial period, before 1957; Nigeria (A, D) is the wrong country, since Armah is a Ghanaian writer. Memory tip: Armah's early novels (The Beautyful Ones Are Not Yet Born, Fragments) are portraits of disappointment after Ghana's independence.",
 "|Based on Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles: \nAngel Clare is depicted as|a rebellious and self-opinionated character|an independent-minded character|a fastidious and garrulous character|a flirtatious and impulsive young man": "Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles (1891) tells of a poor country girl, Tess Durbeyfield, whose life is ruined by Alec d'Urberville and then by the man she loves, Angel Clare. Angel is the youngest son of a clergyman, but he refuses to become a parson because he cannot believe the Church's doctrines, and he chooses to learn farming instead. He makes his own choices in thought and career: an independent-minded character: B. He is not a flirt (D) or a chatterbox (C). The trap: A, rebellious and self-opinionated, because he does break with his family; but he is gentle and thoughtful, not a rebel for its own sake. Memory tip: Angel thinks freely but, when tested, cannot forgive Tess.",
 "|Based on Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles: \n'Phases' in the novel refers to|phases of the moon and their effects on the plot|moments of awareness of Tess' misfortune|points of conflict between good and evil|stages of development in the life of Tess": "Hardy divides Tess of the d'Urbervilles into seven parts called \"Phases\": The Maiden, Maiden No More, The Rally, The Consequence, The Woman Pays, The Convert and Fulfilment. Each phase marks a stage in Tess's life, from innocent village girl to her death, so \"Phases\" refers to stages of development in the life of Tess: D. The word recalls the phases of the moon (A), but the novel's phases are about Tess, not the moon. The trap: A, because students link \"phases\" with the moon. Memory tip: read the seven titles in order and you have the whole story.",
 "|Based on Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles: \nFrom the tragedy of Tess, it can be said that Thomas Hardy is concerned with the evils of|modernization|industrialization|democratization|colonization": "Hardy's novels show the old farming world of \"Wessex\" (his name for south-west England) being changed by the new age. In Tess, the steam threshing machine at Flintcomb-Ash drives the workers without rest, and Tess is worn down by it; the railway and other machines carry the same message. So Hardy is concerned with the evils of industrialization (B), which is also the wider modernization (A) of rural life, and both are accepted. Democratization (C) and colonization (D) have no part in the story. Memory tip: in Hardy, machines and railways mean the death of the old country ways.",
 "|Based on Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles: \nAngel Clare's ambition in marrying Tess d'Urberville is to secure a wife with|high social standing|great fortune|rustic innocence|a wide knowledge of the world": "Angel Clare plans to be a farmer, so he wants a wife who can share farm life, not a fine lady. At Talbothays Dairy he sees Tess as pure and natural; Hardy has him call her \"a fresh and virginal daughter of Nature\". What he wants in a wife is rustic innocence: C. That is why, when Tess confesses her past with Alec on their wedding night, he cannot accept her and leaves: the ideal he married is broken. Social standing (A) and fortune (B) are what his family might want, not what he seeks. Memory tip: Angel loves an idea of Tess, not the real woman.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nBrutus' A Troubadour I Traverse is inspired by his struggle against|injustice|capitalism|colonialism|zealotry": "Dennis Brutus (1924–2009) was a South African poet who fought apartheid, the system of laws that kept black South Africans oppressed. He was banned, jailed on Robben Island and later exiled. In \"A Troubadour I Traverse\", he pictures himself as a troubadour, a wandering poet-knight of the Middle Ages, roaming the land he loves on a quest; critics read this troubadour as his image for his pursuit of justice. The struggle that inspires it is against injustice: A. The trap: C, colonialism, because it sounds like an African struggle; but Brutus fought the racial injustice of apartheid in South Africa. Memory tip: Brutus = apartheid = injustice.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe theme of Wangusa's A Taxi Driver on His Death is|the taxi driver's contemplation of the vehicle as the likely cause of his death|that a driver's death must surely be caused by his vehicle|the danger in the driving profession|that roads are death-traps for drivers": "Timothy Wangusa is a Ugandan poet; this poem is in his collection A Pattern of Dust (1994). The speaker is a taxi driver who reflects on his own death on the road. He expects the taxi he drives so fast, breaking speed limits for little gain, to be what kills him one day. So the theme is the driver's contemplation of his vehicle as the likely cause of his death: A. B turns it into a general rule for all drivers, and C and D talk about the profession or the roads in general, while the poem is one man thinking about his own end. The trap: B, which uses the same idea but makes it a sweeping law. Memory tip: read the title: one taxi driver on HIS death.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nJ.P. Clark's Agbor Dancer portrays|a sophisticated dancer|a girl attuned to her culture|an uncivilized dancer|a wild girl": "In J.P. Clark's \"Agbor Dancer\", the speaker watches a girl dancing to the drums at Agbor, in present-day Delta State, Nigeria. She moves in perfect time with the drum, completely at one with the rhythm and tradition of her people. The educated speaker, cut off from that world, longs to step back into it. So the poem portrays a girl attuned to her culture: B. The poem admires her, so \"uncivilized\" (C) and \"wild\" (D) are wrong, and \"sophisticated\" (A) misses the point that her art comes from tradition. Memory tip: in the poems of Clark and Okigbo, the dancer or drummer often stands for living tradition.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe subject matter of Senghor's I Will Pronounce Your Name is|Elissa|Naett|love|a nameless girl": "Léopold Sédar Senghor (1906–2001), Senegalese poet and first president of Senegal, was a founder of Négritude, the movement that celebrated black African beauty and culture. \"I Will Pronounce Your Name\" is a praise poem to a woman named Naett: the speaker pronounces her name and praises her with images of African beauty. The subject matter, who the poem is about, is Naett: B. Love (C) is its theme, not its subject. The trap: C, because the poem is clearly about love; but subject matter means the person or thing the poem describes. Memory tip: subject matter = who or what; theme = the idea about it.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe tone of Ofeimun's We Must Learn Again to Fly is that|encouragement|discouragement|disappointment|enthusiasm": "Odia Ofeimun is a Nigerian poet and essayist. In \"We Must Learn Again to Fly\", the people are weighed down by hunger and by selfish leaders, yet the speaker urges them to learn again to fly: to rise, try again and not give up. A tone that urges people on in hard times is a tone of encouragement: A. Discouragement (B) and disappointment (C) describe the hardships, not the speaker's call. The trap: D, enthusiasm, which is cheerful excitement; the poem is a serious call to persist, not excited joy. Memory tip: \"we must\" + hope = encouragement.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \n'And the name died before the man.'\n\nFrom the statement above in Housman's To an Athlete Dying Young, what happens to the athlete?|He comes to a disgraceful end|He loses his medal to another|He dies eventually|He dies prematurely": "A.E. Housman's \"To an Athlete Dying Young\" (A Shropshire Lad, 1896) speaks at the funeral of a young runner who once won a race for his town. The speaker calls him a \"smart lad\" for dying while his fame was fresh, unlike the \"runners whom renown outran / And the name died before the man\": champions who lived on to see others break their records and their fame forgotten. The athlete escaped that by dying early, prematurely: D. The trap: C, he dies eventually, which is true of everyone; the point is that he died young. Memory tip: the title says it: dying YOUNG.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Keats' On the Grasshopper and Cricket, poetry of earth refers to|poetry written about earth|songs by grasshopper and cricket|poetry written by mother earth|songs by birds, grasshoppers and cricket": "John Keats wrote \"On the Grasshopper and Cricket\" (1816) as a sonnet. It opens \"The poetry of earth is never dead\": in hot summer, when the birds are silent, the grasshopper sings in the hedges; on a frosty winter night the cricket sings by the stove. So \"the poetry of earth\" means nature's song, the songs of the grasshopper and the cricket: B. The birds are mentioned only as being faint and quiet in the heat. The trap: D, which adds birds; but the poem says the birds have stopped. Memory tip: summer grasshopper, winter cricket: nature's song never stops.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn On His Blindness, Milton uses the experience of his blind state to|condemn himself|appreciate God|abuse God|disregard his fellow men": "John Milton went blind around 1652, and in this sonnet (\"When I consider how my light is spent\") he worries that he can no longer use his talent to serve God. Patience answers him: God does not need man's work or gifts; those who bear his \"mild yoke\" best serve him best, and \"They also serve who only stand and wait.\" Milton turns his suffering into acceptance and honour of God's will: B. He does not condemn himself (A) or abuse God (C). Memory tip: \"They also serve who only stand and wait\" = patient faith.",
 "|The predominant use of the third person pronoun in a novel creates a|sense of detachment on the part of the writer|sense of detachment on the part of the reader|psychological affinity with the characters|balance of opinion among the characters": "Point of view is the position from which a story is told. In third-person narration (\"he\", \"she\", \"they\") the narrator stands outside the story and is not one of the characters, so the writer appears detached and can describe everyone from a distance: A. A first-person narrator (\"I\"), by contrast, creates closeness to one character's mind (compare C). The reader (B) is not the one being described as detached. The trap: B, because \"detachment\" seems to belong to the audience; but it is the writer who stays outside the events. Memory tip: \"I\" = inside the story; \"he/she\" = outside it.",
 "|A poem without a regular rhyme scheme is a|traditional poem|ballad|lines poem|blank verse": "Blank verse is verse with no rhyme at all, usually unrhymed iambic pentameter (lines of five iambs), as in Shakespeare's plays and Milton's Paradise Lost. Of the options, it is the only form that has no regular rhyme scheme: D. Ballads (B) and most traditional poems (A) rhyme regularly (a ballad stanza often rhymes abcb). A poem without regular rhyme or metre is also called free verse, but that is not offered here. Remember: blank verse is unrhymed but still has a regular metre; free verse has neither. Memory tip: blank verse = blank of rhyme, not of rhythm.",
 "|Aesthetics in literature has to do with the creation of|imagery|lines|beauty|themes": "Aesthetics (from the Greek for \"perception\") is the branch of thought concerned with beauty and artistic taste. In literature it deals with how a work creates beauty and pleasure through its form, language and design: C. Imagery (A), lines (B) and themes (D) are elements a writer uses to create that beauty, not what aesthetics itself is about. Memory tip: aesthetic = beautiful; an \"aesthetic\" room is a beautiful one.",
 "|Cast in a play refers to|all the actors|three of the actors|a few of the actors|an exclusive social class in the play": "The cast of a play is the whole group of actors who perform its roles; a \"cast list\" names every actor and the character each plays: A. The \"dramatis personae\" is the list of characters in the script, and the cast are the actors who bring them to life. \"A few of the actors\" (C) would be part of the cast, not all of it. Memory tip: casting = choosing actors for ALL the parts.",
 "|A tragic hero, according to the Aristotelian precept, must be a|noble character with hubris|lowly character who suddenly stumbles on some fortunes|king with deep affection for his subject|central character after whom the play is named": "In his Poetics, Aristotle describes the tragic hero as a person of high rank and good, noble character, who falls from happiness to misery through a flaw or error (hamartia). A common form of this flaw is hubris, excessive pride, as in Oedipus or Okonkwo: A. A lowly person who stumbles on fortune (B) is the opposite of a tragic fall. Many tragedies are named after the hero (D), but that is not Aristotle's requirement. The trap: D, because Hamlet, Macbeth and Oedipus Rex are named after their heroes; but the title is not what makes a tragic hero. Memory tip: tragic hero = high birth + fatal flaw = great fall.",
 "|A careful choice of words dictated by a particular mood or an event refers to|register|cadence|rhyme|tone": "Tone is the attitude or feeling a writer conveys: angry, gentle, mocking, solemn. It is made through careful choice of words suited to the mood or occasion: a funeral calls for grave words, a celebration for lively ones. So word choice dictated by a particular mood is tone: D. The trap: A, register, which is also about choosing language for a situation (formal or informal, legal or medical); but \"mood\" points to tone. Cadence (B) is the rise and fall of the voice and rhyme (C) is matching sounds. Memory tip: tone = tone of voice = the mood your words carry.",
 "|The insertion of material unrelated or distantly related to the specific subject under discussion in a given work is called|digression|flashback|regression|reverie": "A digression is a passage in which a writer wanders away from the main subject to talk about something only loosely connected, before returning to the point: A. Fielding in Tom Jones and many essayists use digressions deliberately. A flashback (B) goes back in time but stays within the story; a reverie (D) is a daydream; \"regression\" (C) means going backwards and is not a literary term for this. Memory tip: digress = di + gress, \"to step aside\".",
 "|When an object is invested with a meaning beyond its immediate reference, it becomes|an irony|a symbol|a subject|an epigram": "A symbol is an object, person or action that stands for something beyond its literal meaning: a dove for peace, a cross for Christianity, the yam in Things Fall Apart for manliness and wealth. When an object is given a meaning beyond its immediate reference, it becomes a symbol: B. Irony (A) is a contrast between what is said and what is meant; an epigram (D) is a short witty saying. Memory tip: symbol = a thing that points to an idea.",
 "|Unity of time in a tragedy implies that all actions should take place|simultaneously|intermittently|in a single revolution of the sun|within the same locale": "The three unities, drawn from Aristotle by Renaissance critics, are unity of action (one main plot), unity of place (one location) and unity of time (the action happens within one day). One day is one \"revolution of the sun\", so the answer is C. \"Within the same locale\" (D) is unity of place, not time. The trap: D, because the unities are learnt together; match \"time\" with \"one day\". Memory tip: time = one day, place = one place, action = one plot.",
 "|A short, carefully phrased expression meant to elicit amusement and surprise is|hyperbole|limerick|tercet|wit": "Wit is clever, quick and neatly phrased expression that surprises and amuses, as in Oscar Wilde's \"I can resist everything except temptation\": D. A witty saying in one sharp sentence is also called an epigram. A limerick (B) is a five-line comic poem rhyming aabba (\"There was an Old Man with a beard...\"), a verse form, not a short expression. Hyperbole (A) is exaggeration and a tercet (C) is a three-line stanza. The trap: B, because a limerick is also meant to amuse; but it is a whole poem, not a carefully phrased remark. Memory tip: wit = a clever line that makes you smile.",
 "|'History's stammerer.\nWhen will your memory master the vowels of your father's name?'\nNiyi Osundare, Waiting Laughters\n\nThe literary device used in the excerpt above is|apostrophe|rhetorical question|pun|run-on-line": "Apostrophe is speaking directly to someone absent or to a thing or idea as if it could hear. Here the poet calls out to \"History's stammerer\" and speaks to him as \"you\": apostrophe, A. The next line, \"When will your memory master the vowels of your father's name?\", is a question asked for effect, not for an answer: a rhetorical question, B. Both devices are clearly present, so both A and B are accepted. Osundare is a Nigerian poet; Waiting Laughters (1990) won the Noma Award. Memory tip: apostrophe = talking TO someone; rhetorical question = asking without wanting an answer.",
 "|'It is a beauteous evening, calm and free,\nThe holy time is quiet as a Nun\nBreathless with adoration; the broad sun\nIs sinking down in its tranquillity;\nThe gentleness of heaven broods o'er the Sea:\nListen! the mighty Being is awake,\nAnd doth with his eternal motion make\nA sound like thunder—everlastingly.'\nW. Wordsworth, It Is a Beauteous Evening\n\nThe rhyme scheme of the stanza above is|cbba cdda|bbac ddac|ddca bbca|abbc addc": "These are the first eight lines (the octave) of Wordsworth's sonnet \"It is a beauteous evening, calm and free\" (1802). Mark the end sounds: free (a), Nun (b), sun (b), tranquillity (a), Sea (a), awake (c), make (c), everlastingly (a). The scheme is abba acca, the Italian octave pattern. None of the four options matches this; D (abbc addc) is the one usually given and gets the first three lines right, so it is marked here as not fully correct. Always label the first sound \"a\" and give each new sound the next letter. Memory tip: a rhyme scheme always starts with \"a\", so options beginning with b, c or d are wrong at once.",
 "|'And the promise pleasure will never ever be found in the face of vanished treasure, in the face of plundered pound hidden beneath roots of greener grasses in a land far from the masses'.\n\nThe tone of the poem above is|pessimistic|optimistic|hyperbole|persuasive": "Tone is the attitude behind the words. Here the \"promise\" of pleasure \"will never ever be found\"; treasure has \"vanished\" and money (\"pound\") has been \"plundered\" and hidden \"far from the masses\". Everything good is lost or stolen, with no hope of recovery, so the tone is gloomy and hopeless: pessimistic, A. Optimistic (B) is the opposite. Hyperbole (C) is a figure of speech, not a tone. Memory tip: \"never ever\" is the sound of pessimism.",
 "|The expression 'The ripest is the saddest encounter' is an example of|euphemism|paradox|hyperbole|metaphor": "A paradox is a statement that seems to contradict itself but contains a truth. Ripeness is the moment of fullness, when a fruit is at its best, so we expect it to be happy; yet the line calls it \"the saddest encounter\". The hidden truth is that ripeness comes just before decay and the end: B. It is not an exaggeration (hyperbole, C), a polite softening (euphemism, A) or a plain comparison (metaphor, D). Memory tip: paradox = sounds wrong, turns out wise (\"the child is father of the man\").",
 "|'My heart is a quiet drum, something it flares like a parched thunder cracking through a damask sky, it lifts me in its fired spectacle.'\nCynthia James, Drumology\n\nThe imagery in the excerpt above is largely|olfactory and visual|olfactory and tactile|tactile and auditory|auditory and visual": "Imagery is language that appeals to the senses. The heart is \"a quiet drum\"; it is \"like a parched thunder cracking\": drum, thunder and cracking are sounds (auditory). It \"flares\", the sky is \"damask\" (rich patterned cloth) and the heart lifts the speaker in a \"fired spectacle\": these are sights (visual). So the imagery is auditory and visual: D. There is no smell (olfactory, A and B) and little touch (tactile, B and C). Memory tip: auditory = audio = hearing; olfactory = smell; tactile = touch.",
 "|'If an eagle be imprisoned on the back of a coin and the coin is tossed into the sky, that coin will flutter but the eagle will never fly'.\nHenry Dumas, America\n\nThe poem above achieves its literary effect through the use of|alliteration|parallelism|repetition|solecism": "Repetition is using the same word or phrase more than once for effect. Henry Dumas, an African-American poet, repeats \"eagle\" and \"coin\" to drive home his image: the American eagle printed on a coin is trapped; the coin may flutter in the air but the eagle \"will never fly\". The repetition sets the free bird against the money that imprisons it: C. There is no clear pattern of the same opening consonant (A), and the lines are not two matching structures (B). Solecism (D) is a grammatical error. Memory tip: when a key word comes back again and again, look for repetition.",
 "|'They came on like a whirlwind, and dashed over the line snorting, kicking, squealing in a compact, piebald tossing mob of bay, brown, grey backs, eyes staring, necks extended, nostrils red, long tails, streaming. As soon as they had leaped upon the road the thick dust flew upwards from under their hoofs, and within six yards of Giorgio only a brown cloud with vague forms of necks and cruppers rolled by, making the soil tremble...'\nJoseph Conrad, Nostromo\n\nThe passage above describes a troop of|cows|elephants|camels|horses": "In Joseph Conrad's Nostromo (1904), set in an imaginary South American republic, a troop of animals charges past old Giorgio. They snort, kick and squeal; they are \"bay, brown, grey\" and \"piebald\" (colours used for horses); they have long streaming tails and \"hoofs\", and \"cruppers\" (the strap under a horse's tail, or a horse's rump). All the details point to horses: D. Cows (A) do not have cruppers or come in bay and piebald. Memory tip: bay, piebald, crupper and hoof are horse words.",
 "|\"She wanted to split herself. But she checked herself. 'Get dressed,' she said earnestly. 'Get dressed and let's go. You know I have a lot of things to do. And if we are going to meet again like this,..' She slapped the bed, 'then it won't be here. It will be at the Samson and Delilah.'\"\nFestus Iyayi, Violence\n\nIn the passage above, the speaker can be described as|domineering|treacherous|friendly|hungry": "In this passage from Festus Iyayi's Violence (1979), a novel about the gap between rich and poor in Nigeria, the woman speaking gives commands (\"Get dressed and let's go\"), reminds him that she has \"a lot of things to do\", and decides alone where they will meet next (\"It will be at the Samson and Delilah\"). She controls the situation and gives orders: she is domineering, A. Slapping the bed and ordering him about is not friendly (C), and nothing shows treachery (B). Memory tip: imperatives (orders) in dialogue usually show a domineering speaker.",
 "|Mr. Beckley exploded: I did not come here to ask for a school report on my son. Did you or did you not savagely attack the child, knowing fully well that his health is most delicate at best of times?\nWole Soyinka, Isara: A Voyage Around Essay\n\nIn the excerpt above, Mr. Beckley is trying to|put the addressee on the defensive|show off to the listener|provoke a fight|find out the truth from the addressee": "Isara: A Voyage Around \"Essay\" (1989) is Wole Soyinka's book about his father's generation. In this excerpt Mr Beckley \"exploded\": he is furious about an attack on his son. He waves aside talk of a school report and asks bluntly, \"Did you or did you not savagely attack the child...?\" A \"did you or did you not\" question demands a straight yes or no: he wants to find out the truth: D. The trap: A, put the addressee on the defensive, because his angry, accusing manner would also do that; but his question itself asks for the facts. Memory tip: ask what the speaker wants to get out of the question.",
 "|'Where has my love blown his horn? The tune of his horn is well-known. Young men of my clan have you heard the horn of my love?'\nOkot p'Bitek, Horn of My Love\n\nThe overriding feeling of the persona in this extract is|anxiety|certainty|frustration|joy": "Okot p'Bitek (1931–1982) was a Ugandan poet, famous for Song of Lawino. In \"Horn of My Love\" a woman asks where her lover has blown his horn, says everyone knows his tune, and begs the young men of her clan to tell her if they have heard it. Asking again and again, without an answer, shows worry and longing: anxiety, A. Certainty (B) and joy (D) do not fit her uncertain questions; frustration (C) would need anger, which the lines do not show. Memory tip: repeated anxious questions (\"Where...? Have you...?\") show anxiety.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn 'We Must Learn Again to Fly', Ofeimun exhorts the reader|not to lose hope in the face of adversity|to enjoy his success|to give up if he does not succeed|to work harder even if he succeeds": "Odia Ofeimun is a Nigerian poet known for public, socially committed verse. \"We Must Learn Again to Fly\" responds to disaster and loss, and its title carries its message: when we have fallen, we must not stay on the ground but rise and learn to fly again. The poet exhorts his readers not to lose hope in the face of adversity: A. Enjoying success (B) and working harder after success (D) assume there has been no fall, and giving up (C) is the opposite of the poem's call. Memory tip: a title that sounds like a command (\"We must...\") usually states the poem's exhortation directly.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nKeats's 'On the Grasshopper and Cricket' is best described as|romantic|medieval|metaphysical|platonic": "John Keats (1795–1821) was one of the leading English Romantic poets, with Wordsworth, Coleridge, Shelley and Byron. \"On the Grasshopper and Cricket\" is a sonnet that opens \"The poetry of earth is never dead\": in hot summer the grasshopper sings, and in frozen winter the cricket takes up the song by the fire. Love of nature, feeling and imagination are hallmarks of Romanticism, so the poem is best described as romantic: A. Metaphysical (C) belongs to Donne and the 17th century; medieval (B) is far too early; platonic (D) describes a kind of love, not a poem. Memory tip: Keats, Shelley, Byron, Wordsworth, Coleridge = the Romantics.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe character addressed in 'Serenade' is|from a poor family|in a joyous mood|in a sad mood|at war with another character": "\"Serenade\" is a famous traditional Swahili love-song from the East African coast, linked with the hero-poet Liyongo. A serenade is a song a lover sings to his beloved, often at night under her window. In this one the singer pleads with the lady to stop weeping, to put away her grief and sing to her suitors, and he offers her gifts to win her. A woman who is weeping and grieving is in a sad mood: C. Nothing in the poem shows her poor (A) or fighting anyone (D), and the singer is trying to bring her out of sorrow, so she is not joyous (B). Memory tip: serenade = a lover's song to win (or cheer) the beloved.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nApart from his lover, the poet in Senghor's 'I Will Pronounce Your Name' is also concerned with his|religion|continent|cinnamon|wealth": "Léopold Sédar Senghor (1906–2001), poet and first president of Senegal, was a founder of Negritude, the movement that celebrated black African identity and culture. In \"I Will Pronounce Your Name\" he sings the name of Naett, a black woman he loves, in rich images from African nature. In Negritude poetry the black woman is regularly also a figure of Africa itself (compare Senghor's \"Black Woman\"), so the poet is concerned with his continent as well as his lover: B. Cinnamon (C) is one of his images, not his concern. Memory tip: in Senghor, the beloved woman = mother Africa.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Housman's 'To an Athlete Dying Young', the poet sees the death of the young athlete as|belated|permanent|temporary|timely": "In \"To an Athlete Dying Young\" (A Shropshire Lad, 1896), A.E. Housman speaks at the funeral of a young runner who once won the town's race. Instead of lamenting, he calls him \"Smart lad, to slip betimes away / From fields where glory does not stay\": the athlete died at the height of his glory, before he could see his record broken and his fame fade. So the death is seen as timely, coming at the right moment: D. Belated (A) means too late, the opposite. Death is of course permanent (B), but that is not the poet's point. Memory tip: \"betimes\" means early, in good time: the lad left at the right time.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nBrutus' 'A Troubadour I Traverse' paints the picture of|peace amidst pestilence|love in a hostile environment|hatred inspired by state terror|hunger in the midst of plenty": "Dennis Brutus (1924–2009) was a South African poet jailed on Robben Island for opposing apartheid. In \"A Troubadour I Traverse\", he pictures himself as a troubadour, a medieval wandering love-singer, roaming \"all my land\", which he treats as his mistress. He laughs at those who banned his movement and tried to arrest him, and goes on loving her in spite of them. The poem therefore paints love in a hostile environment: B. Hatred (C) is wrong because the poet's feeling is love, not hate. Memory tip: in Brutus' poems, \"my land\" is often his beloved, and the police are the rivals who threaten the affair.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Clark's 'Agbor Dancer', the drum is central to the message because it|portrays the drummer as an expert|reminds the poet of his cultural roots|provides the rhythm for the dancer|draws the spectators to the dancing arena": "In \"Agbor Dancer\", J.P. Clark watches a young dancer at Agbor in perfect step with the drum, lost in the rhythm of her people. The poet, \"early sequester'd from my tribe\" by Western education and now \"a lead-tether'd scribe\", wishes he could answer the drum's command as she does. The drum thus stands for the tradition from which he has been cut off: it reminds him of his cultural roots: B. It does provide rhythm for the dancer (C), but that is not why it is central to the message. The trap: C, the literal role of a drum; the question asks about the poem's message. Memory tip: in African poetry the drum often symbolises the call of tradition.",
 "|'He did his work in the same slow obstinate way as he had done it in Jones' time, never shirking and never volunteering for extra work either.'\nGeorge Orwell, Animal Farm\n\nFrom the passage above the character being described is|enthusiastic|altruistic|cynical|lazy": "In Orwell's Animal Farm (1945), Benjamin the old donkey is the one animal who expects nothing to change: he says \"Donkeys live a long time\" and that life will go on as it always has, badly. So under the pigs he works just as he did under Mr Jones, \"never shirking and never volunteering for extra work\". Someone who distrusts all promises of improvement is cynical: C. He is not lazy (D), since he never shirks, and not enthusiastic (A) like Boxer, who keeps saying \"I will work harder\". Memory tip: Boxer = blind loyalty; Benjamin = cynicism.",
 "|'The hunter dies and leaves his poverty to his gun\nThe blacksmith dies and leaves his poverty to his anvil...'\n\nThe extract above is an example of|elegy|ballad|epic|eulogy": "An elegy is a poem of mourning or a serious meditation on death. These lines show one person after another dying and leaving behind nothing but poverty, the tools of a hard life: the hunter his gun, the blacksmith his anvil. The repeated pattern gives the solemn, mournful tone of a lament: A. A ballad (B) tells a story in song; an epic (C) is a long heroic narrative. The trap: D, eulogy, which also concerns the dead but means praise of a particular person; these lines praise no one. Memory tip: elegy mourns, eulogy praises.",
 "|'It was not the apple that tempted Eve, But this grapefruit; thus did Maren revise the tale of Paradise Lost, and even Chris, shocked as he was at such blasphemy, confessed himself tempted to agree whenever Maren returned from raiding the Principal's compound, with a shirtful of booty to share, especially, on a hot afternoon.'\nWole Soyinka, Ibadan: The Penkelemes Years, A Memoir\n\nThe passage above achieves a mock-heroic effect through the use of|meiosis|epigram|allusion|symbolism": "Mock-heroic writing treats something trivial in a grand style, so that the contrast is comic. In this extract from Soyinka's memoir of Ibadan, a schoolboy steals grapefruit from the Principal's compound, and Maren rewrites the story of the Fall: \"It was not the apple that tempted Eve, But this grapefruit\". The reference to Eve and to Milton's epic Paradise Lost is an allusion, a reference to a well-known text or story, and it inflates a petty theft into the Fall of Man: C. Meiosis (A) is understatement, the opposite of inflating. Memory tip: allusion = a borrowed story pointed at; in mock-heroic it is usually a grand one.",
 "|'O fleeting gleam of noon dream\nYou incense my clotted dream!'\nTony E. Afejuku, An Orchard of Wishes\n\nThe literary device used in the lines above is|repetition|refrain|consonance|assonance": "Assonance is the repetition of vowel sounds in nearby words. In \"O fleeting gleam of noon dream\", the long \"ee\" sound comes again and again: fleeting, gleam, dream, and \"dream\" returns in the second line. That chain of identical vowel sounds is assonance: D. Consonance (C) repeats consonant sounds; a refrain (B) is a line repeated at the end of stanzas. The trap: A, repetition, because the word \"dream\" appears twice; but the device that runs through the lines is the echoing vowel sound. Memory tip: Assonance = A vowel sound; consonance = consonants.",
 "|'For God's sake, let us sit upon the ground\nAnd tell sad stories of the death of kings;\nHow some have been deposed, some slain in war,\nSome haunted by the ghosts they have deposed,\nSome poisoned by their wives, some sleeping killed,\nAll murdered.'\nWilliam Shakespeare, King Richard II\n\nThe subject matter of the speech above is|regicide|suicide|infanticide|parricide": "This is King Richard's famous speech in Shakespeare's Richard II (Act 3, Scene 2), when he hears his power is collapsing. He imagines telling \"sad stories of the death of kings\": some deposed, some slain in war, some poisoned by their wives, some killed in their sleep, \"All murdered\". The killing of a king is regicide: A. Suicide is killing oneself, infanticide the killing of a baby, and parricide the killing of a parent or close relative. The trap: D, parricide, because it also ends in \"-cide\" and some kings were killed by kin; but every case listed is a king's death. Memory tip: rex/regis = king, so regicide = king-killing.",
 "|'On the broken walls,\nBats hang away from the sun\nShadows of an ancient stranger\nCast their presence over our shoulders\nA pathway leads to the abandoned gate.'\nMazisi Kunene, The Fearful Ruin\n\nThe dominant mood in the excerpt above is that of|exhilaration|pleasure|rage|terror": "Mood is the feeling the scene creates in the reader. Kunene fills these lines with signs of ruin and the supernatural: \"broken walls\", bats hanging away from the sun, \"Shadows of an ancient stranger\" casting their presence over our shoulders, and an \"abandoned gate\". Darkness, ghosts behind us and desertion all create fear and dread; the title itself calls the ruin \"fearful\". So the dominant mood is terror: D. Rage (C) is anger, and nothing here is joyful (A, B). Memory tip: bats, shadows and ruins are the stock images of a fearful, gothic mood.",
 "|'Your no-good pig, and here I am feeding him everyday, letting him sleep in my house. I got a knife from the kitchen. I am going to kill that pig.'\nPatricia Melo, The Killer\n\nThe speaker in the excerpt above is trying to|brace himself up for the action|report the pig before killing it|contemplate the killing of the pig|justify the action of killing the pig": "The speaker turns the matter over: the pig is \"no-good\", he feeds it every day and lets it sleep in his house, he has fetched a knife, and he says \"I am going to kill that pig.\" He is thinking about and planning the killing, which is to contemplate it: C. The words also give reasons, so some readers see an attempt to justify the act (D), and the line about the knife could suggest bracing himself (A); but the passage stays with the speaker's thoughts before any action. Report (B) is not suggested at all. Memory tip: contemplate = think something over before doing it.",
 "|'I am jealous and passionate\nLike Jehovah, God of the Jews,'\nJ.P. Clark, Olokun\n\nA device used in the second line of the excerpt above is|simile|hyperbole|metaphor|paradox": "A simile compares two different things openly, using \"like\" or \"as\". In these lines from J.P. Clark's \"Olokun\" (a poem to the sea goddess), the second line is \"Like Jehovah, God of the Jews\": the speaker compares his jealousy and passion to those of the God of the Old Testament, who calls himself \"a jealous God\". Because the comparison uses \"like\", it is a simile: A. A metaphor (C) would say \"I am Jehovah\". Hyperbole (B) is exaggeration, which is not the device named by the line's form. The trap: C, metaphor, the other comparison figure. Memory tip: see \"like\" or \"as\"? Simile.",
 "|'...Not a few of us ended our application letters like this: 'If you are kind enough to accommodate this humble application, Sir/Madam, I shall do my uttermost best to render you the greatest services which it is at disposition to your best satisfactory. Yours obedient servant...' Yet without English, you had no education fit for a white collar job.'\nCameron Duodu, The Gab Boys\n\nThe tone of the passage above is|derisive|ironic|melancholic|harsh": "Tone is the writer's attitude to the subject. Cameron Duodu, the Ghanaian writer, quotes the application letters he and his friends wrote: \"I shall do my uttermost best to render you the greatest services which it is at disposition to your best satisfactory. Yours obedient servant\". The grand but broken English is held up to be laughed at, and the last sentence mocks a system in which such English was the passport to a white-collar job. A tone that mocks and ridicules is derisive: A. There is irony too (B), since the \"humble\" letters are pompous, so ironic is a reasonable reading. Memory tip: derision = laughing at; deride = mock.",
 "|'You are the cold nests in which the migrant bird lays no eggs'\nKittobbe, To be Childless\n\nThe device used in the excerpt above is|synecdoche|imagery|metaphor|irony": "A metaphor says one thing IS another, without \"like\" or \"as\". \"You are the cold nests in which the migrant bird lays no eggs\" calls the person addressed a cold, empty nest: a picture of a woman (or a home) where no children come, which suits the poem's title, \"To be Childless\". Because the comparison is direct (\"You are...\"), it is a metaphor: C. The line is certainly an image (B), but imagery is a broad term; the specific device is metaphor. The trap: B, because every metaphor is also an image. Memory tip: \"X is Y\" = metaphor; \"X is like Y\" = simile.",
 "|Based on Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles: \nAngel Clare prefers Tess to Mercy Chant as a wife because she|is an accomplished lady|is from a very good family|understands farm life|is a good Christian": "Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles (1891) is a tragic novel set in rural Wessex (south-west England). Angel Clare, a parson's son who refuses to enter the Church, is learning to be a farmer at Talbothays dairy, where he falls in love with Tess, a milkmaid. His parents hoped he would marry Mercy Chant, a pious, educated young woman. Angel argues that a learned lady would be useless to a farmer; he needs a wife who understands farm life, as Tess does: C. Tess is not from a grand family in practice (B), and though she is devout, that is not his main argument. Memory tip: Angel wants a farmer's wife, not a parson's.",
 "|Based on Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles: \nThe death of Prince can be said to|portray Tess as a fated character|reveal the family's total dependence on the animal|cause Tess' misfortunes|foreshadow Tess' ultimate fate": "Early in Tess, Tess drives the family's cart at night because her father is drunk; she falls asleep, and the mail-cart's shaft pierces the horse Prince, who bleeds to death, spattering her with blood. Feeling guilty for ruining the family's living, Tess agrees to go to the d'Urbervilles to \"claim kin\", where she meets Alec, who seduces her. So the death causes her misfortunes: C. The blood on Tess is also widely read as a foreshadowing of her violent end (D), so both C and D are accepted. The trap: B, the family does depend on the horse, but that is the situation, not the death's role. Memory tip: in Hardy, an early accident is often both cause and omen.",
 "|Based on Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles: \nMost of Tess' misery as a girl-mother comes from the thought of|herself as the parent of a nameless child|the world's concern about her breaking social law|herself as a spouseless mother|her inexperience in childcare": "After Alec's seduction Tess bears a child, Sorrow, who soon dies. Hardy comments that what bowed her head most was \"the thought of the world's concern at her situation\", and that this was \"founded on an illusion\": most people hardly thought about her at all. Her misery came from her fear of society's judgement for breaking its moral law: B. Being an unmarried mother (C) is her situation, but Hardy locates the pain in her imagined view of the world's opinion. The trap: C, because it describes her circumstances rather than the source of her misery. Memory tip: Hardy often blames society's conventions, not nature, for human suffering.",
 "|Based on Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles: \nThe theme of chance is graphically demonstrated by the|meeting of Parson Tringham and Mr. Durbeyfield|rape scene|sudden return of Angel Clare from Brazil|death of Tess' child": "Chance is a major force in Hardy's novels. Tess opens with Parson Tringham happening to meet John Durbeyfield, a poor carrier, on the road and telling him, almost idly, that he is descended from the noble d'Urbervilles. That chance remark leads to John's drinking, Prince's death and Tess being sent to claim kin: the whole tragedy flows from it. So the meeting demonstrates the theme of chance: A. Other chances follow (the letter that slips under the carpet), but the opening meeting is the clearest. Memory tip: in Hardy, small accidents (a meeting, a lost letter) steer great fates.",
 "|Based on Ayi Kwei Armah's Fragments: \nThe message 'State Express 555' is an advertisement for|an imported alcoholic drink|a brand of elitist cigarette|a newly commissioned road network|Gold Coast Transport Corporation": "Ayi Kwei Armah's Fragments (1970) follows Baako, a young Ghanaian who returns from study abroad and is expected to bring \"cargo\": cars, money and imported luxuries. The novel is full of such status symbols. State Express 555 is a brand of expensive imported cigarettes, a mark of status: B. The other options (a drink, a road, a transport company) do not describe this brand. Memory tip: Armah's novels attack the craze for imported goods that marks Ghana's new elite.",
 "|Based on Ayi Kwei Armah's Fragments: \nThat Baako ultimately goes mad indicates that|the visionary is usually a sacrificial lamb|society is incurable|man is a fateful being|the evils that men do live after them": "In Fragments, Baako returns to Ghana as a \"been-to\" and refuses the role his family wants for him: to bring home wealth and goods. He wants to write honest scripts for television and to live by his ideals, but his family and colleagues reject him, and he breaks down and is taken to an asylum. Armah presents him as the visionary whom a materialistic society sacrifices; the novel also links him to ideas of ritual sacrifice. So his madness shows that the visionary is usually a sacrificial lamb: A. Memory tip: in Armah, the sensitive individual who sees clearly is the one society destroys.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \nThe king who is poisoned in the garden in the play-within-the-play is|Gonzago|Negtine|Bernardo|Hamlet": "In Hamlet (about 1600), Hamlet has a troupe of players perform \"The Murder of Gonzago\", which he renames \"The Mousetrap\", with lines he has added. In it a king, Gonzago, is poisoned in his garden by a relative, who then wins the queen. This copies exactly how, according to the Ghost, Claudius murdered old King Hamlet, and Hamlet watches Claudius's guilty reaction. So the poisoned king is Gonzago: A. Bernardo (C) is a soldier on watch in Act 1. Memory tip: \"The play's the thing / Wherein I'll catch the conscience of the king.\"",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \nThe Ghost serves to|accelerate dramatic conflict|intensify dramatic conflict|initiate dramatic conflict|complicate dramatic conflict": "Hamlet is a revenge tragedy: Prince Hamlet must avenge his father's murder. At the start the Ghost of old King Hamlet appears and tells his son that Claudius, now king and married to Hamlet's mother, poisoned him; he commands, \"Revenge his foul and most unnatural murder.\" This revelation sets Hamlet against Claudius and starts the main conflict: C. Later events intensify and complicate it (B, D), but the Ghost is the starting point. The trap: A and B, which describe later stages; the Ghost begins the conflict. Memory tip: initiate = begin; the Ghost's message is the play's starting gun.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \n'But howsoever thou pursuest this act, taint not thy mind, nor let thy soul contrive against thy mother...'\nThe Ghost exhorts young Hamlet not to kill his mother because|his wife is bewitched into falling for Claudius' seduction|the ghost wishes her to be left to the torment of her conscience|the ghost still loves his wife even in death|the young Hamlet will be guilty of matricide": "In Act 1, Scene 5 the Ghost tells Hamlet to take revenge on Claudius but to spare Gertrude: \"nor let thy soul contrive / Against thy mother aught: leave her to heaven, / And to those thorns that in her bosom lodge, / To prick and sting her.\" Her punishment is to be left to God and to the torment of her own conscience: B. He does say she was seduced (A) and he still cares for her (C), but the reason he gives for sparing her is to leave her to heaven and conscience. The trap: A, a true detail from the same speech but not the stated reason. Memory tip: \"thorns in her bosom\" = a guilty conscience.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \nThe king's clown whose skull is exhumed by the Grave-digger is|Rosencrantz|Yorick|Sexton|Fortinbras": "In Act 5, Scene 1 Hamlet and Horatio watch the grave-diggers (clowns) preparing Ophelia's grave. One digs up a skull and says it belonged to Yorick, the king's jester. Hamlet holds it and says, \"Alas, poor Yorick! I knew him, Horatio, a fellow of infinite jest.\" The scene shows death levelling everyone: B. Rosencrantz (A) is Hamlet's false schoolfriend, Fortinbras (D) the Prince of Norway, and a sexton (C) is a church official, which is what the grave-digger himself is. The trap: C, because the grave-digger is a sexton, but the skull is Yorick's. Memory tip: Yorick = the skull = \"Alas, poor Yorick\".",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \nThe seemingly shifty structure of the play is used to|assist in the development of the characters|sustain the reader's interest|reflect the emotional and psychological state of Hamlet|slow down the pace of the plot development": "Hamlet's structure seems to shift and wander: long soliloquies and delays alternate with sudden violent acts (the killing of Polonius), and scenes swing from comedy to grief. Critics link this to Hamlet's mind: his melancholy, doubt and indecision about revenge (\"To be, or not to be\") show in the play's wavering movement. So the structure reflects Hamlet's emotional and psychological state: C. It may also hold interest (B), but that is true of any good plot. The trap: D, since delays do slow the plot; but the reason for the delays is Hamlet's state of mind. Memory tip: in Hamlet, the shape of the play mirrors the hero's hesitation.",
 "|Based on Ahmed Yerima's Attahiru: \nThe character attributes of Attahiru qualify him as|an egocentric patriot|a Muslim fanatic|a religious martyr|a political revolutionary": "Ahmed Yerima's Attahiru (1999) is a history play about Muhammadu Attahiru I, the last independent Caliph of Sokoto. When the British under Lugard advance on Sokoto in 1903, he refuses to surrender the land of Islam to unbelievers, leads his followers on hijra (emigration), and dies fighting at Burmi with many of them. Yerima presents him as a man of faith who gives his life for his religion: a religious martyr: C. \"Muslim fanatic\" (B) is the hostile label the play sets out to correct. Memory tip: a martyr dies for his faith; a fanatic is blindly extreme.",
 "|Based on Ahmed Yerima's Attahiru: \nThe play is historical because it deals with|the jihad led by Caliph Attahiru|real personages and places|Lugard's attempt to amalgamate the southern and northern protectorates|Attahiru's ascension to the caliphate in November 1902": "A historical play dramatises real people and events from the past, often reinterpreting them. Yerima's Attahiru stages Caliph Attahiru, Lord Lugard and the British conquest of Sokoto in 1903, real personages and real places: B. Options A and D name events, but no single event makes a play historical; the amalgamation of Nigeria (C) came in 1914, after Attahiru's death. Memory tip: history play = real people + real places + real past events.",
 "|The physical setting within which the action of a narrative takes place is the|stage|scene|locale|location": "Setting is the time and place of a story's action. Its physical part, the actual place where things happen, is called the location or the locale: D, with C accepted as well. Stage (A) belongs to drama, and a scene (B) is a unit of a play. The trap: C and D are near-synonyms, so a student who knows one may doubt the other; both mean the place. Memory tip: setting = when + where; the \"where\" is the locale or location.",
 "|An anti-social action taken by the tragic hero which results in a catastrophe is called|anagnorisis|premonition|hamartia|empathy": "Hamartia (Greek, \"missing the mark\") is Aristotle's term for the tragic hero's error of judgement or flaw that brings about his catastrophe: Oedipus' rash pride, Okonkwo's violence in Things Fall Apart. Here the \"anti-social action\" that leads to disaster is hamartia: C. Anagnorisis (A) is the hero's moment of recognition, which comes later; premonition (B) is a feeling of coming disaster; empathy (D) is feeling with another. The trap: A, the other Greek term from the same theory of tragedy. Memory tip: hamartia = the hero's mistake; anagnorisis = the hero's discovery.",
 "|A work which takes place in a non-existent world or which concerns an incredible character is a|romance|fantasy|parody|superstition": "Fantasy is fiction set in an imaginary world, or full of impossible creatures and events: magic, monsters, talking animals, journeys to the land of the dead. Amos Tutuola's The Palm-Wine Drinkard and Tolkien's The Lord of the Rings are examples: B. Romance (A) is close, since old romances also had marvels, but the term for a non-existent world is fantasy. Parody (C) imitates another work to mock it. The trap: A, because medieval romances include incredible adventures. Memory tip: fantasy = a world that does not exist.",
 "|A humorous scene, incident or speech in the course of a serious fiction or drama is a|play-within-the-play|comic scene|comic relief|tragi-comedy": "Comic relief is a humorous scene, character or speech placed inside a serious play or story to release tension for a moment and often to sharpen the tragedy by contrast. Famous examples are the drunken Porter in Macbeth and the grave-diggers in Hamlet: C. A comic scene (B) is any funny scene; the term for one inside a serious work is comic relief. Tragi-comedy (D) is a whole play mixing tragic and comic elements. The trap: B, which describes the scene but not its function. Memory tip: relief = a break from the tension.",
 "|A travelogue is|the account of the travails of a character in a novel|a record of the writer's experiences during a journey|a variant of a novel written in a free style on a writer's journey|the account of the experiences of an individual during his lifetime": "A travelogue (travel writing) is a non-fiction record of a writer's journey: the places visited, the people met and the writer's experiences and impressions. It is based on a real journey, not on a character in a novel (A) or a whole life (D, which is autobiography or biography): B. Option C wrongly makes it a kind of novel. Memory tip: travel + -logue (speech, account) = an account of travel.",
 "|Flashback in a literary work helps to|fill the missing link|reveal the character's mindsets|place the story in proper perspective|relate events that are extraneous to the story": "A flashback interrupts the present action to show events that happened earlier. Its purpose is to give the reader background the story needs: how a conflict began, why a character acts as he does. In that sense it fills the missing link in the story: A. The past events it shows belong to the story, so they are not \"extraneous\" (outside or irrelevant), which is why D does not fit well. Flashbacks can also reveal a character's mind (B), but that is only one of their uses. Memory tip: flashback = a step back to fill in the gap.",
 "|An address to a metaphysical source of inspiration by a poet is called|clairvoyance|inspiration|invocation|nemesis": "An invocation is a poet's call to a god, goddess or Muse, a spiritual (metaphysical) source, for inspiration or help. Epics traditionally begin with one: Homer's \"Sing, O goddess\" and Milton's \"Sing, Heavenly Muse\" at the start of Paradise Lost: C. Inspiration (B) is what the poet asks for, not the address itself. Clairvoyance (A) is seeing the future, and nemesis (D) is just punishment. The trap: B, because the address is about inspiration. Memory tip: invoke = call upon.",
 "|The sides of a stage are called|the ways|both sides|the pits|the wings": "In a theatre the wings are the spaces at the left and right sides of the stage, hidden from the audience by curtains or flats, where actors wait to enter and props are kept: D. The pit (C) is the area in front of or below the stage (the orchestra pit, or in Shakespeare's theatre the ground where the poorer audience stood). Memory tip: like a bird's wings, the stage's wings are at its sides.",
 "|A metrical pause occurring in the middle of a line in a poem is called|rhythm|tetrametre|assonance|caesura": "A caesura is a pause in the middle of a line of verse, usually marked by punctuation and shown in scansion by ||: \"To err is human; || to forgive, divine\" (Pope). It varies the rhythm and stresses ideas: D. Rhythm (A) is the overall beat; tetrameter (B) is a line of four feet; assonance (C) is the repetition of vowel sounds. Memory tip: caesura cuts the line (Latin caedere, to cut).",
 "|A complex plot can be described as the plot structure that has|multiple scenes|strands of events running concurrently|well-developed characters|an abstract subject and setting": "A simple plot follows one line of action. A complex plot has a main plot with one or more subplots, several strands of events running at the same time and woven together, as in Shakespeare's King Lear (Lear and his daughters, Gloucester and his sons): B. Many scenes (A) or rich characters (C) can occur in any plot. Memory tip: complex plot = more than one story thread at once.",
 "|'And the fountain of Tololo is an Oasis. It quenches the thirst of travellers. The myriad peaces of the mind fold their wings, for here too, growth emerges from sleep; From a beginning that was the end of an era.'\nMazisi Kunene, The Ancestors and the Sacred Mountain\n\nFrom the excerpt above, it can be said that the poet is being|illogical|tautological|paradoxical|antithetical": "A paradox is a statement that seems to contradict itself but holds a truth. Mazisi Kunene, the South African (Zulu) poet, writes of \"a beginning that was the end of an era\": something cannot normally be both a beginning and an end, yet when one age ends, a new one begins in the same moment. \"Growth emerges from sleep\" works the same way, since sleep seems the opposite of growth. So the poet is being paradoxical: C. He is not illogical (A), because the lines make deep sense, nor tautological (B), which means saying the same thing twice in different words. The trap: D, antithetical, because antithesis also sets opposites side by side; but antithesis balances opposites in contrast (\"man proposes, God disposes\"), while paradox fuses them into one seemingly impossible truth. Memory tip: paradox = \"it can't be true... but it is.\"",
 "|'Emonewua: You it is who own me, and I speak by your permission. When I came home with my husband this morning, believing my mother was ill and needed nursing, I little knew I was walking into a house of ruin.'\nJ.P. Clark-Bekederemo, The Boat\n\nFrom the excerpt above, the speaker is|homeless|a trained nurse|bereaved|recovering from an illness": "Read what the speaker says happened. Emonewua came home \"believing my mother was ill and needed nursing\", that is, expecting illness, but she \"little knew I was walking into a house of ruin\". A \"house of ruin\" in tragedy means a family struck by death and disaster. She has come home to find loss, so she is bereaved: C. She is not homeless (A), since she has a husband and has come to her family home; she was going to nurse her mother, but that does not make her a trained nurse (B); and nobody in the lines is recovering from illness (D). J.P. Clark-Bekederemo's The Boat is one of his Bikoroa plays, a tragedy of a family in the Niger Delta. Memory tip: in persona questions, the image the speaker chooses (here \"house of ruin\") tells you the feeling.",
 "|'If I cannot slaughter other tribesmen if I do not say my prayer in the morning, if I turn my back on some of your advice...'\nIshmael Heron, Pardon Me\n\nThe repetition in the lines above is a poetic device of|anaphora|plagiarism|alliteration|paralipsis": "Anaphora is the repetition of the same word or words at the start of successive lines, clauses or sentences. Here \"if I\" opens each clause: \"If I cannot slaughter... if I do not say my prayer... if I turn my back...\". That is anaphora: A. Martin Luther King's \"I have a dream... I have a dream...\" is a famous example. Alliteration (C) repeats consonant sounds, not whole words. Paralipsis (D) is drawing attention to something by pretending to pass over it (\"I will not speak of his many debts\"). Plagiarism (B) is copying someone else's work and is not a device at all. The trap: C, because alliteration is also a kind of repetition; but it repeats sounds, while anaphora repeats words at the beginning. Memory tip: anaphora = \"again at the front\".",
 "|'Truly sir, all that I live by is with the awl: I meddle with no tradesman's matters nor women's matters, but withal — I am indeed, sir, a surgeon to old shoes. When they are in great danger, I recover them.'\nWilliam Shakespeare, Julius Caesar\n\nThe speaker in the excerpt above is a|cobbler|meddler|surgeon|trader": "These lines come from the opening scene of Shakespeare's Julius Caesar, where the tribunes Flavius and Marullus question the workmen celebrating Caesar's triumph. The Second Commoner plays with words. \"All that I live by is with the awl\" (an awl is a shoemaker's pointed tool, with a pun on \"all\"); he is \"a surgeon to old shoes\", and \"when they are in great danger, I recover them\" (re-cover, mend with new leather). He is a cobbler: A. He says he \"meddles\" with no one's matters, so he is not a meddler (B); \"surgeon\" (C) is his joke; and he says he has nothing to do with \"tradesman's matters\" (D). Memory tip: Shakespeare's common people love puns; look for the trade behind the joke.",
 "|'I will pronounce your name, Obi, I will declaim you, Obi!'\n\nThe device used in the line above is|refrain|parallelism|chronological|repetition": "This line echoes Léopold Sédar Senghor's \"I will pronounce your name, Naett, I will declaim you, Naett!\" Two devices are at work. Repetition: \"I will\" and the name \"Obi\" are said twice, which drums the name in like praise-singing: D. Parallelism: the two halves have exactly the same grammatical shape, \"I will + verb + you/your name + Obi\", which is parallel structure: B. Both labels describe the line fully, so both D and B are accepted. A refrain (A) is a line repeated at intervals through a poem, usually at the end of stanzas, which a single line cannot show. \"Chronological\" (C) describes time order, not a device. Memory tip: repetition repeats words; parallelism repeats the pattern of a sentence.",
 "|'KATE: No shame but mine. I must, forsooth, be forced\nTo give my hand opposed against my heart\nUnto a mad-brain rudesby, full of spleen,\nWho wooed in haste and means to wed at leisure.\nI told you, I, he was a frantic fool,\nHiding his bitter jests in blunt behaviour.'\nWilliam Shakespeare, The Taming of the Shrew\n\nIn the excerpt above, it is clear that the speaker is|enthusiastic|contented|indifferent|anxious": "In Act 3 of Shakespeare's The Taming of the Shrew it is Kate's wedding day, and her groom Petruchio has not arrived. Kate's words show how she feels: \"No shame but mine\"; she has been \"forced to give my hand opposed against my heart\" to a \"mad-brain rudesby\" who \"wooed in haste and means to wed at leisure\", that is, he may not turn up at all. She fears public humiliation and leaves in tears. A speaker so worried about being shamed is anxious: D. She is clearly not enthusiastic (A) or contented (B), and she is not indifferent (C): an indifferent person would not care whether he came. Memory tip: to name a speaker's feeling, look for the words of fear and complaint (\"shame\", \"forced\").",
 "|'Caliph: ...Son, open all your ears. May Allah grant us the fortitude to accept his commands.... Now the rest is my burden.\nI am willing to accept Allah's will in the matter.'\n\nThe language of the speaker above is|imploring|diplomatic|submissive|inciting": "The Caliph tells his son to listen, then prays: \"May Allah grant us the fortitude to accept his commands\", and he says, \"I am willing to accept Allah's will in the matter.\" His words show a man bowing to a higher will and accepting what comes, which is submissive language: C. He is not begging anyone (imploring, A), not carefully avoiding offence in a negotiation (diplomatic, B), and not stirring people to action (inciting, D). He takes \"the rest\" as his own burden, but he puts it in God's hands. The trap: A, because a prayer can sound like pleading; but the Caliph is accepting God's will, not pleading for a change. Memory tip: \"accept... will\" is the language of submission.",
 "|'Though alien to this crime its capacity for bagging crime befits a legendary scion of the soil the brief sack with bulging stomach.'\n\nThe poem achieves its significant effect through the use of|rhyme and simile|alliteration and personification|quatrain and imagery|onomatopoeia and alliteration": "Two sound and meaning devices drive these lines. Alliteration: \"bagging\", \"befits\", \"brief\", \"bulging\", a run of b-sounds that makes the line thump like the stuffed sack. Personification: the sack has a \"capacity for bagging crime\" and a \"bulging stomach\", as if it were a greedy, well-fed person: B. So the answer is alliteration and personification. The sack is of course also an image, which is why some readers choose C; but a quatrain is only a four-line stanza, and the striking effect here comes from the sounds and the human-like sack, not from stanza form. There is no rhyme or \"like/as\" simile (A), and no words that imitate sounds (onomatopoeia, D). Memory tip: say the line aloud; repeated first consonants jump out as alliteration.",
 "|'O, let us have him, for his silver hairs\nWill purchase us a good opinion\nAnd buy men's voices to commend our deeds:\nIt shall be said his judgment ruled our hands;\nOur youths and wildness shall no whit appear,\nBut all be buried in his gravity.'\nWilliam Shakespeare, Julius Caesar\n\nIn the excerpt above, 'his silver hairs' refers to|wealth|old age|make-up|a judicial wig": "In Julius Caesar, Act 2 Scene 1, the conspirators discuss bringing the old senator Cicero into the plot. Metellus says \"his silver hairs / Will purchase us a good opinion\": grey, silver hair stands for old age, and with it the wisdom and respectability of an elder. People will think the murder was guided by \"his judgment\", and the young men's \"wildness\" will be \"buried in his gravity\": B. \"Purchase\" and \"buy\" are metaphors, which tempts some students to choose wealth (A), but they refer to winning public approval, not money. Make-up (C) and a judge's wig (D) are not meant. Memory tip: silver or grey hairs = old age, a common example of metonymy (an associated thing standing for the person).",
 "|'\"The white man of God is coming here for Easter! The white man of God is coming to spend Easter with us!\" This was the talk everywhere in Nkar among the old and young, even among pagans. We had been taught everything about him in advance. When he comes we should observe his beard and see if it is not forked like that of Christ.'\nKenjo Jumbam, The White Man of God\n\nFrom the passage, the White Man of God is treated with|derision|indifference|awe|suspicion": "In Kenjo Jumbam's The White Man of God, set in the Cameroonian village of Nkar, the coming of the missionary is the great event: \"This was the talk everywhere in Nkar among the old and young, even among pagans.\" The children have been taught about him in advance and are told to look at his beard to see whether it is \"forked like that of Christ\", that is, they expect him to look like Christ himself. Such excited reverence and wonder is awe: C. Derision (A) is mockery, and indifference (B) is not caring; neither fits a whole village buzzing with the news. Some readers choose suspicion (D) because the children will \"observe\" the beard, but they are looking for a likeness to Christ, not checking for fraud. Memory tip: awe = wonder mixed with reverence.",
 "|Chanson is a term denoting a|song from the middle ages|poem of varied metrical forms|popular Korean verse|form of love song": "Chanson is the French word for \"song\". In literature it names a French lyric poem or song in stanzas, and over the centuries it has been written in a great many different metrical forms: the medieval chanson de geste (heroic song), the chanson courtoise of the troubadours (a love song), drinking songs, and later popular songs. That is why the definition is \"poem of varied metrical forms\": B. A (a song from the Middle Ages) and D (a form of love song) each describe only one kind of chanson, since chansons go on being written long after the Middle Ages and are not all about love. Korean verse (C) has nothing to do with it. The trap: D, because the best-known medieval chansons are love songs. Memory tip: chanson = song in French, in many shapes.",
 "|Which of the following forms of poetry is an example of monologue?|Ode|Lament|Epic|Sonnet": "A monologue is a long speech by one person. Among the forms listed, the lament is the clearest example: it is the outpouring of one grieving voice, a single speaker mourning a loss, often addressed to the dead or to no one in particular: B. An ode (A) is a formal poem of praise, an epic (C) is a long narrative poem about heroes, and a sonnet (D) is defined by its fourteen lines and rhyme scheme, not by the number of voices. Laments are common in African oral poetry, such as dirges sung by one mourner at a funeral. Memory tip: mono = one; a lament is one voice in grief.",
 "|Monometer is a|single foot used in John Milton's sonnets|line repeated in the first syllable of a word|line of verse consisting of one metrical foot|single metre of Coleridge's poem, 'Christabel'": "Lines of verse are named by how many metrical feet they contain: monometer (one foot), dimeter (two), trimeter (three), tetrameter (four), pentameter (five), hexameter (six). So a monometer is a line of verse consisting of one metrical foot: C. Milton's sonnets use pentameter, not single feet (A), and Coleridge's \"Christabel\" is famous for a metre based on counting stresses, mostly four to a line (D). B describes nothing real. A monometer line is very short, for example \"Thus I / Pass by\" (Herrick, \"Upon His Departure Hence\"). Memory tip: mono-, di-, tri-, tetra-, penta-, hexa- = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 feet.",
 "|Black theatre is drama concerned with|Africans of West Indian origin|the identity of black Americans|mourners dressed in black|actors of black origin": "Black theatre is theatre created by black people: plays written, directed and acted by people of black (African) origin, telling the black experience from the inside. It grew strongly in the United States in the 1960s (the Black Arts Movement, with writers like Amiri Baraka) and has parallels in Africa, Britain and the Caribbean. The answer is actors of black origin: D. Black Americans' search for identity (B) was a major subject of that theatre, so B is close, but black theatre is not limited to America or to that one subject. \"Africans of West Indian origin\" (A) and \"mourners dressed in black\" (C) are distractors. The trap: B, because much famous black theatre is American. Memory tip: black theatre = theatre by black artists about black life.",
 "|The moment of recognition of truth when ignorance gives way to knowledge in a character is known as|anagnorisis|hamartia|anamnesis|hubris": "In his Poetics, Aristotle names anagnorisis (recognition) as the moment a character passes from ignorance to knowledge, usually of the terrible truth: A. The classic case is Oedipus discovering that he has killed his father and married his mother. Hamartia (B) is the tragic error or flaw that causes the fall; hubris (D) is excessive pride, a common form of hamartia; anamnesis (C) means recollection or a patient's medical history, not a dramatic term. The trap: B, because hamartia and anagnorisis are taught together; hamartia is the mistake, anagnorisis is realising it. Memory tip: anagnorisis = \"now I know\".",
 "|Zeugma is a figurative device in which a|pronoun governs two parts of an expression|verb refers to two parts of an expression|conjunction governs two parts of an expression|noun refers to two parts of an expression": "Zeugma (Greek for \"yoking\") is a figure in which one word, usually a verb, governs or applies to two parts of a sentence, often in two different senses: \"She lost her keys and her temper\"; \"He took his hat and his leave.\" The verb \"lost\" or \"took\" is yoked to two objects. So zeugma is when a verb refers to two parts of an expression: B. A pronoun, conjunction or noun (A, C, D) is not the governing word in zeugma. Pope's \"Or stain her honour or her new brocade\" is a famous literary example. Memory tip: zeugma yokes two things to one verb.",
 "|A final stanza of a poem that is shorter than the preceding ones is called|irony|epanalepsis|antithesis|envoi": "The envoi (or envoy) is a short final stanza that closes certain poems, especially the French ballade, which usually ends with a four-line envoi after three longer stanzas. It often addresses a patron or a \"Prince\" and sums up the poem: D. Irony (A) and antithesis (C) are figures of speech, not stanza types. Epanalepsis (B) is repeating at the end of a line or clause the word that began it (\"Rejoice in the Lord always: again I say, Rejoice\"). Memory tip: envoi comes from French envoyer, \"to send\": the stanza that sends the poem on its way.",
 "|A character that heavily relies on cultural types for his manner of speech, his personality and other distinguishing characteristics is|a stock character|an antagonist|a fictional character|a rounded character": "A stock character is a familiar, conventional type that audiences recognise at once by habits of speech and behaviour drawn from cultural stereotypes: the miser, the boastful soldier, the jealous husband, the wise old man, the wicked stepmother: A. An antagonist (B) is the character who opposes the hero, whatever his type. \"Fictional character\" (C) covers every character in fiction. A rounded character (D) is the opposite of a stock type: complex and capable of surprising us. The trap: D, because students mix up \"round\" and \"flat\"; stock characters are flat. Memory tip: stock = taken \"off the shelf\", ready-made.",
 "|That quality in a literary work which evokes tenderness, pity or sorrow is|pataphysics|pathopoeia|patois|pathos": "Pathos (Greek for \"suffering\") is the quality in a work of literature that moves the reader or audience to tenderness, pity or sorrow: D. Scenes of innocent suffering, such as the death of a child or the madness of Ophelia in Hamlet, are full of pathos. Pathopoeia (B) is a rhetorical term for a speech designed to stir strong emotion, so it sounds close, but the quality in the work itself is pathos. Patois (C) is a local dialect, and pataphysics (A) is a mock \"science of imaginary solutions\" invented by Alfred Jarry. The trap: B, pathopoeia, which looks like pathos with a suffix. Memory tip: pathos gives us \"sympathy\" and \"pathetic\", feelings of pity.",
 "|The part of a play when climax approaches is known as|protasis|epitasis|catastasis|catharsis": "Classical and Renaissance critics divided a play into four parts: the protasis (the opening, where characters and situation are introduced), the epitasis (where the action develops and the complications grow), the catastasis (where the action is heightened to its full intensity, the climax) and the catastrophe (the final turn and resolution). The part where the climax is reached is the catastasis: C. Catharsis (D) is not a part of a play but the purging of pity and fear in the audience. The trap: B, epitasis, the stage just before, where tension rises towards the climax. Memory tip: Pro-Epi-Cata-Cata: introduce, complicate, climax, catastrophe.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Senghor's 'I Will Pronounce Your Name', 'cinnamon' is significant for its|extended metaphor|olfactory image|dramatic irony|botanical allusion": "Léopold Sédar Senghor, Senegalese poet and first president of Senegal and a founder of Negritude, wrote \"I Will Pronounce Your Name\" in praise of a black woman, Naett, whom many readers take to stand for Africa itself. He likens her name to cinnamon, the sweet-smelling spice, and to the scent of the lemon grove. An image that appeals to the sense of smell is an olfactory image: B. The comparison is brief, not an extended metaphor (A); there is no dramatic irony (C); and although cinnamon is a plant, the poet uses it for its scent, not as an allusion to botany (D). Memory tip: olfactory = smell (think \"olfactory nerve\"); visual = sight, auditory = hearing, tactile = touch, gustatory = taste.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Odia Ofeimun's 'We Must Learn Again to Fly', the use of 'we' gives the poem|dramatic sensation|symbolic undertone|personifying effect|universal appeal": "Odia Ofeimun, the Nigerian poet, writes \"We Must Learn Again to Fly\" about a nation in hardship (critics link it to hunger and failed leadership in Nigeria) and calls on the people to rise again with the patience of a bird learning to fly. The first-person plural \"we\" speaks not for one person but for the whole community, and it draws every reader in as part of that \"we\". This gives the poem a collective, universal appeal: D. The pronoun does not create drama (A), a hidden symbol (B) or personification (C). Memory tip: \"I\" makes a poem personal; \"we\" makes it communal and universal.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe persona in Housman's 'To an Athlete Dying Young' addresses the dead athlete using|apostrophe|monologue|admonition|dialogue": "In A.E. Housman's \"To an Athlete Dying Young\" (A Shropshire Lad, 1896), the speaker talks directly to a young champion runner who has died: \"Smart lad, to slip betimes away / From fields where glory does not stay.\" Addressing someone dead or absent, who cannot reply, as if present is apostrophe: A. It is not a dialogue (D), since the athlete never answers; \"monologue\" (B) names a long speech by one person, but the device by which the speaker turns to the dead man is apostrophe. An admonition (C) is a warning or reproof. The trap: B, because the whole poem is one voice; the question asks how he addresses the dead athlete. Memory tip: apostrophe = talking to the absent, the dead or the non-human.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe concern of the poet in 'Serenade' is to|examine the gloomy side of marriage|express the lady's sense of grief|boast about the lover's wealth|appeal to the lady in question": "A serenade is a love song that a lover sings, traditionally at night beneath his lady's window, to win her heart. The \"Serenade\" studied for JAMB is generally given as a traditional Swahili love song from the East African coast, in which the singer addresses his beloved directly. Whatever its details, the poet's concern in a serenade is to appeal to the lady: D. It is not a look at the gloomy side of marriage (A), the lady's grief (B) or a boast about wealth (C); a serenade woos. Memory tip: serenade comes from Italian sereno, the calm evening air in which a lover sings to his lady.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Milton's 'On His Blindness', the poet's attitude towards his blindness is one of|apathy|fortitude|sorrow|anger": "John Milton went completely blind in about 1652, in mid-career. In his sonnet \"On His Blindness\" (\"When I consider how my light is spent\") he first frets that he can no longer use his \"one talent\", his gift for poetry, to serve God. Then Patience replies that God does not need man's work: \"who best / Bear his mild yoke, they serve him best\", and \"They also serve who only stand and wait.\" He accepts his blindness with patient, calm courage: fortitude: B. Sorrow (C) is present only in the opening worry, and there is no apathy (A) or anger (D). The trap: C, because the octave sounds sad; the poem ends in acceptance. Memory tip: \"stand and wait\" = fortitude.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Clark's 'Agbor Dancer', the poet watches the dancer with|nostalgia|rhapsody|despair|spite": "In J.P. Clark's \"Agbor Dancer\", the poet watches a young girl at Agbor (in today's Delta State) dancing in perfect harmony with the drums, completely at one with her people's tradition. He, educated in Western ways, feels cut off from those roots and longs to recover them. This longing for a way of life he has lost is nostalgia: A. Rhapsody (B) is ecstatic, excited praise, and the poem does admire the dancer; but the poet's own feeling as he watches is wistful longing. There is no despair (C) or spite (D). Memory tip: nostalgia = home-sickness for a past or a culture one has left.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Keats' 'On the Grasshopper and Cricket', the frost of winter evenings is said to cause|luxury|silence|loneliness|violence": "John Keats wrote \"On the Grasshopper and Cricket\" (1816) on the idea that \"the poetry of earth is never dead\". In summer the grasshopper sings in the hot meadows; in winter, \"On a lone winter evening, when the frost / Has wrought a silence, from the stove there shrills / The cricket's song.\" The frost has \"wrought a silence\": it causes silence, which the cricket then breaks: B. \"Lone\" might tempt students to choose loneliness (C), but it is the evening that is lone; the frost makes silence. Luxury and violence (A, D) do not appear. Memory tip: \"the poetry of earth is never dead\": grasshopper in summer, cricket in the silent winter.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe poet persona in Wangusa's 'A Taxi Driver on His Death' is|pessimistic|sarcastic|optimistic|romantic": "Timothy Wangusa, the Ugandan poet, wrote \"A Taxi Driver on His Death\" (in A Pattern of Dust, 1994). The driver himself speaks, foreseeing that he will die in a road crash, driving at speed in his \"metallic monster\", carrying passengers he does not know. He treats his death as certain and unavoidable, a grim, resigned fatalism. That gloomy view of his future is pessimistic: A. Nothing in his outlook is hopeful (optimistic, C) or romantic (D); he is not mocking anyone with bitter irony (sarcastic, B). The poem is also read as a warning about reckless driving on African roads. Memory tip: a persona who expects the worst is pessimistic; fatalism is pessimism about fate.",
 "|Based on Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles: \nAt what time does Tess often look so transformed before Angel Clare?|At twilight|At 12 noon|At 3am on their way to milking the cows|At 12 midnight while retiring to bed": "Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles (1891) tells of Tess Durbeyfield, a country girl wronged by Alec d'Urberville. At Talbothays dairy she falls in love with Angel Clare, who is learning farming. The dairy folk rise very early, the skimming starting a little after three, and in that grey, ghostly dawn light Tess seems to Angel transformed, \"a visionary essence of woman\", no longer a milkmaid. So the answer is at 3am on their way to milking: C. Hardy himself calls that hour \"the twilight of the morning\", so \"at twilight\" (A) is also accepted. Noon and midnight (B, D) are wrong. Memory tip: Tess and Angel are the \"first persons up of all the world\", like Adam and Eve at dawn.",
 "|Based on Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles: \nOne thing that Angel Clare hates most is|marrying a girl of his parents' choice|Cambridge education|becoming a minister|the notion of old family": "Angel Clare, a parson's son, refuses to become a minister and chooses farming instead, and he holds advanced, independent views. Among them is a strong contempt for aristocratic pride of birth: he dislikes \"old families\" and thinks them worn out. That is why Tess, a descendant of the ancient d'Urbervilles, fears to tell him of her ancestry: D. He does decline to be a minister (C) and does not go to Cambridge like his brothers (B), but these are choices, not what he hates most. His parents hope he will marry Mercy Chant (A), but he chooses Tess. Memory tip: Angel despises \"old family\"; Tess's old family name is part of her tragedy.",
 "|Based on Thomas Hardy's Tess of the d'Urbervilles: \nTess of the d'Urbervilles is an interrogation of the Victorian period's|concept of love|celebration of industrialization|double standard|ideas about women": "Hardy subtitled the novel \"A Pure Woman\", a direct challenge to Victorian morality. On their wedding night Angel confesses a past affair and Tess forgives him at once; when she confesses that Alec wronged her, Angel cannot forgive her and leaves. Men's sins were excused while women were condemned for less: the double standard of sexual morality, which the novel questions: C. \"Ideas about women\" (D) is close, but the novel's sharpest attack is on that unequal judging of men and women. Industrialisation (B) appears (the threshing machine), but the novel does not celebrate it. The trap: D, which is true in a broad way but less precise. Memory tip: double standard = one rule for men, another for women.",
 "|Based on Ayi Kwei Armah's Fragments: \nIt can be safely assumed that the novel is|naturally simple|culturally simple|physically relevant|structurally complex": "Ayi Kwei Armah's Fragments (1970) tells of Baako, a young Ghanaian who returns from study in America and is crushed by his family's demand for money and status from a \"been-to\". The novel is not told in a straight line: it moves between Baako, his grandmother Naana and others, mixes present events with memories, and opens and closes with Naana's poetic monologues. As its title says, it is built of fragments: structurally complex: D. The other options are not real critical descriptions. Memory tip: the title Fragments tells you its form, pieces put together.",
 "|Based on Ayi Kwei Armah's Fragments: \nThe outdooring ceremony of Araba's child, fixed for the fifth day after his birth, is opposed by Naana because the child|is reincarnated in the father|needs the uncle's support|makes the spirits angry|is still in the custody of the spirits": "In Fragments, Baako's sister Araba has a baby, and the family hurries the outdooring (naming and showing) ceremony to the fifth day for the sake of the gifts of money it will bring. In Akan tradition a newborn is not outdoored until about a week has passed, because until then the child still belongs to the spirit world and may return to it. The blind grandmother Naana opposes the early ceremony because the child is still in the custody of the spirits: D. Her fear proves right: the baby dies during the ceremony. The other options are not her reason. Memory tip: in Fragments, greed for money over tradition kills the child.",
 "|Based on Ahmed Yerima's Attahiru: \nThe play is about|disunity among the Hausa states|resistance to British administration|unity in the caliphate|opposition to Christianity": "Ahmed Yerima's Attahiru is a historical play about the fall of the Sokoto Caliphate. Lord Lugard writes to Caliph Muhammadu Attahiru I offering \"friendship\" with the British; the Caliph and his council refuse to submit. Sokoto falls in 1903, and Attahiru leads his followers east to Burmi, where he dies fighting the British. The play is about resistance to British rule: B. It shows the Caliphate under attack, not chiefly disunity among the Hausa states (A), and it is not about Christianity (D). \"Unity in the caliphate\" (C) is shown in the followers' loyalty, but it is not the subject. Memory tip: Attahiru = the Caliph who chose death at Burmi over submission to Lugard.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \n'Fear it, Ophelia, fear it, my dear sister,\nAnd keep you in the rear of your affection,\nOut of the shot and danger of desire.\nThe chariest maid is prodigal enough,\nIf she unmask her beauty to the moon.'\n\nIn the excerpt above, Laertes warns Ophelia to beware of Hamlet's attentions because|of his mournful state|the sincerity of his love is in doubt|of his insanity|such attentions cannot be given by too young a person": "In Act 1 Scene 3 of Shakespeare's Hamlet, Laertes, leaving for France, warns his sister Ophelia about Hamlet's courtship. He calls it \"a fashion and a toy in blood\", like a violet in spring, \"sweet, not lasting\", and says that even if Hamlet loves her now, as heir to the throne \"his will is not his own\". So she must \"fear it\" and guard her honour: \"The chariest maid is prodigal enough / If she unmask her beauty to the moon.\" His reason is that the sincerity and lasting power of Hamlet's love are in doubt: B. Hamlet's mourning (A) and later madness (C) are not Laertes' concern here, and he does not say Hamlet is too young (D). Memory tip: Laertes and Polonius both tell Ophelia, \"Don't trust Hamlet's vows.\"",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \nIn the play, the two revenge plots revolve around|Ophelia against Hamlet and Laertes against Hamlet|Hamlet against Polonius and Laertes against Claudius|Hamlet against Claudius and Laertes against Hamlet|Claudius against Hamlet and Laertes against Gertrude": "Hamlet is a revenge tragedy with parallel revenge plots. Hamlet must avenge his father, murdered by his uncle Claudius, who then took the throne and married Gertrude. Laertes must avenge his father Polonius, killed by Hamlet; Claudius uses Laertes' anger to plot Hamlet's death in the fencing match. So the two plots are Hamlet against Claudius and Laertes against Hamlet: C. (Fortinbras, whose father was killed by old Hamlet, is a third son with a revenge motive.) Laertes never seeks revenge on Gertrude (D) or on Claudius after the plot is set (B). The trap: B, because Laertes first storms in blaming Claudius; Claudius quickly turns his anger on Hamlet. Memory tip: three sons avenge three fathers: Hamlet, Laertes, Fortinbras.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \nHow does Queen Gertrude finally die?|By venomed sword|From poisoned drink|Of snake bite|Through strangulation": "In the final scene of Hamlet, Claudius prepares a poisoned cup for Hamlet in case Laertes' poisoned sword fails. Gertrude drinks from it to toast her son (\"The queen carouses to thy fortune, Hamlet\"), Claudius cannot stop her, and she cries, \"The drink, the drink! I am poison'd,\" and dies: B. The venomed sword (A) kills Laertes and Hamlet. \"Snake bite\" (C) is the lie told about the death of Hamlet's father (he was poisoned in the ear while sleeping in his orchard). Nobody is strangled (D). The trap: A, because the venomed sword kills others in the same scene. Memory tip: in the last scene, the cup kills the queen and Claudius; the sword kills Laertes and Hamlet.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Hamlet: \n'But, O, what form of prayer\nCan serve my turn? \"Forgive me my foul murder\"?\nThat cannot be; since I am still possess'd\nOf those effects for which I did the murder.'\n\nThose effects which Claudius is still possessed of from the excerpt above are|the crown and the queen|Norway and young Fortinbras|young Hamlet and Gertrude|Hamlet's orchard and his crown": "In Act 3 Scene 3 of Hamlet, Claudius tries to pray but cannot. He knows he cannot be forgiven while he still keeps what the murder gained him, and he names them: \"My crown, mine own ambition, and my queen.\" He killed his brother for the throne and for Gertrude, and still holds both, so the effects are the crown and the queen: A. Norway and Fortinbras (B) have nothing to do with the murder; Hamlet (C) is not something Claudius won; and the orchard (D) is only where the murder took place. Memory tip: this is the scene where Hamlet spares the praying Claudius, not knowing his prayers \"go not to heaven\".",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nIn the play, Fosuwa represents the|new culture|younger generation|old order|African civilization": "Sons and Daughters is a play by the Ghanaian dramatist Joe (J.C.) de Graft about a well-to-do father, James Ofosu, who wants to choose professions for his children, while Aaron wants to paint and Maanan wants to dance. Fosuwa is James's elder sister, often described as the archetypal African sister-in-law: she meddles in his household, blames his wife Hannah for the family's troubles and backs the wealthy Lawyer Bonu. She stands for the old order, the traditional way of the elders that the young people are pushing against: C. \"New culture\" (A) and \"younger generation\" (B) are what Aaron and Maanan represent. Memory tip: in a play about change, ask which side each character is on, the old or the new.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \n'I still think you can do something about it... persuade him; make him see how frustrating the whole business is bound to be for you.' The whole business in the excerpt above refers to|marriage proposal|career choice|business contract|political transition": "The whole quarrel in Sons and Daughters is over careers. James Ofosu has chosen professions for his children (law and the like), but Aaron wants to study art and Maanan wants to be a dancer. In this line the speaker urges the child to \"persuade him\" (the father) and make him see \"how frustrating the whole business is bound to be\": that is, how miserable it will be to study a course one does not want. So \"the whole business\" is the career choice forced on the children: B. There is no business contract or political transition in the play, and the conflict is not about a marriage proposal. Memory tip: in this play, \"the business\" nearly always comes back to who chooses the children's careers.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nThe central conflict in the play is|religious|romantic|generational|communal": "A play's central conflict is the main clash that drives the plot. In Sons and Daughters the clash is between the father, James Ofosu, who holds that only \"respectable\" well-paid professions are worth studying, and his younger children, Aaron and Maanan, who want to follow their own talents in painting and dance. Father against children, old ideas against new: this is a generational conflict: C. There is no religious or communal quarrel at the heart of the play, and although Lawyer Bonu's advances to Maanan add tension, the play is not a love story (B). Memory tip: parent versus child over the future = generational conflict, the theme of the play's very title.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nThe change in fortune is achieved in the play when|James agrees that Aaron and Maanan should study what they want|Lawyer harasses Maanan in his office|Hannah, James's wife, convinces him|Aaron and Maanan agree to read the courses James chooses for them": "A change in fortune (the reversal) is the point where the situation of the main characters turns round. In Sons and Daughters the children's fortunes turn when their father finally gives in: James agrees that Aaron and Maanan may study what they want: A. He is won over near the end, when news comes that a painting by Aaron's friend Awere has sold for a large sum (two hundred and twenty pounds), proving that art can pay. Lawyer Bonu's advances to Maanan (B) bring tension, not the happy turn, and the children never agree to take James's courses (D). Memory tip: the change in fortune is the event that settles the conflict, not the incidents that build it up.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'I will be brief, for my short date of breath\nIs not so long as is a tedious tale.'\nThe statement above is in reference to the speaker's|retirement|imminent death|state of health|short-tempered nature": "Romeo and Juliet (about 1595) is Shakespeare's tragedy of two young lovers from the feuding Montague and Capulet families of Verona. In the last scene old Friar Laurence, who secretly married the lovers, begins his account of what happened with \"I will be brief, for my short date of breath / Is not so long as is a tedious tale.\" \"My short date of breath\" means the short time he has left to breathe, that is, to live: he is an old man near the end of his life, so he cannot tell a long story. The line refers to his imminent death: B. He is not speaking of retiring, of an illness or of his temper. Memory tip: \"date of breath\" = the span of life still left.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nRomeo's death becomes inevitable because|Friar Lawrence's letter is not delivered|Juliet is married to Paris|Romeo kills Tybalt in a duel|Juliet has committed suicide": "Friar Laurence gives Juliet a potion that makes her seem dead so that she can escape marrying Paris. He sends Friar John with a letter telling Romeo, in exile in Mantua, of the plan. But Friar John is shut in a house because of the plague and the letter never reaches Romeo. Instead Romeo hears from his servant Balthasar that Juliet is dead, buys poison and kills himself at her tomb. The undelivered letter makes his death inevitable: A. Killing Tybalt (C) only led to his banishment, and Juliet has not committed suicide (D) when Romeo dies; she is only sleeping. Memory tip: in this tragedy, small accidents of timing seal the lovers' fate.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nThe multiple deaths in the play serve as a punishment for|imposing life partners on children|treating others with hatred|obeying one's own parents|shedding kinsmen's blood": "At the end of the play the Prince stands over the dead bodies and tells the two fathers: \"Capulet, Montague, / See what a scourge is laid upon your hate, / That heaven finds means to kill your joys with love.\" The deaths of Mercutio, Tybalt, Paris, Romeo and Juliet are presented as the punishment for the families' long, senseless hatred of each other: B. The fathers then make peace. \"Shedding kinsmen's blood\" (D) sounds close, but the two families are enemies, not kinsmen, and the Prince blames the feud itself. Memory tip: the Prince's word \"hate\" is the key: the feud is punished.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nThe major reason for Juliet's grief is|the death of her cousin|her reluctant marriage to the County|the banishment of her lover|her imminent death": "After Romeo kills Tybalt, Juliet's cousin, he is banished from Verona. When the Nurse brings the news, Juliet mourns Tybalt, but she soon says that the word \"banished\" is far worse: \"that one word 'banished' / Hath slain ten thousand Tybalts.\" Her family thinks she weeps for Tybalt, but her real grief is the banishment of her lover and new husband, Romeo: C. The trap: A, because her parents believe she is grieving for her cousin; Shakespeare uses that mistake for dramatic irony. The marriage to Paris (B) comes later. Memory tip: Juliet's tears are for Romeo, whatever the Capulets think.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nThe chorus in the prologue of the play is essentially meant to|foreshadow the play|highlight the characters|estimate the actions|introduce the play": "The play begins with a Chorus speaking a fourteen-line sonnet, the Prologue. It introduces the play: it sets the scene (\"In fair Verona\") and the \"ancient grudge\" of two households. It also tells the audience the ending in advance: \"A pair of star-cross'd lovers take their life\", whose deaths \"bury their parents' strife\". Telling the outcome beforehand is foreshadowing (A), and giving the setting and story is introducing the play (D), so both are accepted. The Prologue does not describe the characters one by one (B), and \"estimate the actions\" (C) means nothing here. Memory tip: Shakespeare's Prologue gives away the ending so that we watch HOW it happens.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nIn the novel, Big Brother metaphorizes a|totalitarian authority|benevolent leader|revolutionary leader|party brotherhood": "Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949) is George Orwell's novel about Oceania, a state ruled by the Party, where Winston Smith works rewriting history. Big Brother is the face on every poster, with the slogan \"BIG BROTHER IS WATCHING YOU\". He may not even exist as a person; he is the image of the Party's total power, watching and controlling every thought and act. So he stands for totalitarian authority: A. He is shown as kindly (B) only in propaganda, and \"party brotherhood\" (D) is just a play on his name. Memory tip: \"Big Brother\" is now used in English for any all-seeing, controlling state.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nIn the novel, Orwell attempts to|satirize the artificiality of a machine-controlled society|eulogize the beauty of the socialist system|condemn the disgusting hypocrisy of all communist systems|laud the boundless possibilities of the human intellect": "Orwell wrote Nineteen Eighty-Four as a warning and a satire. Oceania runs like a machine: telescreens watch everyone, the Ministry of Truth manufactures news and history, Newspeak strips language of meaning, and people are drilled in rituals such as the Two Minutes Hate. Orwell mocks and attacks this artificial, mechanical control of human life: A. He does not praise any system (B, D). C is tempting because the Party resembles Stalin's Soviet Union, but Orwell, a democratic socialist, attacked totalitarianism of every kind, not \"all communist systems\". The trap: C, because students know the book is anti-Stalinist. Memory tip: Animal Farm satirises one revolution; Nineteen Eighty-Four satirises total control itself.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nWinston Smith begins his rebellion against the power of the state by|purchasing arms|keeping a private diary|opposing the law|educating the youth": "Winston Smith's rebellion begins quietly. He buys an old notebook in a junk shop and, hiding from the telescreen in an alcove of his flat, starts to keep a diary, a crime because private thoughts are forbidden. In it he writes \"DOWN WITH BIG BROTHER\" again and again: B. Only later does he begin a forbidden love affair with Julia and seek out the Brotherhood. He never buys weapons (A) or teaches the young (D). Memory tip: Winston's first rebellion is with a pen: thoughtcrime on paper.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nThe Ministry of Truth is concerned with|news, entertainment, education and fine arts|propaganda, war, publishing and enlightenment|investigation, propaganda and fine arts|economic affairs, education and propaganda": "Oceania has four ministries with ironic names: the Ministry of Truth (lies and propaganda), Peace (war), Love (torture) and Plenty (rationing and shortages). Orwell describes the Ministry of Truth, where Winston works, as concerned \"with news, entertainment, education and the fine arts\": A. In practice this means rewriting old newspapers, producing propaganda and cheap entertainment for the masses. War (B) belongs to the Ministry of Peace and economic affairs (D) to the Ministry of Plenty. Memory tip: each ministry does the opposite of its name; Truth produces lies through the news, the schools and the arts.",
 "|Based on Ferdinand Oyono's The Old Man and the Medal: \nThe events following the ceremony confirm Meka's|disillusion with the whites|satisfaction with the medal|contribution to the colonialists|admiration for the church": "The Old Man and the Medal (1956) by Ferdinand Oyono of Cameroon tells of Meka, an old villager who gave his land to the Catholic mission and lost two sons fighting for France. He is awarded a medal by the colonial administration. After the ceremony he gets drunk, falls asleep, and wakes in a storm; he loses the medal in the dark, is beaten and locked up by the colonial police as a stranger, and returns home bitter, saying he is \"just an old man now\". These events confirm his disillusion with the whites: A. The medal brings him no satisfaction (B) and he loses his faith in the Church (D). Memory tip: Meka's medal is a symbol of empty colonial \"friendship\".",
 "|Based on Ferdinand Oyono's The Old Man and the Medal: \nIn the novel, the expression 'breaking the leg of an antelope' means|hunting|marriage|fighting|honeymoon": "Oyono's novel is full of village proverbs and expressions. Of one man who already had five wives, the novel says he was soon going to \"break the legs of the antelope\" for the sixth time. The expression is a figure for taking a wife: marriage: B. It sounds as if it is about hunting (A), which is why it catches students, but the context of the five wives shows the real meaning. The trap: A, because \"antelope\" makes students think literally of hunting. Memory tip: read an idiom in its context; a sixth time after five wives can only mean a sixth marriage.",
 "|Based on Buchi Emecheta's The Joys of Motherhood: \nNnaife's conscription into the army is portrayed as part of|family conflict|British oppression|family tradition|societal intrigue": "The Joys of Motherhood (1979) by the Nigerian novelist Buchi Emecheta follows Nnu Ego, who moves from Ibuza to colonial Lagos with her husband Nnaife and struggles to raise many children in poverty. Nnaife is forcibly conscripted by the British colonial authorities into the army to fight in the Second World War, leaving Nnu Ego to feed the family alone. The novel shows this as part of British oppression of the colonised: B. It is not a family quarrel or a family custom (A, C). Memory tip: in this novel, colonial power reaches right into the family, taking husbands and sons away.",
 "|Based on Buchi Emecheta's The Joys of Motherhood: \nThe attitude of Nnu Ego when she was told that her chi would not give her a child is one of|indifference|despair|apathy|hope": "In the society of The Joys of Motherhood a woman's worth is measured by the children she bears. In her first marriage, to Amatokwu, Nnu Ego does not conceive, and the herbalists put her barrenness down to her chi, her personal spirit. Her husband rejects her and takes another wife who soon becomes pregnant. Nnu Ego is overwhelmed by shame and hopelessness: her attitude is despair: B. She is anything but indifferent or apathetic (A, C), and the news gives her no hope (D). Memory tip: for Nnu Ego, childlessness means failure, so the news brings despair; even motherhood later brings few \"joys\", which is the irony of the title.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe subject matter of Adeoti's 'Naked Soles' is|soul and mind|hunger and strife|suffering and despair|hatred and bitterness": "Gbemisola Adeoti is a Nigerian poet and scholar; Naked Soles (2005) is the poet's collection of poems. Critics note that what runs through it is a picture of anguish in images of pain and discomfort, with a mood of gloom. In the title poem, the \"naked soles\" are bare-footed, oppressed people \"dancing through blooming thorns\", a \"carnival\" in which they cry in silent pain. The subject matter is suffering and despair: C. Hunger and strife (B) is close, but the poem's images are of pain and hopelessness rather than of hunger or fighting. Memory tip: subject matter = what the poem is mainly about; look at its dominant images (here, thorns and silent crying).",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nRubadiri's 'An African Thunderstorm' appeals to the reader's sense of|sight and taste|feeling and sight|smell, taste and hearing|sight, smell and hearing": "David Rubadiri, a Malawian poet, describes an approaching storm in an African village. Imagery can appeal to each of the senses. We SEE the clouds \"like a plague of locusts\", women darting about with babies on their backs, clothes waving \"like tattered flags\" and \"jagged blinding flashes\". We HEAR the wind that \"whistles by\", the \"screams of delighted children\" and the thunder that will \"rumble, tremble and crack\". We SMELL \"the smell of fired smoke\". So the poem appeals to sight, smell and hearing: D. Nothing in it is tasted. Memory tip: for imagery questions, scan the poem for seeing, hearing, smelling, tasting and touching words and count them.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \n'The experts, of course, disagree; the urine test they ordered said, Negative.'\nThe lines above from Acquah's 'In the Navel of the Soul' depict|contradiction|inevitability|progress|awareness": "Kobena Eyi Acquah is a Ghanaian lawyer and poet. \"In the Navel of the Soul\" opens with a strange birth: \"the experts\" cannot agree, and \"the urine test they ordered said, Negative\", while the midwives who examine the woman find otherwise. The lines set one expert opinion against another and a laboratory test against what is plainly happening: they depict contradiction: A. Critics read the \"experts\" as the leaders who mislead society while the truth stares them in the face. Nothing in the lines suggests progress (C) or that anything is certain (B). Memory tip: when experts disagree and the test denies the facts, the key word is contradiction.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe persona in the poem 'Give Me the Minstrel's Seat' discourages|togetherness|hatred|isolation|poverty": "\"Give Me the Minstrel's Seat\" is a traditional Swahili poem from East Africa whose author is unknown. Its persona celebrates friendship and companionship as the way to bear life's troubles; a life without friends is a poor and lonely one. So the persona discourages isolation: C. Togetherness (A) is what the poem encourages, the opposite of the answer, so read \"discourages\" carefully. Memory tip: in \"encourages/discourages\" questions, find what the persona praises; the answer to \"discourages\" is its opposite.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nMarvell's 'To His Coy Mistress' is written mainly in|alternate rhymes|blank verse|heroic couplets|middle rhymes": "In \"To His Coy Mistress\" (published 1681) Andrew Marvell's speaker urges his lady not to waste time: \"Had we but world enough and time, / This coyness, lady, were no crime.\" The lines rhyme in pairs, aa bb cc (time/crime, way/day), so the poem is written in rhyming couplets, and C is the answer expected. Strictly, however, a heroic couplet is a pair of rhyming iambic pentameter lines (five beats, as in Pope and Dryden), while Marvell's lines are iambic tetrameter (four beats), so the precise term, octosyllabic couplets, is not offered. Blank verse (B) does not rhyme, and alternate rhyme (A) is abab. Memory tip: heroic couplet = rhyming pair of FIVE-beat lines; count the beats.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe attitude of the poet-persona to bats, in Lawrence's 'Bat', is one of|satisfaction|reverence|hatred|repulsion": "In \"Bat\" D.H. Lawrence's speaker watches what he takes for swallows over Florence at dusk and then realises they are bats. He describes them with disgust: they hang themselves up \"like an old rag, to sleep\", \"disgustingly upside down\", and the poem ends with \"Not for me!\" His attitude is repulsion, a feeling of disgust that makes him turn away: D. The trap: C, hatred; but the speaker does not wish the bats harm; he is revolted by them. Reverence (B) is the attitude Lawrence takes to the snake in \"Snake\", not to the bats. Memory tip: repulsion = \"pushed back\" by disgust; look for words like \"disgustingly\".",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe language used in Eliot's 'Journey of the Magi' is generally|difficult|complex|simple|complicated": "In \"Journey of the Magi\" (1927) T.S. Eliot lets one of the three wise men look back on the journey to Bethlehem. He speaks in plain, everyday words, like an old man telling a story: \"A cold coming we had of it, / Just the worst time of the year / For a journey, and such a long journey\". The vocabulary and sentences are simple and conversational, even though the ideas (birth and death) are deep: C. Difficult, complex and complicated (A, B, D) all mean the same thing, which is a clue that none of them is the answer. Memory tip: simple words can carry deep thoughts; judge the language, not the theme.",
 "|An interlude is a brief|presentation in the interval of a dramatic performance|play before the main dramatic performance|presentation after the main dramatic performance|dialogue between two persons": "An interlude (Latin inter, \"between\", + ludus, \"play\") was originally a short, often comic piece performed between the parts of a longer play or entertainment, as in Tudor England. So an interlude is a brief presentation in the interval of a dramatic performance: A. A short piece BEFORE the main play (B) is a curtain-raiser, and one AFTER it (C) an afterpiece. Memory tip: inter = between, as in \"interval\" and \"international\".",
 "|An epic is a heroic story which includes|ballad|lyric|myth|dialogue": "An epic is a long narrative poem about the great deeds of a hero, on a grand scale, in an elevated style. Epics mix history with myth: gods take part (Homer's Iliad and Odyssey), supernatural beings and wonders appear (the Old English Beowulf fights monsters; the Mali epic of Sundiata is full of magic). So an epic includes myth: C. Ballad and lyric (A, B) are other kinds of poem, and dialogue (D) is not a defining part of an epic. Memory tip: epic = hero + great deeds + gods and marvels.",
 "|Farce can be described as a dramatic piece marked by|movement from serious to light-hearted mood|comic and exaggerated actions|actions devoid of meaning|gloomy actions with momentary reliefs": "A farce is a kind of comedy that aims only to make the audience laugh, using wildly exaggerated characters, improbable situations, mistaken identities, chases and slapstick: comic and exaggerated actions: B. A move from serious to light-hearted mood (A) or gloom with moments of relief (D) describes tragicomedy or comic relief in tragedy. Farce is silly but not \"devoid of meaning\" (C). Memory tip: farce = far-fetched fun.",
 "|A long story narrating a series of complicated events is called|saga|discourse|monologue|harangue": "A saga is a long prose story telling a series of connected, often complicated events and adventures, frequently across several generations of a family. The name comes from the Old Norse sagas of Iceland and Norway, but it is now used for any long family chronicle: A. Discourse (B) is a formal talk or piece of writing, a monologue (C) is a long speech by one person, and a harangue (D) is a long, angry speech. Memory tip: a saga goes on and on, like a family story told over many years.",
 "|Oxymoron is the use of two contrasting words that are|placed far apart|different in meaning|placed side by side|similar in meaning": "An oxymoron (Greek \"sharp-dull\") places two words of opposite meaning side by side: \"bitter-sweet\", \"living death\", \"deafening silence\", \"cruel kindness\": C. The words must be next to each other or very close in one phrase; when contradictory ideas form a whole statement that hides a truth, it is a paradox. \"Different in meaning\" (B) is true of almost any two words, so it does not define the figure. The trap: B, which seems to repeat \"contrasting\". Memory tip: oxymoron = two clashing WORDS joined; paradox = a clashing STATEMENT.",
 "|A line or a group of lines repeated in the course of a poem is called|chorus|refrain|repetition|prologue": "A refrain is a line or group of lines that is repeated at regular intervals in a poem or song, usually at the end of each stanza, as in many ballads and African songs. It builds rhythm and stresses the main idea: B. A chorus (A) is either a group of singers or speakers in a play or the part of a song all sing together; repetition (C) is the general device of repeating any word or idea, not this specific feature. The trap: A, because in popular songs the repeated part is called the chorus. Memory tip: refrain = \"re-frain\", the line that comes back again.",
 "|A limerick is a light verse which consists of five lines that are|anapestic|trochaic|spondaic|dactylic": "A limerick is a five-line comic poem rhyming aabba. Lines 1, 2 and 5 are long, with three stresses, and lines 3 and 4 are short, with two. The rhythm is anapestic: two light syllables then a stressed one (da-da-DUM): \"There was an old man with a beard\": A. Trochaic (DUM-da), spondaic (DUM-DUM) and dactylic (DUM-da-da) feet do not give the limerick its galloping rhythm. Memory tip: anapest = da-da-DUM, the rhythm of \"in the night\".",
 "|A short narrative or lyrical poem intended to be sung is a|leich|lay|lauda|letrilla": "A lay is a short narrative or lyrical poem meant to be sung, especially in the Middle Ages, like the Breton lays of Marie de France; later poets used the name for ballad-like poems such as Scott's \"The Lay of the Last Minstrel\": B. A leich (A) is the German form of the medieval French lai, a long lyric of irregular stanzas, so it is related but not short and not narrative; that is why some readers give it, but the precise answer is lay. A lauda (C) is an Italian hymn of praise and a letrilla (D) a short Spanish poem with a refrain. Memory tip: lay rhymes with \"say\"; it tells a story and is sung.",
 "|A figurative device in which a statement is made and then withdrawn is referred to as|metanoia|metaphysical|metalanguage|metalepsis": "Metanoia (Greek \"change of mind\") is a figure in which a speaker makes a statement and then corrects or withdraws it, usually to strengthen it: \"He is rich, no, he is the richest man in the town.\" Metanoia is used for emphasis and to sound natural: A. Metaphysical (B) describes a type of poetry (Donne), metalanguage (C) is language used to talk about language, and metalepsis (D) is a far-fetched metonymy. Memory tip: metanoia = \"Me, no, I mean...\".",
 "|A literary work which imitates another in a distorted form is called|exordium|isocolon|metonymy|burlesque": "A burlesque is a work that imitates another work or style in a distorted, exaggerated way in order to mock it, for example by treating a trivial subject in grand epic style, or a serious subject in a silly way: D. Pope's The Rape of the Lock (a mock-epic) and parodies are close relatives. An exordium (A) is the introduction of a speech, isocolon (B) is a series of phrases of equal length, and metonymy (C) is naming a thing by something associated with it. Memory tip: burlesque = mockery by distorted imitation.",
 "|'You do not have to be brutal to be a soldier, or rather you are brutal not because you are a soldier, but because there is a sadist, a rapist, a fascist and a murderer in you who is waiting for war and army uniforms to give them expression.'\n\nThe speaker here contends that|soldiers are generally wicked|human nature has to do with a profession|soldiers are characteristically gentle|the latent brutality in man finds expression in war": "Read what the speaker says, not what you expect. He denies that soldiering makes people brutal: \"you are brutal not because you are a soldier\". Instead he says there is \"a sadist, a rapist, a fascist and a murderer in you\" waiting for \"war and army uniforms to give them expression\". So brutality is hidden (latent) inside human beings, and war simply lets it out: D. Option A contradicts the first sentence (\"You do not have to be brutal to be a soldier\"), and B reverses his point, since he says brutality has nothing to do with the profession. Memory tip: \"latent\" means hidden and waiting, exactly what \"waiting for war\" describes.",
 "|'But the towering earth was tired of sitting in one position. She moved, suddenly, and the houses crumbled, the mountains heaved horribly, and the work of a million years was lost.'\n\nThe subject matter of the passage is|earthquake|demolition|flood|storm": "Subject matter means what the passage describes. The earth \"was tired of sitting in one position\" (a personification), then \"moved, suddenly\"; the houses crumbled and the mountains heaved. A sudden movement of the earth itself that shakes down houses and mountains is an earthquake: A. A flood (C) or storm (D) would bring water and wind, which are not mentioned, and demolition (B) is people pulling buildings down on purpose. Memory tip: name the force that does the action; here it is the earth itself moving.",
 "|'But the towering earth was tired of sitting in one position. She moved, suddenly, and the houses crumbled, the mountains heaved horribly, and the work of a million years was lost.'\n\nThe image depicted is one of|destruction|dejection|happiness|admiration": "An image is a picture created by words. Houses crumble, mountains heave \"horribly\" and \"the work of a million years was lost\": everything built over ages is ruined in a moment. The picture is one of destruction: A. Dejection (B) is a sad human feeling, but the passage shows ruin, not anyone's mood. Happiness and admiration (C, D) do not fit the \"horribly\" of the scene. Memory tip: for an image question, ask \"what would I see?\" Here: ruins.",
 "|'She even thinks that up in heaven\nHer class lies late and snores,\nWhile poor black cherubs rise at seven\nTo do celestial chores.'\nCountee Cullen, For a Lady I Know\n\nThe tone of the poem above is|satiric|affectionate|indifferent|sympathetic": "Countee Cullen was an African-American poet of the Harlem Renaissance. \"For a Lady I Know\" is a four-line epitaph-style poem about a rich white woman who is so used to black servants that she imagines that even in heaven her class will \"lie late and snore\" while \"poor black cherubs rise at seven / To do celestial chores.\" Cullen mocks her snobbery and racism by showing it carried absurdly into heaven: the tone is satiric: A. It is not affectionate or sympathetic (B, D) towards her, and certainly not indifferent. Memory tip: satire exposes a fault by making it look ridiculous.",
 "|'Life, though a vanity yet, not purposeful in drawling resignation. Life's spice is in strife.'\n\nFrom the extract above, the poet suggests that|life is full of care|there can't be life without strife|life is worthwhile without care|strife makes life worth living": "The extract says that life, though \"a vanity\" (empty, passing), has no purpose when lived in \"drawling resignation\", that is, in lazy giving up. Then: \"Life's spice is in strife.\" Spice is what gives food its flavour; so struggle is what gives life its flavour and worth: strife makes life worth living: D. Some readers give B, \"there can't be life without strife\", but the poet does not say life cannot exist without struggle, only that it is tasteless and purposeless without it. A and C are not in the lines. Memory tip: \"spice\" = what makes something worth having.",
 "|'An unlucky creation\nHis mother, a street walker;\nHis lying father; A champion at producing bastards.'\nB.S. Tibenderana, The Bastard\n\nThe subject of the poem above is|a prostitute|the lying father|the son|the orphan": "The subject of a poem is the person or thing it is about. The poem describes \"an unlucky creation\": \"His mother, a street walker; / His lying father; A champion at producing bastards.\" Each line describes the parents of \"him\", the child. The title, The Bastard, names him: the son: C. The mother and father (A, B) are mentioned only to show the circumstances of his birth, and he is not called an orphan (D): both parents are alive. The trap: B, because \"the lying father\" is described at length. Memory tip: find whose story the possessives (\"his mother\", \"his father\") belong to.",
 "|'Sweet smile in time of snarl\nGives pride in spite of sneer\nSing, rid this world of despair\nAnd save, a snared heart from cascading stream of strife.'\n\nThe dominant rhetorical device in the excerpt above is|rhyme|alliteration|repetition|onomatopoeia": "Alliteration is the repetition of the same consonant sound at the start of nearby words. These lines are full of \"s\" sounds: \"Sweet smile ... snarl\", \"spite of sneer\", \"Sing\", \"save, a snared heart from ... stream of strife.\" The repeated s-sounds dominate the excerpt: B. There is no regular rhyme scheme (A), no word or line is repeated (C), and no word imitates a sound (D). The trap: C, because students confuse repeated SOUNDS (alliteration) with repeated WORDS (repetition). Memory tip: alliteration = \"same starting sound\": Peter Piper picked.",
 "|'My heart leaps up when I behold\nA rainbow in the sky:\nSo was it when my life began;\nSo is it now I am a man;\nSo be it when I shall grow old,\nOr let me die!\nThe Child is father of the Man;\nAnd I could wish my days to be\nBound each to each by natural piety.'\nWilliam Wordsworth, My Heart Leaps Up When I Behold\n\nThe expression 'The Child is father of the Man' in the poem above is an example of|paradox|metaphor|simile|ellipsis": "In \"My Heart Leaps Up\" (1802) William Wordsworth says the joy he felt at a rainbow as a child is still with him, and he hopes it will stay till he dies. Then: \"The Child is father of the Man.\" A child cannot literally be the father of a man, so the line seems absurd; yet it holds a truth: the adult grows out of the child he was, and childhood shapes the man. A statement that seems self-contradictory but is true is a paradox: A. The line is built like a metaphor (B), but what makes it famous is the apparent contradiction. There is no \"like\" or \"as\" (C) and nothing is left out (D). Memory tip: paradox = sounds impossible, turns out true.",
 "|'Today's my last day here. I'm done banging my head against the glass ceiling. They think I don't know why they really hired me. They're talking to dad behind my back. Begging for favours. Work permit renewals. Licences, tax and duty waivers. Contracts and tenders.'\nKen Kamoche, A Fragile Hope\n\nThe technique employed in narrating the excerpt above is|flashback|stream of consciousness|foreshadowing|play-within-the-play": "Stream of consciousness presents a character's thoughts as they flow through the mind, often in short, broken, unordered fragments. Here the narrator thinks in the present (\"Today's my last day here\"), and the thoughts tumble out: \"Begging for favours. Work permit renewals. Licences, tax and duty waivers. Contracts and tenders.\" These are not full sentences but the jumble of a mind going over grievances: B. A flashback (A) would take us back to show a past scene; foreshadowing (C) hints at what is to come; a play-within-the-play (D) belongs to drama. The trap: A, because the narrator mentions past events, but he is remembering them in the present, not taking us back to them. Memory tip: fragments + inner voice = stream of consciousness.",
 "|'If the earth were mortal\nWhere would the flower live?\nIf you left the earth\nWhat would happen to beauty?'\n\nThe poet's tone above can be described as|lamentative|satiric|imploring|adoring": "Tone is the attitude and feeling of the speaker. These lines turn on loss and death: \"If the earth were mortal\" (if it could die) and \"If you left the earth\" (if you were gone, perhaps through death). The speaker asks where the flower would live and \"What would happen to beauty?\", grieving over the thought of beauty dying with the one addressed. That brooding, mournful note is lamentative: A. The questions also praise the person addressed, so some readers hear an adoring tone (D), but the mood of loss is stronger. There is no mockery (B), and the speaker does not plead (C). Memory tip: words of death, loss and \"what would happen\" point to a lamenting tone.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nJames Ofosu's desire is to see Maanan trained as|a choreographer|an attorney|an accountant|a surgeon": "Sons and Daughters is a play by the Ghanaian dramatist Joe (J.C.) de Graft about a well-to-do father, James Ofosu, who wants to choose professions for his children. He plans for Aaron to study engineering and for Maanan to study law, and he even asks his friend Lawyer Bonu to help. But Aaron wants to paint and Maanan wants to dance. So James's desire is to see Maanan trained as a lawyer, an attorney: B. A choreographer (A) is close to what Maanan herself wants, since she loves dance, but it is the opposite of her father's plan. The trap: A catches students who remember Maanan's dancing and forget that the question asks about James's wish. Memory tip: in this play the father picks \"respectable\" careers (law, engineering); the children pick the arts.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nThe plot of the play is|linear|cyclical|complicated|episodic": "A linear plot tells events in the order in which they happen, from beginning to end, without jumping back and forth in time. Sons and Daughters moves straight forward: the quarrel in James Ofosu's house over his children's careers, the pressure from relatives and from Lawyer Bonu, Bonu's misconduct towards Maanan, and finally James's change of heart. There is no circular return to the start (cyclical), no string of loosely linked separate episodes (episodic), and \"complicated\" is not a type of plot. So the plot is linear: A. Memory tip: linear = a straight line through time; episodic = beads on a string; cyclical = ends where it began.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nWhat causes the strained relationship between James and Lawyer Bonu?|Betrayal of trust|Standard of education|Working experience|Family differences": "Lawyer Bonu is a wealthy, respected friend of James Ofosu. James trusts him so much that he asks Bonu to help arrange his children's education abroad. But Bonu abuses that trust: he makes advances to James's daughter Maanan, first at his office and then in James's own house, where James catches him. A friend who uses his position to try to seduce your child has betrayed your trust, and that is what sours the relationship: A. Education, work experience and family differences are not what turns James against him. Memory tip: when a trusted friend becomes an enemy in a play, look first for a betrayal.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \n'Now look what we have: a permanent bloom of ugly paper flowers!'\nThe device used by Aaron in the excerpt above is|suspense|rhetorical question|oxymoron|allegory": "An oxymoron joins two words or ideas that contradict each other, as in \"bitter-sweet\" or \"living death\". Aaron, the young artist in Sons and Daughters, sneers at artificial flowers. A \"bloom\" is the brief flowering of a living plant, yet here it is \"permanent\"; flowers are meant to be lovely, yet these are \"ugly\". Putting such opposites side by side creates the contradiction: C. The line ends with an exclamation mark, not a question, so it is not a rhetorical question (B); it creates no suspense and is not a story with a hidden meaning (allegory). Memory tip: oxymoron = two clashing words in one phrase; paradox = a whole statement that seems to contradict itself.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'... So tedious is this day\nAs is the night before some festival\nTo an impatient child that hath new robes\nAnd may not wear them.'\n\nWhat makes Juliet's night so tedious is the|murder of Tybalt by Romeo|arranged marriage between her and Paris|eagerness to be married to Romeo|unconsummated marriage with Romeo": "Romeo and Juliet (about 1595) is Shakespeare's tragedy of two young lovers from the feuding families of Verona. In Act 3, Scene 2, Juliet has secretly married Romeo that day and waits for night, when he will come to her room. She says the day is as tedious \"as is the night before some festival / To an impatient child that hath new robes / And may not wear them.\" She has the new \"robes\" (the marriage) but cannot yet enjoy them: the marriage is not yet consummated: D. She does not yet know of Tybalt's death or the match with Paris. The trap: C sounds right, but the wedding has already taken place. Memory tip: new robes she cannot wear = a marriage she cannot yet enjoy.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'... So tedious is this day\nAs is the night before some festival\nTo an impatient child that hath new robes\nAnd may not wear them.'\n\nThe literary device used in the excerpt is|euphemism|simile|sarcasm|bathos": "A simile compares two things openly, using \"like\" or \"as\". Juliet says, \"So tedious is this day / As is the night before some festival / To an impatient child\". The long, slow day is compared to the endless night before a feast, through the word \"as\". That is a simile: B. A euphemism is a mild word for something unpleasant; sarcasm is mocking irony aimed at someone; bathos is a sudden fall from the grand to the ridiculous. None of them fits this comparison. Memory tip: spot \"like\" or \"as\" joining two different things, and you have a simile.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nThe atmosphere of tragedy in the play is created through the use of|dreams and foreboding|fights and quarrels|wars and hatred|ideas and dramatization": "Atmosphere is the feeling that hangs over a work. In Romeo and Juliet, Shakespeare builds a sense of doom long before the deaths. The Prologue calls the lovers \"star-cross'd\". Before the Capulet feast Romeo says his \"mind misgives / Some consequence yet hanging in the stars\" that will end his life; he speaks of a dream; and Juliet, watching him leave, cries \"O God, I have an ill-divining soul!\" These dreams and forebodings make the audience feel tragedy coming: A. The fights and quarrels (B) are events that drive the plot, but the brooding atmosphere comes from the forebodings. The trap: B is tempting because the street brawls lead to deaths, but they are actions, not the atmosphere. Memory tip: atmosphere comes from hints and feelings; plot comes from actions.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nShakespeare depicts love and idealism through the|party at the Capulets'|lovers' tomb at the end|brawl between Romeo and Tybalt|balcony scene in the second Act": "The balcony scene, Act 2, Scene 2, is the most famous love scene in English drama. Romeo stands in the Capulet orchard below Juliet's window and hears her say, \"O Romeo, Romeo, wherefore art thou Romeo?\" The lovers swear their love in rich, idealistic poetry, ignoring the family hatred and the danger; Juliet calls her love \"as boundless as the sea\". This is where Shakespeare shows love at its purest and most idealistic: D. The party (A) is where they first meet, the tomb (B) is where love ends in death, and the brawl with Tybalt (C) shows hatred. Memory tip: Act 1 they meet, Act 2 they pledge (balcony), Act 3 the brawl, Act 5 the tomb.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nIn the novel, the Party approves separation of couples only|on condition of infidelity|where there are no children|in situations of irreconcilable differences|on the death of one of the couples": "Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949) is George Orwell's novel about Winston Smith in Oceania, a state ruled by the Party and its leader Big Brother. The Party controls even marriage. Winston remembers his wife Katharine, from whom he has been separated for years. He notes that the Party did not permit divorce, but it encouraged separation in cases where there were no children. Winston and Katharine had no children, so they simply lived apart: B. The Party's interest is in breeding new members, not in infidelity or \"irreconcilable differences\". Memory tip: in Oceania, marriage exists to produce children for the Party, so no children means no reason to stay together.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nOne of the themes of the novel is|colonialism|repression|senility|destruction": "A theme is a central idea a work explores. In Nineteen Eighty-Four the Party watches its members through telescreens, rewrites history, punishes \"thoughtcrime\" and tortures rebels in the Ministry of Love until they love Big Brother. Every part of life, even private thought and love, is held down by force. That is repression: B. The novel is not about colonialism (A), old age (C) or destruction as such (D). Other themes worth knowing are totalitarianism, surveillance and the manipulation of truth through language. Memory tip: \"Big Brother is watching you\" sums up the novel's theme of repression.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nNewspeak, the official language of Oceania, is devised to|meet the ideological needs of Ingsoc|help devoted party men and women|provide a medium of expression for world-view only|assist by cutting the choice of words for party members": "Newspeak is the official language of Oceania in Nineteen Eighty-Four. The novel's appendix explains that its purpose was to provide a medium of expression for the world-view and mental habits proper to the devotees of Ingsoc (English Socialism, the Party's ideology) and to make all other modes of thought impossible. So it was devised to meet the ideological needs of Ingsoc: A. Option C says nearly the same thing (a medium of expression for the Party's world-view only), so both A and C are accepted. D describes how the purpose was helped along, by cutting down the number of words, not why Newspeak was devised. Memory tip: Newspeak shrinks language so that rebellious thoughts have no words.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nThe main concern of Winston in the novel is to|take power for himself|acquire wealth|overthrow the Party|bring social change": "Winston Smith works at the Ministry of Truth, rewriting history for the Party, but he secretly hates Big Brother. He keeps a forbidden diary, begins an affair with Julia, and with her goes to O'Brien to join the Brotherhood, the secret organisation he believes is working to bring down the Party. His deepest wish is to overthrow the Party: C. He never wants power or wealth for himself (A, B), and although overthrowing the Party would bring change, D is too vague to be his \"main concern\". The trap: D sounds similar, but Winston's aim is the specific one of destroying the Party's rule. Memory tip: Winston's diary says it all: \"DOWN WITH BIG BROTHER\".",
 "|Based on Ferdinand Oyono's The Old Man and the Medal: \n'We are here to die of rage! Say die of rage because this time the uncircumcised have gone too far...'\nThe word 'uncircumcised' in the statement above by Nti refers to|Nua and Mvondo|the whites|the people of Doum|the white farmers": "The Old Man and the Medal (1956), by the Cameroonian Ferdinand Oyono, is a satire of French colonial rule. Meka, an old villager of Doum, has given his land to the church and lost his two sons in the war, and the French reward him with a medal; soon after, he is humiliated and thrown in a cell by the colonial police. The Africans in the novel scornfully call the white men \"the uncircumcised\", since for them circumcision marks a real man. When Nti says that \"this time the uncircumcised have gone too far\", he means the whites: B. Nua and Mvondo and the people of Doum are Africans; \"white farmers\" is too narrow. Memory tip: in the village's eyes the white colonisers are \"the uncircumcised\".",
 "|Based on Ferdinand Oyono's The Old Man and the Medal: \nThe only two sons of Meka die fighting for|independence|the colonialists|the church|their people": "Meka in The Old Man and the Medal has served the French loyally: he gave his land to the Catholic mission, and his two sons were killed fighting in the French army during the war. The colonial government rewards this loyalty with nothing more than a medal. So his sons died fighting for the colonialists: B. They were not fighting for independence (A), for the church (C) or for their own people (D). Oyono uses this sacrifice to show how much Africans gave to the colonial power and how little they received in return; Meka's wife Kelara's grief makes the medal look cheap. Memory tip: two dead sons and a land gift bought Meka only a medal.",
 "|Based on Buchi Emecheta's The Joys of Motherhood: \nIn the society of Nwokocha Agbadi, a man's social rating is determined by his|oratorical power|physical prowess|gentleness|altruistic actions": "The Joys of Motherhood (1979), by Buchi Emecheta, follows Nnu Ego, daughter of the great chief Nwokocha Agbadi of Ogboli (Ibuza), whose whole worth is measured by motherhood. In describing Agbadi, Emecheta says he was a very wealthy chief and a great wrestler, living at a time when a man's standing depended on physical strength, so people accepted him as a leader. His social rating rested on physical prowess: B. Agbadi is also a fine speaker, which makes A the trap, but the novel ties social standing to strength. Gentleness and kindness (C, D) were not what made a man great in his world. Memory tip: Agbadi = wrestler, hunter, chief; in his age, strength made the man.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThorn in Adeoti's 'Naked Soles' symbolizes|victory|bleakness|destruction|suffering": "Naked Soles (2005) is a collection by the Nigerian poet Gbemisola Adeoti. In the title poem the bare feet (\"naked soles\") of the common people are pictured \"dancing through blooming thorns\", crying in silent pain. A thorn pierces the bare foot: it is a symbol of the pain and hardship that ordinary people go through, under bad rulers and hard times. So the thorn symbolizes suffering: D. It does not stand for victory (A), and bleakness (B) and destruction (C) miss the idea of pain endured. Memory tip: bare feet on thorns = people walking through suffering.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \n'The silence of the battlefield heralds the widow's anguish'\nThe widow in the lines above in Launko's 'End of the War' is in anguish because of the|confirmation of her husband's death|uncertainty over her husband returning alive|delay in the husband's return from the battlefield|death of all the soldiers in the battlefield": "Okinba Launko is the pen name under which the Nigerian playwright Femi Osofisan publishes poetry. His \"End of the War\" bitterly says that the end of a war brings no real peace: the poor still suffer and the \"butchers fill the parliaments\". The poem opens: \"The silence of the battlefield heralds the widow's anguish\". When the guns fall silent, the soldiers who are coming home return, and the one who does not return is known to be dead. The silence confirms the woman's loss and makes her a widow: A. She is called a widow, so there is no longer any doubt (B) or delay (C). Memory tip: read the noun carefully: \"widow\" already tells you the husband is dead.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Marvell's 'To His Coy Mistress', the poet-persona tries to|build up the climax of expectation in women|discuss how a woman labours hard|meet the people's expectation in a religious adventure|discuss the nature of man and the value of time": "Andrew Marvell's \"To His Coy Mistress\" (published 1681) is a metaphysical poem in which a lover urges a shy lady to give in to love. Its argument is about time: \"Had we but world enough and time\" he would court her for centuries, but \"at my back I always hear / Time's wingèd chariot hurrying near\", and the grave awaits. So they must seize the moment. The poem therefore reflects on human mortality and the value of time: D. It is not about women labouring (B) or a religious journey (C), and it is not \"building up expectation in women\" (A). Memory tip: carpe diem, \"seize the day\": life is short, so use time well.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Lawrence's 'Bat', the poet's attitude to bats is one of|fear|sarcasm|contempt|flattering": "D.H. Lawrence's \"Bat\" (1923) describes an evening in Florence. The speaker first thinks the darting shapes are swallows, then realises they are bats, and his feelings turn to disgust. He describes them hanging upside down \"like an old rag\" and declares \"Not for me!\", adding that in China the bat is a symbol of happiness. He looks down on the creatures with scorn: contempt, C. He is not afraid of them (A); sarcasm (B) is mocking speech aimed at someone, not an attitude to an animal; and he certainly does not flatter them (D). Memory tip: compare Lawrence's \"Snake\", where he admires the creature; in \"Bat\" he despises it.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe first stanza of Eliot's 'Journey of the Magi' depicts the|hazards of the journey|increment of fortune for the pilgrims|blind acceptance of faith|journey as an influential factor": "T.S. Eliot's \"Journey of the Magi\" (1927) is a dramatic monologue in which one of the wise men who visited the infant Jesus looks back on the journey. The first stanza is a catalogue of hardships: \"A cold coming we had of it, / Just the worst time of the year\", the camels \"galled, sore-footed, refractory\", the camel men running away, \"the cities hostile and the towns unfriendly / And the villages dirty and charging high prices\". It depicts the hazards of the journey: A. The fortune of the pilgrims does not increase (B), and faith is questioned later, not accepted blindly (C). Memory tip: stanza 1 = the hard road; stanza 2 = the arrival; stanza 3 = the meaning (birth and death).",
 "|A poetic device in which an idea in a line runs into another is called|rhyme scheme|enjambment|end rhyme|pathos": "Enjambment (from French, \"striding over\") happens when a sentence or idea runs on from one line of verse into the next without a pause at the line's end. In Wordsworth's \"My heart leaps up when I behold / A rainbow in the sky\", the sense flows across the line break. The opposite is an end-stopped line, which closes with a pause or punctuation. A rhyme scheme (A) is the pattern of end rhymes, end rhyme (C) is rhyme at line ends, and pathos (D) is the quality that arouses pity. So the answer is enjambment: B. Memory tip: enjambment = the idea \"jumps\" the line break; caesura = a pause inside the line.",
 "|The use of sound pattern to suggest meaning in poetry is called|rhythm|mimics|lyric|metonymy": "Rhythm is the pattern of sound in a line of verse: the beat made by stressed and unstressed syllables. Poets use it to suggest meaning: quick, light rhythm for joy or speed, slow and heavy rhythm for grief or effort, as in the steady beat of a marching song or the rocking beat of a lullaby. So the use of a sound pattern to suggest meaning here is rhythm: A. \"Mimics\" (B) is not a literary term, a lyric (C) is a short song-like poem, and metonymy (D) is a figure of association. Onomatopoeia, where single words imitate sounds, is a related idea not offered here. Memory tip: rhythm is the poem's heartbeat, and it can quicken or slow with the meaning.",
 "|A conscious imitation of a literary work by another with the aim of ridicule is|mimesis|performance|mockery|parody": "A parody is a conscious imitation of another writer's work or style, exaggerated in order to make fun of it. Wendy Cope's poems in the voice of \"Jason Strugnell\", for example, parody famous poets. Mimesis (A) is Aristotle's word for the imitation of life in art generally, not imitation for ridicule. \"Performance\" (B) is not a term for imitation, and \"mockery\" (C) is the general act of making fun, not a literary form that copies a particular work. So the answer is parody: D. The trap: A, because mimesis also means imitation, but it is serious imitation of reality, not of another work, and not to mock. Memory tip: parody = imitate to mock; burlesque = treat a serious subject absurdly (or the reverse).",
 "|The use of gestures to communicate in drama is known as|burlesque|soliloquy|mime|melodrama": "Mime is acting that tells a story or expresses feeling through gestures, body movement and facial expression, without words. Many plays include mimed scenes, and African festival drama often uses mime and dance. Burlesque (A) is a mocking imitation; soliloquy (B) is a speech by a character alone on stage, which is words, not gestures; melodrama (D) is drama of exaggerated emotion and simple heroes and villains. So the use of gestures to communicate in drama is mime: C. Memory tip: mime = movement and meaning without speech.",
 "|A short and witty remark is|a limerick|a tercet|an epigram|a litotes": "An epigram is a short, witty saying, often with a twist at the end, such as Oscar Wilde's \"I can resist everything except temptation\" or Pope's \"To err is human, to forgive divine.\" It can also be a very short witty poem. A limerick (A) is a five-line comic poem rhyming AABBA; a tercet (B) is a stanza of three lines; litotes (D) is understatement by denying the opposite (\"not bad\"). So a short and witty remark is an epigram: C. Memory tip: epigram = a short sharp \"gram\" of wit.",
 "|An expression that signifies a whole through its significant parts is|synesthesia|synecdoche|metonymy|allegory": "Synecdoche is a figure of speech in which a part stands for the whole (or the whole for a part). \"All hands on deck\" uses hands for sailors; \"a new set of wheels\" means a car; \"Nigeria won\" means the national team. The question describes exactly this, a whole signified through its significant parts: B. Metonymy (C) names something by a thing closely associated with it, not one of its parts (\"the crown\" for the king). Synesthesia mixes the senses (\"a loud colour\"), and allegory is a whole story with a second meaning. The trap: C, metonymy, is the near twin of synecdoche. Memory tip: synecdoche = part of the thing; metonymy = something next to the thing.",
 "|A structural arrangement of units of composition by which one element of equal importance with another is similarly placed is called|repetition|paradox|refrain|parallelism": "Parallelism is the arrangement of words, phrases or clauses of equal importance in the same grammatical pattern, so that they balance each other: \"I came, I saw, I conquered\", or \"Ask not what your country can do for you; ask what you can do for your country.\" Each unit is placed in the same way as its partner: D. Repetition (A) simply repeats words, without the stress on matching structure; a refrain (C) is a line repeated at intervals in a poem or song; a paradox (B) is a statement that seems self-contradictory. The trap: A, since parallel structures often repeat words, but the defining feature is the matching pattern. Memory tip: parallel lines run side by side, and so do parallel clauses.",
 "|The pride that contributes to the fall of a tragic character in a play is known as|hamartia|catharsis|hubris|epitasis": "Hubris is the excessive pride or arrogance that makes a tragic hero defy the gods, fate or good advice, and so brings about his fall. Okonkwo's pride and Creon's stubbornness in Antigone are classic examples. Hamartia (A) is the wider term for the tragic flaw or error of judgement; pride is one kind of hamartia, but the word for the pride itself is hubris. Catharsis (B) is the audience's release of pity and fear, and epitasis (D) is the part of a play where the complication intensifies. So the answer is hubris: C. The trap: A, because hamartia also causes the fall; remember that hubris names the pride specifically. Memory tip: Hubris = High pride; Hamartia = the Hero's flaw of any kind.",
 "|A metrical foot of two stressed or long syllables is called|a spondee|a trochee|an anapest|an iamb": "A metrical foot is a unit of rhythm in verse. A spondee has two stressed (or, in classical verse, long) syllables together: DUM-DUM, as in \"heartbreak\" or \"deep peace\". A trochee is stressed then unstressed (DUM-da: \"garden\"); an anapest is two unstressed then a stressed (da-da-DUM: \"understand\"); an iamb is unstressed then stressed (da-DUM: \"about\"). So two stressed syllables make a spondee: A. Memory tip: spondee = \"Strong-Strong\"; iamb = \"i-AM\".",
 "|A group of three lyric stanzas in classical Greek poetry is referred to as|triplet|triolet|trilogy|triad": "The Greek choral ode, perfected by Pindar, is built from groups of three stanzas: the strophe (sung as the chorus moved one way), the antistrophe (sung as it moved back, in the same metre) and the epode (sung standing still, in a different metre). This group of three is called a triad: D. A triplet (A) is three lines rhyming together; a triolet (B) is an eight-line French form with repeated lines; a trilogy (C) is a set of three related plays, novels or other works, such as Aeschylus's Oresteia. The trap: C, because Greek tragedies did come in trilogies, but a trilogy is three works, not three stanzas. Memory tip: strophe + antistrophe + epode = one triad.",
 "|Biokun: I did it for her! Let them laugh! Let them condemn me. I who have always derided superstition. I have gone back to the shrine of Olokun. At least if he dies, Oroki will know I tried everything.\nFemi Osofisan, No More the Wasted Breed\n\nBiokun's mood in this passage is one of|desperation|frustration|anger|anxiety": "Mood here means the state of feeling of the speaker. Biokun has always laughed at superstition, yet now he has gone back to the shrine of Olokun, the sea god, ready to be laughed at and condemned. \"At least if he dies, Oroki will know I tried everything.\" A man who abandons his own beliefs and will try anything, however hopeless, to save someone is desperate: A. He is not angry (C), and frustration (B) and anxiety (D) do not capture his willingness to try \"everything\". The trap: D, since he is clearly worried, but his words show desperation, a last-resort mood. Memory tip: \"I tried everything\" is the voice of desperation.",
 "|'My song is the root\ntouching other roots\nin a covenant below the crust...'\nNiyi Osundare, Waiting Laughters\n\nThe imagery used in the second line of the extract above is|olfactory|visual|tactile|auditory": "Imagery is language that appeals to the senses: sight (visual), sound (auditory), smell (olfactory), touch (tactile), taste (gustatory). In Niyi Osundare's lines, \"My song is the root / touching other roots / in a covenant below the crust\", the second line, \"touching other roots\", appeals to the sense of touch: the roots meet and press against one another under the soil, as the poet's song connects with other people. So the imagery is tactile: C. Nothing in the line is smelt or heard, and although we can picture roots, the key word is \"touching\". Memory tip: tactile = touch (think of \"contact\").",
 "|'The huge sacrifice that wards off death, the big offering that prevents diseases...'\nFemi Osofisan, Morountodun\n\nThe poem above can best be described as|epic|heroic|praise|quatrain": "Praise poetry honours a person, god or thing by piling up praise names and epithets; in Yoruba it is called oriki. The lines \"The huge sacrifice that wards off death, the big offering that prevents diseases\" do exactly this: each phrase is a title of honour for the one being praised. Femi Osofisan's play Morountodun draws on the legend of Moremi, the Ife heroine who made a great sacrifice for her people, and its songs use this praise style. So the lines are best described as praise poetry: C. An epic (A) is a long narrative poem about heroic deeds; these lines tell no story. The trap: A, because Moremi's legend is heroic, but the form is praise. Memory tip: praise names = praise poem; long heroic story = epic.",
 "|Why blame he who denies love for the asking and not the birds and moths and beetles that after deep draughts from rich nectar wells flee the drained petals to virgin flowers.\n\nThe lines above are an indictment of|adultery|gluttony|flirtation|infertility": "The lines contrast a person who refuses love when asked with \"the birds and moths and beetles\" that drink deeply from one flower (\"deep draughts from rich nectar wells\") and then \"flee the drained petals to virgin flowers\". The insects stand for lovers who take what they want from one partner and quickly move on to fresh ones. The speaker blames this fickle, playful moving from one love to another: flirtation, C. Gluttony (B) is greed for food; the drinking here is a picture of love. Adultery (A) needs a marriage, which the lines never mention, and infertility (D) is not in view. Memory tip: insects flitting from flower to flower are a classic image of the flirt.",
 "|Oh, the market is set like the hub of a wheel and it pulls like a magnet strong, it is easy to tell how it casts its spell and draws all the crowds along.\n\nThe sound device used in the extract above is|rhyme|alliteration|emphasis|assonance": "Assonance is the repetition of vowel sounds in nearby words; rhyme is the matching of end sounds. In \"it is easy to tell how it casts its spell\", the short \"e\" sound runs through \"set\", \"tell\" and \"spell\": that is assonance, D. The lines also rhyme: \"tell\" and \"spell\" rhyme inside the line, and \"strong\" rhymes with \"along\". So both assonance (D) and rhyme (A) are accepted. Alliteration (B), repeated consonant sounds at the start of words, is not prominent, and emphasis (C) is not a sound device. Memory tip: assonance = vowels echo; alliteration = opening consonants echo; rhyme = endings match.",
 "|A reference beyond the immediate purview of what is being presented is known as|allusion|diversion|inversion|imagery": "An allusion is a brief reference to a person, place, event or text outside the work, which the reader is expected to recognise: calling a traitor \"a Judas\", or describing a strong man as \"a Samson\". It reaches beyond the immediate subject being presented. Diversion (B) means a turning aside, inversion (C) is a change in normal word order, and imagery (D) is language that appeals to the senses within the work. So the answer is allusion: A. Memory tip: allusion = a \"link\" to something outside the text, like a hyperlink.",
 "|'The hoe is her barber'\nThe line above is an example of|metaphor|hyperbole|personification|litotes": "\"The hoe is her barber\" says, without \"like\" or \"as\", that a hoe is a barber: a direct comparison, which makes it a metaphor (A). At the same time it gives the farming tool a human occupation: the hoe \"shaves\" her as a barber shaves a head, as when the farmer's hoe clears the grass from the earth. Giving human qualities or actions to a thing is personification (C). The line is both, so A and C are accepted. It is not an exaggeration (hyperbole, B) or an understatement (litotes, D). Memory tip: a metaphor says \"X is Y\"; when Y is a person or a human job, it personifies X as well.",
 "|'From the sky suspended the strings of tattered pants marched a trembling feet under stone heavy-kits marched, on their breast scared, monogram of skulls and bones.'\nOssie Enekwe, Mass for the Dead\n\nThe subject matter of the extract above is|wastage|war|love|hatred": "Subject matter is what a passage is literally about. Ossie Enekwe is a Nigerian poet whose work draws on the Nigerian Civil War. In these lines, figures in \"tattered pants\" march on \"trembling feet\" under \"stone heavy-kits\", and on their breasts is the \"monogram of skulls and bones\", the death's-head badge. Marching men, military kits and the skull-and-crossbones all picture soldiers going to fight and die. So the subject matter is war: B. \"Wastage\" (A) may be part of the message about war's cost, but it is not what the lines describe. Memory tip: subject matter = what the lines are about on the surface; theme = the message behind it.",
 "|'Tonderai was assisted by the villagers in placing his trunk box into the back of the car and he got into the passenger side of the truck. Initially, he was a bit nervous and clearly uncomfortable, his young mind was asking how someone whom he had almost caused a serious accident could be his benefactor.'\nDaniel T. Manyika, Two Worlds Apart\n\nThe narrative point of view used in the extract above can best be described as|omniscient|subjective|panoramic|scenic": "Point of view is the angle from which a story is told. In this extract the narrator speaks in the third person (\"Tonderai\", \"he\", \"his\"), standing outside the story, yet also tells us what is going on inside Tonderai's head: \"his young mind was asking how someone whom he had almost caused a serious accident could be his benefactor.\" A third-person narrator who can see into a character's thoughts is omniscient: A. \"Subjective\" (B) suggests a first-person, personal account; \"panoramic\" (C) and \"scenic\" (D) describe ways of presenting scenes, not who tells the story. Memory tip: \"he/she\" plus inside thoughts = omniscient narrator.",
 "|'Me to the Orangery solitude invites, a wagtail, to tell the tangled-wood-tale'\nChristopher Okigbo, Heavensgate\n\nThe complexity of the poem can be ascribed to the poet's use of|archaic diction|structural inversion|compound words|extended metaphor": "Christopher Okigbo is known for difficult, musical poetry. In these opening lines of \"Heavensgate\" two features make the meaning hard to grasp. First, the word order is inverted: in plain English it reads \"Solitude invites me, a wagtail, to the orangery to tell the tangled-wood-tale\". Second, Okigbo coins compound words such as \"tangled-wood-tale\" that pack several ideas into one. Both structural inversion (B) and compound words (C) are accepted. The diction is not archaic (A), and there is no extended metaphor running through the lines (D). Memory tip: if a poem is hard to follow, check the word order first, then look for coined compounds.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nMaanan expresses dislike for Lawyer B because of|his condemnation of her choice of career|his recent advances towards her|the betrayal of her father's trust|the betrayal of his wife's trust": "Sons and Daughters is a play by the Ghanaian dramatist Joe (J.C.) de Graft about James Ofosu, a well-to-do father who wants to choose professions for his children, while Aaron wants to paint and Maanan wants to dance. Lawyer Bonu, a married friend of the family, makes advances to Maanan when she goes to his office, and this is why she dislikes him: B. His behaviour also betrays her father's trust and his own wife's (C and D), but what Maanan herself reacts to is his pursuit of her. Memory tip: when asked why a character dislikes someone, look for what that person did to the character directly.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nThe traditional order in the play is represented by|Mrs. B|Hannah|Maanan|Aunt": "In Sons and Daughters the young people, Aaron and Maanan, want to follow their own talents, while the older generation clings to the old ways. Aunt Fosuwa, James Ofosu's elder sister, is the clearest voice of the old order: she meddles in her brother's household, blames his wife Hannah for the family's troubles and backs the wealthy Lawyer Bonu. So the traditional order is represented by Aunt: D. Hannah (B) is a gentle, understanding mother, and Maanan (C) is one of the children who rebel. Memory tip: in a play about change, sort the characters into the old side and the new side.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nWhere does the play take place?|On the street|In George's place|In Aunt's house|In Ofosu's place": "A play's setting is where (and when) its action happens. Sons and Daughters is a domestic drama: the quarrel between James Ofosu and his children over their careers is played out at home, in the Ofosu family's house, where visitors such as Lawyer Bonu, Aunt Fosuwa and Aaron's friend Awere come and go. So the play takes place in Ofosu's place: D. It is not a street play (A), and neither George's place (B) nor Aunt's house (C) is the scene. Memory tip: a \"family quarrel\" play is nearly always set in the family's own house.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'O deadly sin! O rude unthankfulness!\nThy fault our law calls death; but the kind Prince,\nTaking thy part, hath rushed aside the law,\nAnd turned that black word...'\nDeadly sin refers to the|suicide of Juliet|suicide of Romeo|murder of Paris|murder of Tybalt": "Romeo and Juliet (about 1595) is Shakespeare's tragedy of two young lovers from the feuding Montague and Capulet families of Verona. In Act 3, Scene 3, Romeo, hiding in Friar Laurence's cell, cries that banishment is worse than death. The Friar rebukes him: his \"fault\", which the law punishes with death, is killing Tybalt, and the kind Prince has \"rushed aside the law\" and changed the \"black word\" death into banishment. So the \"deadly sin\" is the murder of Tybalt: D. The suicides (A, B) and Paris's death (C) all come later, in the tomb. Memory tip: banishment in this play always goes back to one act, Tybalt's death.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nThe play is mostly written in|blank verse|free verse|metres|foot": "Blank verse is unrhymed iambic pentameter: lines of ten syllables with five beats, da-DUM five times. It is Shakespeare's main medium, and most of Romeo and Juliet is written in it (\"But soft, what light through yonder window breaks?\"), with rhymed passages such as the sonnet-form Prologue, and prose for servants and jokes. So the play is mostly in blank verse: A. Free verse (B) has no regular metre and belongs to modern poetry; \"metres\" and \"foot\" (C, D) are the units verse is measured in, not kinds of verse. The trap: B, free verse, which students confuse with blank verse because both lack rhyme. Memory tip: blank verse is blank of rhyme but not of metre.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'O serpent heart, hid with a flowering face!'\nThe statement above refers to|Juliet|Romeo|Tybalt|Benvolio": "In Act 3, Scene 2, the Nurse tells Juliet that Romeo has killed her cousin Tybalt. In shock, Juliet pours out a string of oxymorons about her new husband: \"O serpent heart, hid with a flowering face!\", \"Beautiful tyrant! fiend angelical!\" The fair face is Romeo's; the serpent heart is the one she now fears hides behind it. So the line refers to Romeo: B. She soon takes it back and defends him. Tybalt (C) is the one who has been killed, and Juliet is the speaker, not the subject (A). Memory tip: a run of contradictions (\"fiend angelical\") is Juliet torn between love and grief.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nThe spatial setting of the play is|Athens|Verona|Padua|Venice": "Spatial setting means the place where a work's action happens (as opposed to its time). Romeo and Juliet's Prologue says it at once: \"In fair Verona, where we lay our scene.\" The feuding families live in Verona, and the only scenes outside it are in Mantua, where Romeo is banished. So the answer is Verona: B. Padua (C) is the setting of The Taming of the Shrew, Venice (D) of The Merchant of Venice and Othello, and Athens (A) of A Midsummer Night's Dream. The trap: the other Italian cities, which are settings of other Shakespeare plays. Memory tip: \"Two households... in fair Verona.\"",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nRomeo is banished to Mantua because he|kills Tybalt in a street duel|marries Juliet without parental consent|attends Capulet's party uninvited|attempts to kill Paris his rival": "In Act 3, Scene 1, Tybalt kills Romeo's friend Mercutio in a street fight. Romeo, in fury, fights Tybalt and kills him. The Prince, who had already forbidden street brawls on pain of death, spares Romeo's life but banishes him from Verona, and he flees to Mantua. So he is banished because he kills Tybalt in a street duel: A. His secret marriage to Juliet (B) is unknown to the families, his going uninvited to the Capulet feast (C) only angers Tybalt, and he kills Paris (D) only later, at the tomb. Memory tip: Mercutio dies, Tybalt dies, Romeo is banished, in that order.",
 "|Based on Buchi Emecheta's The Joys of Motherhood: \nIn the novel, the society puts high value on|egalitarianism|male ascendancy|procreation|gender equity": "The Joys of Motherhood (1979) is a novel by the Nigerian writer Buchi Emecheta. It follows Nnu Ego from her village of Ibuza to colonial Lagos, and shows a society in which a woman's worth is measured by the children she bears, above all sons. Nnu Ego's first marriage fails because she does not conceive, and she gives her whole life to bearing and raising children. So the society places high value on procreation: C. Male dominance is real in the novel, but the question asks what the society values most, and the whole story turns on childbearing. Egalitarianism and gender equity (A, D) are exactly what this society lacks. Memory tip: the ironic title points to the theme, motherhood.",
 "|Based on Buchi Emecheta's The Joys of Motherhood: \nThe medicine man links the lump discovered on the head of Nnu Ego at birth, to the|possession of physical admirable qualities that makes her an epitome of perfection|wound inflicted on the slave woman buried with Agbadi's wife|coming back of the Agunwa to the society to live again|ill-luck and tragic events attributed to a predestined fate": "At the start of The Joys of Motherhood, the chief Agbadi's senior wife, Agunwa, dies, and by custom her slave woman is to be buried with her. The slave resists and is struck on the head before she is pushed into the grave, and she vows to come back. When Agbadi's daughter Nnu Ego is born with a lump on her head, the medicine man (dibia) says the child is the slave woman returned as her chi, and the lump marks that blow. So he links it to the wound inflicted on the slave woman buried with Agbadi's wife: B. The trap: C, because it is Agunwa's funeral, but Agunwa is the mistress who died, not the one who returns. Memory tip: the lump on the head = the blow to the slave woman's head.",
 "|Based on Buchi Emecheta's The Joys of Motherhood: \nThe constant companions of Nnaife's family are|togetherness and happiness|poverty and hunger|sickness and joblessness|disagreement and humiliation": "In The Joys of Motherhood, Nnu Ego's second husband, Nnaife, works as a washerman for a white family in Lagos, and later as a soldier and labourer. His wages are small and irregular, the family keeps growing, and Nnu Ego is forever trading and scraping to feed the children; often they go hungry. So poverty and hunger are the family's constant companions: B. Togetherness and happiness (A) are what the novel ironically lacks. The trap: C, sickness and joblessness, because Nnaife is out of work at times, but the steady misery of the family is want of money and food. Memory tip: the novel's \"joys\" are ironic; its constant reality is poverty.",
 "|Based on Ferdinand Oyono's The Old Man and the Medal: \nMeka can be best described as|an egocentric old man|a simple-hearted old man|an impulsive old man|an old religious bigot": "The Old Man and the Medal (1956, English 1967) is a novel by the Cameroonian Ferdinand Oyono. Meka, an old villager, has given his land to the Catholic mission and lost two sons fighting for France, and the colonial administration rewards him with a medal. He trusts the whites, believes the medal makes him their friend, and is bitterly disillusioned when he is later beaten and jailed. This trust and naivety make him a simple-hearted old man: B. He is not selfish (A), rash (C) or a religious bigot (D), although he is a devout Christian. Memory tip: Meka's tragedy is his innocence: he takes the colonisers at their word.",
 "|Based on Ferdinand Oyono's The Old Man and the Medal: \nIn the novel, the colonialists treat the Africans with|kid gloves|disdain|indifference|honour": "In The Old Man and the Medal, the colonial whites look down on the Africans. They praise Meka with speeches and a medal, but the \"friendship\" is empty: at the reception the Africans are kept apart, and the same night Meka, lost in the rain, is arrested, beaten and jailed as a stranger. The novel shows the colonisers' contempt, so they treat the Africans with disdain: B. \"Kid gloves\" (A) means very gentle treatment, the opposite. The trap: C, indifference, but the whites do not simply ignore the Africans; they actively scorn and abuse them. Memory tip: disdain = looking down on someone; indifference = not caring either way.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nThe Ministry of Peace is concerned with making|instruments|weapons|wars|reconciliation": "Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949) is George Orwell's novel of Oceania, a state ruled by the Party and Big Brother. Its four ministries are named in \"doublethink\", the opposite of what they do: the Ministry of Truth makes propaganda and lies, the Ministry of Love tortures, the Ministry of Plenty rations scarcity, and the Ministry of Peace (Minipax) runs the endless war. So the Ministry of Peace is concerned with making wars: C. Reconciliation (D) is what its name suggests, which is exactly the trick. Memory tip: in Oceania, every ministry's name is the opposite of its job.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nThe subject matter of the novel is|totalitarian dictatorship|exploitation and cruelty|retributive justice|class segregation": "The subject matter of a work is what it is mainly about. Nineteen Eighty-Four shows Oceania under the Party and Big Brother: telescreens watch everyone, the Thought Police punish even private thoughts, history is rewritten and language is cut down to make rebellion unthinkable. Winston Smith's failed rebellion shows how total that control is. So the subject matter is totalitarian dictatorship: A. Cruelty (B) and class division (D) appear in the novel, but they are parts of the larger picture of total state control. Memory tip: Big Brother is watching you = totalitarianism, the state controls everything.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nHow did Winston start his rebellion against the state?|By engaging in anti-party activities|By keeping a private diary|When he started a secret affair|When he spied on the party": "Winston Smith works at the Ministry of Truth rewriting old newspapers. His rebellion begins quietly: in the novel's first chapter he buys an old notebook and, out of sight of the telescreen, begins a private diary, in which he writes \"DOWN WITH BIG BROTHER\". Keeping a diary is itself a \"thoughtcrime\" in Oceania. So he starts his rebellion by keeping a private diary: B. His secret affair with Julia (C) and his approach to O'Brien to join the Brotherhood (A) come later. Memory tip: Winston's rebellion grows step by step: diary, then Julia, then O'Brien.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nThe party seeks power for|the nation|its own sake|its members|peoples' sake": "In Part Three of Nineteen Eighty-Four, O'Brien, while torturing Winston, explains the Party's real aim: \"The Party seeks power entirely for its own sake. We are not interested in the good of others; we are interested solely in power.\" So the Party seeks power for its own sake: B. It does not seek it for the nation, its members or the people (A, C, D); that is the claim other tyrants make, and O'Brien scorns it. Memory tip: O'Brien's line, \"Power is not a means; it is an end.\"",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nRubadiri's 'An African Thunderstorm' says that during the thunderstorm in the village|women cook their food|children play in the rain|children are delighted while women move in and out|both women and children are delighted": "David Rubadiri, a Malawian poet, describes a storm sweeping into an African village. Clouds come \"like a plague of locusts\", the wind \"whistles by\", and in the village there are \"Screams of delighted children\" while \"Women, / Babies clinging on their backs, / Dart about / In and out / Madly\". So the children are delighted while the women move in and out: C. The trap: D, both women and children are delighted; the women are not enjoying the storm but rushing about anxiously. The poem does not show women cooking (A). Memory tip: children scream with delight; women dart about madly.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \n'Yet in their finger upon\nOur navel\nThe midwives of the spirit say\nThey feel a foetal throb.'\nThe dominant literary device used in the extract above from Acquah's 'In the Navel of the Soul' is|epigram|allegory|enjambment|rhythm": "Kobena Eyi Acquah is a Ghanaian lawyer and poet. In \"In the Navel of the Soul\" the lines \"Yet in their finger upon / Our navel / The midwives of the spirit say / They feel a foetal throb\" form one sentence that runs on over four lines, with no pause at the end of the first lines (\"upon / Our navel\"). Running the sense of a line on into the next is enjambment: C. The passage is not a short witty saying (epigram, A) or a sustained symbolic story (allegory, B), and rhythm (D) is in every poem rather than a special device here. Memory tip: enjambment = the line \"strides over\" into the next; end-stopped lines end with a pause.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe predominant device in Launko's 'End of the War' is|onomatopoeia|antithesis|oxymoron|paradox": "Okinba Launko is a pen name of the Nigerian writer Femi Osofisan. \"End of the War\" looks at what \"peace\" really means after a war, and it does so through statements that seem to contradict themselves yet carry a hard truth, such as that the war has ended because war is now with us: the fighting has stopped, but its suffering has entered everyday life. A seemingly self-contradictory statement that holds a truth is a paradox: D. The trap: B, antithesis, which only sets opposite ideas side by side in balance without the contradiction. Memory tip: paradox = \"this cannot be true... yet it is.\"",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe theme of the poem 'Give Me the Minstrel's Seat' centres on|divorce|fortune|marriage|companionship": "\"Give Me the Minstrel's Seat\" is a traditional Swahili poem from East Africa whose author is unknown. Its persona celebrates friendship and company: the pleasure of being among friends is what helps people bear life's troubles, and a life without companions is poor and lonely. So the theme centres on companionship: D. The poem is not about divorce, money or marriage (A, B, C). Memory tip: a \"seat\" among minstrels and friends = companionship.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe poet persona in Marvell's 'To His Coy Mistress' is willing to praise the lady's eyes for|thirty thousand years|six decades|two centuries|a century": "Andrew Marvell's \"To His Coy Mistress\" (17th century) is a persuasion poem: the speaker tells a shy lady that if they had all the time in the world he would court her slowly: \"An hundred years should go to praise / Thine eyes, and on thy forehead gaze; / Two hundred to adore each breast, / But thirty thousand to the rest.\" So her eyes would get a hundred years, a century: D. The trap: A, thirty thousand years, which is in the same passage but goes to \"the rest\", and C, two centuries, which is for each breast. Memory tip: 100 for the eyes, 200 for each breast, 30,000 for the rest.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Lawrence's 'Bat', the poet persona mistakes the bats for|owls|swallows|pipistrello|sparrows": "In D.H. Lawrence's \"Bat\" (1923), the speaker sits at sunset in Florence watching dark shapes flit over the river Arno. At first he takes them for swallows (\"Swallows with spools of dark thread sewing the shadows together\"), then realises with disgust: \"Never swallows! / Bats!\" So he mistakes the bats for swallows: B. The trap: C, pipistrello, which is the Italian word for a bat that the poem uses, so it names what they really are, not the mistake. Memory tip: first swallows, then the shudder: \"Bats!\"",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Eliot's 'Journey of the Magi', the magi are aided on their journey by|donkeys|horses|camels|chariots": "T.S. Eliot's \"Journey of the Magi\" (1927) is told by one of the wise men, looking back on the hard journey to Bethlehem. The travellers ride camels, and the speaker remembers their misery: \"And the camels galled, sore-footed, refractory, / Lying down in the melting snow\", and \"the camel men cursing and grumbling\". So the Magi are aided by camels: C. A white horse appears later, galloping away in a meadow, but it is not their mount (B). Memory tip: \"the camels galled\" is in the poem's first lines.",
 "|A play which mainly aims at provoking excessive laughter is called|tragi-comedy|comedy|a farce|satire": "A farce is a kind of comedy that aims above all at loud laughter, through absurd situations, mistaken identity, slapstick and exaggerated characters, with little concern for real life. So a play that mainly aims at provoking excessive laughter is a farce: C. The trap: B, comedy, which also makes us laugh but has a fuller plot and characters and usually some comment on life; farce is comedy pushed to the extreme. Tragi-comedy (A) mixes serious and comic, and satire (D) uses laughter to attack folly. Memory tip: comedy smiles; farce roars.",
 "|Both comedy and tragedy have|happy ending|climax|tragic hero|stanza": "Every well-made play, comic or tragic, has a plot that rises to a climax, the point of greatest tension or the turning point, before the falling action and resolution. So comedy and tragedy both have a climax: B. A happy ending (A) belongs to comedy; a tragic hero (C) to tragedy; a stanza (D) is a unit of a poem, not of a play. Memory tip: exposition, rising action, CLIMAX, falling action, resolution: every plot has them.",
 "|A formal dignified speech or writing praising a person or a thing for past or present deeds is|premiere|eulogy|anthology|lampoon": "A eulogy is a formal speech or piece of writing that praises a person or thing, often someone who has just died, as at a funeral. So a dignified speech praising someone's past or present deeds is a eulogy: B. A premiere (A) is a first performance, an anthology (C) is a collection of writings, and a lampoon (D) is the opposite of a eulogy: a piece that mocks and ridicules a person. Memory tip: \"eu-\" means good (as in euphony); a eulogy speaks well of someone.",
 "|The narrative style in which the hero tells his own story directly is the|objective|subjective|first-person|third-person": "In first-person narration the story is told by a character in it, using \"I\": the hero tells his own story directly, as Pip does in Dickens's Great Expectations. So the answer is first-person: C. In third-person narration (D) an outside narrator says \"he\" and \"she\". \"Objective\" and \"subjective\" (A, B) describe how much a narrator reveals of feelings, not who tells the story. Memory tip: \"I\" telling my own story = first person.",
 "|The physical, historical or cultural background of a literary work is referred to as|episode|plot|time|setting": "Setting is the background against which a work's action happens: the place, the time, and the social, historical or cultural surroundings. So the physical, historical or cultural background of a work is its setting: D. Time (C) is only one part of setting. The plot (B) is the arranged series of events, and an episode (A) is one incident in it. The trap: C, time, which is part of setting but not the whole of it. Memory tip: setting = where + when + what kind of world.",
 "|A plot structure that defies chronology can be described as|open-ended|circular|episodic|organic": "An episodic plot is made of a loose chain of incidents or episodes, each more or less complete in itself, rather than a tight sequence in which each event follows from the one before in strict time order, as in many picaresque novels. So a plot that defies chronology can be described as episodic: C. An organic plot (D) is the opposite: tightly unified, with each part growing out of the last. A circular plot (B) ends where it began, and an open-ended one (A) leaves the outcome unresolved. Memory tip: episodic = a string of episodes, not a straight line.",
 "|Pun as a literary device deals with|placing two opposite phrases|placing words side by side|playing on words|arrangement of words": "A pun is a play on words: it uses a word with two meanings, or two words that sound alike, for humour or effect. Mercutio, dying in Romeo and Juliet, says \"ask for me tomorrow, and you shall find me a grave man\": grave means both serious and in the grave. So a pun deals with playing on words: C. Placing opposite phrases together (A) is antithesis, and arrangement of words (D) is syntax or word order. Memory tip: pun = one word, two meanings.",
 "|In a narrative poem, the poet attempts to|summarize a story|describe a place|preach a sermon|tell a story": "A narrative poem is a poem that tells a story, with characters, events and a plot. Ballads (such as \"The Rime of the Ancient Mariner\") and epics (such as Homer's Iliad) are narrative poems. So in a narrative poem the poet attempts to tell a story: D. The trap: A, summarize a story, which sounds close, but a narrative poem tells the story fully in its own right, not as a summary. Describing a place (B) belongs to descriptive poetry and preaching (C) to didactic verse. Memory tip: narrate = tell a story; narrative poem = story poem.",
 "|The account of experiences of an individual during the course of a journey is known as|a travelogue|an autobiography|a catalogue|a memoir": "A travelogue is a piece of writing (or a film or talk) describing a person's experiences while travelling: the places, people and adventures met on a journey. So the account of an individual's experiences during a journey is a travelogue: A. An autobiography (B) is the story of the writer's whole life, and a memoir (D) recalls particular memories or periods of one's life; neither is defined by a journey. A catalogue (C) is a list. The trap: D, memoir, because it also records personal experience. Memory tip: travel + log = travelogue.",
 "|Satirical writing employs|epigram|synecdoche|irony|onomatopoeia": "Satire ridicules people, institutions or society in order to expose and correct their faults. Its chief tool is irony, saying one thing while meaning another or showing the gap between appearance and reality, as when Orwell names the war ministry the Ministry of Peace. So satirical writing employs irony: C. Satire may use a witty epigram (A) now and then, but irony is its essential device. Synecdoche and onomatopoeia (B, D) are general figures of speech with no special link to satire. Memory tip: satire's sharpest weapon is irony.",
 "|'Basha: You dumb skull of a bone head . . . you will face court martial for this. You look everywhere? You search inside toilet bowl?'\nWole Soyinka, King Baabu\n\nThe person being addressed above is a|soldier|student|domestic servant|lawyer": "Wole Soyinka's King Baabu (2001) is a satire on military dictatorship in Africa: General Basha Bash seizes power and turns himself into King Baabu. In this extract Basha abuses someone for a failed search and threatens: \"you will face court martial for this.\" A court martial is a military court that tries members of the armed forces, so the person addressed is a soldier: A. A student, domestic servant or lawyer (B, C, D) could not be court-martialled. Memory tip: court martial = trial of a soldier (martial = military, from Mars, the god of war).",
 "|'Basha: You dumb skull of a bone head . . . you will face court martial for this. You look everywhere? You search inside toilet bowl?'\nWole Soyinka, King Baabu\n\nFrom the tone of the speech above, the speaker is obviously|enraged|lackadaisical|elated|happy": "Tone is the speaker's attitude, shown by word choice. Basha's words are full of insults (\"You dumb skull of a bone head\"), a threat (\"you will face court martial for this\") and contemptuous questions (\"You search inside toilet bowl?\"). These show a man in a rage, so he is enraged: A. Lackadaisical (B) means lazy and careless, the opposite of this fury, and elated or happy (C, D) do not fit abuse and threats. Memory tip: insults + threats + angry questions = an enraged tone.",
 "|'That year the harvest was sad, like a funeral, and many farmers wept as they dug up the miserable yams. One man tied his cloth to a tree branch and hanged himself.'\nChinua Achebe, Things Fall Apart\n\nThe mood conveyed in the excerpt above is one of|sadness|frustration|sympathy|dilemma": "Things Fall Apart (1958) is Chinua Achebe's novel of Okonkwo and Igbo village life. In this passage from the year of drought and floods, \"the harvest was sad, like a funeral\", farmers \"wept\" and one man \"hanged himself\". The simile of a funeral, the weeping and the suicide all create a mood of sadness: A. The trap: B, frustration; the farmers' efforts have been wasted, but the words Achebe chooses (funeral, wept) are of grief rather than of irritation at being blocked. Sympathy (C) is what the reader may feel, not the mood of the passage. Memory tip: mood = the feeling the words create; \"funeral\" and \"wept\" = sadness.",
 "|'That age is best which is the first,\nWhen youth and blood are warmer;\nBut being spent, the worse, and worst\nTimes still succeed the former.'\n\nThe rhyme scheme in the excerpt above is|bbaa|aabb|abab|abba": "To find a rhyme scheme, give each new end sound a letter. In these lines from Robert Herrick's \"To the Virgins, to Make Much of Time\": \"first\" (a), \"warmer\" (b), \"worst\" (a, rhyming with first), \"former\" (b, rhyming with warmer). The rhymes alternate, so the scheme is abab: C. The trap: D, abba, which students choose when they misread \"former\" as a new sound. Memory tip: always write the letters beside each line end before choosing.",
 "|'But the towering earth was tired of sitting in one position. She moved, suddenly, and the houses crumbled, the mountains heaved horribly, and the work of a million years was lost.'\n\nThe subject matter of the extract above is|storm|sea waves|house movement|earthquake": "The subject matter is what a passage is about. Here \"the towering earth\" is personified as tired of sitting still; she \"moved, suddenly\", houses crumbled, mountains \"heaved horribly\" and \"the work of a million years was lost\". The ground itself shaking and moving is an earthquake: D. A storm (A) or sea waves (B) would involve wind and water, which the passage does not mention, and \"house movement\" (C) is only one result of the event. Memory tip: when the earth itself moves, the subject is an earthquake.",
 "|'And your laughter like a flame piercing the shadows\nHas revealed Africa to me beyond the snow of yesterday.'\n\nFrom the poem above, shadow means|famine|bleak future|period of sufferings|abstract ideas": "In these lines the speaker says someone's laughter, \"like a flame piercing the shadows\", has revealed Africa to him \"beyond the snow of yesterday\". Light (the flame) breaking through darkness (the shadows) is a familiar image of hope ending a dark time. The shadows stand for the gloomy past the speaker and Africa have lived through: a period of suffering: C. The trap: B, bleak future; the shadows are being pierced now and \"yesterday\" points to the past, not the future. Memory tip: in poems, light piercing darkness usually means hope breaking through past suffering.",
 "|'Don't panic. Be calm, if you are somehow upset ...try to regain your composure.'\n\nThe speaker in the excerpt above is|hopeless|uncertain|afraid|confident": "The speaker gives short, firm commands to someone else: \"Don't panic. Be calm... try to regain your composure.\" Someone who is calm enough to steady another person is in control of himself, so the speaker is confident: D. The words \"if you are somehow upset\" express doubt only about how the listener feels, not about the speaker's own state, so \"uncertain\" (B) does not fit. Hopeless or afraid (A, C) describe the person who might panic, not the one reassuring him. Memory tip: judge the speaker by how he speaks; calm commands show confidence.",
 "|'Move him into the sun—\nGently its touch awoke him once,\nAt home, whispering of fields half-sown.\nAlways it woke him, even in France,\nUntil this morning and this snow.\nIf anything might rouse him now\nThe kind old sun will know.\n\nThink how it wakes the seeds—\nWoke, once, the clays of a cold star.\nAre limbs, so dear-achieved, are sides\nFull-nerved, still warm, too hard to stir?\nWas it for this the clay grew tall?\nO what made fatuous sunbeams toil\nTo break earth's sleep at all?'\n\nThe poem can be described as|a lyric|an epic|a sonnet|an elegy": "This is \"Futility\" by Wilfred Owen (1893-1918), the English poet of the First World War. Soldiers move a dead comrade into the sun, hoping it will wake him as it used to \"At home\"; the speaker then asks why life was ever created if it ends so. A poem of mourning for the dead is an elegy, so the poem is best described as an elegy: D. It has fourteen lines, so some take it for a sonnet (C), but its two seven-line stanzas do not follow a sonnet's pattern; this is an arguable item, and \"elegy\" names what the poem does. All short poems of feeling are lyrics (A), but that is too general. Memory tip: elegy = poem of mourning, like Owen's lament for the soldier.",
 "|'Move him into the sun—\nGently its touch awoke him once,\nAt home, whispering of fields half-sown.\nAlways it woke him, even in France,\nUntil this morning and this snow.\nIf anything might rouse him now\nThe kind old sun will know.\n\nThink how it wakes the seeds—\nWoke, once, the clays of a cold star.\nAre limbs, so dear-achieved, are sides\nFull-nerved, still warm, too hard to stir?\nWas it for this the clay grew tall?\nO what made fatuous sunbeams toil\nTo break earth's sleep at all?'\n\nThe theme of the poem is|futility of life|distortion of life|creation of life|vanity of life": "In \"Futility\", Wilfred Owen's soldiers move a dead comrade into the sun that \"woke him even in France\", but now it cannot. The speaker remembers that the sun once woke \"the clays of a cold star\" into life, and asks bitterly: \"Was it for this the clay grew tall? / O what made fatuous sunbeams toil / To break earth's sleep at all?\" If life ends like this, creating it seems pointless. So the theme is the futility of life: A, as the title says. The trap: D, vanity of life, which sounds similar but means pride or emptiness of worldly things, not the pointlessness of creation that Owen laments. Memory tip: the title often names the theme: \"Futility\".",
 "|'A cursing rogue with a merry face,\nA bundle of rags upon a crutch,\nStumbled upon that windy place\nCalled Cruachan, and it was as much...'\n\nThe rhyme scheme of the stanza above is|aabb|abab|bbaa|abba": "These lines open W.B. Yeats's \"The Hour Before Dawn\". Mark each line end with a letter: \"face\" (a), \"crutch\" (b), \"place\" (a, rhyming with face), \"much\" (b, rhyming with crutch). The rhymes alternate, so the scheme is abab: B. Note the \"merry face\" of a \"cursing rogue\": the poem enjoys such contrasts. The trap: D, abba, which comes from reading the rhyme pairs in the wrong order. Memory tip: alternate rhyme (abab) is the commonest four-line pattern; check by writing letters at the line ends.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nFrom its resolution of conflicts, the play can be described as a|tragedy|comedy|farce|melodrama": "Sons and Daughters is a play by the Ghanaian dramatist Joe (J.C.) de Graft about a well-to-do father, James Ofosu, who wants to choose professions for his children, while Aaron wants to paint and Maanan wants to dance. A play is classed partly by how its conflict is resolved. A tragedy ends in death or ruin; a comedy ends happily, with the conflict settled and the family at peace. At the end James gives in and lets his children follow their own talents, so the play is a comedy: B. A farce depends on wild, improbable situations and slapstick, and a melodrama on exaggerated villains and sensational events, which this thoughtful family drama does not have. Memory tip: happy resolution = comedy; ruinous end = tragedy.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nThe final harassment of Maanan takes place in|Ofosu's office|Lawyer Bonu's house|Lawyer Bonu's chamber|Ofosu's house": "In Sons and Daughters, Lawyer Bonu, a married man old enough to be Maanan's father, pursues her while she works in his office, promising to win her father round if she yields. His last and boldest attempt comes in James Ofosu's own house: Bonu finds Maanan alone, blocks her way and tries to kiss and fondle her; she bites him, and James comes down from upstairs and sees what is happening. After this, Bonu is no longer welcome in the house. So the final harassment takes place in Ofosu's house: D. The earlier advances happened at Bonu's workplace, which is what options B and C point to. Memory tip: the last harassment happens where the father can finally see it with his own eyes.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \n'Everything in this room outrages my sense of beauty, undermines my will to create pictures of lasting appeal...' The speaker in the quotation above is|happy|frustrated|excited|tired": "In Sons and Daughters, Aaron is the son who wants to be a painter, while his father insists on a \"respectable\" profession. The speaker here talks of creating \"pictures of lasting appeal\", so he is the artist, and every word shows his state of mind: the room \"outrages\" his sense of beauty and \"undermines\" his will to paint. Someone whose desire to create is blocked by his surroundings is frustrated: B. Nothing in the words suggests happiness or excitement, and \"tired\" (D) describes the body, while his complaint is about a will that is being crushed. Memory tip: to name a speaker's mood, look at the verbs: \"outrages\", \"undermines\" = frustration.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'Farewell! God knows when we shall meet again.\nI have a faint cold fear thrills through my veins,\nThat almost freezes up the heat of life:\nI'll call them back again to comfort me:\nNurse! What should she do here?\nMy dismal scene I needs must act alone.\nCome, vial.'\nThe intention of the speaker above is to|commit suicide|take a temporary harmful substance|commit murder|escape from harsh realities of life": "Romeo and Juliet (about 1595) is Shakespeare's tragedy of two young lovers from the feuding Montague and Capulet families of Verona. To escape a forced marriage to Paris, Juliet agrees to Friar Laurence's plan: she will drink a potion that makes her seem dead for forty-two hours, and Romeo will come to take her away when she wakes in the tomb. In this soliloquy (Act 4, Scene 3) she sends the Nurse away, fights her fear and says \"Come, vial\": she means to take the potion, a substance whose effect is meant to be temporary: B. She is not yet planning to kill herself (A); that comes later, when she wakes to find Romeo dead. Memory tip: the \"vial\" is the sleeping potion; the dagger comes at the end.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nThe play reaches the point of denouement|at the family feast|when Romeo kills Paris at the tomb|at the reconciliation of the feuding families|when Romeo is informed of Juliet's death": "The denouement (French for \"untying\") is the final part of a plot, after the climax, when the knots are untied and the story settles. In Romeo and Juliet the climax is the deaths of the lovers in the tomb. Afterwards the Prince, the Friar and the two families gather; Capulet and Montague, seeing what their hatred has cost, take hands and promise golden statues of each other's child. This reconciliation of the feuding families is the denouement: C. The feast (A) is early in the play, and Romeo hearing of Juliet's \"death\" (D) and killing Paris (B) lead up to the climax. Memory tip: denouement = the knot untied, after the climax.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nThe news of Juliet's death is broken to Romeo in Mantua by|Balthasar|Friar Lawrence|a boy|Friar John": "In Romeo and Juliet, Romeo is banished to Mantua after killing Tybalt. Friar Laurence sends Friar John with a letter explaining that Juliet is only drugged, but plague keeps Friar John from leaving Verona. Meanwhile Romeo's servant Balthasar sees Juliet laid in the Capulet tomb and rides to Mantua to tell him she is dead: A. Romeo then buys poison and returns to die beside her. The trap: Friar John (D) is the man who should have brought Romeo the true news, but he never reached Mantua. Memory tip: Balthasar brings the false news fast; Friar John's true news never arrives.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nIn the play, Mercutio can be described as|fraudulent|quarrelsome|gentle|kind-hearted": "In Romeo and Juliet, Mercutio is Romeo's witty friend, a kinsman of the Prince. He is full of jokes and wordplay, but also hot-tempered and ready to fight; he even accuses Benvolio of quarrelling over trifles, a charge that fits himself better. When Romeo refuses to fight Tybalt, Mercutio draws his sword and fights Tybalt himself, and is killed, cursing \"A plague o' both your houses!\" So he is best described as quarrelsome: B. He is not a cheat (A), and \"gentle\" (C) does not fit his fiery temper. Memory tip: Mercutio = mercurial, quick in wit and quick to fight.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nThe plot of the play is|simple|complicated|convoluted|chronological": "A chronological (linear) plot tells events in the order in which they happen, with no flashbacks or jumps in time. Romeo and Juliet moves straight forward over about four days: the street brawl, the Capulet feast, the balcony scene, the secret marriage, Tybalt's death and Romeo's banishment, the potion, and the deaths in the tomb. Its plot is therefore chronological: D. It is not convoluted (C) or especially complicated (B): there are no subplots that need untangling. Memory tip: if the story never goes back in time, the plot is chronological.",
 "|Based on Ferdinand Oyono's The Old Man and the Medal: \nFather Vandermayer's attitude and action toward Meka illustrate the church's|despondency|suspicion|infuriation|hypocrisy": "The Old Man and the Medal (1956) by the Cameroonian writer Ferdinand Oyono is a satire of French colonial rule. Meka, an old villager, gave his land to the Catholic Church and lost his two sons in the colonial war, and is to be rewarded with a medal. Father Vandermayer, the priest, praises Meka as a model Christian because of his gifts, yet the Church gives him nothing in return and treats Africans with contempt; it preaches love and brotherhood but does not practise them. That gap between words and deeds is hypocrisy: D. Memory tip: in colonial satires, \"they preach one thing and do another\" usually points to hypocrisy.",
 "|Based on Buchi Emecheta's The Joys of Motherhood: \nIn the novel, Nwokocha Agbadi is famous for his oratorical powers and|height|treachery|illiteracy|wealth": "The Joys of Motherhood (1979) by the Nigerian novelist Buchi Emecheta follows Nnu Ego, who lives for her children but finds motherhood brings more sorrow than joy. The novel opens with the story of her parents. Her father, Nwokocha Agbadi, is a very wealthy local chief, a great wrestler, and known for his gifted oratory; he has many wives but spends years pursuing the proud Ona, Nnu Ego's mother. So he is famous for his oratorical powers and his wealth: D. Nothing in the novel makes him treacherous or ignorant. Memory tip: Agbadi is the traditional \"big man\": rich, strong and a fine speaker.",
 "|Based on Buchi Emecheta's The Joys of Motherhood: \nIn the novel, the handing over of a baby boy in a dream to Nnu Ego by her personal god signifies|reincarnation|future blessing|idol worship|doom": "In The Joys of Motherhood, a chi is a person's personal god in Igbo belief. Nnu Ego's chi is the spirit of a slave woman buried alive with Agbadi's senior wife. Married to Nnaife in Lagos and longing for a child, Nnu Ego dreams that her chi hands her a baby boy. Soon afterwards she bears her first son, Ngozi. The dream foretells that a child is coming to her, so it signifies future blessing: B. The novel makes the blessing bitter, since Ngozi dies young, but what the gift in the dream points to is the child. The dream is not about reincarnation (A) or idol worship (C). Memory tip: in this novel, the chi's gift of a baby always comes before a birth.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nThe novel draws a picture of|a useless past|a totalitarian future|an unstable moment|a peaceful atmosphere": "Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949) by the English writer George Orwell is a dystopian novel set in Airstrip One (Britain) in the year 1984, a future seen from the 1940s. The Party, led by Big Brother, controls every part of life: telescreens watch people, the Thought Police punish forbidden thoughts and history is rewritten. Orwell draws a picture of a totalitarian future: B. It is a warning about where dictatorship could lead, not a description of the past (A) or a peaceful society (D). Memory tip: dystopia = a nightmare future society; Orwell's is totalitarian.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nThe power and oppression of an irresistible evil debased Winston's dreams of|freedom and democracy|internal security|wealth and capitalism|sovereignty": "In Nineteen Eighty-Four, Winston Smith works at the Ministry of Truth rewriting history, but he secretly hates the Party. He dreams of a free life: writing his own thoughts in a diary, loving Julia in private, and joining the Brotherhood to overthrow Big Brother. The Party's crushing power, through the Thought Police and O'Brien's torture, destroys those hopes; by the end he has given up his freedom of thought. His debased dreams were of freedom and democracy: A. Memory tip: what a totalitarian state destroys first is freedom.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nRoom 101 symbolizes a place of|rest|fun|humiliation|torture": "In Nineteen Eighty-Four, Room 101 is the cell in the Ministry of Love where prisoners face \"the worst thing in the world\", whatever each person fears most. For Winston it is a cage of rats about to be fastened to his face; terrified, he begs them to \"Do it to Julia!\", betraying the one person he loved. The room stands for torture, the final weapon the Party uses to break the mind: D. Humiliation (C) is part of what Winston suffers, but the room exists to torture. Memory tip: Room 101 = your worst fear, used as torture.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nThe novel can be described as|optimistic|antagonistic|persuasive|pessimistic": "A pessimistic work expects the worst and offers no hope. Nineteen Eighty-Four ends with Winston released, broken and empty, sitting in the Chestnut Tree Café; the last line says \"He loved Big Brother.\" The Party has won completely and no rebellion succeeds. So the novel is pessimistic: D. It is not optimistic (A), and although it warns readers, \"persuasive\" (C) does not describe its outlook. Memory tip: a hero who loses everything, with no hope left = pessimistic.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Naked Soles, Adeoti writes that the carnival of naked soles dances through|scorching sun|a dirty room|blooming thorns|a cloudy atmosphere": "Naked Soles is a poem by the Nigerian poet and scholar Gbemisola Adeoti, from the collection of the same name (2005). The \"carnival of naked soles\" is a crowd of barefoot people, the poor, who still dance and carry on with life. They dance \"through blooming thorns\": C. The image joins beauty and pain: \"blooming\" suggests flowers and life, but thorns pierce bare feet, showing suffering endured with courage. Memory tip: naked soles + thorns = the pain of the poor, borne while they still dance.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Rubadiri's An African Thunderstorm, the thunderstorm begins with|rain from the west|clouds from the east|rain from the east|clouds from the west": "An African Thunderstorm is a poem by the Malawian poet David Rubadiri describing a storm sweeping over an African village. It opens: \"From the west / Clouds come hurrying with the wind / Turning / Sharply / Here and there / Like a plague of locusts\". So the storm begins with clouds from the west: D. The rain comes only later, when the storm breaks on the village. The trap: B, \"clouds from the east\", has the right thing in the wrong direction. Memory tip: Rubadiri's storm starts in the west, with clouds \"like a plague of locusts\".",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe theme of Acquah's In the Navel of the Soul is|the conflict of traditions|ensuring that traditions and beliefs were strictly observed|the futility of man and his tradition|the strength in diversity of culture and traditional views": "In the Navel of the Soul is a poem by the Ghanaian poet Kobena Eyi Acquah. It sets two ways of knowing against each other: the \"experts\" find the urine test negative, but \"the midwives of the spirit\", feeling with their fingers upon the navel, say they sense new life. Modern, scientific ways clash with old, traditional, intuitive ways, so the poem's theme is a conflict of traditions (often called a conflict of cultures): A. The poem does not insist that traditions be strictly kept (B) or call them futile (C). Memory tip: lab test versus midwife's touch = modern against traditional.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \n'Woman cannot exist except by man, what is there in that to vex some of them so?' The statement above from the poem Give Me the Minstrel's Seat exemplifies|litotes|rhetorical question|transferred epithet|synecdoche": "Give Me the Minstrel's Seat is a traditional Swahili poem. A rhetorical question is asked for effect, to make a point, not to get an answer. In \"Woman cannot exist except by man, what is there in that to vex some of them so?\" the speaker does not expect a reply; the question suggests that the point is so obvious that nobody should be upset by it. That is a rhetorical question: B. Litotes (A) is understatement by denying the opposite (\"not bad\"), and synecdoche (D) is a part standing for the whole. Memory tip: a question that already holds its own answer is rhetorical.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nMarvell, in To His Coy Mistress, uses the imagery of death to|appreciate God's power|underscore life's transience|condemn the lady|scare the lady": "To His Coy Mistress is a famous carpe diem (\"seize the day\") poem by the English poet Andrew Marvell (1621–1678). The speaker tells his shy lady that if they had all the time in the world her coyness would be fine, but \"at my back I always hear / Time's winged chariot hurrying near\". Then come images of death: her beauty will not be found in the \"marble vault\", and \"worms shall try / That long-preserved virginity\". These images underscore how short life is, so that she will love him now: B. He is not praising God or condemning her. Memory tip: carpe diem poems use death to say \"life is short, act now\".",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nTo sustain the interest of readers, Lawrence in Bat uses|elision|hyperbole|suspense|oxymoron": "Bat is a poem by the English writer D.H. Lawrence, set at dusk in Florence. The speaker watches dark creatures flitting over the River Arno and takes them for swallows, describing their jerky flight in detail; only later does he realise the truth: \"Never swallows! / Bats!\" By holding back what the creatures really are, Lawrence keeps the reader curious, which is suspense: C. Hyperbole (B) is exaggeration, elision (A) is leaving out a sound, and oxymoron (D) joins two opposite words. Memory tip: a poem that keeps you guessing what something is uses suspense.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \n'With a running stream and a water-mill beating the darkness,\nAnd three trees on the low sky.'\nIn the excerpt above from Eliot's Journey of the Magi, the dominant literary device is|oxymoron|personification|hyperbole|alliteration": "Journey of the Magi (1927) is T.S. Eliot's poem in which one of the Wise Men looks back on the hard journey to the birth of Christ. On reaching a valley at dawn they see \"a running stream and a water-mill beating the darkness, / And three trees on the low sky\". The water-mill is given the action of a living being, \"beating\" the darkness as if it were striking an enemy: personification: B. The phrase has no contradiction (oxymoron) and no exaggeration (hyperbole). Memory tip: a machine or object that acts like a person signals personification.",
 "|The large space above the proscenium in a theatre from which the scenes are controlled is called|aside|setting|anachronism|flies": "In a proscenium theatre the stage is framed by the proscenium arch. High above the stage, hidden from the audience, is the space called the flies (or fly loft, fly tower), from which scenery, curtains and lights are \"flown\" in and out on ropes: D. An aside (A) is a speech meant for the audience only; setting (B) is the time and place of the action; an anachronism (C) is something out of its proper time. Memory tip: scenery \"flies\" up into the flies.",
 "|'Good warriors make others come to them and not go to others... When you induce opponents to come to you, then their force is always empty, like attacking emptiness with fullness, like throwing stones on eggs.'\nZhang Yu: The Art of War\n\nThe theme of the passage above is|folly of soldiers|inspiration|spurring people to action|war": "The theme of a passage is the idea it is about, not just its general subject. This passage, quoted from a commentary on Sun Tzu's ancient Chinese text The Art of War, is about war, but its point is how a good warrior moves others: he \"makes others come to him\" and \"induces opponents to come\", so that they act on his terms and their force is wasted. The idea is getting people to act, spurring them to action: C. \"War\" (D) is the broad subject rather than the theme of these lines. Memory tip: subject = what it is about; theme = what it says about it.",
 "|The repetition of single words or phrases at the beginning of lines is|assonance|parallelism|onomatopoeia|alliteration": "Repeating the same word or phrase at the start of successive lines or clauses is called anaphora, as in Martin Luther King's \"I have a dream... I have a dream\". This repetition gives the lines the same shape, a parallel structure, so among the options the device is parallelism: B. Alliteration (D) repeats only the first consonant sounds of nearby words, assonance (A) repeats vowel sounds, and onomatopoeia (C) is words that sound like what they mean. The trap: D, alliteration, also works at the start of words, but it repeats sounds, not whole words or phrases. Memory tip: anaphora = same words at the start = parallel lines.",
 "|A ballad is meant to be|acted|sung|discussed|read": "A ballad is a short poem that tells a story in simple, songlike stanzas, often quatrains with a refrain. Folk ballads were passed down orally and sung, and even literary ballads keep that musical form. So a ballad is meant to be sung: B. Plays are acted (A); ballads may be read today (D), but that is not what they were made for. Memory tip: ballad comes from the same root as \"ballet\" and old words for dancing and song.",
 "|In drama, a dramaturge is he who|writes or edits plays|features in a play|directs a play|acts a film": "A dramaturge (or dramaturg) is a person who writes plays, or a theatre's literary adviser who chooses, adapts and edits plays for production. So in drama the dramaturge writes or edits plays: A. The director (C) guides the actors and production; actors (B) perform in it. The trap: C, because the dramaturge works closely with the director, but directing is a different job. Memory tip: dramaturge = drama + work on the script.",
 "|A travelogue is a work of art written|by a famous playwright|before the death of the author|by an unpopular novelist|on a journey": "A travelogue is a piece of travel writing: a book, article, talk or film describing the places, people and experiences met on a journey: D. It can be written by anyone, famous or not, so A and C are wrong, and B has nothing to do with the form. Memory tip: travel + logue (account) = an account of a journey.",
 "|Plays are basically meant to|change the world|keep people out of trouble|be ready for pleasure|be presented on stage": "Drama differs from other genres because it is written to be performed: actors take the roles, speak the dialogue and follow the stage directions before an audience. Even a play read at home is best imagined on a stage. So plays are basically meant to be presented on stage: D. A play may entertain or try to change minds, but those are purposes some plays have, not what makes a play a play. Memory tip: drama comes from a Greek word for \"action\": it is meant to be acted.",
 "|A character who re-enacts familiar experiences that readers easily identify with is a|round character|flat character|stock character|static character": "A stock character is a familiar type that turns up again and again in stories: the wicked stepmother, the boastful soldier, the wise old man, the trickster. Readers recognise such figures at once because they act out familiar experiences: C. A round character (A) is complex and surprising; a static character (D) does not change. The trap: B, a flat character, is also simple, but \"flat\" describes a character built on one trait; \"stock\" means a recognisable type borrowed from tradition. Memory tip: stock = taken from the common stock of familiar types.",
 "|The plot of a story generally refers to the|intrigue made by a character against the hero|way the writer ends the story|way in which the events of the story are organized|way in which the writer begins the story": "The plot of a story is the arrangement of its events: how they are ordered and linked by cause and effect, usually through exposition, rising action, climax, falling action and resolution. So plot is the way the events of the story are organized: C. How a writer begins (D) or ends (B) a story are only parts of the plot, and an intrigue against the hero (A) is one kind of event a plot may contain. Memory tip: story = what happens; plot = how the happenings are arranged.",
 "|The metric pattern in a line of poetry with five stressed and five unstressed syllables is|trochaic decametre|dactylic metre|iambic pentameter|anapaestic metre": "Metre is the pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables in a line of verse. An iamb is one unstressed syllable followed by a stressed one (da-DUM), and \"pentameter\" means five feet. Five iambs give five unstressed and five stressed syllables: iambic pentameter: C, as in Shakespeare's \"Shall I compare thee to a summer's day?\" A trochee is DUM-da, a dactyl DUM-da-da, an anapaest da-da-DUM. Memory tip: penta = five (pentagon); iambic pentameter = five da-DUMs.",
 "|THESEUS: Now, fair Hippolyta, our nuptial hour\nDraws on apace; four happy days bring in\nAnother moon: but, O, methinks, how slow\nThis old moon wanes! she lingers my desires,\nLike to a step-dame or a dowager\nLong withering out a young man's revenue.\nWilliam Shakespeare: A Midsummer Night's Dream\n\nThe literary devices used in the excerpt above are|personification and simile|irony and suspense|alliteration and synecdoche|rhyme and refrain": "A Midsummer Night's Dream is Shakespeare's comedy of lovers and fairies; it opens with Duke Theseus waiting impatiently for his wedding to Hippolyta in four days, at the new moon. He speaks of the old moon as a \"she\" who \"lingers\" (delays) his desires: the moon is given human action, so this is personification. He then compares her, using \"like\", to a stepmother or widow who keeps a young heir waiting for his inheritance (\"Like to a step-dame or a dowager\"): a simile. So the devices are personification and simile: A. Memory tip: \"she\" for a thing = personification; \"like\" or \"as\" = simile.",
 "|'You are the silent code of pleasure locked in wordless wonder.\nYou are the hive of treasure, no dragon can plunder.'\nGbemisola Adeoti: Dream Code\n\nThe excerpt above achieves its rhetorical effect through the use of|repetition and meiosis|metaphor and rhyme|caesura and hyperbole|alliteration and irony": "Gbemisola Adeoti is a Nigerian poet. In these love lines the speaker says the beloved IS \"the silent code of pleasure\" and IS \"the hive of treasure\": direct comparisons without \"like\" or \"as\", which are metaphors. The lines also rhyme internally and at the end: \"pleasure\" with \"treasure\", \"wonder\" with \"plunder\", giving a songlike effect. So the effect comes from metaphor and rhyme: B. There is no understatement (meiosis) or exaggeration (hyperbole) here. Memory tip: \"You are the...\" is the classic shape of a metaphor.",
 "|'It was not yet closing time, but already most staffs were trooping out of their offices. The lift was working now and he squeezed himself into it, breathing with difficulty the body odour emitted by one of the passengers. He sighed with relief when they got to the ground floor and tumbled out of the lift.'\nKen Saro-Wiwa: A Forest of Flowers\n\nIn the excerpt above, the subject's experience in the lift is|timely|comfortable|unpleasant|amusing": "A Forest of Flowers (1986) is a collection of short stories by the Nigerian writer Ken Saro-Wiwa. In this extract the man \"squeezed himself\" into the crowded lift, breathed \"with difficulty the body odour\" of a passenger, and \"sighed with relief\" when he \"tumbled out\". The crush, the smell and the relief all show that the ride was unpleasant: C. It was certainly not comfortable (B), and nothing in it is funny to him (D). Memory tip: \"sighed with relief\" tells you what came before was unpleasant.",
 "|'Do not thank me, instead, let me ask you one question. Now you have all come here sprawling, vomiting, rubbing tears on one another, begging me to do my duty and help you. But what about you yourselves? What have you done to help yourselves? Answer, or is the land at peace? Are not people ailing and dying?'\nOla Rotimi: The Gods Are Not to Blame\n\nIn the excerpt above, the land is not at peace because of|chieftaincy tussle|famine and war|political unrest|sickness and death": "The Gods Are Not to Blame (1971) is Ola Rotimi's Yoruba retelling of Sophocles' Oedipus the King. Early in the play King Odewale meets his people, who have come to the palace because a plague is killing Kutuje. He asks them what they have done to help themselves, ending: \"Are not people ailing and dying?\" The people come \"vomiting\" and \"rubbing tears\", and \"ailing and dying\" names the trouble directly: sickness and death: D. Nothing in these lines speaks of famine, war or a chieftaincy quarrel. Memory tip: in Kutuje, as in Oedipus's Thebes, the land suffers a plague, sickness and death.",
 "|'In those days\nWhen civilization kicked us in the face\nWhen holy water slapped our cringing brows\nThe vultures built in the shadow of their talons...'\nDavid Diop: The Vultures\n\nThe dominant literary device used in the lines above is|pun|metaphor|personification|simile": "The Vultures is a poem by the Senegalese poet David Diop attacking colonialism and the missionaries who came with it. In the lines \"When civilization kicked us in the face / When holy water slapped our cringing brows\", civilization and holy water are given human actions, kicking and slapping: personification: C. The colonisers are also called \"vultures\", which is a metaphor, but the striking device in these lines is the human violence given to ideas and things. The trap: B, metaphor, because the vultures are a metaphor; but the dominant device here is personification. Memory tip: an abstract idea that kicks or slaps = personification.",
 "|'I am not afraid of anything,' he told them. 'I have done almost everything in this world. I have committed all crimes you can think of and been jailed for most of them. I have been in prison more hours than I have been out of it within the last five years.'\n\nIn recounting his criminal life, the speaker's tone is|regretful|boastful|subdued|repentant": "Tone is the speaker's attitude, shown by his words. This man announces \"I am not afraid of anything\", claims to have \"done almost everything in this world\", to have \"committed all crimes you can think of\", and to have spent more time in prison than out. He shows no shame; he is showing off his criminal record. That is a boastful tone: B. Regretful (A) and repentant (D) would mean he was sorry, and subdued (C) means quiet and restrained, the opposite of this proud display. Memory tip: \"I have done everything... I fear nothing\" = boasting.",
 "|'I have said too much unto a heart of stone,\nAnd laid mine honour too unchary on't:\nThere's something in me that reproves my fault;\nBut such a headstrong potent fault it is,\nThat it but mocks reproof.'\nWilliam Shakespeare: Twelfth Night\n\n'A heart of stone' in the lines above is an example of|metonymy|litotes|assonance|metaphor": "Twelfth Night is Shakespeare's comedy of disguise and love. Olivia has fallen for \"Cesario\" (Viola in disguise), who does not return her love, and she says: \"I have said too much unto a heart of stone\". She does not say the heart is like stone; she says it IS a heart of stone, a direct comparison meaning a cold, unfeeling heart: a metaphor: D. Metonymy (A) names a thing by something associated with it (e.g. \"the crown\" for the king). Memory tip: hard, cold heart = \"heart of stone\", one of the commonest metaphors in English.",
 "|'Blood was to prove no solace to the king. The rejection he had suffered at Idawa's hands pushed his spirit into a comfortless hole in which, alone with himself, he searched in vain for ways to run from his inner emptiness.'\nAyi Kwei Armah: Two Thousand Seasons\n\nThe narrator's attitude to the king is one of|envy|sympathy|suspicion|contempt": "Two Thousand Seasons (1973) by the Ghanaian novelist Ayi Kwei Armah is told by a collective \"we\" voice recounting a thousand years of African history. Koranche, the weak and envious king, is rejected by the proud woman Idawa. In the extract the narrator shows him as a hollow man: blood gives him \"no solace\", his spirit is pushed into a \"comfortless hole\", and he searches \"in vain\" to escape his \"inner emptiness\". The words expose his worthlessness rather than invite pity, so the narrator's attitude is contempt: D. The trap: B, sympathy, because the passage describes his pain; but it presents his emptiness to show how small he is, not to make us feel for him. Memory tip: ask whether the narrator wants you to pity a character or look down on him.",
 "|'Homage to Peregede, the triumphant mother of mourning, radiant in Chameleon's velvet. Let today's dawn bring on its rail trains of good tidings.'\nGbemisola Adeoti: Salutation to the Gods\n\nThe excerpt above is an example of|invocation|limerick|ode|elegy": "An invocation is a poem or passage that calls upon a god, a muse or a spirit for help or blessing. In Adeoti's Salutation to the Gods the speaker offers \"Homage to Peregede\", praising the deity as \"the triumphant mother of mourning\", and then prays: \"Let today's dawn bring on its rail trains of good tidings.\" Praise of a god plus a request is an invocation: A. A limerick (B) is a five-line comic poem, an elegy (D) mourns the dead, and an ode (C) is a long formal poem of praise, not a prayer. Memory tip: in-voke = to call in a god.",
 "|'The woods decay, the woods decay and fall,\nThe vapours weep their burthen to the ground,\nMan comes and tills the field and lies beneath,\nAnd after many a summer dies the swan.'\n\nThe subject matter of the lines above is|death|rainfall|famine|storm": "These lines open Tithonus by the English poet Alfred, Lord Tennyson. Tithonus was granted eternal life but not eternal youth, and he envies everything that is allowed to die. Every image is of mortality: the woods \"decay and fall\", the vapours (mist) \"weep\" their load to the ground, man \"tills the field and lies beneath\" (is buried in it), and \"after many a summer dies the swan\". The subject matter is death: A. The falling \"vapours\" are a passing image of mist, not the subject, so rainfall (B) is wrong. Memory tip: decay, fall, lies beneath, dies = death.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nWho is the paternal aunt to Aaron and Maanan?|Mrs Bonu|Hannah|Fosuwa|Adwoa": "Sons and Daughters is a play by the Ghanaian dramatist Joe (J.C.) de Graft about James Ofosu, a well-to-do father who wants to choose careers for his children, while Aaron wants to paint and Maanan wants to dance. A paternal aunt is your father's sister. Fosuwa is James's elder sister, the meddling relative who blames his wife Hannah for the family's troubles, so she is Aaron and Maanan's paternal aunt: C. Hannah (B) is their mother, and Mrs Bonu (A) is the wife of the lawyer who pursues Maanan. Memory tip: paternal = father's side (Latin pater, father); maternal = mother's side.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nFrom the play, George is a|laboratory assistant|pharmacist|nurse|medical doctor": "James Ofosu is proud of his older children because they have entered the \"respectable\", well-paid professions he believes in. George, the eldest son, has qualified from medical school and is a practising doctor, while Kofi is training as an accountant. That is exactly why James wants Aaron to be an engineer and Maanan a lawyer instead of following art and dance. So George is a medical doctor: D. The trap: B, pharmacist, because it is another medical profession and sounds close. Memory tip: George and Kofi show what James values (medicine, accountancy); Aaron and Maanan show what he fears (art, dance).",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \n'If you touch me, I shall smash your face with this bottle.'\n\nThe statement is made by|Maanan to Lawyer B|Maanan to Mrs Bonu|James to Awere|Awere to Aaron": "Lawyer Bonu is a rich, middle-aged friend of the family whom James admires. Behind his respectable front he desires Maanan, and in one tense scene he tries to force himself on her when they are alone. Maanan defends herself by snatching up a bottle and warning him, \"If you touch me, I shall smash your face with this bottle.\" So the words are spoken by Maanan to Lawyer B (Bonu): A. Mrs Bonu (B) is not the one threatened; James and Awere (C) and Awere and Aaron (D) never have such a fight. Memory tip: Lawyer Bonu is the \"respectable\" man whose behaviour exposes the hollowness of James's values.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \n'If you touch me, I shall smash your face with this bottle.'\n\nThe issue at stake is that|Maanan is trying to compromise|Lawyer B is trying to kiss Maanan|James sees Awere as a bad influence|Mrs Bonu is taunting Maanan for loving her husband": "The bottle threat comes at the moment Lawyer Bonu makes advances to Maanan and tries to kiss her against her will. The issue at stake is his attempt on her, not any compromise by Maanan (A) or a quarrel about Awere (C). Mrs Bonu (D) later suspects Maanan, but the scene itself is about the lawyer's assault: B. The incident shows that the \"respectable\" men James admires are not as decent as he thinks, and it strengthens Maanan's case against the career her father chooses. Memory tip: ask what has just happened on stage when a character reaches for a weapon.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'From forth the fatal loins of these two foes\nA pair of star-crossed lovers take their life...'\nThe lines above suggest that the tragedy in the play|could have been averted|is predestined|is brought on by enmity|brought misfortune on the lovers": "Romeo and Juliet (about 1595) is Shakespeare's tragedy of two young lovers from the feuding Montague and Capulet families of Verona. The Prologue calls them \"star-crossed lovers\" born from the \"fatal loins\" of the two enemies. \"Star-crossed\" means thwarted by the stars, that is, by fate; \"fatal\" means bringing death and decided by fate. These words tell us that the tragedy is fixed in advance: it is predestined: B. The feud (C) is the means fate uses, but the words \"star-crossed\" and \"fatal\" point to destiny. The trap: C, because the \"two foes\" are mentioned in the same line. Memory tip: stars = fate; \"star-crossed\" = doomed by fate.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'O, she doth teach the torches to burn bright!\nIt seems she hangs upon the cheek of night\nLike a rich jewel in an Ethiop's ear.'\nFrom the lines above, Juliet's beauty is presented|in contrast to the dark night|as a source of envy to all|in terms of riches|as being outstanding": "At the Capulet feast Romeo sees Juliet for the first time and cries, \"O, she doth teach the torches to burn bright! / It seems she hangs upon the cheek of night / Like a rich jewel in an Ethiop's ear.\" He sets her brightness against darkness: the night, the dark ear of an Ethiopian. The contrast makes her beauty shine out, so she seems to outshine even the torches. Both readings are fully right: the beauty is presented in contrast to the dark night (A) and as being outstanding (D), so both are accepted. \"Rich jewel\" (C) is only the comparison used. Memory tip: light against dark is Romeo's favourite image for Juliet; later she is \"the sun\".",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'The all-seeing sun\nNe'er saw her match since first the world begun.'\nThe lines above were spoken by|Count Paris in praise of Juliet|Romeo in praise of Juliet|Romeo in praise of Rosaline|Lady Capulet in praise of Rosaline": "At the start of the play Romeo is lovesick for Rosaline, a girl who will not return his love. In Act 1 Scene 2 Benvolio says he will show him fairer women at the Capulet feast, and Romeo replies, \"One fairer than my love? The all-seeing sun / Ne'er saw her match since first the world begun.\" \"My love\" here is Rosaline: C. The irony is that at the very feast Romeo forgets Rosaline the moment he sees Juliet. The trap: B, because students assume any praise from Romeo is of Juliet. Memory tip: Act 1 Romeo loves Rosaline; his speed in changing shows how young and impulsive he is.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nThe major role of Mercutio in the play is to|serve as a contrast to Romeo|aid and abet Romeo's passion|annoy Tybalt|accompany Romeo to Friar Lawrence": "Mercutio is Romeo's witty friend, a kinsman of the Prince. He mocks love, makes bawdy jokes and delivers the fantastic Queen Mab speech. His down-to-earth, sceptical view of love is the opposite of Romeo's dreamy passion, so he serves as a foil or contrast to Romeo: A. He does not help Romeo's love affair (B); he does not even know about Juliet. His death at Tybalt's hands (\"A plague o' both your houses!\") turns the play towards tragedy. Memory tip: a foil is a character whose contrast shows up the hero's qualities.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nThe play shares the feature of classical tragedy through the use of|violence on stage|chorus|comic relief|flashback": "Classical Greek tragedy used a Chorus: a group (or speaker) who comments on the action and tells the audience what has happened. Shakespeare borrows this device: a Chorus speaks the Prologue in sonnet form and another sonnet before Act 2. That is how the play shares a feature of classical tragedy: B. Greek tragedy kept violence offstage, so A is the opposite of the classical rule; comic relief (C) is a Shakespearean, not classical, feature. Memory tip: Chorus = the Greek voice that tells the story around the action.",
 "|Based on Ferdinand Oyono's The Old Man and the Medal: \n'Meka, kneeling down in his usual fashion with his behind up in the air. Kelara knelt down beside him. Amalia and her husband knelt down as well.'\nThe actions of Meka, Kelara, Amalia and her husband signify|parade|dance|prayer|celebration": "The Old Man and the Medal (1956) by Ferdinand Oyono of Cameroon follows Meka, a devout old Catholic villager who gave his land to the mission and is to be awarded a medal by the French colonial administration. Kneeling side by side, with Meka \"in his usual fashion with his behind up in the air\", is the posture of people at prayer: C. Meka and his family pray often; the comic picture of his kneeling is part of Oyono's gentle mockery of his blind faith in the whites' religion. Memory tip: kneeling together in a Christian home means prayer, not a parade or dance.",
 "|Based on Ferdinand Oyono's The Old Man and the Medal: \n'He had knocked his toes against so many things that he had no toenails anymore and the yaws he had suffered from his youth had twisted his toes up so that they pointed to the sky.'\nThe description above is in reference to the foot of|Kelara|Meka|Engamba|Mvondo": "Meka has gone barefoot all his life. Oyono describes his feet in comic detail: the toenails are gone from knocking against things, and yaws he caught as a boy have twisted his toes so that they point to the sky. This matters in the story because for the medal ceremony Meka squeezes these feet into new shoes, which torment him as he stands in the sun waiting for the white chief. So the foot is Meka's: B. Kelara (A) is his wife, Engamba (C) his brother-in-law. Memory tip: Meka's suffering feet in European shoes stand for his painful effort to fit into the colonial world.",
 "|Based on Ferdinand Oyono's The Old Man and the Medal: \n'They said their prayers in a monotonous sing-song, kneeling on their bamboo bed like camels waiting to be loaded.'\nThe dominant figure of speech in the excerpt above is|rhetorical question|simile|metaphor|mixed metaphor": "A simile compares two unlike things using \"like\" or \"as\". Here Meka and Kelara, kneeling to pray on their bamboo bed, are said to be \"like camels waiting to be loaded\". The word \"like\" shows that the comparison is open: a simile: B. The image is comic, mocking the old couple's mechanical \"monotonous sing-song\" of prayer. There is no question (A), and a metaphor (C) would drop \"like\" (\"they were camels\"). The trap: C, because a simile and a metaphor are both comparisons. Memory tip: \"like\" or \"as\" = simile; \"is\" = metaphor.",
 "|Based on Buchi Emecheta's The Joys of Motherhood: \nAs a symbol of material success and fulfilment, Ibuza community places a lot of importance on|childbirth|wealth|male child|female child": "The Joys of Motherhood (1979) by the Nigerian novelist Buchi Emecheta follows Nnu Ego from her village, Ibuza, to colonial Lagos. In Ibuza a woman is judged by whether she bears children, and above all sons, who carry on the husband's lineage; a man's success too is counted in sons. Nnu Ego's first husband rejects her because she does not conceive, and Adaku is later despised because she has no son. Both childbirth (A) and the male child (C) are right, so both are accepted. Wealth (B) is not what Ibuza prizes most. Memory tip: in this novel, children, especially sons, are a woman's \"riches\", which is the bitter irony of the title.",
 "|Based on Buchi Emecheta's The Joys of Motherhood: \nOna on her dying bed appeals to Agbadi to|give her a befitting burial|take good care of her children|take another wife|allow Nnu Ego marry a man of her choice": "Ona is Nnu Ego's mother, a proud, independent woman who never married the great chief Agbadi because her father would not allow it. When she is dying after a difficult childbirth, she asks Agbadi to let their daughter have a life of her own, \"a husband if she wants one\": in other words, to allow Nnu Ego to marry a man of her choice: D. Agbadi does not really keep the promise: he marries Nnu Ego to Amatokwu and later sends her to Lagos to marry Nnaife. Memory tip: Ona wanted for her daughter the freedom she herself had; the novel shows how little of it Nnu Ego gets.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nThe novel is mainly classified as a|metaphor|hyperbole|satire|fiction": "Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949) by George Orwell pictures Oceania, a state where the Party controls everything: telescreens watch people, history is rewritten and thought itself is a crime. A satire uses exaggeration and ridicule to attack faults in society or politics. Orwell's novel attacks totalitarian government by showing it pushed to its extreme, so it is mainly classified as a satire (a political, dystopian satire): C. It is of course a work of fiction (D), but that only says it is invented; it does not classify what kind of novel it is. The trap: D, because every novel is fiction. Memory tip: classify a novel by its purpose; Orwell's purpose is to attack tyranny.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nWinston writes that the hope of the country lies on the|ministry of the truth|proles|party|children": "Winston Smith keeps a secret diary. Looking at the masses of ordinary people, the proles, who make up about 85 per cent of Oceania's population and are hardly watched by the Party, he writes: \"If there is hope, it lies in the proles.\" Only they are numerous and strong enough to overthrow the Party, if they ever became conscious of their power: B. The Party (C) and the Ministry of Truth (A) are the oppressors, and Party children (D) are trained to spy on their parents. Memory tip: proles = proletariat, the working masses Winston pins his hope on.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nIn the novel, two minutes hate is a programme designed for|parents|thought police|the community|children": "The Two Minutes Hate is a daily ritual in which Party members stop work, gather before a large telescreen and watch films of Emmanuel Goldstein, the \"Enemy of the People\", screaming abuse at him until the face of Big Brother appears. It is designed for the whole community of Party workers, to channel their frustrations into hatred of the state's enemies and loyalty to Big Brother: C. It is not meant only for parents, children or the Thought Police. Memory tip: the Hate is mass, organised emotion; it binds the community to the Party.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nTo drop his philosophy of life and imbibe the tenets of the party, Winston is subjected to all forms of torture and inhuman treatment by|O'Brien|thought police|Big Brother|Goldstein": "O'Brien is a member of the Inner Party whom Winston believes to be a secret rebel. In fact he is a loyal agent of the Party who traps Winston and Julia. In the Ministry of Love O'Brien personally tortures Winston, with pain, starvation and finally Room 101, until Winston betrays Julia and gives up his beliefs, loving Big Brother: A. The Thought Police (B) arrest Winston, but O'Brien carries out the \"re-education\". Big Brother (C) may not even exist as a person. The trap: B, because the Thought Police make the arrest. Memory tip: O'Brien is both Winston's trusted friend and his torturer, the novel's cruellest irony.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe movement in Adeoti's Naked Soles is characterized by|hope and agreement|freedom and self-determination|pricks and tears|disappointment and disarray": "Gbemisola Adeoti is a Nigerian poet and scholar; Naked Soles (2005) is the title poem of the collection. The \"naked soles\" are bare-footed, oppressed people dancing in a strange \"carnival\", \"dancing through blooming thorns\", hopping on \"thorns and glass chips\" along a \"pin-ful path\". Critics describe the collection as a picture of anguish in images of pain. Their movement is marked by pricks (the thorns and pins) and tears (their silent crying): C. Nothing in the dance suggests hope (A) or freedom (B). Memory tip: look at what the feet touch in this poem: thorns, glass, pins.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nOne of the dominant themes of Rubadiri's An African Thunderstorm is the|relationship between man and nature|activities of man during rainy seasons|effect of rain on women and children|problem of climate change": "David Rubadiri, a Malawian poet, describes a storm sweeping towards an African village: the wind \"whistles by\", clouds gather \"like a plague of locusts\", women rush about with babies clinging to their backs, and the thunder will \"rumble, tremble and crack\". The poem shows people's fear and helplessness before the power of nature, so one dominant theme is the relationship between man and nature: A. Women and children (C) appear only as part of that picture, and the poem is not about climate change (D). Memory tip: storm poems usually pit human weakness against nature's power.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \n'...the youthful hue\nSits on thy skin like morning dew...'\nThe excerpt above from Marvell's To His Coy Mistress is an example of|simile|anaphora|paradox|onomatopoeia": "Andrew Marvell's \"To His Coy Mistress\" (17th century) is a persuasion poem: the speaker urges a shy woman to love him now because time is short. In the last section he says, \"Now therefore, while the youthful hue / Sits on thy skin like morning dew\", her fresh young colour is compared to dew, which is fresh but soon dries away. The word \"like\" makes this a simile: A. Anaphora (B) is repeating the same word at the start of lines; onomatopoeia (D) imitates sound. Memory tip: dew vanishes in the morning sun; the simile also warns that youth will not last.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nIn Lawrence's Bat, the poet compares bats with|sparrows|swans|swallows|crows": "In D.H. Lawrence's \"Bat\", the speaker sits in Florence at sunset watching creatures flicker over the river. At first he takes them for swallows, but then realises his mistake: \"Swallows? ... Not swallows. Bats!\" The poem goes on to describe the bats with disgust. So the comparison is with swallows: C. Swallows are birds that also dart and swoop in the evening air, which is why the confusion is natural. Memory tip: the poem turns on the moment the graceful swallows turn out to be bats.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nEliot's Journey of the Magi could be said to examine the issues of|three trees on the low sky|empty wine-skins|spiritual rebirth|holy pilgrimage": "T.S. Eliot's \"Journey of the Magi\" (1927) is spoken by one of the wise men, years later, remembering the hard journey to see the infant Christ. He asks, \"were we led all that way for / Birth or Death?\" and says the Birth was \"hard and bitter agony for us, like Death\". After it the Magi are no longer at ease in their old religion. The poem examines spiritual rebirth: the death of the old life and the painful birth of a new faith: C. A and B are images in the poem, not its issues. The trap: D, because the poem describes a journey to a holy birth, but its real subject is what the journey did to the traveller's soul. Memory tip: \"Birth or Death?\" is the poem's key question.",
 "|A literary work in which the characters and events are used as symbols is known as|characterization|allegory|metaphor|parallelism": "An allegory is a story in which characters and events stand as symbols for ideas, so the story has a second, hidden meaning. In John Bunyan's The Pilgrim's Progress, Christian's journey stands for the soul's road to heaven; in Orwell's Animal Farm, the animals' revolution stands for the Russian Revolution: B. A metaphor (C) is a single figure of speech, not a whole work; characterisation (A) is the presentation of characters; parallelism (D) is balanced structure. The trap: C, because an allegory is sometimes called an extended metaphor. Memory tip: allegory = a whole story that means something else.",
 "|Characterization in a novel refers to the|writer's opinion of the characters|way the characters are revealed to the reader|characters and the way they behave|reader's opinion of the characters": "Characterisation is the way a writer creates and reveals characters to the reader: by direct description, by what they say and do, by what others say about them and by their thoughts. So it refers to the way the characters are revealed to the reader: B. It is not simply the characters and how they behave (C), which is what is revealed rather than the method of revealing it, and it is not anyone's opinion of them (A, D). The trap: C, because behaviour is one of the ways characters are shown. Memory tip: characterisation is a writer's technique, the HOW of presenting people.",
 "|In literary work, verbal irony refers to a|device in which the speaker means the opposite of what he says|situation in which a character speaks or acts against the trend of events|difficult situation which defies a logical or rational resolution|device in which the actor on stage means exactly what he says": "Verbal irony is the use of words to mean the opposite of what they say: calling a clumsy person \"Grace\", or saying \"What lovely weather!\" in a storm. So it is a device in which the speaker means the opposite of what he says: A. D describes the very opposite of irony. C, a situation with no logical way out, is a dilemma or paradox. B, acting against the trend of events, is nearer situational irony. Memory tip: verbal irony lives in words; situational irony lives in events; dramatic irony lives in what the audience knows.",
 "|In the theater, words spoken by a character that are meant to be heard by the audience but not by the other characters on stage is called|aside|soliloquy|acoustic|tone": "An aside is a short remark a character speaks to the audience (or to one other character) that the others on stage are supposed not to hear, as when Hamlet says \"A little more than kin, and less than kind.\" So the answer is A. A soliloquy (B) is a long speech by a character ALONE on stage, revealing thoughts; it is not hidden from others because no one else is present. The trap: B, because both are spoken to the audience. Memory tip: aside = others on stage but not hearing; soliloquy = alone on stage.",
 "|Drama is the representation of a complete series of actions by means of|movement and gesture for the screen and audience|speech, movement and gesture for the stage only|speech, movement and gesture for the stage, screen and radio|speech, gesture and movement for the screen and radio": "Drama is the representation of a story through action: actors use speech (dialogue), movement and gesture to act out the events. It is not confined to the theatre: plays are also written for film and television (the screen) and for radio (where speech carries all the action). The fullest definition is \"speech, movement and gesture for the stage, screen and radio\": C. A leaves out speech, B wrongly says \"stage only\", and D leaves out the stage, drama's first home. Memory tip: drama = action performed, wherever it is performed.",
 "|A poet's use of regular rhythm is known as|allegory|assonance|metre|onomatopoeia": "Metre is the regular pattern of stressed and unstressed syllables in a line of poetry, which gives it a regular rhythm. Shakespeare's \"Shall I compare thee to a summer's day?\" is in iambic pentameter: five pairs of an unstressed and a stressed syllable. So a poet's regular rhythm is metre: C. Assonance (B) is repeated vowel sounds, onomatopoeia (D) is words imitating sounds, and allegory (A) is a symbolic story. Memory tip: metre = measure (Greek metron); it measures the beat of a line.",
 "|A literary genre which directly imitates human action is|drama|comedy|prose|poetry": "Aristotle called drama an \"imitation of an action\": instead of telling us about events (as prose and poetry do), drama shows them directly, with actors who speak and act as the characters. So the genre that directly imitates human action is drama: A. Comedy (B) is only one type of drama, not a genre on the same level as prose and poetry. Prose and poetry narrate or describe. Memory tip: the three main genres are drama, prose and poetry; drama is the one that is acted.",
 "|A fable is a story in which|allegations are made about characters|animals or things are used as characters|there is an important setting|the story is told in poetic form": "A fable is a short story with a moral lesson in which the characters are usually animals (sometimes plants or objects) that speak and act like people. Aesop's \"The Tortoise and the Hare\" and African tortoise tales are examples. So a fable is a story in which animals or things are used as characters: B. The other options describe nothing special to fables. Memory tip: fable = talking animals + a moral at the end.",
 "|The juxtaposition of two contrasting ideas in a line of poetry is|euphemism|synecdoche|catharsis|oxymoron": "An oxymoron joins two contradictory or contrasting words or ideas side by side, as in \"sweet sorrow\", \"living death\" or \"cold fire\". Among the options it is the only figure based on contrast: D. Euphemism (A) is a mild word for something unpleasant; synecdoche (B) uses a part for the whole; catharsis (C) is the emotional cleansing an audience feels at a tragedy. Strictly, opposites balanced in a sentence are antithesis, but antithesis is not offered here. Memory tip: oxymoron is itself an oxymoron: Greek oxys \"sharp\" + moros \"dull\".",
 "|The main aim of caricature is to|describe|expose|emphasize|ridicule": "A caricature is a picture or description that exaggerates a person's most striking features or habits, a big nose, a pompous manner, in order to make them look ridiculous. Its main aim is to ridicule: D. Exaggeration may expose (B) or emphasise (C) a fault, but these are the means; the purpose of the distortion is to make people laugh at the subject. The trap: C, because caricature does work by emphasising features. Memory tip: cartoonists' caricatures of politicians make us laugh at them.",
 "|'O Ceremony, show me but thy worth:\nWhat is thy soul of adoration?'\n\nThe figure of speech in the lines above is|antithesis|invocation|personification|apostrophe": "These lines come from Shakespeare's Henry V, where the King, alone at night before battle, speaks to \"Ceremony\", the pomp that surrounds kings: \"O Ceremony, show me but thy worth! / What is thy soul of adoration?\" Speaking directly to an absent person or an abstract idea as if it could hear and answer is apostrophe: D. Treating Ceremony as a person is also personification (C), but the dominant device, marked by \"O\" and the direct \"thy\", is the address itself. The trap: C, because every apostrophe to an idea also personifies it. Memory tip: \"O + you/thou\" to something that cannot reply = apostrophe.",
 "|'What eyes will watch our large mouths\nShaped by the laughter of big children?\nWhat eyes will watch our large mouths?'\nBirago Diop, Vanity\n\nThe tone of the lines above is one of|sarcasm|sacrilege|chiasmus|eulogy": "Birago Diop (1906–1989) was a Senegalese poet of the Negritude movement. In \"Vanity\" he asks who will listen to his people's \"sad complaining voices\", since they themselves never listened to the voices of their ancestors. Here he pictures their \"large mouths / Shaped by the laughter of big children\": grown people laughing foolishly like children. The mocking picture of his own people gives the lines a bitter, sarcastic tone: A. Study notes describe the poem's tone as lament mixed with sarcasm. A eulogy (D) is praise, usually of the dead, and nothing here is praised; chiasmus (C) is a word pattern, not a tone; sacrilege (B) is disrespect to something holy. Memory tip: tone = the poet's attitude; mocking laughter signals sarcasm.",
 "|The old man slept in his favourite chair\nThe wind ran its fingers through his hair\nHe looked like a tree gone dry of sap\nAnd his hands were dry upon his lap\n\nThe rhyme scheme of the poem above is|bbaa|aabb|abab|baba": "A rhyme scheme is shown with letters: lines that rhyme get the same letter, starting with a. Here \"chair\" and \"hair\" rhyme (a, a), then \"sap\" and \"lap\" rhyme (b, b). The pattern is aabb: B, which means the lines rhyme in pairs (rhyming couplets). The other options either start with b (A, D), which is never done, or give alternate rhyme (C, abab), which would need chair/sap and hair/lap to rhyme. Memory tip: always begin with \"a\" and give a new letter to each new rhyme sound.",
 "|'...Unequal laws unto a savage race,\nThat hoard, and sleep, and feed....'\n\nThe lines above show that the speaker|detects discrimination|is desirous of adventure|hates his old wife|knows much of his city men": "Tennyson's \"Ulysses\" (1842) is spoken by the old Greek hero, back home as king of Ithaca. He complains that it profits little that \"an idle king\" should \"mete and dole / Unequal laws unto a savage race, / That hoard, and sleep, and feed, and know not me.\" He is aware of inequality in the laws he hands out to a people he thinks are uncivilised: he detects discrimination: A. The whole poem goes on to show his longing for adventure (B), but these particular lines describe his dissatisfaction with ruling. \"An aged wife\" appears just before, but he does not say he hates her (C). Memory tip: read the exact lines quoted before using what you know of the whole poem.",
 "|'...How can I look at Oyo and say I hate long shiny cars? How can I come to the children and despise international schools? And Koomson comes, and the family sees Jesus Christ in him....'\n\nThe feeling conveyed by the speaker above is one of|anger|alienation|hope|despair": "These lines come from The Beautyful Ones Are Not Yet Born (1968) by the Ghanaian novelist Ayi Kwei Armah. \"The man\", an honest railway clerk, refuses bribes, while his wife Oyo and the family admire Koomson, a corrupt, rich politician with long shiny cars, and \"see Jesus Christ in him\". The man cannot share their values, yet he cannot condemn the things his own family longs for. He feels cut off and alone among those closest to him: alienation: B. Despair (D) is near, but the lines stress his separation from the family. The trap: D, because the man's mood is dark. Memory tip: alienation = feeling a stranger among your own people.",
 "|'Hide me now, when night children haunt the earth'\nWole Soyinka, Night\n\nNight children in the stanza above reflects the consciousness of|birds|armed robbers|animals|spirit beings": "In Wole Soyinka's \"Night\" the speaker, overwhelmed by the night, begs, \"Hide me now, when night children haunt the earth\". The \"night children\" are those who come out under cover of darkness to do harm. The poem also speaks of something \"silent as night thieves\", and in Nigerian readings the night children are the robbers and evil-doers who prowl at night: B. Some readers also see in them spirits of the night (D), since \"haunt\" suggests ghosts; the reading that fits the poem's thieves best is the robbers. Memory tip: \"children of the night\" = those who work in darkness, often criminals.",
 "|'Serrated shadows, through dark leaves\nTill, bathed in warm suffusion of your dappled cells,\nSensation pained me, faceless, silent as night thieves.'\nWole Soyinka, Night\n\nThe dominant mood in the lines above is one of|apprehension|defiance|joy|indifference": "Mood is the feeling the lines create. Here the speaker describes \"serrated\" (jagged, saw-toothed) shadows through dark leaves, then says \"Sensation pained me\", coming \"faceless, silent as night thieves\". Sharp shapes, pain, and a silent, faceless thief in the night all produce a feeling of fear and unease about what is coming: apprehension: A. Nothing in the lines is joyful (C), defiant (B) or indifferent (D). Memory tip: thieves, shadows and pain in the dark almost always signal apprehension.",
 "|'The drums overwhelmed the guns...'\nJ.P. Clark, Casualties\n\nThe poet in the excerpt above uses|litotes|symbolism|onomatopoeia|alliteration": "J.P. Clark (Clark-Bekederemo) wrote \"Casualties\" about the Nigerian Civil War (1967–70). In \"The drums overwhelmed the guns\" the drums and guns stand for more than themselves: the drums for the noise of propaganda, celebration and war-talk, the guns for the real fighting and dying, which the noise drowns out. Using concrete things to stand for ideas is symbolism: B. Onomatopoeia (C) needs a word that imitates a sound, such as \"boom\" or \"rat-a-tat\"; \"drums\" and \"guns\" only name noisy things. The trap: C, because drums and guns make loud sounds. Memory tip: ask whether the word itself sounds like the noise; if not, it is not onomatopoeia.",
 "|'...They do not see the funeral piles\nAt home eating up the forests...'\nJ.P. Clark, Casualties\n\nThe imagery created in the above excerpt is achieved through|metaphor|personification|synecdoche|metonymy": "In \"Casualties\", J.P. Clark says that those abroad who talk about the war \"do not see the funeral piles / At home eating up the forests\". Funeral piles (the heaps of wood for burning the dead) cannot really eat, but the poet gives them this living, greedy action to show how many dead there are, so many that the pyres consume whole forests. Giving human or living qualities to a thing is personification: B. A metaphor (A) would say the piles ARE something else; here they are simply given a living action. Memory tip: things that eat, weep or walk = personification.",
 "|'I cannot rest from travel: I will drink\nLife to the lees: all times I have enjoyed\nGreatly, have suffered greatly...'\nA.L. Tennyson, Ulysses\n\nThe lines above inform the reader that the poet|is determined to suffer|has his poetic imagination kindled|will cure his sour mood|will not drink much": "In Tennyson's \"Ulysses\", the old hero is bored and discontented as an idle king at home. He declares, \"I cannot rest from travel: I will drink / Life to the lees\": he will live life to the very last drop (lees are the dregs at the bottom of a wine cup). Setting out again is his cure for the restless, sour mood of idleness: C. He does not seek suffering for its own sake (A); he mentions it only as part of a full life (\"enjoyed greatly, have suffered greatly\"). \"Drink\" is a metaphor, so D misreads it. Memory tip: \"drink life to the lees\" = live to the full, not drink less.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nJames: Let me swear, woman. And I will swear by my father's coffin that if....\n\nThe lines depict James as a|traditionalist|Christian|pagan|Muslim": "Sons and Daughters is a play by the Ghanaian dramatist Joe (J.C.) de Graft about James Ofosu, a well-to-do father who wants to choose careers for his children, while Aaron wants to be an artist and Maanan a dancer. In many African cultures a person makes a solemn promise by swearing on the dead, on the ancestors or on a parent's grave. When James says he \"will swear by my father's coffin\", he uses this traditional form of oath, so the lines show him as a traditionalist: A. Nothing in the words points to Christian or Muslim prayer, and \"pagan\" is a loaded word, not a description of the oath. Memory tip: what a character swears by tells you what he holds sacred.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nAaron: '...All I need really is a place in an Art school, engineering can go hang itself.'\n\nThe dominant figure of speech in the excerpt above is|metonymy|synecdoche|personification|metaphor": "Personification gives human qualities or actions to something that is not human. Aaron, who wants a place in an art school, says \"engineering can go hang itself\". Engineering is a profession, an idea, yet it is told to do something only a person can do: hang itself. That makes the figure personification: C. It is not metonymy (A) or synecdoche (B), because \"engineering\" is not standing in for something associated with it or for a part of a whole; it simply means the career his father wants for him. The trap: D, metaphor, because both are figures of comparison; but nothing is said to \"be\" something else here. Memory tip: if a thing or an idea does a human action, think personification.",
 "|Based on J.C. de Graft's Sons and Daughters: \nFrom the play, the character of Aaron represents the|painters|art work|new generation|old generation": "The central conflict of Sons and Daughters is between parents and children: James Ofosu insists that his children take \"respectable\", well-paid professions, while Aaron wants to paint and Maanan wants to dance. Aaron stands for the young people who want to choose their own careers and follow their talents rather than their parents' ambitions. So he represents the new generation: C. James is the voice of the old generation (D). Aaron is himself a painter, but he is not a symbol of \"painters\" in general (A), and \"art work\" (B) is what he produces, not what he represents. Memory tip: in a play about sons and daughters, ask whether a character speaks for the parents or the children.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'Uncle, this is a Montague, our foe;\nA villain that is hither come in spite,\nTo scorn at our solemnity this night.'\n\nThe villain in the excerpt above is|attempting to steal|attending a feast uninvited|engaging in a shouty match|holding a sword to commit murder": "Romeo and Juliet is Shakespeare's tragedy of two young lovers from the feuding Montague and Capulet families of Verona. Romeo, a Montague, goes masked and uninvited to the Capulets' feast. Tybalt, Juliet's hot-tempered cousin, recognises his voice and complains to Lord Capulet: \"Uncle, this is a Montague, our foe; / A villain that is hither come in spite, / To scorn at our solemnity this night.\" The \"villain\" is Romeo, and his offence is attending the feast uninvited: B. Romeo carries no sword at the feast and steals nothing; Capulet even tells Tybalt to leave him alone. Memory tip: \"solemnity this night\" = the Capulet feast, where Romeo first sees Juliet.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'What, drawn, and talk of peace? I hate the word,\nAs I hate hell, all Montagues, and thee.\nHave at thee, coward!'\n\nThe lines above reveal the speaker as a|violence seeker|peace maker|real Montague|trouble shooter": "In the opening scene Benvolio draws his sword to part the brawling servants. Tybalt arrives, sees him with sword drawn and sneers: \"What, drawn, and talk of peace? I hate the word, / As I hate hell, all Montagues, and thee. / Have at thee, coward!\" He rejects peace outright and attacks at once, so the lines reveal him as a violence seeker: A. Benvolio is the peace maker (B), not Tybalt. Tybalt is a Capulet, Lady Capulet's nephew, so C is wrong. A \"troubleshooter\" is someone who solves problems, the opposite of Tybalt (D). Memory tip: Tybalt \"hates the word\" peace; it is his fiery temper that later kills Mercutio.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \nRomeo's mood at the beginning of the play can be described as|melancholic and sentimental|dreamy and hopeful|frustrated and pensive|gay and elated": "At the start of the play Romeo is in love with Rosaline, who has sworn never to marry and does not return his love. His father says he walks alone before dawn, weeps and shuts himself in his darkened room; Romeo himself speaks in sad, sighing, exaggerated lines about love. He is melancholic (sad and gloomy) and sentimental (ruled by tender feelings): A. \"Gay and elated\" (D) describes him after he meets Juliet, not before. The trap: C, frustrated and pensive, because his love is indeed thwarted; but the play stresses his sadness and lovesick sighing, not frustration. Memory tip: Act 1 Romeo = lovesick, sighing, melancholy for Rosaline.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'O deadly sin! O rude unthankfulness!\nThy fault our law calls death, but the kind Prince,\nTaking thy part, hath rushed aside the law,\nAnd turned that black word \"death\" to banishment.'\n\nThe speaker in the passage above is|Lord Montague|Friar Lawrence|Apothecary|Lord Capulet": "After Romeo kills Tybalt, the Prince banishes him from Verona instead of sentencing him to death. Romeo hides in Friar Lawrence's cell and cries that banishment is worse than death. The Friar rebukes him: \"O deadly sin! O rude unthankfulness! / Thy fault our law calls death, but the kind Prince, / Taking thy part, hath rushed aside the law, / And turned that black word 'death' to banishment.\" The speaker is Friar Lawrence: B. He is Romeo's adviser, who married the lovers secretly. The Apothecary (C) only sells Romeo poison later in Mantua; the fathers (A, D) are not in this scene. Memory tip: a speech that lectures Romeo and talks of law and mercy is usually the Friar's.",
 "|Based on William Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet: \n'...Put up thy sword,\nOr manage it to part these men with me.'\n\nThe speech above was made when|Tybalt challenges Romeo to duel|Prince Escalus arrives to make peace between the families|Romeo and Paris engaged themselves in a fight|Benvolio tries to separate the servants of the feuding families": "The play opens with a street fight between servants of the Capulets and Montagues. Benvolio, Romeo's peace-loving cousin, draws his sword to part them: \"Part, fools! Put up your swords.\" Then Tybalt arrives and challenges him, and Benvolio replies, \"I do but keep the peace. Put up thy sword, / Or manage it to part these men with me.\" So the words are spoken when Benvolio tries to separate the servants of the feuding families: D. The Prince (B) arrives only after the brawl has spread; Tybalt challenges Romeo (A) much later, in Act 3. Memory tip: Benvolio's name means \"good will\"; he is always the peacemaker.",
 "|Based on Ferdinand Oyono's The Old Man and the Medal: \nTo the white men, the medal that is given to Meka symbolizes|harmonious relationship|love|peace|friendship": "The Old Man and the Medal (1956) by Ferdinand Oyono of Cameroon is a satire of French colonial rule. Meka, a devout old villager, gave his land to the Catholic mission and lost two sons fighting for France, and the colonial administration decides to give him a medal. At the ceremony the white Chief makes a speech saying that the whites and Meka's people are \"more than friends\". For the white men, then, the medal stands for friendship: D. The bitter irony is that the same evening Meka is arrested and beaten, and he realises the \"friendship\" was empty. The trap: A, harmonious relationship, which sounds similar, but the word the novel keeps returning to is friendship. Memory tip: medal = a token of the white man's \"friendship\" that Meka finally sees through.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nThe Ministry of Love is concerned with|peace and freedom|torture and pain|joy and peace|hatred and pain": "Nineteen Eighty-Four (1949) by George Orwell pictures Oceania, a state where the Party controls every thought. Its four ministries have names that mean the opposite of what they do: the Ministry of Peace makes war, the Ministry of Plenty rations food, the Ministry of Truth spreads lies, and the Ministry of Love is where prisoners are tortured until they love Big Brother. So the Ministry of Love is concerned with torture and pain: B. Winston is broken there by O'Brien, finally in Room 101. The trap: D, hatred and pain, which is close; but the Ministry's work is torture, not hatred (hatred is stirred up by the Two Minutes Hate). Memory tip: in Newspeak, every ministry's name is a lie.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nThe instruments of power and torture belong to|the government|the party|the thought police|individuals": "In Oceania everything belongs to the Party: the Thought Police, the telescreens, the Ministry of Love and all the means of torture. Big Brother is the Party's face, and O'Brien, an Inner Party member, carries out Winston's torture in its name. So the instruments of power and torture belong to the Party: B. Ordinary individuals (D) own nothing and have no power. The trap: C, the Thought Police, because they arrest people; but they are themselves one of the Party's instruments, not the owner of the power. Memory tip: in Nineteen Eighty-Four, \"the Party\" is the answer to every question about power.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nThe action in the novel is built around|Winston Smith|O'Brien|Julia|Big Brother": "The novel follows one man, Winston Smith, a minor Party member who secretly hates the Party. The story is told through his eyes: his secret diary, his love affair with Julia, his trust in O'Brien, his arrest and torture, and his final surrender, when he \"loves Big Brother\". Everything in the plot is built around him: A. O'Brien (B) and Julia (C) are important only through what they do to or with Winston, and Big Brother (D) may not even exist as a person. Memory tip: the protagonist is the character whose experience carries the whole plot.",
 "|Based on George Orwell's Nineteen Eighty-Four: \nWinston Smith works in the Records Department of the Ministry of|love|truth|peace|plenty": "Winston works in the Records Department of the Ministry of Truth (Minitrue). His job is to rewrite old newspaper articles and records so that the past always agrees with what the Party says now; the originals go down the \"memory hole\" to be burnt. So the answer is truth: B. The irony is part of Orwell's satire: the Ministry of Truth produces lies, just as the Ministry of Love tortures and the Ministry of Peace makes war. Memory tip: Winston rewrites history at the Ministry of Truth: \"Who controls the past controls the future.\"",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nRubadiri's An African Thunderstorm can be described as|didactic|dramatic|traditional|satirical": "An African Thunderstorm by the Malawian poet David Rubadiri describes a storm approaching an African village: the clouds \"come hurrying\", the wind whirls \"like a madman chasing nothing\", trees bend, women dash about and the lightning and thunder break. The poem is full of action, movement and suspense, with short broken lines that copy the wind's sudden changes. Writing that presents events with the tension and movement of a stage scene is called dramatic: B. The poem does not mainly teach a lesson (A), mock anyone (D) or follow a traditional oral form (C). Memory tip: lots of movement and suspense = dramatic.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \n'Let me ask for what reason or rhyme women refuse to marry?\nWoman cannot exist except by man,\nwhat is there in that to vex some of them so?'\n\nThe lines above from Give Me The Minstrel's Seat is an example of|pathetic fallacy|chiasmus|ironical statement|rhetorical question": "A rhetorical question is asked for effect, to make a point, not to get an answer. The speaker in Give Me the Minstrel's Seat asks \"for what reason or rhyme women refuse to marry?\" and \"what is there in that to vex some of them so?\" He does not wait for a reply: he is really stating his view that women need men and should not object. So the lines are an example of rhetorical question: D. Pathetic fallacy (A) gives human feelings to nature, and chiasmus (B) reverses the order of words in two parallel phrases; neither is here. The trap: C, ironical statement; but the lines are plainly in question form. Memory tip: a question mark with no answer expected = rhetorical question.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \n'Time's winged chariot'\n\nThe line above from Marvell's To His Coy Mistress depicts|how fast time flies|the usefulness of time|the measurement of time|how fast events unfold": "In To His Coy Mistress Andrew Marvell's speaker urges a shy lady not to delay their love: \"But at my back I always hear / Time's wingèd chariot hurrying near.\" A chariot is a fast war vehicle, and giving it wings makes it faster still; it is always \"hurrying near\". The image shows how fast time flies and how soon youth and life will end: A. The poem is about the shortness of time, not its usefulness (B) or measurement (C). The trap: D, because \"how fast events unfold\" also speaks of speed; but the chariot is time itself, not events. Memory tip: To His Coy Mistress = carpe diem, \"seize the day\", because time flies.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nLawrence's Bat opens with the description of the|scene|creatures|bats|scenery": "Bat by D.H. Lawrence begins on a terrace in Florence at evening: the sun sets \"beyond Pisa, beyond the mountains of Carrara\", light lingers on the river Arno and the Ponte Vecchio. Only after this description of the landscape does the speaker notice the darting creatures he first takes for swallows, then realises are bats, which disgust him. So the poem opens with a description of the scenery: D. The creatures and bats (B, C) come later. The trap: A, scene, which sounds close; but \"scene\" is vague (it can mean any setting or episode), while \"scenery\" is exactly the natural landscape the poem paints. Memory tip: Bat moves from a calm sunset picture to the speaker's disgust at the bats.\n'Scene' (A) describes the same opening, so it is accepted too.",
 "|Based on the prescribed poems: \nThe theme of Eliot's Journey of the Magi is|quest for salvation|escape from persecution|nature|journey": "In Journey of the Magi T.S. Eliot lets one of the three wise men look back on the hard journey to the birth of Christ: the cold, the complaining camel men, the hostile towns. At the end he wonders whether they were led \"all that way for / Birth or Death\", because seeing Christ meant the death of his old life. The poem's theme is the painful search for salvation and spiritual rebirth: A. \"Journey\" (D) is only the subject on the surface; the theme is what the journey means. Nothing in the poem is about nature (C) or escaping persecution (B). The trap: D, because \"journey\" is in the title. Memory tip: the theme is the idea behind the story, not the story itself.",
 "|A device used by a writer to recall past events in a literary work is|interlude|anti-climax|flashback|foreshadowing": "A flashback is a scene that interrupts the present action of a story to show something that happened earlier, so the writer can reveal background or explain a character. That is the device used to recall past events: C. An interlude (A) is a short break between parts; an anti-climax (B) is a disappointing let-down after build-up. The trap: D, foreshadowing, because it also breaks normal time order; but foreshadowing hints at what will happen LATER, while a flashback goes BACK. Memory tip: flashback = back to the past; foreshadowing = shadow of the future.",
 "|A paragraph in prose is equivalent to a|trope in poetry|verse in poetry|stanza in poetry|meter in poetry": "A paragraph is a group of sentences that develops one idea and forms a unit of a prose work. In poetry, the matching unit is the stanza: a group of lines set apart and often sharing a rhyme pattern. So a paragraph in prose is equivalent to a stanza in poetry: C. The trap: B, verse, which is close because some people call a stanza a \"verse\"; but in literary terms a verse is a single line of poetry (or poetry in general), which matches a sentence more than a paragraph. A trope (A) is a figure of speech, and metre (D) is the pattern of beats. Memory tip: sentence : line :: paragraph : stanza.",
 "|A fable is a brief narrative illustrating wisdom and|urgency|origin|custom|truth": "A fable is a short story, often with animals as characters, that ends in a moral lesson, such as Aesop's \"The Tortoise and the Hare\" (slow and steady wins the race). Its purpose is to illustrate wisdom and truth about how people behave: D. Fables are not about urgency (A); a story explaining the origin of things (B) is a myth or legend; and custom (C) is not what a fable sets out to teach. Memory tip: fable = animals + moral truth.",
 "|A device used in poetry to achieve emphasis or stress a point is known as|rhyme|assonance|repetition|alliteration": "Repetition is the use of the same word, phrase, line or idea more than once, and poets use it to emphasise or stress a point and to build rhythm. A refrain that returns at the end of each stanza, or a phrase repeated at the start of lines, drives the idea home. So the device is repetition: C. Rhyme (A), assonance (B) and alliteration (D) are sound devices; they add music, but stressing an idea is the special job of repetition. The trap: D, alliteration, because it is itself a repeating of sounds; but it repeats consonant sounds, not the point being made. Memory tip: to stress it, say it again.",
 "|A literary work that ridicules the shortcomings of people or ideas is|a masque|a satire|an irony|a fable": "Satire is writing that uses humour, irony, exaggeration and ridicule to expose and criticise the faults of people, ideas or society, often hoping to correct them. Animal Farm and Nineteen Eighty-Four by Orwell and Oyono's The Old Man and the Medal are satires. So a work that ridicules the shortcomings of people or ideas is a satire: B. A masque (A) is a courtly entertainment with music and dance; a fable (D) teaches a moral. The trap: C, irony, because satire often uses irony; but irony is a device, not a kind of literary work. Memory tip: satire = ridicule with a purpose.",
 "|The figure of speech in which the writer means the exact opposite of what he says is|satire|irony|paradox|metaphor": "Irony is the figure in which the words say one thing but the speaker means the opposite, as when someone looks at heavy rain and says, \"Lovely weather.\" That is verbal irony: B. A paradox (C) is a statement that seems to contradict itself but holds a truth (\"The child is father of the man\"); a metaphor (D) is a direct comparison. The trap: A, satire, because satire often uses irony; but satire is a kind of work that ridicules, not a figure of speech. Memory tip: irony says the opposite of what it means.",
 "|Action without speech in a play is|soliloquy|aside|epilogue|mime": "Mime is acting without words: the performer tells the story through gesture, facial expression and movement alone. A scene in a play acted out in silence is mime (also called dumb show): D. A soliloquy (A) and an aside (B) are both forms of speech; an epilogue (C) is a speech at the end of a play. Memory tip: mime = mute.",
 "|A literary work that teaches morals is said to be|impressive|didactic|instructive|corrective": "A didactic work is one written mainly to teach, especially to teach morals, such as fables, parables and many morality plays. \"Didactic\" is the literary term: B. \"Instructive\" (C) has a similar everyday meaning, and so does \"corrective\" (D), but they are not the critical term for a moralising work. The trap: C, instructive, because it is almost a synonym; when JAMB asks what a work \"is said to be\", it wants the technical term. Memory tip: didactic comes from the Greek for \"teach\".",
 "|A mistake committed by the hero which leads to his downfall is known as|comic relief|terse|climax|tragic flaw": "In tragedy, the tragic flaw (Greek hamartia) is the error of judgement or weakness of character in the hero that leads to his downfall: Okonkwo's rashness and fear of weakness, Macbeth's ambition, Othello's jealousy. So the answer is tragic flaw: D. The climax (C) is the turning point of the plot, not the hero's mistake; comic relief (A) is a light moment that eases tension. Memory tip: hamartia = the hero's fatal flaw.",
 "|The speech made by a character to himself on stage is|monologue|epilogue|aside|soliloquy": "A soliloquy is a speech in which a character, usually alone on stage, speaks his thoughts aloud as if to himself, so the audience learns what he really feels, like Hamlet's \"To be or not to be\". That is the answer: D. A monologue (A) is a long speech by one character, but it is usually spoken to other characters. An aside (C) is a short remark to the audience that others on stage do not hear. The trap: A, monologue, because both are long speeches by one person; the soliloquy is the one spoken to oneself. Memory tip: solo + loquy (speech) = speaking alone.",
 "|'Woman as a clam, on the sea's crescent\nI saw your jealous eye quench the sea's\nFluorescence, dance on the pulse incessant.'\nWole Soyinka, Night\n\nThe lines above suggest that women are|magicians|covetous|dogmatic|seers": "In Night Wole Soyinka addresses the night, picturing it as a woman: \"Woman as a clam, on the sea's crescent / I saw your jealous eye quench the sea's / Fluorescence.\" The key word is \"jealous\": the eye puts out the sea's glowing light, as if it envies the brightness and wants to take it away. Wanting what belongs to another is being covetous: B. Nothing in the lines suggests magic (A), stubborn belief (C) or prophecy (D). Memory tip: jealous eye = covetous, envious desire.",
 "|'Busy old fool, unruly sun,\nWhy dost thou thus,\nThrough windows, and through curtains call on us?'\nJ. Donne, The Sun Rising\n\nThe excerpt above suggests|praise of nature|invitation to the sun|welcoming the sun|indictment of the sun": "In The Sun Rising John Donne's speaker, in bed with his beloved, is woken by the sunrise and scolds the sun: \"Busy old fool, unruly sun, / Why dost thou thus, / Through windows, and through curtains call on us?\" He calls it a fool and unruly and demands why it disturbs them. To indict is to accuse or condemn, so the lines are an indictment of the sun: D. The speaker is not praising nature (A), and far from inviting or welcoming the sun (B, C) he wants it to go away. Memory tip: insults and an angry \"why\" = indictment.",
 "|'Busy old fool, unruly sun,\nWhy dost thou thus,\nThrough windows, and through curtains call on us?'\nJ. Donne, The Sun Rising\n\nThe figure of speech involved in the lines above is|simile|personification|epigram|pun": "Personification gives human qualities to something non-human. Donne treats the sun as a person: it is a \"busy old fool\", \"unruly\", and it \"calls on\" the lovers through windows and curtains like a nosy visitor. So the figure is personification: B. (He also speaks to the sun directly, which is apostrophe, but that is not among the options.) There is no \"like\" or \"as\" comparison (A), no witty saying (C) and no play on a word's double meaning (D). Memory tip: a sun that is an \"old fool\" has been given a human character.",
 "|'Will no one tell me what she sings?\nPerhaps the plaintive numbers flow\nFor old, unhappy, far-off things,\nAnd battles long ago.\nOr is it some more humble lay,\nFamiliar matter of today?'\n\nThe lines above show that the persona|does not understand the girl's language|is so much in love with the girl|so hates the words of the girl|understands the girl's songs": "These lines come from The Solitary Reaper by William Wordsworth. In the Scottish Highlands the speaker hears a girl singing as she reaps, in a language he cannot follow (Gaelic). He asks, \"Will no one tell me what she sings?\" and then can only guess: perhaps an old sad song of \"battles long ago\", perhaps some \"humble lay\" about everyday life. His questions and guesses show that he does not understand the girl's language: A. He loves her song, not the girl (B), and he certainly does not understand it (D). Memory tip: \"Will no one tell me...?\" = he cannot understand.",
 "|'Will no one tell me what she sings?\nPerhaps the plaintive numbers flow\nFor old, unhappy, far-off things,\nAnd battles long ago.\nOr is it some more humble lay,\nFamiliar matter of today?'\n\nThe lines end in a literary device known as|transferred epithet|rhetorical question|irony|conceit": "A rhetorical question is a question asked for effect, without expecting an answer. The extract ends: \"Or is it some more humble lay, / Familiar matter of today?\" The speaker is not really asking anyone; he is wondering aloud about the song he cannot understand. So the lines end in a rhetorical question: B. A transferred epithet (A) moves an adjective to the wrong noun (as in \"a sleepless night\"); a conceit (D) is an elaborate, far-fetched comparison. Memory tip: question mark + no one to answer = rhetorical question.",
 "|'Oh incomprehensible God!\nShall my pilot be\nMy inborn stars to that\nFinal call to thee...'\n\nThe literary device used in the first line is|passion|apostrophe|burlesque|rhetoric": "Apostrophe is addressing someone absent, dead, divine or a thing as if it could hear. The lines, from Gabriel Okara's The Call of the River Nun, open with \"Oh incomprehensible God!\", a cry spoken directly to God, so the device in the first line is apostrophe: B. \"Passion\" (A) is a feeling, not a literary device; burlesque (C) is mocking imitation; rhetoric (D) is the art of persuasive speech in general. Memory tip: \"O\" + name of someone who cannot reply = apostrophe.",
 "|'Busy old fool, unruly sun,\nWhy dost thou thus...'\nJ. Donne, The Sun Rising\n\nFrom the lines above, the poet sees the sun as|a necessary evil|a light provider|illumination after darkness|an unnecessary evil": "In The Sun Rising Donne's lover scolds the sun as a \"busy old fool\" for waking him and his beloved. Yet the sun is not wicked: it gives light and warmth, and later in the poem the speaker even says the sun's duty is to warm the world. To the lovers it is something the world cannot do without but which annoys them by intruding: a necessary evil: A. \"A light provider\" (B) and \"illumination after darkness\" (C) are true of the sun but miss the speaker's irritation. The trap: D, an unnecessary evil, because the speaker clearly wishes the sun away; but he never suggests the world could do without it. Memory tip: necessary evil = something you need but resent.",
 "|'The body perishes, the heart stays young.\nThe platter wears away with serving food.\nNo log retains its bark when old,\nNo lover peaceful while the rival weeps.'\n\nThe theme of the poem above is|permanence of love|decaying nature of wood|non-peaceful nature of love|diminishing nature of love": "The extract lists things that wear away with time: the body perishes, the platter wears thin from serving food, the old log loses its bark. Against all this decay stands one thing that does not age: \"the heart stays young\". The heart is the seat of love, so the poem's theme is the permanence of love, which outlasts the body: A. The decaying wood (B) is only one example. The trap: D, diminishing nature of love, because the poem is full of things that diminish; but the heart is the exception that \"stays young\". Memory tip: in a list of contrasts, the theme usually lies in the one item that is different.",
 "|'The body perishes, the heart stays young.\nThe platter wears away with serving food.\nNo log retains its bark when old,\nNo lover peaceful while the rival weeps.'\n\n'No lover peaceful while the rival weeps' means that|there is true and permanent love|the two lovers weep together|the pain of one lover is felt by the other|there is no permanent love": "\"No lover peaceful while the rival weeps\" says that a lover cannot be at peace while the other person in the love relationship is weeping: the sorrow of one is shared by the other, so neither can rest while the other suffers. That is option C, the pain of one lover is felt by the other. Nothing in the line says the lovers weep together (B), and it does not state that love is permanent or impermanent (A, D). Memory tip: read the line as cause and effect: \"while X weeps, Y cannot be at peace\".",
 "|'Will college make you a better Olokun priest?\nWill it make you serve our ancestors better?\nLook at me. An able-bodied, strong-hearted priest of\nOlokun. Did I go to college?'\nGrace Osifo, Dizzy Angel\n\nThe literary device used in the passage above is|simile|parallelism|onomatopoeia|metaphor": "Parallelism is the repeated use of the same grammatical pattern in successive phrases or sentences. Here the speaker, a priest of Olokun (the Yoruba sea deity), asks: \"Will college make you a better Olokun priest? / Will it make you serve our ancestors better?\" Both questions share the same pattern (\"Will college/it make you... better?\"), which gives the speech force and rhythm: B. There is no \"like\" or \"as\" comparison (A), no word imitating a sound (C) and no direct comparison of one thing to another (D). Memory tip: same pattern, side by side = parallelism."
};
